window.TRIVIA_ARTICLES = [{"id":"kanbun-yomi-invention","icon":"📜","tags":["japan","reading","history"],"title":{"en":"How Japan Kept 訓読み: Reading Chinese Characters with Native Words","ja":"日本が訓読みを守った道：漢字を大和言葉で読む","ko":"일본은 어떻게 훈독(訓読み)을 지켜냈는가","zh":"日本如何留住训读：用大和语词读汉字","yue":"日本點樣留住訓讀：用大和話讀漢字","vi":"Nhật Bản đã giữ được Kun-yomi như thế nào: đọc chữ Hán bằng từ bản địa","th":"คุนโยมิ: ญี่ปุ่นอ่านอักษรจีนด้วยคำของตัวเอง","id":"Kun-yomi: cara Jepang membaca aksara Tionghoa dengan kata asli","hi":"जापान ने कुन-योमि को कैसे बचाए रखा: चीनी वर्णों को देशी शब्दों से पढ़ना","de":"Wie Japan Kun-yomi bewahrte: Chinesische Schriftzeichen mit einheimischen Wörtern lesen","fr":"Comment le Japon a conservé le kun-yomi : lire les sinogrammes avec des mots autochtones","it":"Come il Giappone conservò il kun-yomi: leggere i caratteri cinesi con parole native","es":"Cómo Japón conservó el kun-yomi: leer caracteres chinos con palabras nativas","pt":"Como o Japão preservou o kun-yomi: lendo caracteres chineses com palavras nativas","ru":"Как Япония сохранила кунъёми: чтение китайских иероглифов с помощью родных слов","uk":"Як Японія зберегла кунйомі: читання китайських ієрогліфів рідними словами","ar":"كون-يومي: قراءة الحروف الصينية بكلمات يابانية","he":"איך יפן שמרה על קון-יומי: סימנים סיניים במילים יפניות","sw":"Jinsi Japani ilivyodumisha kun-yomi: kusoma herufi za Kichina kwa maneno ya asili"},"summary":{"en":"By around 700 CE, Japanese scribes used 漢字 both for Chinese-derived readings and for native Yamato words. This practice established the distinction between on-yomi and kun-yomi readings.","ja":"700年頃、日本の文人たちは漢字を中国語由来の音と大和言葉の双方に対応させた。これが音読みと訓読みという二つの読みの基礎になった。","ko":"700년경 일본 서기들은 한자를 중국어계 음뿐 아니라 고유 야마토어 단어에도 대응시켰다. 이 관습이 음독과 훈독의 구분으로 이어졌다.","zh":"约公元700年，日本文人不再只用汉字记录汉语读音，而是将其对应到固有大和语词，使一个汉字同时拥有音读与训读两套系统。","yue":"大約公元700年，日本嘅文人唔單止用漢字嚟記中文讀音，仲將漢字對應到固有大和語詞，令一個漢字同時有音讀同訓讀兩套讀法。","vi":"Khoảng năm 700, các nhà chép sách Nhật Bản dùng chữ Hán không chỉ với âm gốc Hán mà còn để ghi các từ Yamato bản địa. Cách dùng này dẫn đến sự phân biệt giữa âm on và âm kun.","th":"ราวปี ค.ศ. 700 นักเขียนชาวญี่ปุ่นก้าวกระโดดครั้งใหญ่ คือไม่ได้ใช้อักษรจีนแค่ตามเสียงจีน แต่ยังผูกอักษรเข้ากับคำพื้นเมืองยามาโตะ อักษรตัวเดียวจึงมีวิธีอ่านสองแบบที่ต่างกันโดยสิ้นเชิง","id":"Sekitar tahun 700 M, para juru tulis Jepang memakai 漢字 baik untuk bacaan turunan Tionghoa maupun untuk kata-kata asli Yamato. Praktik inilah yang memantapkan pembedaan bacaan on'yomi dan kun'yomi.","hi":"लगभग 700 ई. तक जापानी लिपिक 漢字 का प्रयोग चीनी से आए पाठों के लिए भी करते थे और मूल यामातो शब्दों के लिए भी। इसी चलन से ओन-योमि और कुन-योमि पाठों का भेद स्थापित हुआ।","de":"Um 700 n. Chr. nutzten japanische Schreiber 漢字 sowohl für chinesisch abgeleitete Lesungen als auch für einheimische Yamato-Wörter. Daraus entstand die Unterscheidung zwischen On-yomi und Kun-yomi.","fr":"Vers 700 apr. J.-C., les scribes japonais utilisaient les 漢字 à la fois pour des lectures d'origine chinoise et pour des mots yamato indigènes. Cette pratique a fixé la distinction entre lectures on-yomi et kun-yomi.","it":"Intorno al 700 d.C. i copisti giapponesi usavano i 漢字 sia per le letture di origine cinese sia per le parole native yamato. Da questa pratica nacque la distinzione tra letture on-yomi e kun-yomi.","es":"Hacia el año 700 d.C., los escribas japoneses usaban los 漢字 tanto para lecturas de origen chino como para palabras yamato nativas. Esa práctica estableció la distinción entre las lecturas on-yomi y kun-yomi.","pt":"Por volta de 700 d.C., os escribas japoneses usavam os 漢字 tanto para leituras de origem chinesa quanto para palavras yamato nativas. Essa prática estabeleceu a distinção entre as leituras on-yomi e kun-yomi.","ru":"Примерно к 700 году н. э. японские писцы использовали 漢字 и для чтений китайского происхождения, и для исконных слов ямато. Так сложилось различие между чтениями онъёми и кунъёми.","uk":"Приблизно до 700 р. н. е. японські писарі використовували 漢字 і для читань китайського походження, і для рідних слів ямато. Так усталилося розрізнення читань онйомі та кунйомі.","ar":"بحلول نحو عام 700م، كان الكتبة اليابانيون يستخدمون 漢字 للقراءات المأخوذة من الصينية وللكلمات اليامَتو الأصيلة معًا. ومن هذه الممارسة نشأ التمييز بين قراءات أون-يومي وقراءات كون-يومي.","he":"כבר בסביבות שנת 700 לספירה השתמשו סופרים יפנים ב-漢字 גם לקריאות שמקורן בסינית וגם למילים יפניות מקומיות (יאמאטו). מכאן נולדה ההבחנה בין און-יומי לקון-יומי.","sw":"Karibu mwaka 700 BK, waandishi wa Kijapani walitumia 漢字 kwa usomaji uliotokana na Kichina na pia kwa maneno ya asili ya Yamato. Desturi hii ndiyo iliyoweka tofauti kati ya usomaji wa on-yomi na kun-yomi."},"body":{"en":"<h3>A Script Adopted, Then Transformed</h3><p>When Chinese writing first reached the Japanese archipelago — probably via Korea in the 4th–5th centuries CE — it arrived as a complete package: a set of characters each bearing a fixed Chinese pronunciation and meaning. At first, writing in Japan meant writing Chinese. Characters were borrowed purely for their sounds, mainly to spell Japanese names, as on the Inariyama sword inscription (usually dated 471 CE, though 531 has also been argued). This sound-only use, later developed into what scholars call <em>man'yōgana</em> (万葉仮名), soon spread to whole words and poems. The word 「あめ」 might be written 阿米 — characters meaning 'hillock' and 'rice', chosen purely for their sound, <em>a-me</em>, with no regard to meaning.</p><h3>Semantic Readings</h3><p>By around 700 CE, the age of the <em>Kojiki</em> (712) and <em>Nihon Shoki</em> (720), another practice is in full view. Scribes read a character not by its Chinese sound but by its <em>meaning</em>, reaching into the native Yamato lexicon for the Japanese word that corresponded. The character 山 (Chinese: <em>shān</em>) was now also read <em>yama</em>, the native word for mountain. 水 (<em>shuǐ</em>) was also <em>mizu</em>. This is <strong>訓読み (kun-yomi)</strong>, \"semantic reading\". At first it was anything but tidy. A single character could carry many different native glosses, and the 12th-century dictionary <em>Ruiju Myōgishō</em> lists more than 30 for some characters. Only by the Muromachi period (14th–16th centuries) had most characters settled down to a few fixed kun readings.</p><div class='trivia-actions'><button type='button' class='trivia-action' data-action='setchar' data-char='山'>Show 山 on map</button><button type='button' class='trivia-action' data-action='focus' data-code='ja_kun'>Highlight Japanese kun-yomi layer</button></div><h3>Comparison with Other Sinosphere Traditions</h3><p>Korea, Vietnam, and other Sinosphere societies received Chinese characters too, and the idea of reading a character with a native word occurred to them as well. Korean scribes annotated Literary Chinese with 석독구결 (釋讀口訣) so that a Chinese text could be read out in native Korean words and Korean word order, and Korean hanja are still <em>named</em> by a native gloss bound to the Sino-Korean sound: 水 is 물 수 (<em>mul su</em>), 'water-SU'. Vietnamese Chữ Nôm went further, writing words with characters picked for their meaning so routinely that a diacritic (<em>nháy</em>, 𖿱) was devised to flag the indigenous reading — 本 read the Sino-Vietnamese way is <em>bản</em>, but 本𖿱 is <em>vốn</em>. In Japan, native readings became part of ordinary writing and remain in use; comparable practices elsewhere were tied more closely to classical scholarship or to scripts that later fell out of use. Japan institutionalized the dual system: a modern kanji dictionary lists each character's <em>on-yomi</em> (音読み, Chinese-derived sound) alongside its <em>kun-yomi</em> (訓読み, native word), if it has one. Some characters never got a native reading because the thing itself came from China: 菊 \"chrysanthemum\" is <em>kiku</em>, which sounds native but is actually its Chinese reading. Japan also kept the matching technique for whole texts: <em>kanbun kundoku</em>, reading Classical Chinese straight off the page as Japanese, with small marks such as レ and 一・二 telling the reader which word to jump back to.</p><h3>Man'yōgana: The Bridge</h3><p>The <em>Man'yōshū</em> anthology (c. 759) is the classic example of pre-kana phonetic writing. Poets used characters as pure syllables: 安 (<em>ān</em>) = <em>a</em>, 以 (<em>yǐ</em>) = <em>i</em>, 宇 (<em>yǔ</em>) = <em>u</em>. Over the following centuries these phonetic uses were simplified into the two kana syllabaries: <em>hiragana</em>, from flowing cursive forms of whole characters, and <em>katakana</em>, from fragments of characters that monks squeezed between the lines of Chinese texts as reading aids, but the semantic kun-yomi layer was retained in parallel. As a result, modern Japanese runs four scripts side by side, kanji (with both on and kun readings), hiragana, katakana and rōmaji, each with its own partly overlapping jobs.</p><h3>Readings in Modern Dictionaries</h3><p>Today, the kanji 生 alone carries <em>on-yomi</em> セイ (<em>sei</em>) and ショウ (<em>shō</em>), plus <em>kun-yomi</em> い(きる) <em>i(kiru)</em>, うまれる <em>umareru</em>, は(える) <em>ha(eru)</em>, なま <em>nama</em> — at least a dozen valid readings. The on-yomi come from different waves of borrowing (<em>shō</em> is the older Go-on, <em>sei</em> the Tang-era Kan-on, and dictionaries even record a rare later Tō-on, <em>san</em>), while the kun-yomi preserve native Yamato vocabulary. Modern mixed-script text can therefore make several historical layers visible at once.</p><div class='trivia-actions'><button type='button' class='trivia-action' data-action='panto' data-lat='34.69' data-lng='135.50' data-zoom='6'>Pan to Nara / Osaka</button></div>"},"sources":["Seeley, Christopher. A History of Writing in Japan. Leiden: Brill, 1991.","Frellesvig, Bjarke. A History of the Japanese Language. Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 2010.","Miller, Roy Andrew. The Japanese Language. Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1967.",{"text":"Man'yōshū — Wikipedia overview","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Man%27y%C5%8Dsh%C5%AB"},{"text":"On'yomi and Kun'yomi — Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kanji#On%27yomi_and_Kun%27yomi"},"Vovin, Alexander. A Descriptive and Comparative Grammar of Western Old Japanese. Folkestone: Global Oriental, 2005."]},{"id":"kanbun-kaeriten-system","icon":"🔢","tags":["japan","literacy","writing"],"title":{"en":"返り点: How Japan Reads Chinese Texts Backwards into Japanese","ja":"返り点：漢文をひっくり返して日本語に読む技法","ko":"반점(返り点): 일본이 한문을 일본어 어순으로 읽는 방법","zh":"返り点：日本如何将汉文倒读成日语","yue":"返り點：日本點樣將漢文倒讀成日語","vi":"返り点: cách Nhật Bản đọc ngược văn bản Hán thành tiếng Nhật","th":"返り点: ญี่ปุ่นอ่านข้อความจีนย้อนกลับเป็นภาษาญี่ปุ่นอย่างไร","id":"返り点: membaca teks Tionghoa secara terbalik dalam bahasa Jepang","hi":"返り点: जापान कैसे चीनी पाठ को उल्टे क्रम में जापानी में पढ़ता है","de":"返り点: Wie Japan chinesische Texte rückwärts auf Japanisch liest","fr":"返り点 : comment le Japon lit les textes chinois à rebours en japonais","it":"返り点: come il Giappone legge il cinese all'incontrario","es":"返り点: cómo Japón lee los textos chinos en orden japonés","pt":"返り点: como o Japão lê o chinês de trás para a frente","ru":"返り点: как Япония читает китайские тексты в японском порядке","uk":"返り点: як Японія читає китайські тексти японською у зворотному порядку","ar":"返り点: كيف يقرأ اليابانيون النصوص الصينية معكوسةً باليابانية","he":"返り点: איך יפן קוראת סינית בסדר הפוך","sw":"返り点: jinsi Japani inavyosoma maandishi ya Kichina kinyumenyume kwa Kijapani"},"summary":{"en":"Japanese scholars devised 返り点 (kaeriten) — tiny diacritical marks placed beside Chinese characters — to reorder SVO Chinese syntax into SOV Japanese without rewriting a single character.","ja":"日本の学者たちは漢字の隣に小さな記号「返り点」を付けることで、一字も書き換えずにSVO語順の漢文をSOV語順の日本語に読み直す独自技法を作り出した。","ko":"일본 학자들은 한자 옆에 작은 반점(返り点)을 붙임으로써, 한 글자도 바꾸지 않고 SVO 어순의 한문을 SOV 어순의 일본어로 읽어낼 수 있는 독자적 기법을 만들어냈다.","zh":"日本学者在汉字旁边附上小记号「返り点」，无需改动任何文字，即可将SVO语序的汉文转读为SOV语序的日语。","yue":"日本學者喺漢字旁邊加上細小嘅符號「返り點」，唔使改動任何文字，就可以將SVO語序嘅漢文轉讀為SOV語序嘅日語。","vi":"Các học giả Nhật Bản đã tạo ra 返り点 (kaeriten) — những ký hiệu nhỏ bên cạnh chữ Hán — để sắp xếp lại cú pháp SVO của tiếng Trung thành SOV tiếng Nhật mà không cần viết lại một chữ.","th":"นักวิชาการญี่ปุ่นคิดค้น 返り点 (ไคเอริเท็น) เครื่องหมายเล็ก ๆ ข้างอักษรจีน เพื่อเรียงไวยากรณ์ SVO ของจีนใหม่เป็น SOV แบบญี่ปุ่น โดยไม่ต้องเขียนตัวอักษรใหม่แม้แต่ตัวเดียว","id":"Para cendekiawan Jepang merancang 返り点 (kaeriten), tanda kecil di samping aksara Tionghoa, untuk mengubah urutan SVO bahasa Tionghoa menjadi SOV bahasa Jepang tanpa menulis ulang satu aksara pun.","hi":"जापानी विद्वानों ने 返り点 (काएरिटेन) बनाए — चीनी अक्षरों के बगल में लगाए जाने वाले छोटे चिह्न, जिनसे एक भी अक्षर बदले बिना SVO क्रम वाला चीनी वाक्य SOV क्रम में जापानी की तरह पढ़ा जा सकता है।","de":"Japanische Gelehrte entwickelten 返り点 (Kaeriten), kleine diakritische Zeichen neben chinesischen Schriftzeichen, um die SVO-Wortstellung des Chinesischen in die SOV-Wortstellung des Japanischen umzuordnen, ohne ein einziges Zeichen neu zu schreiben.","fr":"Les érudits japonais inventèrent les 返り点 (kaeriten), de minuscules signes placés à côté des sinogrammes, pour réordonner la syntaxe SVO du chinois en SOV japonais sans réécrire un seul caractère.","it":"I dotti giapponesi idearono i 返り点 (kaeriten), piccoli segni accanto ai caratteri cinesi, per leggere la sintassi SVO del cinese nell'ordine SOV del giapponese senza riscrivere un solo carattere.","es":"Los eruditos japoneses idearon los 返り点 (kaeriten), pequeñas marcas junto a los caracteres chinos, para pasar la sintaxis SVO del chino al orden SOV japonés sin reescribir ni un solo carácter.","pt":"Os estudiosos japoneses criaram os 返り点 (kaeriten) — pequenas marcas diacríticas ao lado dos caracteres chineses — para reorganizar a sintaxe SVO do chinês em SOV japonês sem reescrever um único caractere.","ru":"Японские учёные разработали 返り点 (каэритэн) — маленькие диакритические знаки рядом с китайскими иероглифами — для преобразования SVO-синтаксиса китайского языка в SOV-японский без изменения ни одного знака.","uk":"Японські вчені розробили 返り点 (каерітен), маленькі позначки поруч із китайськими ієрогліфами, щоб читати китайський порядок слів SVO як японський SOV, не переписуючи жодного знака.","ar":"ابتكر العلماء اليابانيون 返り点 (كايريتن)، وهي علامات تشكيل صغيرة توضع بجانب الحروف الصينية، لإعادة ترتيب بنية SVO الصينية إلى SOV اليابانية دون إعادة كتابة حرف واحد.","he":"מלומדים יפנים המציאו את 返り点 (קאֶריטֶן), סימונים זעירים ליד הסימנים הסיניים, כדי להפוך את סדר ה-SVO הסיני לסדר SOV יפני בלי לשנות אף סימן.","sw":"Wasomi wa Japani walibuni 返り点 (kaeriten), alama ndogo za diakriti pembeni ya herufi za Kichina, ili kupanga upya sintaksia ya SVO ya Kichina kuwa SOV ya Kijapani bila kuandika upya herufi moja."},"body":{"en":"<h3>The Problem: Two Languages, Opposite Word Order</h3><p>Classical Chinese (漢文, kanbun) is Subject–Verb–Object: 「我愛汝」 means 'I love you' in that order. Classical Japanese is Subject–Object–Verb: the same idea is 「我汝ヲ愛ス」. When Japanese scholars received the Chinese Confucian and Buddhist canon, they faced a dilemma: how to read it aloud in intelligible Japanese without translating it into a separate Japanese text. Their solution was a set of annotations that supplied Japanese readings and marked a different reading order.</p><h3>レ点: The Simplest Mark</h3><p>The <strong>レ点 (re-ten)</strong> looks like the katakana レ and is placed at the lower-left corner of a character. It means: 'read the next character first, then come back here.' If 読書 ('read books') has a レ point on 読, you read 書 first, then 読 — giving the SOV reading 書ヲ読ム ('books [object] read'). A single tiny mark reverses two adjacent characters.</p><div class='trivia-actions'><button type='button' class='trivia-action' data-action='setchar' data-char='見'>Show 見 on map</button><button type='button' class='trivia-action' data-action='focus' data-code='ja'>Highlight the on-yomi layer</button></div><h3>一二点 and 上下点: Multi-Level Reordering</h3><p>For longer inversions — where the return jump reaches back two or more characters, past at least one character in between — scholars used <strong>一二点 (ichi-ni-ten)</strong>: the numerals 一, 二, 三 written beside characters to number the return. You read straight down the line, passing over the character marked 二; when you reach the character marked 一 you read it, then jump back up to 二. In 「登二泰山一」 you read 泰, then 山, then 登 — 'climb Mount Tai' comes out as 泰山に登る. For even deeper inversions, <strong>上下点 (jō-ge-ten)</strong> and <strong>甲乙点 (kō-otsu-ten)</strong> were added. In principle, any SVO phrase — no matter how embedded — can be rerouted to SOV purely through these marks, without touching the original text.</p><h3>Regional Comparisons and Continued Use</h3><p>Chinese texts were read throughout the Sinosphere: in Korea (as 한문, hanmun), in Vietnam (as Hán văn), in Ryūkyū. Vietnamese, like Chinese, puts the verb before the object, so Vietnamese readers could simply pronounce the text in Sino-Vietnamese. Korean, verb-final like Japanese, faced the same mismatch, and Korea did the same thing on the page. Goryeo-period <strong>석독구결</strong> (釋讀口訣 <em>seokdok gugyeol</em>, also called 역독구결 'reverse-reading gugyeol'), attested from the 11th century and either written in ink or scratched into the paper with an inkless stylus (角筆), marked both the native Korean word to substitute and the order in which to jump back through the line. Similar return-reading experiments are attested for Khitan and Uyghur. What sets Japan apart is that it never stopped using the system. In Korea, seokdok gugyeol gave way to 순독구결 (順讀口訣), which reads straight through in the Chinese order; Japan's 返り点 were standardised, printed alongside the text, and taught continuously into the modern era, so that 漢文訓読 (kanbun kundoku) still lets a scholar read the <em>original unchanged Chinese text</em> aloud in natural Japanese today.</p><h3>The Pedagogical Legacy</h3><p>Until the Meiji era (1868+) and well into the 20th century, kanbun literacy was the mark of an educated Japanese person. Samurai, monks, scholars, and bureaucrats all drilled 返り点 parsing from childhood. Some Meiji-era reformers proposed abolishing kanji altogether (Mori Arinori even floated adopting English), but the proposals never won out. Even today, kanbun appears in Japanese high school curricula, and the ability to parse a phrase like 「不亦楽乎」 (<em>mata tanoshikarazu ya</em> — 'is that not also a joy?') using 一二点 (read 亦 and 楽 first, then jump back to 不) is exactly the kind of skill the kanbun section of the national university entrance exam tests.</p><div class='trivia-actions'><button type='button' class='trivia-action' data-action='panto' data-lat='35.68' data-lng='139.69' data-zoom='5'>Pan to Japan</button></div><h3>Influence on Written Japanese</h3><p>Kanbun remains visible in the formal written register of modern Japanese. Legal Japanese and newspaper editorials still use words and set phrases that came out of 漢文訓読: 〜ごとし (\"like, as\") is a hallmark of kanbun-reading style, and 〜に於いて (\"in, at\") renders the Chinese preposition 於. When a formal speech or editorial reaches for 「努力せざるべからず」 ('one must not fail to make an effort'), the stacked double negative ざる…べからず is kanbun reading style alive in the 21st century. The conventions associated with 返り点 also influenced the syntax of modern written Japanese.</p>"},"sources":["Lurie, David B. Realms of Literacy: Early Japan and the History of Writing. Cambridge: Harvard University Asia Center, 2011.","Miner, Earl. An Introduction to Japanese Court Poetry. Stanford: Stanford University Press, 1968.","Gottlieb, Nanette. Language and Society in Japan. Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 2005.",{"text":"Kanbun — Wikipedia overview","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kanbun"},{"text":"Kaeriten — Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kaeriten"},"Whitman, John, et al. 'Toward an International Vocabulary for Research on Vernacular Readings of Chinese Texts (漢文訓読 Hanwen Xundu).' Scripta 2 (2010): 61–83."]},{"id":"tangut-3000-syllables","icon":"𗼇","tags":["tangut","xixia","sinosphere","script","history"],"title":{"en":"Tangut Script: 6,000 Characters for the Tangut Language","ja":"西夏文字：西夏語のための6000字","ko":"서하 문자: 서하어를 위한 6,000자","zh":"西夏文字：采用汉字式方块结构的六千字","yue":"西夏文字：為西夏語而造嘅六千字","vi":"Chữ Tangut: 6.000 ký tự cho tiếng Tangut","th":"อักษรเซี่ยเซี่ย: 6,000 ตัวอักษรสำหรับภาษาตังกุต","id":"Aksara Tangut: 6.000 karakter untuk bahasa Tangut","hi":"तंगुत लिपि: तंगुत भाषा के लिए 6,000 वर्ण","de":"Tangutische Schrift: 6.000 Zeichen für die tangutische Sprache","fr":"L'écriture tangoute : 6 000 caractères pour la langue tangoute","it":"La scrittura tangut: 6.000 caratteri per una lingua","es":"La escritura tangut: 6.000 caracteres para la lengua tangut","pt":"A escrita tangut: 6.000 caracteres para a língua tangut","ru":"Тангутское письмо: 6000 знаков для тангутского языка","uk":"Тангутське письмо: 6000 знаків для тангутської мови","ar":"الخط التانغوتي: 6000 حرف للغة التانغوتية","he":"הכתב הטנגוטי: 6,000 סימנים לשפה הטנגוטית","sw":"Maandishi ya Tangut: herufi 6,000 za lugha ya Tangut"},"summary":{"en":"In 1036 the Tangut state launched a script of nearly 6,000 characters that look like Chinese at a glance but were invented from scratch, for a language that is only a distant cousin of Chinese.","ja":"1036年、タングートの国家は6,000字近い文字を世に出した。一見すると漢字のようだが、すべてゼロから作られた文字で、しかも書き表す言語は中国語の遠い親戚にすぎない。","ko":"1036년 탕구트 국가는 6,000자 가까운 문자를 내놓았다. 언뜻 보면 한자 같지만 완전히 새로 만든 글자들로, 중국어와는 먼 친척뻘인 언어를 적기 위한 것이었다.","zh":"1036年，西夏推出了一套近6000字的文字。乍看像汉字，其实全是从零发明的，记录的语言与汉语只是远亲。","yue":"1036年，西夏國推出一套接近6,000個字嘅文字：乍睇似漢字，但其實係由零創造，用嚟寫一門同漢語只係遠親嘅語言。","vi":"Năm 1036, nhà nước Tây Hạ cho ra đời một hệ chữ gần 6.000 ký tự, thoạt nhìn giống chữ Hán nhưng được sáng tạo hoàn toàn từ đầu, để ghi một ngôn ngữ chỉ là họ hàng xa của tiếng Hán.","th":"ในปี ค.ศ. 1036 รัฐตังกุตประกาศใช้อักษรเกือบ 6,000 ตัวที่แวบแรกดูเหมือนอักษรจีน แต่ประดิษฐ์ขึ้นใหม่ทั้งหมด เพื่อเขียนภาษาที่เป็นแค่ญาติห่าง ๆ ของภาษาจีน","id":"Pada 1036, negara Tangut meluncurkan aksara berisi hampir 6.000 karakter yang sekilas tampak seperti aksara Tionghoa, padahal diciptakan dari nol, untuk bahasa yang hanya sepupu jauh bahasa Tionghoa.","hi":"1036 में तंगुत राज्य ने लगभग 6,000 वर्णों की एक लिपि चलाई, जो पहली नज़र में चीनी जैसी दिखती है पर शून्य से गढ़ी गई थी — एक ऐसी भाषा के लिए जो चीनी की बस दूर की रिश्तेदार है।","de":"1036 führte der Tangutenstaat eine Schrift aus fast 6.000 Zeichen ein, die auf den ersten Blick chinesisch aussehen, aber von Grund auf neu erfunden wurden, und zwar für eine Sprache, die mit dem Chinesischen nur entfernt verwandt ist.","fr":"En 1036, l'État tangoute lança une écriture de près de 6 000 caractères qui ressemblent au chinois au premier coup d'œil mais furent inventés de toutes pièces, pour une langue qui n'est qu'une lointaine cousine du chinois.","it":"Nel 1036 lo Stato tangut varò una scrittura di quasi 6.000 caratteri che a prima vista sembrano cinesi ma furono inventati da zero, per una lingua che del cinese è solo una lontana cugina.","es":"En 1036 el Estado tangut puso en marcha una escritura de casi 6.000 caracteres que a primera vista parecen chinos pero se inventaron desde cero, para una lengua que solo es pariente lejana del chino.","pt":"Em 1036, o Estado tangut lançou uma escrita de quase 6.000 caracteres que à primeira vista parecem chineses, mas foram inventados do zero, para uma língua que é apenas prima distante do chinês.","ru":"В 1036 году тангутское государство ввело письменность почти из 6000 знаков, которые на первый взгляд похожи на китайские, но изобретены с нуля — для языка, который китайскому лишь дальний родственник.","uk":"1036 року тангутська держава запровадила письмо з майже 6000 знаків, які на перший погляд схожі на китайські, але винайдені з нуля для мови, що доводиться китайській лише далекою родичкою.","ar":"في عام 1036م أطلقت دولة التانغوت نظام كتابة يضم قرابة 6000 حرف تبدو صينية للوهلة الأولى، لكنها اختُرعت من الصفر، للغة لا تمتّ إلى الصينية إلا بقرابة بعيدة.","he":"בשנת 1036 השיקה המדינה הטנגוטית כתב של כמעט 6,000 סימנים, שבמבט ראשון נראים סיניים אבל הומצאו מאפס, לשפה שהיא רק קרובה רחוקה של הסינית.","sw":"Mwaka 1036 dola ya Tangut ilizindua maandishi ya karibu herufi 6,000 zinazofanana na Kichina kwa mtazamo wa kwanza lakini zilizobuniwa kutoka mwanzo, kwa ajili ya lugha ambayo ni binamu wa mbali tu wa Kichina."},"body":{"en":"<h3>A State-Sponsored Script</h3><p>The Western Xia kingdom (<em>Xī Xià</em>, 西夏, 1038–1227) used writing as part of its state administration and cultural policy. In 1036 the Tangut ruler <strong>Li Yuanhao</strong> (元昊, later the first Xia emperor, Jingzong) introduced a national writing system for the Tangut language. The official <em>History of Song</em> says Yuanhao \"himself created the Tangut script\" and had the official <strong>Yeli Renrong</strong> (野利仁榮) work it out in full, in twelve volumes; other accounts credit Yeli Renrong as the designer. Scholars now count about <strong>5,900 characters</strong> (5,863 by a 2004 count, excluding variants). They are dense, square and multi-component like Chinese characters, but the characters themselves were newly constructed for Tangut.</p><p>The <em>History of Song</em> describes the new characters as \"square and regular, like clerical script, but with rather repetitive strokes\", and the state soon had the Confucian <em>Classic of Filial Piety</em> and the <em>Erya</em> dictionary translated into Tangut. The visual resemblance to Chinese was very likely deliberate. Located between Song China and the Liao Khitan kingdom, the Tangut state used its own script to support administration and to assert cultural parity with its neighbours. The components nevertheless carry Tangut-specific phonetic and semantic values, so knowledge of Chinese characters does not make a Tangut text readable.</p><h3>Structure Without Chinese Meaning</h3><p>Like Chinese, Tangut characters are built from recurring graphic elements, and most combine two of them. Unlike Chinese, not one Tangut character is a picture of anything. The logic of the parts is different too. In Chinese a component usually brings the same meaning or sound wherever it appears. The 12th-century Tangut dictionary <em>Sea of Characters</em> instead explains each character as borrowing parts of other whole characters: \"wild goose\" is made from part of \"bird\" plus part of \"longevity\" (which supplies the sound <em>dze</em>). The same small component can stand for \"bird\" in one character and bone, smoke, food or time in others. Pairs of characters with the same parts in a different order (AB and BA) often have related meanings, like \"finger\" and \"toe\". Only about one character in ten has a sound element, against more than nine in ten in Chinese, according to Sofronov.</p><p>The language it wrote was Sino-Tibetan, related to Chinese only very distantly; since the 2010s specialists have placed it in the Gyalrongic (Qiangic) branch, whose living relatives are spoken in western Sichuan. So the script applies a Chinese-looking design to a language with a very different structure, including verbs that agree with their subject and object.</p><div class=\"trivia-actions\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"panto\" data-lat=\"38.5\" data-lng=\"106.27\" data-zoom=\"5\">📍 Pan to Yinchuan (Xia capital)</button></div><h3>The Bilingual Glossary 番漢合時掌中珠</h3><p>Our best window into Tangut vocabulary is a 12th-century bilingual glossary, the <strong><em>Fānhàn héshí zhǎngzhōng zhū</em></strong> (番漢合時掌中珠, &ldquo;Pearl in the Palm, Tangut and Chinese for the Times&rdquo;). Compiled in 1190 by a Tangut scholar named Gule Maocai (骨勒茂才), it arranges Tangut words alongside Chinese equivalents by theme. Each entry has four parts: the Tangut word, its Chinese equivalent, the Tangut pronunciation spelled with Chinese characters, and the Chinese pronunciation spelled with Tangut characters. That double spelling made it the key that let Aleksei Ivanov (1909) and Berthold Laufer (1916) make the first guesses at how Tangut sounded. The preface explains the purpose plainly: Tanguts and Chinese cannot deal with each other without learning each other's words.</p><h3>Later Use and Modern Decipherment</h3><p>When the Mongols destroyed the Western Xia capital Zhongxing (modern Yinchuan) in 1227, the Tangut state was erased, but the script was not. Tangut communities went on writing and printing it under the Yuan and into the Ming; the last known example is a pair of Tangut dharani pillars erected at Baoding, in present-day Hebei, in <strong>1502</strong>. The script then ceased to be used for roughly four centuries. In <strong>1908–09</strong>, the Russian explorer <strong>Pyotr Kozlov</strong> excavated the ruined city of Khara-Khoto in the Gobi Desert and unearthed a cache of thousands of Tangut manuscripts — Buddhist sutras, legal codes, dictionaries, poems — now held at the Institute of Oriental Manuscripts in St. Petersburg.</p><p>Systematic decipherment began in the 20th century, and its pioneer paid a terrible price. <strong>Nikolai Nevsky</strong> compiled the first Tangut dictionary and worked out the meanings of many grammatical particles, which made it possible to actually read Tangut texts, but he was arrested by the NKVD in 1937 and shot; his <em>Tangut Philology</em> appeared only in 1960 and won a posthumous Lenin Prize in 1962, and some manuscripts he had at the time of his arrest resurfaced at the St Petersburg institute only in 1991. The first comprehensive frameworks for reading the language came from the Japanese scholar <strong>Nishida Tatsuo</strong> (西田龍雄), whose <em>Seikago no kenkyū</em> (西夏語の研究, 1964–66) paired a reconstruction of Tangut phonology with a decipherment of the script, and from the Russian scholar <strong>Mikhail Sofronov</strong>, whose <em>Grammatika tangutskogo yazyka</em> (1968) set out a reconstruction alongside a full grammar. <strong>Gong Hwang-cherng</strong> (龔煌城) later refined the phonological system in a series of studies; his reconstruction was adopted for Li Fanwen's Tangut–Chinese dictionary (<em>Xià-Hàn zìdiǎn</em>, 夏漢字典, 1997) and summarised for English readers in his &lsquo;Tangut&rsquo; chapter of <em>The Sino-Tibetan Languages</em> (2003). Five full Tangut dictionaries now exist (Nevsky, Nishida, Li Fanwen, Kychanov and Han Xiaomang), and newer work by scholars such as Marc Miyake and Gong Xun uses living Gyalrongic languages to refine how Tangut sounded.</p><div class=\"trivia-actions\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"focus\" data-code=\"txg\">🔍 Focus Tangut on map</button></div><h3>Digital Encoding and Historical Significance</h3><p>Tangut is a documented case of a large script created to order by a state, and its Chinese look reflects the politics of its design. Unicode finally encoded Tangut in 2016 (6,125 characters at U+17000–U+187FF in version 9.0, plus 755 components), giving the script its first digital home nearly 800 years after the kingdom that created it was destroyed.</p>"},"sources":["Gong Hwang-cherng, 'Tangut (Xixia)' in <em>The Sino-Tibetan Languages</em>, ed. Thurgood & LaPolla, Routledge, 2003","Nishida Tatsuo, <em>Seikago no kenkyū</em> (西夏語の研究), Tokyo: Zauhō Kankōkai, 1964–1966","Nevsky, N.A., <em>Tangutskaya filologiya: Issledovaniya i slovar'</em> (Tangut Philology: Studies and Dictionary), 2 vols., Moscow: Izdatel'stvo vostochnoi literatury, 1960",{"text":"Unicode Tangut block (U+17000–U+187FF) — Unicode 9.0, 2016","url":"https://www.unicode.org/charts/PDF/U17000.pdf"},{"text":"Khara-Khoto collection, Institute of Oriental Manuscripts, St. Petersburg","url":"https://www.orientalstudies.ru/eng/index.php?option=com_content&task=view&id=7"},"Dunnell, Ruth, <em>The Great State of White and High: Buddhism and State Formation in Eleventh-Century Xia</em>, University of Hawaii Press, 1996"]},{"id":"khitan-large-small-scripts","icon":"𰀀","tags":["khitan","liao","sinosphere","script","history","undeciphered"],"title":{"en":"Khitan Large and Small Scripts: Two Writing Systems for Khitan","ja":"契丹大字と小字：ひとつの言語に生まれた2種類の文字","ko":"거란 대자와 소자: 하나의 언어를 위한 두 가지 문자","zh":"契丹大字与小字：契丹语的两种文字","yue":"契丹大字同小字：契丹語嘅兩套文字","vi":"Chữ Khiết Đan lớn và nhỏ: hai hệ thống chữ viết cho tiếng Khiết Đan","th":"อักษรขีตันใหญ่และเล็ก: สองระบบอักษรสำหรับภาษาขีตัน","id":"Aksara Khitan Besar dan Kecil: dua sistem tulisan untuk satu bahasa","hi":"खितान बड़ी और छोटी लिपि: खितान भाषा की दो लेखन प्रणालियाँ","de":"Kitanische Groß- und Kleinschrift: Zwei Schriften für das Kitanische","fr":"Grands et petits caractères khitans : deux écritures pour le khitan","it":"Le scritture kitan grande e piccola: due sistemi per il kitan","es":"Las escrituras kitan grande y pequeña: dos sistemas para el kitan","pt":"As escritas kitan grande e pequena: dois sistemas de escrita para o khitan","ru":"Большое и малое киданьское письмо: две письменности для киданьского языка","uk":"Велике і мале кіданьське письмо: дві системи письма для кіданьської мови","ar":"الخطان الخيتاني الكبير والصغير: منظومتا كتابة للغة الخيتانية","he":"הכתב הח'יתאני הגדול והקטן: שני כתבים לח'יתאנית","sw":"Maandishi makubwa na madogo ya Khitan: mifumo miwili ya uandishi kwa lugha ya Khitan"},"summary":{"en":"The Liao dynasty (907–1125) created two scripts for Khitan: a Large Script in 920 CE modeled on Chinese characters, and a Small Script around 924–925 CE that spells each word as a block of phonetic signs. Both remain only partly deciphered.","ja":"遼（907〜1125年）は契丹語のために大字（920年、漢字がモデル）と小字（924〜925年ごろ、表音文字を組んで1語を1ブロックで綴る）の2種の文字を作ったが、どちらもいまだ部分的にしか解読されていない。","ko":"요(遼, 907~1125)는 거란어를 위해 920년에 한자를 본뜬 대자를, 924~925년 무렵에는 낱말마다 표음 기호를 블록으로 묶어 적는 소자를 만들었지만, 둘 다 아직 일부만 해독되었다.","zh":"辽朝（907—1125年）先后为契丹语创制了两套文字：920年仿汉字的大字，以及约924—925年的小字——后者把每个词拼写成一个由表音字符组成的方块。两者至今都只解读了一部分。","yue":"遼朝（907—1125年）為契丹語創製咗兩套文字：920年仿漢字嘅大字，同大約924至925年、將每個詞拼成一塊表音字符嘅小字；兩者到而家都仍然只係部分解讀。","vi":"Nhà Liêu (907–1125) đã tạo ra không phải một mà là hai hệ thống chữ viết riêng biệt cho tiếng Khiết Đan: chữ lớn năm 920 mô phỏng chữ Hán, và chữ nhỏ ra đời khoảng năm 924–925, ghép các ký hiệu ghi âm thành từng khối để viết mỗi từ — và đến nay cả hai vẫn chỉ mới được giải mã một phần.","th":"ราชวงศ์เหลียว (907–1125) สร้างอักษรสำหรับภาษาขีตันถึงสองชุด คืออักษรใหญ่ในปี 920 ที่ออกแบบตามอักษรจีน และอักษรเล็กราวปี 924–925 ที่สะกดแต่ละคำเป็นบล็อกของสัญลักษณ์แทนเสียง แต่ทั้งสองยังถอดรหัสได้เพียงบางส่วน","id":"Dinasti Liao (907–1125) menciptakan bukan satu melainkan dua aksara yang berbeda untuk bahasa Khitan: Aksara Besar pada 920 M yang dimodelkan dari karakter Tionghoa, dan Aksara Kecil sekitar 924–925 M yang mengeja setiap kata sebagai satu blok tanda fonetis. Keduanya baru terpecahkan sebagian.","hi":"लियाओ राजवंश (907–1125) ने खितान भाषा के लिए एक नहीं बल्कि दो अलग-अलग लिपियाँ बनाईं: 920 ई. में चीनी वर्णों पर आधारित बड़ी लिपि, और लगभग 924–925 ई. में छोटी लिपि, जो हर शब्द को ध्वन्यात्मक चिह्नों के एक खंड में लिखती है — और दोनों आज भी केवल आंशिक रूप से ही पढ़ी जा सकी हैं।","de":"Die Liao-Dynastie (907–1125) schuf gleich zwei verschiedene Schriften für Kitanisch: die Großschrift von 920 nach dem Vorbild chinesischer Zeichen und um 924/925 die Kleinschrift, die jedes Wort als Block aus Lautzeichen schreibt. Beide sind bis heute nur teilweise entschlüsselt.","fr":"La dynastie Liao (907–1125) créa non pas une mais deux écritures distinctes pour le khitan : la grande écriture en 920, inspirée des caractères chinois, et vers 924-925 la petite écriture, qui épelle chaque mot sous forme d'un bloc de signes phonétiques. Toutes deux ne sont encore que partiellement déchiffrées.","it":"La dinastia Liao (907–1125) creò per il kitan non uno ma due sistemi di scrittura: la grande scrittura, nel 920, modellata sui caratteri cinesi, e la piccola scrittura, verso il 924–925, che compone ogni parola in un blocco di segni fonetici. Entrambe sono decifrate solo in parte.","es":"La dinastía Liao (907–1125) creó no una sino dos escrituras distintas para el kitan: la escritura grande en el año 920, modelada sobre los caracteres chinos, y hacia 924–925 la escritura pequeña, que escribe cada palabra como un bloque de signos fonéticos. Ambas siguen descifradas solo en parte.","pt":"A dinastia Liao (907–1125) criou não uma, mas duas escritas distintas para o kitan: a escrita grande em 920 d.C., modelada nos caracteres chineses, e a escrita pequena por volta de 924–925 d.C., que grafa cada palavra como um bloco de sinais fonéticos — e ambas continuam apenas parcialmente decifradas.","ru":"Династия Ляо (907–1125) создала для киданьского языка два письма: большое в 920 году по образцу китайских иероглифов и малое около 924–925 годов, где каждое слово записывается блоком фонетических знаков. Оба до сих пор расшифрованы лишь частично.","uk":"Династія Ляо (907–1125) створила для кіданьської мови два письма: велике у 920 році за зразком китайських ієрогліфів і мале близько 924–925 року, яке записує кожне слово блоком фонетичних знаків. Обидва досі розшифровані лише частково.","ar":"أنشأت أسرة لياو (907–1125م) خطَّين للغة الخيتانية: الخط الكبير عام 920م المُستوحى من الأحرف الصينية، والخط الصغير نحو عام 924–925م الذي يكتب كل كلمة كتلةً من العلامات الصوتية. ولم يُفكّ أيٌّ منهما حتى اليوم إلا جزئيًا.","he":"שושלת ליאו (907–1125) יצרה שני כתבים לח'יתאנית: הכתב הגדול ב-920 לספירה, בדגם הסימנים הסיניים, והכתב הקטן בסביבות 924–925 לספירה, שמאיית כל מילה כגוש של סימנים פונטיים. שניהם מפוענחים עד היום רק בחלקם.","sw":"Nasaba ya Liao (907–1125) iliunda si moja bali maandishi mawili tofauti kwa lugha ya Khitan: Maandishi Makubwa mwaka 920 BK yaliyoundwa kwa mfano wa herufi za Kichina, na Maandishi Madogo karibu mwaka 924–925 BK yanayoandika kila neno kama kitalu cha alama za sauti. Yote mawili bado yamefumbuliwa kwa sehemu tu."},"body":{"en":"<h3>One Language, Two Scripts: Why?</h3><p>The Khitan people founded the Liao dynasty in 907 and ruled a vast steppe-and-settled empire stretching from Mongolia to the Yellow Sea for over two centuries. Literate and administratively sophisticated, they needed writing. The surprise is that they invented <em>two</em>, five years apart, and kept the first.</p><p>The <strong>Khitan Large Script</strong> (契丹大字, <em>Qìdān dà zì</em>) appeared in 920 CE. Modeled visually on Chinese characters, it has around 830 distinct characters identified so far (about 1,000 if one disputed epitaph is counted), mostly logograms, perhaps with some phonetic signs for grammatical endings. The emperor Abaoji (耶律阿保機) commissioned it.</p><p>Just a few years later, around 924–925 CE, Abaoji's younger brother <strong>Yelü Diela</strong>, who had studied the Uyghur alphabet with a visiting Uyghur envoy, invented the <strong>Khitan Small Script</strong> (契丹小字, <em>Qìdān xiǎo zì</em>). The Small Script works quite differently: it has only a few hundred characters, many of them phonetic, and stacks two to seven of them into a square block that spells out one word, written in pairs from left to right. In this respect it resembles the way Korean Hangul assembles consonants and vowels into syllabic squares — though the resemblance is typological, not historical.</p><div class=\"trivia-actions\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"panto\" data-lat=\"43.8\" data-lng=\"120.5\" data-zoom=\"4\">📍 Pan to Liao heartland (Inner Mongolia)</button></div><h3>An Agglutinative Language in Square Blocks</h3><p>Khitan is classified as Para-Mongolic: not a descendant of Proto-Mongolic but an extinct sister branch of the Mongolic languages (Janhunen). It is agglutinative, stringing suffixes onto roots, which makes a logographic system like the Large Script a poor fit. The Small Script's phonetic blocks suit this morphology far better, since a suffix is simply one more character in the block.</p><p>Yet the Large Script was never abandoned (dated inscriptions run from 986 to 1176), and scholars are not entirely sure <em>why</em> both scripts continued to coexist for two centuries. One theory holds that the Large Script was prestigious for monumental and memorial inscriptions while the Small Script was the everyday administrative workhorse. Another is that they mapped onto different dialects or registers of Khitan. The honest answer is that nobody knows, because nobody can fully read either script.</p><h3>The Great Decipherment Problem</h3><p>After the Jurchen Jin dynasty defeated Liao in 1125, the Jin went on using Khitan writing for decades, until an imperial order suppressed it in 1191. The last dated Khitan inscription is from 1176. By the Mongol period the language was dead and the scripts forgotten. Only about fifty monumental inscriptions survive (some seventeen in the Large Script and around thirty-three in the Small Script), almost all in northeastern China, with two in Mongolia and a Khitan-inscribed bronze mirror from Kaesong in Korea. Systematic scholarly analysis began only in the 20th century. It followed a discovery of 1922, reported by the Belgian missionary Louis Kervyn: the Small Script memorial tablets of Emperor Xingzong and Empress Renyi at the Qingling (慶陵) Liao imperial tombs in Bairin Right Banner, Inner Mongolia.</p><p>The decipherment strategy exploits parallel texts. Only one true Khitan–Chinese bilingual inscription is known, the <em>Langjun xingji</em>, long mistaken for Jurchen, but many Khitan epitaphs honour people whose lives are also recorded in Chinese, so the two can be compared. By aligning the Chinese text with blocks of Khitan, scholars can infer the general meaning of Khitan passages. Khitan names of people and places known from Chinese records help pin down sound values for the Small Script.</p><div class=\"trivia-actions\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"panto\" data-lat=\"41.5\" data-lng=\"119.0\" data-zoom=\"5\">📍 Qinglong stele area</button></div><h3>Aisin Gioro and Recent Progress</h3><p>A milestone was the 1985 book by Chinggeltei's Khitan Script Research Group in China, which gathered the whole known corpus and summarised the readings proposed so far. Since the 1990s <strong>Aisin Gioro Ulhicun</strong> (愛新覚羅烏拉熙春), a Beijing-born Manchu scholar working in Japan (a descendant of the Qianlong Emperor, and daughter of the Jurchen specialist Jin Qizong), has published extensively on both scripts. Working with her husband, the historian Yoshimoto Michimasa, she has proposed readings for hundreds of Small Script blocks and identified grammatical suffixes, personal name patterns, and honorific vocabulary.</p><p>Even so, progress remains partial: by one older count (Kara, 1986), of the 378 known Small Script characters about 125 have an established meaning and 115 a phonetic value, while the rest were still undeciphered. The Large Script is understood far less well: beyond numerals, the twelve zodiac animals and a few other words, most of its characters remain unread. The field remains open: newly excavated steles are still awaiting full publication, and new comparative tools — including computational alignment with related Mongolic languages — are being brought to bear. Khitan is one of the great unsolved puzzles of East Asian historical linguistics.</p><p>Unicode encoded the Khitan Small Script in 2020 (U+18B00–U+18CFF, 470 characters in that first release, Unicode 13.0), which makes digital research on it far easier: a modest technical victory for a script silent for more than eight centuries. The Large Script is still only at the proposal stage.</p><div class=\"trivia-actions\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"focus\" data-code=\"zh\">🔍 View Sinosphere region</button></div>"},"sources":["Kane, Daniel, <em>The Kitan Language and Script</em>, Brill, 2009","Aisin Gioro Ulhicun & Yoshimoto Michimasa, <em>Shinshutsu Kittan shiryō no kenkyū</em> (新出契丹史料の研究), Kyoto: Shōkadō Shoten, 2012","Wu Yingzhe & Janhunen, Juha (eds.), <em>New Materials on the Khitan Small Script</em>, Folkestone: Global Oriental, 2010",{"text":"Unicode Khitan Small Script (U+18B00–U+18CFF), added in Unicode 13.0, 2020","url":"https://www.unicode.org/charts/PDF/U18B00.pdf"},"Chinggeltei, 'On the Problems of Reading Kitan Characters', <em>Acta Orientalia Academiae Scientiarum Hungaricae</em> 55 (2002): 99–114",{"text":"Wikipedia: Khitan small script","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Khitan_small_script"},{"text":"Wikipedia: Khitan large script","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Khitan_large_script"}]},{"id":"phagspa-universal-script","icon":"🔡","tags":["mongol","yuan","script","history","hangul-hypothesis"],"title":{"en":"Phags-pa Script: A Yuan-Dynasty Multilingual Alphabet","ja":"八思巴字：元代の多言語アルファベット","ko":"파스파 문자: 원나라의 다언어 문자","zh":"八思巴字：元代的多语言字母","yue":"八思巴字：元代嘅多語言字母","vi":"Chữ Phags-pa: bộ chữ đa ngôn ngữ thời nhà Nguyên","th":"อักษรพักปา: อักษรหลายภาษาแห่งราชวงศ์หยวน","id":"Aksara Phags-pa: aksara multibahasa Dinasti Yuan","hi":"फग्स-पा लिपि: युआन राजवंश की बहुभाषी वर्णमाला","de":"Phags-pa: Ein mehrsprachiges Alphabet der Yuan-Dynastie","fr":"Le Phags-pa : un alphabet multilingue de la dynastie Yuan","it":"Phags-pa: un alfabeto multilingue della dinastia Yuan","es":"Phags-pa: un alfabeto multilingüe de la dinastía Yuan","pt":"Phags-pa: um alfabeto multilíngue da dinastia Yuan","ru":"Пагспа: многоязычный алфавит династии Юань","uk":"Паґспа: багатомовний алфавіт династії Юань","ar":"خط باغسبا: أبجدية متعددة اللغات من عهد أسرة يوان","he":"כתב פאגס-פא: אלפבית רב-לשוני משושלת יואן","sw":"Hati ya Phags-pa: alfabeti ya lugha nyingi ya nasaba ya Yuan"},"summary":{"en":"In 1269 CE, the Tibetan monk Drogön Chögyal Phagpa created an alphabet on Kublai Khan's orders, used to write Mongolian, Chinese, Tibetan, Uyghur, Sanskrit and probably Persian. It was used in official contexts during the Yuan dynasty.","ja":"1269年、チベット僧ドゴン・チョギャル・パクパがクビライ・ハンの命で作ったアルファベット。モンゴル語・漢語・チベット語・ウイグル語・サンスクリット語、そしておそらくペルシャ語を書くのに使われ、元代の公的な場で用いられた。","ko":"1269년 티베트 승려 드로곤 최걀 파스파가 쿠빌라이 칸의 명으로 파스파 문자를 만들었다. 몽골어·중국어·티베트어·위구르어·산스크리트어, 그리고 아마도 페르시아어까지 적는 데 쓰였고, 원나라 때 공적인 자리에서 사용되었다.","zh":"1269年，西藏僧侣八思巴奉忽必烈之命创制八思巴字，用来书写蒙古语、汉语、藏语、回鹘语、梵语，很可能还有波斯语，元代曾用于官方场合，然而这一帝国梦想未逾百年便烟消云散。","yue":"1269年，西藏僧人八思巴奉忽必烈之命，創制八思巴字，用嚟書寫蒙古語、漢語、藏語、回鶻語、梵語，可能仲有波斯語，元代曾經用喺官方場合，但呢個宏大嘅帝國夢，唔夠一個世紀就消失咗。","vi":"Năm 1269, nhà sư Tây Tạng Drogön Chögyal Phagpa theo lệnh của Hốt Tất Liệt sáng tạo ra một bộ chữ cái, được dùng để ghi tiếng Mông Cổ, tiếng Hán, tiếng Tây Tạng, tiếng Duy Ngô Nhĩ, tiếng Phạn và có lẽ cả tiếng Ba Tư. Bộ chữ này được dùng trong các văn cảnh chính thức dưới thời nhà Nguyên.","th":"ในปี ค.ศ. 1269 พระภิกษุทิเบต ดรอกอน โชเกียล พักปา สร้างอักษรพักปาตามพระราชโองการของกุบไลข่าน ใช้เขียนภาษามองโกล จีน ทิเบต อุยกูร์ สันสกฤต และน่าจะรวมถึงเปอร์เซียด้วย แต่ความฝันอันยิ่งใหญ่นี้อยู่ได้ไม่ถึงศตวรรษ","id":"Pada 1269 M, biksu Tibet Drogön Chögyal Phagpa menciptakan satu abjad atas perintah Kubilai Khan yang dipakai untuk menulis bahasa Mongolia, Tionghoa, Tibet, Uyghur, Sanskerta, dan kemungkinan Persia. Abjad ini digunakan dalam urusan resmi pada masa Dinasti Yuan.","hi":"1269 ई. में तिब्बती भिक्षु द्रोगों च्योग्याल फागपा ने कुबलई ख़ान के आदेश पर एक वर्णमाला बनाई, जिससे मंगोलियाई, चीनी, तिब्बती, उइगुर, संस्कृत और शायद फारसी भी लिखी गई। युआन राजवंश के दौरान इसका प्रयोग आधिकारिक कामकाज में हुआ।","de":"1269 erfand der tibetische Mönch Drogön Chögyal Phagpa auf Befehl Kublai Khans ein Alphabet, mit dem Mongolisch, Chinesisch, Tibetisch, Uigurisch, Sanskrit und wahrscheinlich Persisch geschrieben wurden. In der Yuan-Dynastie diente es amtlichen Zwecken.","fr":"En 1269, le moine tibétain Drogön Chögyal Phagpa inventa, sur ordre de Kubilaï Khan, un alphabet qui servit à écrire le mongol, le chinois, le tibétain, l'ouïghour, le sanskrit et probablement le persan. Il fut employé dans les usages officiels sous la dynastie Yuan.","it":"Nel 1269 il monaco tibetano Drogön Chögyal Phagpa inventò, su ordine di Kublai Khan, un alfabeto usato per scrivere il mongolo, il cinese, il tibetano, l'uiguro, il sanscrito e probabilmente il persiano. Sotto la dinastia Yuan fu impiegato in ambito ufficiale.","es":"En 1269, el monje tibetano Drogön Chögyal Phagpa inventó, por orden de Kublai Kan, un alfabeto con el que se escribieron el mongol, el chino, el tibetano, el uigur, el sánscrito y probablemente el persa. Se usó en contextos oficiales durante la dinastía Yuan.","pt":"Em 1269, o monge tibetano Drogön Chögyal Phagpa criou, a mando de Kublai Khan, um alfabeto usado para escrever mongol, chinês, tibetano, uigur, sânscrito e provavelmente persa. Foi usado em contextos oficiais durante a dinastia Yuan.","ru":"В 1269 году тибетский монах Дрогон Чогьял Пагба по приказу Хубилая создал алфавит, которым записывали монгольский, китайский, тибетский, уйгурский, санскрит и, вероятно, персидский. Им пользовались в официальной сфере при династии Юань.","uk":"У 1269 році тибетський чернець Дрогон Чог'ял Пагпа за наказом Хубілая створив абетку, якою записували монгольську, китайську, тибетську, уйгурську мови, санскрит і, ймовірно, перську. Її вживали в офіційній сфері за династії Юань.","ar":"في عام 1269م، ابتكر الراهب التبتي دروغون تشوغيال باغسبا، بأمر من قوبلاي خان، أبجدية كُتبت بها المغولية والصينية والتبتية والأويغورية والسنسكريتية، وعلى الأرجح الفارسية. وقد استُعملت في السياقات الرسمية في عهد أسرة يوان.","he":"בשנת 1269 המציא הנזיר הטיבטי דרוגון צ'וגיאל פאגפא, בפקודת קובלאי חאן, אלפבית ששימש לכתיבת מונגולית, סינית, טיבטית, אויגורית, סנסקריט וכנראה גם פרסית. הוא שימש בהקשרים רשמיים בתקופת שושלת יואן.","sw":"Mwaka 1269 BK, mtawa wa Tibet Drogön Chögyal Phagpa alibuni alfabeti kwa amri ya Kublai Khan, iliyotumika kuandika Kimongolia, Kichina, Kitibeti, Kiuygur, Kisanskriti na huenda Kiajemi. Ilitumika katika shughuli rasmi wakati wa nasaba ya Yuan."},"body":{"en":"<h3>One Script for an Empire</h3>\n<p>The <strong>Mongol Yuan dynasty</strong> (1271–1368) was the eastern heartland of the Mongol empire, the largest contiguous land empire in history, whose western khanates by then ruled separately from Persia to Russia. Its court spoke Mongolian, Mandarin, Tibetan, Uyghur and Persian, each with its own script. In 1269, <strong>Kublai Khan</strong> commissioned his imperial preceptor, the Tibetan monk <strong>Drogön Chögyal Phagpa</strong> (1235–1280), to solve this problem with a single alphabet.</p>\n<div class=\"trivia-actions\">\n  <button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"focus\" data-code=\"zh_phagspa\">📍 See Phags-pa readings on map</button>\n  <button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"panto\" data-lat=\"40.4\" data-lng=\"116.4\" data-zoom=\"4\">🏯 Fly to Yuan-era Dadu (Beijing)</button>\n</div>\n<h3>The Design</h3>\n<p>Phagpa adapted the <strong>Tibetan script</strong> — itself derived from an Indian Brahmic alphabet — into a new vertical form. The result was <strong>八思巴字</strong> (<em>Bāsībā zì</em> in Mandarin, named after the monk himself). Key features:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Written <strong>top-to-bottom in columns, left-to-right</strong> — unlike its Tibetan parent, which runs horizontally. The vertical, left-to-right column layout follows the Uyghur-derived Mongolian script that Phags-pa was meant to replace.</li>\n  <li>An <strong>abugida</strong>: each consonant carries an inherent vowel, with diacritics for other vowels.</li>\n  <li><strong>41 basic letters</strong> — extended to roughly 48 with extra letters for Tibetan and Sanskrit — designed to capture the sounds of Mongolian, Chinese, Tibetan, Uyghur, Sanskrit and probably Persian.</li>\n  <li>Official use on <strong>seals, paper currency and imperial edicts</strong>, and on stone inscriptions across Yuan China. One of them is a Christian tombstone carved in 1314 in the port city of Quanzhou.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Why It Failed</h3>\n<p>The script was never truly popular. Chinese literati clung to Han characters; Mongolians already had their own Uyghur-derived script. Phags-pa was required for official documents but hardly used in daily life. Even its official role faded from the early 1350s, after which it served mostly as a pronunciation guide for Mongols learning Chinese characters, and it fell out of use once the Ming took over in 1368. In Tibet, a seal script known by the same name was still carved on seals in the 20th century, though some scholars regard that later script as a separate Tibetan seal script rather than true Phags-pa. Only scattered stone inscriptions, coins, and a handful of manuscripts survive.</p>\n<h3>The Hangul Hypothesis</h3>\n<p>Here is where things get controversial. In 1443, <strong>King Sejong of Joseon</strong> created <strong>Hangul</strong> (訓民正音, <em>Hunminjeongeum</em>). Some scholars, most notably <strong>Gari Ledyard</strong> in his 1966 dissertation, have argued that a handful of basic Hangul consonants, such as ㄱ, ㄷ, ㄹ and ㅂ, were modelled on Phags-pa letters. They point to a tantalising line in the royal annals for 1443: the king's new letters \"imitate the Old Seal\" (其字倣古篆). Ledyard read \"Old Seal\" as a veiled reference to the Mongol seal script. He never claimed Hangul was a copy. In his view Sejong combined original design with several models, Phags-pa among them.</p>\n<p>Others counter that the 1446 commentary on the script, the <em>Haerye</em>, explains the basic consonants as pictures of the speech organs, sorted by the five places of articulation (牙舌脣齒喉: molars, tongue, lips, teeth, throat). ㄱ, for example, shows the root of the tongue blocking the throat, and ㅁ shows the mouth. On that view the resemblances to Phags-pa are coincidence. Most scholars still treat Hangul as largely an original invention, and the Phags-pa link remains a minority view, though a serious one.</p>\n<div class=\"trivia-actions\">\n  <button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"focus\" data-code=\"ko\">📍 See Korean (Hangul) on map</button>\n  <button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"setchar\" data-char=\"人\">🔢 View 「人」 in Phags-pa and all readings</button>\n</div>\n<h3>A Ghost in the Machine</h3>\n<p>Phags-pa had one lasting indirect effect: it is one of the <strong>richest phonetic records of Chinese ever written in an outsider's alphabet</strong>. Tibetan-script transcriptions of Chinese from Dunhuang are some four centuries older, but Phags-pa covers the lexicon far more systematically, and what it captures is Old Mandarin, not Middle Chinese. Because the script spells out sounds instead of whole words, linguists can use Yuan-era Phags-pa spellings to recover consonants and vowels that Han characters alone never reveal. They do so with care: Phags-pa Chinese followed a conservative reading standard, keeping, for example, voiced initials that the spoken Mandarin of Dadu had probably already lost. Tone is the one thing it withholds: Phags-pa writes no tone marks at all, so syllables differing only in tone are spelled identically. The four tone categories have to be read off the Yuan rhyme dictionary <em>Menggu Ziyun</em> (蒙古字韻), which lists its Han characters under each Phags-pa spelling in the order level, rising, falling, entering. So Kublai Khan's failed universal script became, by accident, a record of how Chinese once sounded.</p>"},"sources":["Ledyard, Gari. 1966. 'The Korean Language Reform of 1446: The Origin, Background, and Early History of the Korean Alphabet.' PhD dissertation, University of California, Berkeley.","Coblin, W. South. 2007. 'A Handbook of 'Phags-pa Chinese.' Honolulu: University of Hawai'i Press.","Poppe, Nicholas. 1957. 'The Mongolian Monuments in hP'ags-pa Script.' Wiesbaden: Otto Harrassowitz.",{"text":"Unicode Consortium — Phags-pa block (U+A840–U+A87F)","url":"https://www.unicode.org/charts/PDF/UA840.pdf"},"Tumurtogoo, Domiin. 2010. 'Mongolian Monuments in 'Phags-pa Script: Introduction, Transliteration, Transcription and Bibliography.' Taipei: Institute of Linguistics, Academia Sinica.",{"text":"Wikipedia: Phags-pa script","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Phags-pa_script"},{"text":"Wikipedia: ʼPhags-pa inspiration for Hangul hypothesis","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/%CA%BCPhags-pa_inspiration_for_Hangul_hypothesis"}]},{"id":"hangul-sinosphere-context","icon":"📜","tags":["hangul","joseon","sinosphere","literacy","script-creation"],"title":{"en":"King Sejong and the Creation of Hangul","ja":"世宗大王とハングルの創製","ko":"세종대왕과 한글의 창제","zh":"世宗大王与谚文的创制","yue":"世宗大王同諺文嘅創製","vi":"Vua Sejong và sự ra đời của Hangul","th":"พระเจ้าเซจงกับการประดิษฐ์ฮันกึล","id":"Raja Sejong dan Penciptaan Hangul","hi":"राजा सेजोंग और हांगुल की रचना","de":"König Sejong und die Erfindung des Hangul","fr":"Le roi Sejong et la création du hangul","it":"Il re Sejong e la nascita dell'hangul","es":"El rey Sejong y la creación del hangul","pt":"O rei Sejong e a criação do hangul","ru":"Король Седжон и создание хангыля","uk":"Король Седжон і створення хангиля","ar":"الملك سيجونغ وابتكار الهانغول","he":"המלך סג'ונג ויצירת ההאנגול","sw":"Mfalme Sejong na uundaji wa Hangul"},"summary":{"en":"In 1443, Joseon's fourth king introduced a new alphabet intended to make reading more accessible. Confucian scholars opposed it, while women, Buddhist monks, and writers helped extend its use in Korean society.","ja":"1443年、朝鮮の第4代王は、読み書きをもっと身近にするための新しい文字を世に出した。儒学者たちは反対したが、女性や僧侶、書き手たちが朝鮮社会でこの文字の使用を広げていった。","ko":"1443년 조선의 제4대 왕은 읽기를 더 쉽게 하려고 새 문자를 내놓았다. 유학자들은 반대했지만, 여성과 불교 승려, 글 쓰는 이들이 이 문자의 쓰임을 조선 사회에 넓혀 갔다.","zh":"1443年，朝鲜第四代国王推出了一套新字母，好让更多人能够阅读。儒家学者表示反对，而女性、佛教僧人和作者们帮助它在朝鲜社会中推广开来。","yue":"1443年，朝鮮第四代國王推出一套新字母，等更多人讀得到書。儒家學者反對，而女性、佛教僧人同作者就幫手將佢喺朝鮮社會推廣開去。","vi":"Năm 1443, vị vua thứ tư của Triều Tiên giới thiệu một bảng chữ cái mới nhằm giúp việc đọc dễ tiếp cận hơn. Giới Nho sĩ phản đối, còn phụ nữ, nhà sư và các cây bút đã góp phần mở rộng việc dùng nó trong xã hội Triều Tiên.","th":"ในปี ค.ศ. 1443 กษัตริย์องค์ที่สี่แห่งโชซอนทรงประกาศอักษรใหม่เพื่อให้คนทั่วไปอ่านออกได้ง่ายขึ้น นักวิชาการขงจื๊อคัดค้าน แต่สตรี พระสงฆ์ และนักเขียนช่วยกันทำให้อักษรนี้แพร่หลายในสังคมเกาหลี","id":"Pada 1443, raja keempat Joseon memperkenalkan alfabet baru agar kemampuan membaca lebih mudah dijangkau. Para sarjana Konfusius menentangnya, sementara perempuan, biksu Buddha, dan para penulis membantu memperluas pemakaiannya di masyarakat Korea.","hi":"1443 में जोसियोन के चौथे राजा ने एक नई वर्णमाला प्रस्तुत की, ताकि पढ़ना ज़्यादा लोगों की पहुँच में आए। कन्फ़्यूशियाई विद्वानों ने इसका विरोध किया, जबकि महिलाओं, बौद्ध भिक्षुओं और लेखकों ने कोरियाई समाज में इसका प्रयोग बढ़ाने में मदद की।","de":"1443 führte Joseons vierter König ein neues Alphabet ein, das das Lesen zugänglicher machen sollte. Konfuzianische Gelehrte lehnten es ab, während Frauen, buddhistische Mönche und Schriftsteller halfen, es in der koreanischen Gesellschaft zu verbreiten.","fr":"En 1443, le quatrième roi de Joseon introduisit un nouvel alphabet destiné à rendre la lecture plus accessible. Les lettrés confucéens s'y opposèrent, tandis que des femmes, des moines bouddhistes et des écrivains contribuèrent à en étendre l'usage dans la société coréenne.","it":"Nel 1443 il quarto re di Joseon introdusse un nuovo alfabeto pensato per rendere la lettura più accessibile. Gli studiosi confuciani si opposero, mentre donne, monaci buddisti e scrittori contribuirono a diffonderne l'uso nella società coreana.","es":"En 1443, el cuarto rey de Joseon introdujo un nuevo alfabeto pensado para hacer la lectura más accesible. Los eruditos confucianos se opusieron, mientras que mujeres, monjes budistas y escritores ayudaron a extender su uso en la sociedad coreana.","pt":"Em 1443, o quarto rei de Joseon apresentou um novo alfabeto destinado a tornar a leitura mais acessível. Os estudiosos confucianos se opuseram, enquanto mulheres, monges budistas e escritores ajudaram a ampliar seu uso na sociedade coreana.","ru":"В 1443 году четвёртый король династии Чосон представил новый алфавит, чтобы сделать чтение доступнее. Конфуцианские учёные выступили против, а женщины, буддийские монахи и литераторы помогли его распространению в корейском обществе.","uk":"1443 року четвертий король династії Чосон представив новий алфавіт, щоб зробити читання доступнішим. Конфуціанські вчені виступили проти, а жінки, буддійські ченці й літератори допомогли поширити його в корейському суспільстві.","ar":"في عام 1443م، قدّم الملك الرابع لمملكة جوسون أبجدية جديدة تهدف إلى تيسير القراءة. وعارضها علماء الكونفوشيوسية، بينما أسهمت النساء والرهبان البوذيون والكتّاب في توسيع استخدامها في المجتمع الكوري.","he":"בשנת 1443 הציג המלך הרביעי של ג'וסון אלפבית חדש שנועד להנגיש את הקריאה. מלומדים קונפוציאנים התנגדו לו, ואילו נשים, נזירים בודהיסטים וסופרים הפיצו אותו בחברה הקוריאנית.","sw":"Mwaka 1443, mfalme wa nne wa Joseon alianzisha alfabeti mpya iliyolenga kufanya usomaji upatikane kwa urahisi zaidi. Wasomi wa Confucius waliipinga, huku wanawake, watawa wa Kibuddha na waandishi wakisaidia kupanua matumizi yake katika jamii ya Korea."},"body":{"en":"<h3>Classical Chinese and Access to Literacy</h3>\n<p>In 15th-century Joseon, classical Chinese (<em>hanmun</em>, 漢文) was the only prestige writing system. Mastering it required years of rote study — years that farmers, craftsmen, and women could not spare. The result was a literacy gap that split society: a thin layer of male <em>yangban</em> (aristocratic) scholars monopolised the written word, while most Koreans had limited access to written texts.</p>\n<div class=\"trivia-actions\">\n  <button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"focus\" data-code=\"ko\">📍 See Korean on the HanMap</button>\n  <button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"focus\" data-code=\"ko_mid\">📍 See Middle Korean readings</button>\n</div>\n<h3>Design and Publication</h3>\n<p><strong>King Sejong</strong> (r. 1418–1450) was a polymath who reformed astronomy, music theory and military technology. In the early 1440s he set about designing a new alphabet from scratch. Scholars of his Hall of Worthies helped, but the court annals for the last month of 1443 credit the king himself: \"This month, His Majesty personally created the 28 letters of <em>eonmun</em> … simple and concise, yet endlessly versatile.\" The result, completed in 1443 and formally promulgated in 1446 with the publication of the <strong>Hunminjeongeum</strong> (訓民正音, <em>Correct Sounds for the Instruction of the People</em>, in the annotated <em>Haerye</em> 解例 edition), was <strong>Hangul</strong>: 28 letters (17 consonants + 11 vowels, of which 24 remain in use today).</p>\n<p>The design follows a systematic theory. Consonant letters visually depict the <em>position of the vocal tract</em> during articulation: ㄱ (g/k) shows the tongue root blocking the throat; ㄴ (n) shows the tongue tip touching the palate; ㅁ (m) shows the closed mouth. Vowels are built from three philosophical elements: a dot (heaven ·), a horizontal line (earth ㅡ), and a vertical line (man ㅣ). Letters combine into syllable blocks — so 한 = ㅎ + ㅏ + ㄴ — making the script both alphabetic and visually syllabic.</p>\n<h3>The Scholar's Revolt: Choe Manri's Memorial</h3>\n<p>In early 1444, <strong>Choe Manri</strong> (崔萬理), deputy head of the Hall of Worthies, led a group of its scholars in submitting a formal protest to Sejong. Their objections focused on relations with Ming China, scholarship and government:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Cultural subordination:</strong> Korea belonged to Chinese civilisation, and within China itself, however much regional speech differed, no dialect had ever been given its own script. Only \"barbarians\" did that: the memorial names the Mongols, the Tangut, the Jurchen, the Japanese and the Tibetans. To make a separate script was to abandon China and become like them.</li>\n  <li><strong>Practical objection:</strong> <em>Idu</em>, the old system of writing Korean with borrowed Chinese characters, had served officials for centuries without trouble. And clear writing would not end unjust verdicts; that depended on the officials who handled the case, not on the script.</li>\n  <li><strong>Fear for learning:</strong> If a clerk could get ahead with a script of just 27 letters (as the memorial counted them), who would toil over the Confucian classics? Within decades, they warned, hardly anyone would know Chinese characters.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Sejong answered in person. Idu, he pointed out, also used characters for their sound, and it had been made to help the people. \"If that was to help the people, then my <em>eonmun</em> is to help the people too.\" He then sent Choe and six co-signers to the State Tribunal. They were freed the next day, and only one of them, Jeong Chang-son, lost his post. The work went ahead.</p>\n<h3>Who Actually Used It First</h3>\n<p>Early users of Hangul included <strong>women</strong> and <strong>Buddhists</strong>. Yangban men wrote their official documents and literary poetry in classical Chinese, viewing Hangul as beneath them. Its usual name, <em>eonmun</em> (諺文, \"vernacular script\"), was the word Sejong's own court used, but it was set against <em>jinmun</em> (眞文), the \"true script\" of Chinese characters, and the contrast made the ranking plain. But aristocratic women, barred from the Confucian examination system, adopted Hangul for personal letters, diaries, and household records. Buddhist monks used it to translate sutras and write devotional texts for lay audiences.</p>\n<p>The genre of <strong>vernacular fiction</strong> (<em>gososel</em>) grew through Hangul, including the widely read 18th-century novel <em>Chunhyangjeon</em> (春香傳), whose heroine's story of loyalty across class lines became a national classic. Works like these carried Hangul well beyond the court and the government office.</p>\n<div class=\"trivia-actions\">\n  <button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"focus\" data-code=\"ko_hun\">📍 See historical Hangul (hanja-era) readings</button>\n  <button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"setchar\" data-char=\"人\">🔢 View 「人」 across Korean variants</button>\n</div>\n<h3>Restrictions and Later Standardisation</h3>\n<p>In 1504, King <strong>Yeonsangun</strong> banned the teaching and use of Hangul after anonymous writings criticising him circulated in the script. The ban was lifted after his overthrow in 1506, but Hangul's prestige remained low for centuries. The late 19th-century nationalist movement reclaimed it as a symbol of Korean identity distinct from Chinese cultural dominance — a decree of November 1894, during the Gabo Reform, made it the preferred script for official documents under the name <em>gungmun</em> (국문, national script), although mixed script was still allowed. In 1896 the newspaper <em>The Independent</em> (<em>Dongnip sinmun</em>) printed its Korean pages entirely in Hangul. The name <strong>Hangul</strong> (한글, great/Korean script) came a generation later: <strong>Ju Si-gyeong</strong> and his circle of language reformers coined it in the early 1910s to replace the old pejorative <em>eonmun</em>, and it became the general term only after the journal <em>Hangul</em> began publication in 1927. After the Japanese colonial period (1910–1945), independence in 1945 made Hangul the unambiguous national script of both Koreas.</p>\n<p>Today Hangul is studied for the systematic relationship between its letter shapes and phonetic features. It is the principal script of Korean in both North and South Korea, with spelling and typographic conventions that differ between the two states.</p>"},"sources":["Ledyard, Gari. 1966. 'The Korean Language Reform of 1446.' PhD dissertation, University of California, Berkeley.","Kim-Renaud, Young-Key (ed.). 1997. 'The Korean Alphabet: Its History and Structure.' Honolulu: University of Hawai'i Press.","Sejong the Great. 1446. 'Hunminjeongeum Haerye' (訓民正音解例). Facsimile edition, National Museum of Korea.",{"text":"UNESCO Memory of the World — Hunminjeongeum (1997)","url":"https://www.unesco.org/en/memory-world"},"Sampson, Geoffrey. 1985. 'Writing Systems: A Linguistic Introduction.' Stanford University Press. (Chapter on Korean)",{"text":"Wikipedia: Hangul","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hangul"}]},{"id":"chu-nom-vietnam-script","icon":"📜","tags":["sinosphere","vietnam","writing-system","history","colonial"],"title":{"en":"Chữ Nôm: A Vietnamese Han-Character Writing Tradition","ja":"字喃（Chữ Nôm）：漢字から生まれたベトナムの書記伝統","ko":"쯔놈: 한자에서 나온 베트남의 문자 전통","zh":"字喃：越南的汉字书写传统","yue":"字喃：越南嘅漢字書寫傳統","vi":"Chữ Nôm: một truyền thống chữ viết gốc Hán của Việt Nam","th":"จื๋อโนม: ธรรมเนียมการเขียนด้วยอักษรจีนของเวียดนาม","id":"Chữ Nôm: tradisi tulisan Vietnam berbasis aksara Han","hi":"चु नोम: हान अक्षरों पर आधारित वियतनामी लेखन परंपरा","de":"Chữ Nôm: Eine vietnamesische Schrifttradition mit Han-Zeichen","fr":"Le Chữ Nôm : une tradition d'écriture vietnamienne en caractères han","it":"Chữ Nôm: una tradizione di scrittura vietnamita in caratteri han","es":"Chữ Nôm: una tradición vietnamita de escritura con caracteres han","pt":"Chữ Nôm: uma tradição vietnamita de escrita com caracteres han","ru":"Тьы-ном: вьетнамская письменная традиция на основе китайских иероглифов","uk":"Тьи-ном: в'єтнамська писемна традиція на основі китайських ієрогліфів","ar":"تشو نوم: تقليد فيتنامي في الكتابة بالحروف الصينية","he":"צ'ו נום: וייטנאמית בסימני האן","sw":"Chữ Nôm: mila ya Vietnam ya kuandika kwa herufi za Han"},"summary":{"en":"For nearly a thousand years, Vietnamese scholars wrote their language in an adapted Han-character script called Chữ Nôm. French colonial rule later replaced it with the Latin alphabet devised by Jesuit missionaries, limiting direct access to much of the earlier literary tradition.","ja":"約千年にわたり、ベトナムの学者たちは「字喃（チュノム）」と呼ばれる独自の漢字変形文字でベトナム語を書いてきた。だがフランス植民地支配のもとで、この文字はイエズス会宣教師が考案したラテン文字に置き換えられ、それ以前の文学の多くは、直接読むことが難しくなった。","ko":"거의 천 년 동안 베트남 학자들은 한자를 변형한 쯔놈으로 자기 언어를 적었다. 이후 프랑스 식민 통치가 예수회 선교사들이 고안한 라틴 문자로 이를 대체하면서, 이전 문학 전통의 상당 부분은 직접 읽기 어려워졌다.","zh":"近千年来，越南学者用一种改造汉字而成的文字“字喃”书写自己的语言。后来法国殖民统治用耶稣会传教士设计的拉丁字母取代了它，此前的大量文学传统也因此难以直接读到。","yue":"近千年嚟，越南學者用一種改造漢字而成嘅文字「字喃」寫自己嘅語言。後來法國殖民統治用耶穌會傳教士設計嘅拉丁字母取代咗佢，之前大量文學傳統亦因此難以直接讀到。","vi":"Gần một nghìn năm, các học giả Việt Nam viết tiếng mình bằng một hệ chữ phỏng theo chữ Hán gọi là chữ Nôm. Về sau, chế độ thuộc địa Pháp thay nó bằng bảng chữ cái Latinh do các nhà truyền giáo Dòng Tên tạo ra, khiến phần lớn truyền thống văn chương trước đó khó tiếp cận trực tiếp.","th":"เกือบพันปีที่นักวิชาการเวียดนามเขียนภาษาของตนด้วยจื๋อโนม อักษรที่ดัดแปลงจากอักษรจีน ต่อมาฝรั่งเศสสมัยอาณานิคมเอาอักษรละตินที่มิชชันนารีเยสุอิตคิดขึ้นมาใช้แทน ทำให้วรรณกรรมยุคก่อนส่วนใหญ่อ่านโดยตรงได้ยาก","id":"Selama hampir seribu tahun, para sarjana Vietnam menulis bahasanya dalam aksara adaptasi karakter Han yang disebut Chữ Nôm. Pemerintahan kolonial Prancis kemudian menggantinya dengan alfabet Latin yang dirancang para misionaris Yesuit, sehingga akses langsung ke banyak karya sastra terdahulu menjadi terbatas.","hi":"लगभग एक हज़ार साल तक वियतनामी विद्वानों ने अपनी भाषा को हान अक्षरों से ढली लिपि चु नोम में लिखा। बाद में फ्रांसीसी औपनिवेशिक शासन ने इसकी जगह जेसुइट मिशनरियों की गढ़ी लैटिन वर्णमाला ला दी, जिससे पहले की साहित्यिक परंपरा के बड़े हिस्से तक सीधी पहुँच सीमित हो गई।","de":"Fast tausend Jahre lang schrieben vietnamesische Gelehrte ihre Sprache in einer aus Han-Zeichen abgeleiteten Schrift namens Chữ Nôm. Die französische Kolonialherrschaft ersetzte sie später durch das von Jesuitenmissionaren entwickelte lateinische Alphabet, was den direkten Zugang zu großen Teilen der älteren Literatur einschränkte.","fr":"Pendant près d'un millénaire, les érudits vietnamiens ont écrit leur langue dans une écriture adaptée des caractères han, le Chữ Nôm. La colonisation française l'a ensuite remplacée par l'alphabet latin conçu par des missionnaires jésuites, ce qui a limité l'accès direct à une grande partie de la littérature antérieure.","it":"Per quasi mille anni gli studiosi vietnamiti scrissero la loro lingua in una scrittura adattata dai caratteri han, chiamata Chữ Nôm. Il dominio coloniale francese la sostituì poi con l'alfabeto latino ideato dai missionari gesuiti, limitando l'accesso diretto a gran parte della tradizione letteraria precedente.","es":"Durante casi mil años, los estudiosos vietnamitas escribieron su idioma con una escritura adaptada de los caracteres han, llamada Chữ Nôm. El dominio colonial francés la reemplazó después por el alfabeto latino ideado por misioneros jesuitas, lo que limitó el acceso directo a gran parte de la tradición literaria anterior.","pt":"Durante quase mil anos, os estudiosos vietnamitas escreveram sua língua em uma escrita adaptada de caracteres han chamada Chữ Nôm. Mais tarde, o domínio colonial francês a substituiu pelo alfabeto latino criado por missionários jesuítas, o que limitou o acesso direto a boa parte da tradição literária anterior.","ru":"Почти тысячу лет вьетнамские учёные записывали свой язык тьы-номом — письмом, приспособленным из китайских иероглифов. Позднее французская колониальная власть заменила его латиницей, разработанной миссионерами-иезуитами, и это ограничило прямой доступ к значительной части прежней литературной традиции.","uk":"Майже тисячу років в'єтнамські вчені записували свою мову тьи-номом — письмом, пристосованим із китайських ієрогліфів. Згодом французька колоніальна влада замінила його латиницею, яку розробили місіонери-єзуїти, і це обмежило прямий доступ до значної частини давнішої літературної традиції.","ar":"لما يقارب ألف عام، كتب العلماء الفيتناميون لغتهم بكتابة مقتبسة من الحروف الصينية تُعرف بـ«تشو نوم». ثم استبدلها الحكم الاستعماري الفرنسي بالأبجدية اللاتينية التي وضعها المبشرون اليسوعيون، مما حدّ من الوصول المباشر إلى جانب كبير من التراث الأدبي السابق.","he":"כמעט אלף שנה כתבו מלומדים וייטנאמים את שפתם בצ'ו נום, גרסה מעובדת של סימני האן. השלטון הקולוניאלי הצרפתי החליף אותו באלפבית הלטיני של המיסיונרים הישועים, וכך הוגבלה הגישה הישירה לחלק גדול מהספרות שקדמה לו.","sw":"Kwa karibu miaka elfu moja, wasomi wa Vietnam waliandika lugha yao kwa maandishi ya herufi za Han zilizorekebishwa, yanayoitwa Chữ Nôm. Baadaye utawala wa kikoloni wa Ufaransa uliyabadilisha kwa alfabeti ya Kilatini iliyobuniwa na wamishonari wa Kiyesuiti, na hilo likapunguza uwezo wa kufikia moja kwa moja sehemu kubwa ya mila ya fasihi ya awali."},"body":{"en":"<h3>A script born from necessity</h3>\n<p>Northern Vietnam was ruled by successive Chinese dynasties for about a thousand years, from 111 BCE until independence in 938, and classical Chinese (<em>chữ Hán</em>) remained the written language of administration, law and literature long after. But Vietnamese is not Chinese. It belongs to the Austroasiatic family (its distant relatives include Khmer), with its own grammar and its own native vocabulary. Writing the spoken language in borrowed Chinese characters was as awkward as writing English in Japanese kanji.</p>\n<p>Probably after independence in the 10th century, Vietnamese writers developed a solution (the oldest firmly dated example, a list of names on a stele at Báo Ân pagoda, is from <strong>1209</strong>): <strong>Chữ Nôm</strong> (字喃), literally \"Southern script\" or \"vernacular script.\" Instead of inventing an entirely new alphabet, they took Chinese characters and bent them to Vietnamese ends using two main strategies:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Phonetic borrowing:</strong> A Chinese character was chosen for its sound, whatever its meaning. 沒 means \"not have\" in Chinese, but its Sino-Vietnamese reading is <em>một</em>, so Nôm used it to write the native word <em>một</em>, \"one\".</li>\n  <li><strong>Semantic + phonetic compounds:</strong> Two characters were fused, one giving the meaning and one the sound, to make a new glyph found in no Chinese dictionary. 𠀧 <em>ba</em> \"three\" joins 巴 <em>ba</em> (for the sound) with 三 \"three\" (for the meaning); 媄 <em>mẹ</em> \"mother\" joins 女 \"woman\" with 美 <em>mỹ</em>.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"trivia-actions\">\n  <button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"panto\" data-lat=\"16.0\" data-lng=\"108.0\" data-zoom=\"5\">📍 Fly to Vietnam</button>\n  <button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"setchar\" data-char=\"南\">🔢 Show 南 (south) across languages</button>\n</div>\n\n<h3>The scale: a repertoire of 20,000 characters</h3>\n<p>The Chinese standard repertoire (GB 18030) encodes around 87,000 characters; the Nôm Na Group's 2005 list of Chữ Nôm runs to nearly <strong>20,000 characters</strong>, of which several thousand are Vietnamese inventions — glyphs that appear in no Chinese text, created purely to represent Vietnamese words. Vietnam's national standard TCVN 6909:2001 fixes 9,299 Nôm glyphs, about half of them Vietnam-specific; the full range may never be precisely counted because many manuscripts are still undigitized.</p>\n<p>The script was never standardized. Different writers could spell the same word with different characters, and a reader also had to know classical Chinese to make sense of the components, which made Nôm hard to learn. That did not stop it spreading: popular Nôm works, <em>Truyện Kiều</em> among them, were printed from carved woodblocks and sold widely in the 19th century. Twice, under the Hồ dynasty (1400–1407) and the Tây Sơn (1778–1802), rulers even made Nôm the official script, but each policy died with the regime.</p>\n\n<h3>The masterpiece: Truyện Kiều</h3>\n<p>The single greatest monument of Chữ Nôm literature is <strong>Truyện Kiều</strong> (The Tale of Kiều), a 3,254-line epic poem by <strong>Nguyễn Du</strong> (1765–1820). The story, adapted from the Chinese novel <em>Jin Yun Qiao zhuan</em>, follows a young woman named Kiều through fifteen years of tragedy, prostitution, war and eventual reunion. It is written in <em>lục bát</em>, the native Vietnamese \"six-eight\" metre of alternating six- and eight-syllable lines, and is widely regarded as Vietnam's national poem; many Vietnamese can quote lines from it.</p>\n<p>Vietnam's greatest work of literature was written in a script that almost nobody alive today can read in its original form. When you read Truyện Kiều in a modern edition, you are reading a transliteration into the Latin-based <em>quốc ngữ</em>, not the original glyphs Nguyễn Du wrote.</p>\n\n<h3>The erasure</h3>\n<p>French colonization arrived in stages from 1858. Catholic missionaries had already been promoting the Latin-based quốc ngữ since the 17th century (see the companion article on Alexandre de Rhodes). French officials promoted it, partly to weaken the hold of Chinese learning and of the scholar class behind it; critics, then and since, point out that a population reading only the new script was cut off from pre-colonial texts and cultural memory. The Confucian examinations, which tested classical Chinese, were abolished in Tonkin in 1915 and held for the last time at Huế on 4 January <strong>1919</strong>. Reading Nôm had always rested on that same classical training, and now the training itself was gone. Within a generation, functional literacy in the script collapsed.</p>\n\n<h3>The revival that might not arrive in time</h3>\n<p>The <strong>Hán Nôm Institute</strong> (Viện Nghiên cứu Hán Nôm), founded in Hanoi in 1970, has been digitizing manuscripts before they decay. After Vietnam's own national standards (TCVN 5773:1993 and TCVN 6909:2001), Unicode took in thousands of Chữ Nôm characters, most of the Vietnam-specific ones in CJK Unified Ideographs Extension B and later extension blocks, so the script can now be typed with standard fonts on any device. A small but passionate community of scholars and hobbyists is relearning it. But the Vietnamese Nôm Preservation Foundation estimates that fewer than 100 scholars worldwide can read it, so time is short.</p>"},"sources":["Nguyễn Đình Hoà, 'Chữ Nôm: The Demotic System of Writing in Vietnam', Journal of the American Oriental Society, 1959","Hồ Xuân Hương (trans. John Balaban), 'Spring Essence: The Poetry of Hồ Xuân Hương', Copper Canyon Press, 2000",{"text":"Hán Nôm Institute — digitisation project","url":"https://hannom.vass.gov.vn/"},{"text":"Unicode CJK Unified Ideographs Extension G (including Nôm)","url":"https://www.unicode.org/charts/PDF/U30000.pdf"},"Nguyễn Du (trans. Huỳnh Sanh Thông), 'The Tale of Kiều', Yale University Press, 1983","DeFrancis, John, 'Colonialism and Language Policy in Viet Nam', Mouton, 1977"]},{"id":"quoc-ngu-rhodes","icon":"✍️","tags":["sinosphere","vietnam","writing-system","missionary","colonial","phonology"],"title":{"en":"Quốc Ngữ and Alexandre de Rhodes's 1651 Dictionary","ja":"クオックグーとアレクサンドル・ド・ロードスの1651年の辞書","ko":"꾸옥응으와 알렉산드르 드 로드의 1651년 사전","zh":"国语字与亚历山大·德·罗德的1651年字典","yue":"國語字同亞歷山大·德·羅德嘅1651年字典","vi":"Chữ quốc ngữ và cuốn từ điển năm 1651 của Alexandre de Rhodes","th":"กว๊อกหงือกับพจนานุกรมปี 1651 ของอาเลกซานดร์ เดอ โรด","id":"Quốc Ngữ dan Kamus Alexandre de Rhodes Tahun 1651","hi":"क्वोक न्गू और अलेक्ज़ेंडर डी रोड्स का 1651 का शब्दकोश","de":"Quốc Ngữ und Alexandre de Rhodes' Wörterbuch von 1651","fr":"Le Quốc Ngữ et le dictionnaire d'Alexandre de Rhodes de 1651","it":"Il Quốc Ngữ e il dizionario di Alexandre de Rhodes del 1651","es":"El Quốc Ngữ y el diccionario de Alexandre de Rhodes de 1651","pt":"O quốc ngữ e o dicionário de 1651 de Alexandre de Rhodes","ru":"Куок-нгы и словарь Александра де Рода 1651 года","uk":"Куок-нги та словник Александра де Рода 1651 року","ar":"كواك نغو وقاموس ألكسندر دو رود لعام 1651","he":"קואוק נגוּ והמילון של אלכסנדר דה רוד מ-1651","sw":"Quốc ngữ na kamusi ya Alexandre de Rhodes ya 1651"},"summary":{"en":"Alexandre de Rhodes published a trilingual dictionary in 1651 and helped systematize Latin-script Vietnamese. The writing system also drew on Portuguese predecessors, uses diacritics for six tones, and expanded under colonial administration.","ja":"アレクサンドル・ド・ロードスは1651年に三言語辞典を出版し、ラテン文字によるベトナム語表記の体系化に貢献した。この表記はポルトガル人の先人の仕事も土台とし、6声調を補助記号で示し、植民地行政のもとで広まった。","ko":"알렉산드르 드 로드는 1651년 삼언어 사전을 출판해 라틴 문자 베트남어 표기의 체계화에 힘을 보탰다. 이 표기는 포르투갈 선배들의 작업에도 기대고 있으며, 6성조를 구별 부호로 나타내고, 식민지 행정 아래에서 퍼져 나갔다.","zh":"亚历山大·德·罗德于1651年出版三语字典，帮助拉丁字母越南语文字走向系统化。这套文字也借鉴了葡萄牙先驱者的成果，用变音符号标注六个声调，并在殖民行政下推广开来。","yue":"亞歷山大·德·羅德喺1651年出版三語字典，幫手令拉丁字母越南文走向系統化。呢套文字亦借鑒咗葡萄牙先驅嘅成果，用變音符號標六個聲調，並喺殖民行政之下推廣開去。","vi":"Alexandre de Rhodes xuất bản một cuốn từ điển ba thứ tiếng năm 1651 và góp phần hệ thống hóa chữ Việt dùng ký tự Latinh. Hệ chữ này còn dựa vào những người đi trước người Bồ Đào Nha, dùng dấu để ghi sáu thanh điệu, và được mở rộng dưới chính quyền thuộc địa.","th":"อาเลกซานดร์ เดอ โรดตีพิมพ์พจนานุกรมไตรภาษาในปี 1651 และช่วยจัดระบบการเขียนภาษาเวียดนามด้วยอักษรละติน ระบบนี้ยังอาศัยงานของผู้บุกเบิกชาวโปรตุเกส ใช้เครื่องหมายแสดงวรรณยุกต์หกเสียง และแพร่หลายภายใต้การบริหารอาณานิคม","id":"Alexandre de Rhodes menerbitkan kamus tiga bahasa pada 1651 dan membantu menyistematiskan penulisan bahasa Vietnam dalam aksara Latin. Sistem tulisan ini juga berutang pada pendahulu Portugis, memakai diakritik untuk enam nada, dan meluas di bawah pemerintahan kolonial.","hi":"अलेक्ज़ेंडर डी रोड्स ने 1651 में एक त्रिभाषी शब्दकोश प्रकाशित किया और लैटिन लिपि में वियतनामी लेखन को व्यवस्थित करने में मदद की। इस लिपि ने पहले के पुर्तगाली काम से भी बहुत कुछ लिया, यह छह स्वराघात विशेषक चिह्नों से दिखाती है, और औपनिवेशिक प्रशासन के ज़रिए फैली।","de":"Alexandre de Rhodes veröffentlichte 1651 ein dreisprachiges Wörterbuch und half, das lateinschriftliche Vietnamesisch zu systematisieren. Das Schriftsystem baute auch auf portugiesischen Vorläufern auf, kennzeichnet sechs Töne mit Diakritika und breitete sich unter der Kolonialverwaltung aus.","fr":"Alexandre de Rhodes publia un dictionnaire trilingue en 1651 et contribua à systématiser le vietnamien en alphabet latin. Ce système d'écriture doit aussi à des précurseurs portugais, note six tons par des diacritiques et s'est étendu sous l'administration coloniale.","it":"Alexandre de Rhodes pubblicò un dizionario trilingue nel 1651 e contribuì a sistematizzare il vietnamita in alfabeto latino. Il sistema di scrittura si rifaceva anche a precursori portoghesi, usa i diacritici per sei toni e si diffuse sotto l'amministrazione coloniale.","es":"Alexandre de Rhodes publicó un diccionario trilingüe en 1651 y ayudó a sistematizar el vietnamita en alfabeto latino. El sistema de escritura también se apoyó en precursores portugueses, usa diacríticos para seis tonos y se extendió bajo la administración colonial.","pt":"Alexandre de Rhodes publicou um dicionário trilíngue em 1651 e ajudou a sistematizar o vietnamita em alfabeto latino. O sistema de escrita também se apoiou em predecessores portugueses, usa diacríticos para seis tons e se expandiu sob a administração colonial.","ru":"Александр де Род опубликовал трёхъязычный словарь в 1651 году и помог упорядочить запись вьетнамского латиницей. Система опиралась и на португальских предшественников, передаёт шесть тонов диакритическими знаками и распространилась при колониальной администрации.","uk":"Александр де Род опублікував тримовний словник 1651 року й допоміг упорядкувати запис в'єтнамської латиницею. Система спиралася й на португальських попередників, передає шість тонів діакритичними знаками та поширилася за колоніальної адміністрації.","ar":"نشر ألكسندر دو رود قاموسًا ثلاثي اللغات عام 1651 وأسهم في تنظيم كتابة الفيتنامية بالحروف اللاتينية. ويدين هذا النظام أيضًا لأسلاف برتغاليين، ويرسم ست نبرات بعلامات التشكيل، وقد انتشر في ظل الإدارة الاستعمارية.","he":"אלכסנדר דה רוד פרסם מילון תלת-לשוני ב-1651 ועזר להסדיר את כתיבת הווייטנאמית באותיות לטיניות. הכתב נשען גם על עבודת קודמיו הפורטוגזים, מסמן שישה טונים בסימנים דיאקריטיים, והתפשט בחסות המינהל הקולוניאלי.","sw":"Alexandre de Rhodes alichapisha kamusi ya lugha tatu mwaka 1651 na kusaidia kupanga Kivietinamu kwa herufi za Kilatini. Mfumo huo pia ulitegemea watangulizi wa Kireno, hutumia alama kuonyesha toni sita, na ulienea chini ya utawala wa kikoloni."},"body":{"en":"<h3>The man who didn't start it, but finished it</h3>\n<p><strong>Alexandre de Rhodes</strong> (1593–1660; some sources give 1591 for his birth) was a Jesuit missionary from Avignon, then a papal enclave inside what is now France. He arrived in Vietnam in 1624. He is widely, and somewhat unfairly, credited as the sole inventor of <strong>quốc ngữ</strong> (國語, \"national language script\"), the Latin-based writing system that Vietnamese uses today. The real story has more people in it, and is more interesting for it.</p>\n<p>The real pioneer was <strong>Francisco de Pina</strong>, a Portuguese Jesuit who arrived in Vietnam around 1617 and became the first European known to have mastered spoken Vietnamese. De Pina began developing a romanization system and training other missionaries to use tonal marks, but he drowned in a shipwreck in 1625, leaving his work unfinished. Rhodes had learned Vietnamese from de Pina. He also drew on dictionaries compiled by two other Portuguese Jesuits, Gaspar do Amaral and António Barbosa, and it was Rhodes who pulled all this earlier work together into one system.</p>\n\n<div class=\"trivia-actions\">\n  <button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"panto\" data-lat=\"16.0\" data-lng=\"108.0\" data-zoom=\"5\">📍 Fly to Vietnam</button>\n  <button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"setchar\" data-char=\"口\">🔢 See 口 (mouth/language) across scripts</button>\n</div>\n\n<h3>The 1651 dictionary: a linguistic triumph</h3>\n<p>Rhodes' <em>Dictionarium Annamiticum Lusitanum et Latinum</em> (\"Vietnamese-Portuguese-Latin Dictionary\") was printed in Rome in 1651 by the press of the Propaganda Fide, the Church's missionary congregation. It has about 8,000 Vietnamese entries, and each one is rendered in the new romanized script, then glossed in Portuguese and Latin. Its real achievement, though, is the way it handles sound:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Vietnamese has <strong>six lexical tones</strong> (in northern Vietnamese). Rhodes had to show all six with Latin letters and accent marks. De Pina and his colleagues had already started marking tones, but it was the 1651 dictionary that set out the full system in print.</li>\n  <li>He used the acute accent (´), grave accent (`), hook above (̉), tilde (˜), and a dot below (.) — some stacked on top of each other — to distinguish five of the six tones. The level tone is unmarked.</li>\n  <li>He also devised digraphs (nh, ng, kh, gh, ph, th, tr, ch) and modified vowels (ă, â, ê, ô, ơ, ư) to capture Vietnamese phonemes that Latin had no letters for.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The result was a script that was <em>far quicker to learn than Chữ Nôm</em>: a few dozen letters and marks instead of thousands of characters.</p><p>It was not yet modern Vietnamese, though. Rhodes spelled consonant clusters that the language has since lost, writing <em>bl-</em>, <em>ml-</em> and <em>tl-</em> at the start of words. Words spelled with <em>tl-</em> are written with <em>tr-</em> today, and early quốc ngữ documents spell the word for 'big', modern <em>lớn</em>, as <em>mlớn</em> or <em>blớn</em>. The spellings show the <em>tl-</em> to <em>tr-</em> change was complete by the end of the 18th century.</p>\n\n<h3>Why Vietnam and not Japan or Korea?</h3>\n<p>Christian missionaries attempted romanization in Japan too. The Portuguese Jesuit João Rodrigues produced a superb <em>Arte da Lingoa de Iapam</em> (1604) and romanized dictionaries, but Japan banned Christianity and expelled the missionaries in 1614, barred Portuguese ships in 1639, and the romanization died with them. Korea saw no comparable attempt at all: Catholicism only reached the peninsula in the 1780s, and the earliest Western romanizations of Korean are nineteenth-century (Siebold 1832; the Ross system, 1882). By then Korea already had <strong>Hangul</strong> — a domestically invented alphabet, long dismissed by the Confucian literati as 諺文 <em>eonmun</em>, \"vulgar script,\" but ideally suited to the vernacular, and the missionaries simply adopted it rather than inventing an alphabet of their own.</p>\n<p>Vietnam had no equivalent of Hangul. Chữ Nôm was brilliant but never became truly popular: it was unstandardized, difficult, and closely associated with the Vietnamese elite. When French colonizers aggressively promoted quốc ngữ — first making it the required script for official documents in the south (1882), then abolishing the Confucian examinations in 1919 — the population had no other script to rally round, and the Latin script filled the gap.</p>\n\n<h3>Tone diacritics: the system that survived</h3>\n<p>The tone marks Rhodes printed are still used today, almost unchanged:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Tone 1 (ngang — level): unmarked — <em>ma</em> (ghost)</li>\n  <li>Tone 2 (huyền — falling): grave accent — <em>mà</em> (but)</li>\n  <li>Tone 3 (sắc — rising): acute — <em>má</em> (mother)</li>\n  <li>Tone 4 (hỏi — dipping): hook — <em>mả</em> (tomb)</li>\n  <li>Tone 5 (ngã — broken): tilde — <em>mã</em> (horse)</li>\n  <li>Tone 6 (nặng — heavy): dot below — <em>mạ</em> (rice seedling)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Six one-syllable words with completely different meanings are told apart only by the mark on a single vowel. The system is elegant but hard on typesetters: one Vietnamese vowel can carry two marks at once (e.g., <em>ộ</em> = circumflex + dot below), which gave early Vietnamese printers and keyboard designers real trouble.</p>\n\n<h3>The irony of legacy</h3>\n<p>Rhodes was expelled from Vietnam by Vietnamese authorities in 1645 for proselytizing. He made his way back to Rome by 1649 and spent the next five years in Italy and France lobbying for bishops and funding for the Vietnam mission — the groundwork for the Paris Foreign Missions Society, founded after he left. In November 1654 his superiors sent him not to Vietnam but to Persia, where he died at Isfahan in 1660. His 1651 dictionary, published in Rome while he was thousands of miles from Vietnam, became an important early reference for a writing system now used throughout the country. Missionaries developed quốc ngữ for Christian texts; it was later adopted for secular publishing, nationalist writing, education and state administration.</p>"},"sources":["Rhodes, Alexandre de, 'Dictionarium Annamiticum Lusitanum et Latinum', Sacra Congregatio de Propaganda Fide, Rome, 1651","Alves, Mark J., 'A Grammar of Pacoh: A Mon-Khmer Language of the Central Highlands of Vietnam', Pacific Linguistics 580, Australian National University, 2006 [context on Mon-Khmer phonology of the Vietnamese area]","DeFrancis, John, 'Colonialism and Language Policy in Viet Nam', Mouton, 1977",{"text":"Phan, Peter C., 'Mission and Catechesis: Alexandre de Rhodes and Inculturation in Seventeenth-Century Vietnam', Orbis Books, 1998","url":"https://www.worldcat.org/title/mission-and-catechesis"},"Marr, David G., 'Vietnamese Tradition on Trial 1920–1945', University of California Press, 1981",{"text":"Hội Bảo tồn Di sản chữ Nôm — digitisation and quốc ngữ history","url":"https://www.nomfoundation.org/"},{"text":"Vũ Đức Nghiệu (2019). \"Vietnamese initial consonant clusters in quốc ngữ documents from the 17th to early 19th centuries.\" Journal of the Southeast Asian Linguistics Society 12(1): 143–162.","url":"http://hdl.handle.net/10524/52448"},{"text":"Wikipedia: Dictionarium Annamiticum Lusitanum et Latinum","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dictionarium_Annamiticum_Lusitanum_et_Latinum"}]},{"id":"go-on-kan-on-to-on","icon":"📜","tags":["sinosphere","kanji","history","sino-japanese","phonology"],"title":{"en":"Go-on, Kan-on and Tō-on: Three Sino-Japanese Reading Layers","ja":"呉音・漢音・唐音：なぜ一つの漢字に三種の音読みがあるのか","ko":"오음·한음·당음: 하나의 한자에 세 가지 음독이 있는 이유","zh":"吴音、汉音、唐音：为何一个汉字有三种日语读音","yue":"吳音、漢音、唐音：點解一個漢字有三種日語讀音","vi":"Ngô âm, Hán âm, Đường âm: vì sao một chữ Hán có ba cách đọc trong tiếng Nhật","th":"โกะอง คังอง โทอง: ทำไมคันจิตัวเดียวจึงอ่านได้สามแบบ","id":"Go-on, Kan-on, Tō-on: Tiga Lapisan Bacaan Sino-Jepang","hi":"गो-ऑन, कान-ऑन, तो-ऑन: चीनी-जापानी पाठों की तीन परतें","de":"Go-on, Kan-on, Tō-on: Drei Schichten sinojapanischer Lesungen","fr":"Go-on, Kan-on, Tō-on : trois couches de lectures sino-japonaises","it":"Go-on, Kan-on, Tō-on: tre strati di letture sino-giapponesi","es":"Go-on, Kan-on, Tō-on: tres capas de lecturas sino-japonesas","pt":"Go-on, Kan-on e Tō-on: três camadas de leitura sino-japonesa","ru":"Го-он, Кан-он и То-он: три слоя китайско-японских чтений","uk":"Ґо-он, Кан-он і То-он: три шари китайсько-японських читань","ar":"غو-أون وكان-أون وتو-أون: ثلاث طبقات من القراءات الصينية-اليابانية","he":"גו-און, קאן-און, טו-און: שלוש שכבות של קריאות סינו-יפניות","sw":"Go-on, Kan-on na Tō-on: matabaka matatu ya usomaji wa Kisino-Kijapani"},"summary":{"en":"Japanese borrowed Chinese characters three separate times over a millennium, each time from a different dialect and era — leaving a single kanji with up to three distinct Sino-Japanese readings stacked inside it.","ja":"日本語は千年以上にわたって漢字を三度借用したが、そのたびに異なる時代・異なる方言から取り入れたため、一つの漢字に最大三種類の音読みが重層的に残っている。","ko":"일본어는 천 년 이상에 걸쳐 한자를 서로 다른 시대와 방언에서 세 번 차용하였고, 그 결과 하나의 한자에 최대 세 가지 한자음이 공존하게 되었다.","zh":"日语在逾千年间三度借入汉字，每次均来自不同时代与方言，因此单个汉字最多叠加了三种不同的汉字音读。","yue":"日語喺超過一千年嘅時間入面，分咗三次從唔同時代同方言借入漢字，結果一個漢字入面最多疊埋三種唔同嘅漢字音讀。","vi":"Tiếng Nhật đã vay mượn chữ Hán ba lần riêng biệt trong hơn một nghìn năm, mỗi lần từ một phương ngữ và thời đại khác nhau, khiến một chữ Hán có thể mang tới ba cách đọc Hán-Nhật khác nhau.","th":"ภาษาญี่ปุ่นยืมเสียงอ่านอักษรจีนสามระลอกตลอดกว่าพันปี แต่ละระลอกมาจากสำเนียงและยุคสมัยต่างกัน คันจิตัวเดียวจึงมีเสียงอ่านแบบจีน-ญี่ปุ่นได้ถึงสามแบบ","id":"Bahasa Jepang meminjam bacaan aksara Tionghoa tiga kali selama lebih dari seribu tahun, masing-masing dari dialek dan zaman berbeda, sehingga satu kanji bisa punya hingga tiga cara baca Sino-Jepang.","hi":"हज़ार साल से ज़्यादा के दौरान जापानी ने अलग-अलग बोलियों और दौरों से तीन बार चीनी अक्षरों के पाठ उधार लिए, इसलिए एक कांजी के तीन तक अलग चीनी-जापानी पाठ हो सकते हैं।","de":"Das Japanische entlehnte chinesische Schriftzeichen über mehr als ein Jahrtausend hinweg dreimal aus verschiedenen Dialekten und Epochen, sodass ein einziges Kanji bis zu drei sinojapanische Lesungen hat.","fr":"Le japonais a emprunté les caractères chinois à trois reprises sur plus d'un millénaire, chaque fois à partir d'un dialecte et d'une époque différents, laissant à un seul kanji jusqu'à trois lectures sino-japonaises.","it":"In più di mille anni il giapponese ha preso in prestito le letture dei caratteri cinesi tre volte, ogni volta da un dialetto e da un’epoca diversi, e così un solo kanji può avere fino a tre letture sino-giapponesi.","es":"El japonés tomó prestados los caracteres chinos tres veces separadas a lo largo de más de un milenio, cada vez de un dialecto y una época distintos, de modo que un mismo kanji puede tener hasta tres lecturas sino-japonesas.","pt":"O japonês tomou emprestados caracteres chineses em três momentos separados, ao longo de mais de um milênio, cada vez de um dialeto e época diferentes, deixando um único kanji com até três leituras sino-japonesas.","ru":"Японский язык заимствовал китайские иероглифы трижды на протяжении более тысячи лет, каждый раз из разных диалектов и эпох, оставив в одном кандзи до трёх различных китайско-японских чтений.","uk":"Японська понад тисячу років тричі запозичувала китайські читання ієрогліфів, щоразу з інших діалектів та епох, тому одне кандзі може мати до трьох різних китайсько-японських читань.","ar":"اقترضت اليابانية الحروف الصينية ثلاث مرات على مدى أكثر من ألف عام، في كل مرة من لهجة وحقبة مختلفتين، فصار للحرف الواحد ما يصل إلى ثلاث قراءات صينية-يابانية.","he":"היפנית שאלה את קריאות הסימנים הסיניים שלוש פעמים במשך יותר מאלף שנה, בכל פעם מניב ומתקופה אחרים, ולכן לקאנג'י אחד יכולות להיות עד שלוש קריאות סינו-יפניות.","sw":"Kijapani kilikopa herufi za Kichina mara tatu tofauti katika zaidi ya miaka elfu moja, kila wakati kutoka lahaja na enzi tofauti, na kusababisha kanji moja kuwa na hadi usomaji tatu wa Kisino-Kijapani."},"body":{"en":"<h3>Three waves across a millennium</h3>\n<p>Open any Japanese dictionary and look up a common character like <strong>行</strong> (go, travel). You will find not one but <em>three</em> Sino-Japanese (音読み <em>on'yomi</em>) readings: <em>gyō</em>, <em>kō</em>, and <em>an</em>. They are not alternative spellings of the same pronunciation. They are three separate historical imports, each reflecting a different stage of Chinese phonology, borrowed centuries apart.</p>\n\n<div class=\"trivia-actions\">\n  <button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"setchar\" data-char=\"行:1\">📖 Show 行 on the map</button>\n  <button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"setchar\" data-char=\"日\">☀️ Show 日 on the map</button>\n</div>\n\n<h3>Layer 1: 呉音 Go-on (5th–6th century)</h3>\n<p>The oldest layer arrived before Japan had any formal diplomatic contact with the Tang court. It came via the <strong>Korean peninsula</strong> — by tradition from Baekje — carrying the character readings associated with the <em>Southern Dynasties</em> capital at Jiankang (modern Nanjing), in the old land of Wu 呉. The main carriers were Buddhist monks bringing sutras. The name <em>Go-on</em> (\"Wu sound\") is not evidence for that route, though: it only appears from the mid-Heian period, coined by promoters of Kan-on who borrowed the Tang capital's dismissive term for southern provincial speech. Until then these readings were simply called <em>wa-on</em> 和音, and the alternative traditional names 百済音 (<em>Kudara-on</em>) and 対馬音 (<em>Tsushima-on</em>) point at Korea instead. No contemporary document ties them directly to southern Chinese.</p>\n<p>Go-on preserves features of Middle Chinese that the Tang capital's speech was already losing: voiced consonants stay voiced, and nasal consonants stay nasal. Examples: <strong>経</strong> <em>kyō</em>, <strong>男</strong> <em>nan</em>, <strong>行</strong> <em>gyō</em>. The voiced <em>g-</em> in <em>gyō</em> reflects the Middle Chinese 匣 initial of 行 — the voiced fricative of *<em>ɦˠæŋ</em>, which Old Japanese, having no voiced fricative of its own, took in as <em>g-</em>. By the Chang'an standard behind Kan-on that initial had already lost its voicing, giving <em>kō</em>.</p>\n\n<h3>Layer 2: 漢音 Kan-on (7th–9th century)</h3>\n<p>From the 7th century onward, Japanese government missions (<em>kentōshi</em> 遣唐使) traveled directly to Tang Chang'an and brought back the prestige pronunciation of the capital. This layer — <em>Kan-on</em>, \"Han sound\" — was pushed hard as the correct, scholarly standard. In 792 Emperor Kanmu ordered students of the Confucian classics to learn it, and monks who would not study Kan-on were refused passage to China. Yet it never drove Go-on out of everyday words or out of the temples. Most sutras are still chanted in Go-on: 如是我聞 \"Thus have I heard\" is <em>nyoze gamon</em>, and only the few sutras traditionally read in Kan-on turn it into <em>josi gabun</em>.</p>\n<p>Kan-on mirrors the speech of Chang'an in the 8th century, where two changes were under way. Voiced consonants were losing their voicing, so 行 <em>gyō</em> became <em>kō</em>. And nasal consonants were turning into voiced stops (<em>m</em> to <em>mb</em>, <em>n</em> to <em>nd</em>), so 男 <em>nan</em> became <em>dan</em> and 馬 \"horse\" became <em>ba</em>. Vowels shifted too: 経 <em>kyō</em> became <em>kei</em>. That is why Kan-on has so few readings starting with <em>m-</em> or <em>n-</em>.</p>\n\n<div class=\"trivia-actions\">\n  <button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"panto\" data-lat=\"34.26\" data-lng=\"108.95\" data-zoom=\"5\">🏯 Fly to Tang Chang'an (Xi'an)</button>\n  <button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"focus\" data-code=\"ja\">📍 Highlight Sino-Japanese on the map</button>\n</div>\n\n<h3>Layer 3: 唐音 Tō-on (12th century onward)</h3>\n<p>Confusingly, <em>Tō-on</em> (\"Tang sound\") does not reflect the Tang dynasty at all. It is a later stratum arriving from Song, Yuan, and Ming dynasty China, mainly through Zen Buddhist temples, the tea ceremony, and cuisine. The name is an anachronism: long after the dynasty fell, 唐 went on serving in Japanese as a general word for China.</p>\n<p>Tō-on imports are fewer but recognisable in everyday objects: <strong>行灯</strong> <em>andon</em> (paper lantern), <strong>蒲団</strong> <em>futon</em>, <strong>椅子</strong> <em>isu</em> (chair), <strong>饅頭</strong> <em>manjū</em>. The <em>an-</em> in <em>andon</em> reflects a later Chinese pronunciation. By then the Middle Chinese 匣 initial of 行 — a voiced fricative *<em>ɦ-</em>, never a nasal — had dropped away altogether (*<em>ɦaŋ</em> → <em>aŋ</em>). Japanese took in the remaining final -ŋ as -n, something neither Go-on nor Kan-on captured.</p>\n\n<h3>Why does this matter?</h3>\n<p>The three layers preserve evidence from different periods of Chinese phonological history. By comparing Go-on, Kan-on, and Middle Chinese reconstructions, linguists can triangulate what sounds existed in Chinese across many centuries. Chinese rhyme books sort characters into sound classes, but because they spell characters only with other characters, they cannot show directly what anything sounded like; a kana spelling such as <em>dan</em> gives an outside clue, if only an approximate one. Japanese kanji readings therefore complement Chinese rhyme books, modern varieties and other Sino-Xenic traditions in historical reconstruction.</p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Go-on</strong>: Buddhist vocabulary, older temple readings — 経 <em>kyō</em>, 行 <em>gyō</em>, 男 <em>nan</em></li>\n  <li><strong>Kan-on</strong>: Scholarly/official vocabulary — 経 <em>kei</em>, 行 <em>kō</em>, 男 <em>dan</em></li>\n  <li><strong>Tō-on</strong>: Zen/cuisine/everyday objects — 行灯 <em>andon</em>, 蒲団 <em>futon</em></li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"trivia-actions\">\n  <button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"setchar\" data-char=\"南\">🧭 Try 南 (Go-on nan or na, as in 南無 namu / Kan-on dan)</button>\n  <button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"setchar\" data-char=\"東\">🧭 Try 東 (Go-on tsū vs Kan-on tō)</button>\n</div>\n\n<p>So 行 has three on-yomi because each was borrowed at a different point in roughly a thousand years of contact with China.</p>"},"sources":["Miyake, Marc Hideo (2003). Old Japanese: A Phonetic Reconstruction. RoutledgeCurzon.","Pulleyblank, Edwin G. (1984). Middle Chinese: A Study in Historical Phonology. University of British Columbia Press.","Frellesvig, Bjarke (2010). A History of the Japanese Language. Cambridge University Press. (Chapter 3: Sino-Japanese layers.)","Karlgren, Bernhard (1954). Compendium of Phonetics in Ancient and Archaic Chinese. Bulletin of the Museum of Far Eastern Antiquities 26.",{"text":"Wikipedia: Sino-Japanese vocabulary — Go-on, Kan-on, Tō-on","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sino-Japanese_vocabulary"},{"text":"Wiktionary appendix: Sino-Japanese readings","url":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Appendix:Japanese_glossary#on%27yomi"},{"text":"Japanese Wikipedia: 漢音 (Kan-on; 792 edict, denasalization)","url":"https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/漢音"}]},{"id":"min-nan-wenbai","icon":"🗣️","tags":["sinosphere","min-nan","hokkien","phonology","historical-linguistics"],"title":{"en":"Min Nan Literary and Colloquial Readings","ja":"閩南語の文白異読：同じ漢字にある二つの読み","ko":"민난어의 문백이독: 한자에 공존하는 두 독음","zh":"闽南语的文白异读：同一汉字的两套读音","yue":"閩南語嘅文讀同白讀","vi":"Văn–bạch dị độc trong tiếng Mân Nam: hai cách đọc của cùng một chữ","th":"การอ่านแบบวรรณกรรมและแบบภาษาพูดในภาษาหมิ่นหนาน","id":"Bacaan Sastra dan Bacaan Sehari-hari dalam Min Nan","hi":"मिन नान के साहित्यिक और बोलचाल के पठन","de":"Literarische und umgangssprachliche Lesungen im Min Nan","fr":"Lectures littéraires et vernaculaires du Min Nan","it":"Letture letterarie e colloquiali del Min Nan","es":"Lecturas literarias y coloquiales del Min Nan","pt":"Leituras literárias e coloquiais do Min Nan","ru":"Литературные и разговорные чтения в миньнань","uk":"Книжні й розмовні читання в міньнань","ar":"القراءات الأدبية والعامية في مين نان","he":"קריאות ספרותיות ועממיות במין-נאן","sw":"Usomaji wa fasihi na wa mazungumzo katika Min Nan"},"summary":{"en":"Min Nan (Hokkien/Taiwanese) gives many characters two readings, a colloquial one (白讀) and a literary one (文讀). The two layers come from different periods of Chinese, so they preserve evidence of older sound changes and bear on where Min sits within the Chinese family.","ja":"閩南語（ホーロー語・台湾語）では、多くの漢字に口語の「白讀」と文語の「文讀」という二つの読みがある。二つの層は中国語の異なる時代に由来するため、古い音変化の証拠を今に伝え、閩語が漢語の中でどこに位置するのかという問題にも深く関わっている。","ko":"민난어(호키엔어/대만어)는 많은 한자에 두 가지 독음, 곧 구어음(白讀)과 문어음(文讀)을 둔다. 두 층은 서로 다른 시대의 중국어에서 왔기 때문에, 더 오래된 음운 변화의 증거를 간직하고 있으며 중국어 가족 안에서 민어가 어디에 자리하는지를 가늠하는 데도 실마리가 된다.","zh":"闽南语（福建话/台湾话）里，许多汉字都有两种读音：口语的白读和读书的文读。这两层来自汉语的不同时期，因而保存着更古老的音变证据，也关系到闽语在汉语大家族中的位置。","yue":"閩南語（福建話/台灣話）好多漢字都有兩個讀音：口語嘅白讀同讀書嘅文讀。兩層來自唔同時代嘅漢語，所以保存住更古老音變嘅證據，亦關乎閩語喺漢語大家族入面究竟企喺邊個位。","vi":"Tiếng Mân Nam (Hokkien/Đài Loan) cho nhiều chữ Hán hai cách đọc: một âm khẩu ngữ (白讀) và một âm đọc sách (文讀). Hai tầng này đến từ những thời kỳ khác nhau của tiếng Hán, nên chúng lưu giữ dấu vết của những biến đổi ngữ âm xa xưa và góp phần trả lời câu hỏi tiếng Mân đứng ở đâu trong đại gia đình tiếng Hán.","th":"ในภาษาหมิ่นหนาน (ฮกเกี้ยน/ไต้หวัน) อักษรจำนวนมากมีเสียงอ่านสองแบบ คือแบบภาษาพูด (白讀) และแบบวรรณกรรม (文讀) สองชั้นนี้มาจากภาษาจีนต่างยุคกัน จึงเก็บหลักฐานการเปลี่ยนแปลงเสียงในยุคเก่าไว้ และช่วยตอบว่าหมิ่นอยู่ตรงไหนในตระกูลภาษาจีน","id":"Min Nan (Hokkien/Taiwan) memberi banyak aksara dua bacaan: bacaan sehari-hari (白讀) dan bacaan sastra (文讀). Kedua lapisan itu berasal dari zaman yang berbeda, sehingga menyimpan bukti perubahan bunyi yang lebih tua dan ikut menentukan kedudukan Min di dalam keluarga bahasa Tionghoa.","hi":"मिन नान (होक्किएन/ताइवानी) में बहुत-से अक्षरों के दो पठन होते हैं — एक बोलचाल का (白讀) और एक साहित्यिक (文讀)। ये दोनों परतें चीनी के अलग-अलग दौर से आई हैं, इसलिए इनमें पुराने ध्वनि-परिवर्तनों के सबूत बचे हैं, और ये इस सवाल पर भी रोशनी डालती हैं कि चीनी परिवार में मिन की जगह कहाँ है।","de":"Min Nan (Hokkien/Taiwanesisch) gibt vielen Zeichen zwei Lesungen: eine umgangssprachliche (白讀) und eine literarische (文讀). Die beiden Schichten stammen aus verschiedenen Epochen des Chinesischen. Deshalb bewahren sie Spuren älterer Lautwandel und sagen etwas darüber, wo das Min in der chinesischen Sprachfamilie steht.","fr":"Le Min Nan (hokkien/taïwanais) donne à beaucoup de caractères deux lectures : une vernaculaire (白讀) et une littéraire (文讀). Ces deux couches viennent d'époques différentes du chinois : elles gardent donc la trace de changements phonétiques plus anciens, et pèsent dans le débat sur la place du min au sein de la famille chinoise.","it":"Il Min Nan (hokkien/taiwanese) dà a molti caratteri due letture: una vernacolare (白讀) e una letteraria (文讀). I due strati vengono da epoche diverse del cinese, e così custodiscono le tracce di mutamenti fonetici più antichi e pesano nel dibattito su dove collocare il min all'interno della famiglia cinese.","es":"El Min Nan (hokkien/taiwanés) da a muchos caracteres dos lecturas, una coloquial (白讀) y otra literaria (文讀). Las dos capas proceden de épocas distintas del chino, así que conservan huellas de cambios fonéticos antiguos y pesan en el debate sobre el lugar del min dentro de la familia china.","pt":"O Min Nan (hokkien/taiwanês) dá a muitos caracteres duas leituras: uma coloquial (白讀) e uma literária (文讀). As duas camadas vêm de épocas diferentes do chinês, por isso guardam vestígios de mudanças sonoras antigas — e pesam no debate sobre o lugar do min dentro da família chinesa.","ru":"Миньнань (хоккиен/тайваньский) даёт многим иероглифам два чтения — разговорное (白讀) и литературное (文讀). Эти два слоя восходят к разным эпохам китайского языка, поэтому хранят свидетельства более древних звуковых изменений и помогают понять, какое место минь занимает в китайской семье.","uk":"Міньнань (хоккієн/тайванська) дає багатьом ієрогліфам по два читання — розмовне (白讀) і книжне (文讀). Ці шари походять із різних епох китайської, тож зберігають свідчення давніших звукових змін і важать у суперечці про те, яке місце мінь посідає в китайській родині.","ar":"تمنح لغة مين نان (الهوكينية/التايوانية) كثيراً من الحروف قراءتين: عامية (白讀) وأدبية (文讀). والطبقتان آتيتان من حقبتين مختلفتين من تاريخ الصينية، فهما تحفظان أدلة على تحولات صوتية أقدم، وتُلقيان ضوءاً على موقع المين داخل العائلة الصينية.","he":"במין-נאן (הוקיין/טייוואנית) יש לסימנים רבים שתי קריאות: קריאה עממית (白讀) וקריאה ספרותית (文讀). שתי השכבות באות מתקופות שונות של הסינית, ולכן הן משמרות עדויות לשינויי הגה עתיקים יותר, והן משפיעות גם על השאלה היכן בדיוק ממוקמת המין במשפחת הלשונות הסיניות.","sw":"Min Nan (Hokkien/Kitaiwani) huzipa herufi nyingi usomaji mara mbili: wa mazungumzo (白讀) na wa fasihi (文讀). Matabaka hayo mawili yanatoka katika vipindi tofauti vya Kichina, kwa hiyo yanahifadhi ushahidi wa mabadiliko ya zamani ya sauti na yanagusa swali la mahali Kimin kinaposimama ndani ya familia ya Kichina."},"body":{"en":"<h3>One character, two completely different words</h3>\n<p>In most languages, a written word has one pronunciation (give or take dialectal variation). Min Nan — the mother tongue of about 34 million people (a 2025 estimate) in Fujian, Taiwan, Southeast Asia and diaspora communities worldwide — breaks this rule systematically. Many common characters have <em>two</em> pronunciations, drawn from two different layers of the sound system: the <strong>白讀 pe̍h-tho̍k</strong> (colloquial/vernacular reading) and the <strong>文讀 bûn-tho̍k</strong> (literary/formal reading).</p>\n\n<div class=\"trivia-actions\">\n  <button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"setchar\" data-char=\"一\">🔢 Show 一 on the map</button>\n  <button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"setchar\" data-char=\"日\">☀️ Show 日 on the map</button>\n  <button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"setchar\" data-char=\"二\">🔢 Show 二 on the map</button>\n</div>\n\n<h3>The famous pairs</h3>\n<p>Three common characters show the contrast:</p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>一</strong> (one): everyday <em>tsi̍t</em> (as in <em>tsi̍t ê lâng</em>, \"one person\") vs. literary <em>it</em> (as in <em>tē-it</em>, \"first place\"). These are not two layers of one word but two different words. <em>it</em> is the reading of 一; <em>tsi̍t</em> is a separate Min word for \"one\" whose own character is 蜀 (the <em>Fangyan</em>, 1st century BC: \"in southern Chu, one is called 蜀\"). It is written 一 by borrowing, which makes it a 訓讀, not a 白讀.</li>\n  <li><strong>兩</strong> (two of something): colloquial <em>nn̄g</em> (as in <em>nn̄g ê</em>, \"two of them\") vs. literary <em>lióng</em>. The character <strong>二</strong> has no colloquial reading of its own — only <em>jī</em> (Zhangzhou-type, southern Taiwan) or <em>lī</em> (Quanzhou/Xiamen-type, northern Taiwan), which is a regional split rather than a literary/colloquial one — so everyday \"two + classifier\" is written 兩.</li>\n  <li><strong>十</strong> (ten): colloquial <em>tsa̍p</em> (as in <em>tsa̍p ê</em>, \"ten of them\") vs. literary <em>si̍p</em> (as in <em>si̍p-jī-kè</em>, \"cross\", literally \"ten-character frame\"). Here the initial consonant and the vowel both differ.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"trivia-actions\">\n  <button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"focus\" data-code=\"nan\">📍 Highlight Taiwanese Min Nan</button>\n  <button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"panto\" data-lat=\"25.0\" data-lng=\"121.5\" data-zoom=\"6\">🗾 Fly to Taiwan</button>\n</div>\n\n<h3>Why two systems?</h3>\n<p>The colloquial layer is the <strong>older</strong> of the two, and it is itself layered. Northern settlers brought Chinese into Fujian in waves: the oldest vocabulary is usually traced to the Han dynasty (206 BC – AD 220), a second layer to the Northern and Southern Dynasties (420–589). Fujian's mountains kept it off the main north–south migration routes, which ran further west through the Gan and Xiang river valleys, so its speech went its own way for centuries.</p>\n\n<p>The literary layer arrived later, in the Tang dynasty (618–907), modelled on the prestige speech of the capital, Chang'an. Scholars reading the Confucian classics aloud needed the \"correct\" capital pronunciations, which by then were <em>very different</em> from how the same characters had evolved locally. Rather than replacing the vernacular — which was used for everything domestic, emotional, and embodied — speakers layered the literary pronunciations on top, reserving them for formal reading, poetry, and religious recitation.</p>\n\n<h3>Min Nan as a linguistic time capsule</h3>\n<p>Min Nan, and its colloquial layer above all, preserves features of Old Chinese and Early Middle Chinese that Mandarin and most northern varieties have lost or levelled:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Final stop consonants</strong> (-p, -t, -k entering tones). Standard Mandarin has lost these entirely; Min Nan retains them vigorously, as do Cantonese and Hakka: <em>tsi̍t</em> ends in a clear -t stop.</li>\n  <li><strong>Old dental stops.</strong> Where Mandarin has <em>zh-</em> or <em>ch-</em>, Min Nan often keeps the plain <em>t-</em> of Old Chinese: 豬 <em>ti</em> \"pig\" (Mandarin <em>zhū</em>), 茶 <em>tê</em> \"tea\" (Mandarin <em>chá</em>). That Amoy <em>tê</em>, carried to Europe by Dutch traders, is where the English word <em>tea</em> comes from.</li>\n  <li><strong>No <em>f</em> yet.</strong> Old Chinese had no <em>f</em> sound, and the colloquial layer often keeps the older <em>p-</em>: 飛 \"fly\" is colloquial <em>pue</em> (or <em>pe</em>) but literary <em>hui</em>, and Mandarin <em>fēi</em>.</li>\n  <li><strong>The 日母 initial</strong>: 二 <em>jī</em> still carries a consonant inherited from Middle Chinese 日母 (Old Chinese *<em>ni[j]-s</em> in Baxter &amp; Sagart's reconstruction), whereas Mandarin has worn the initial away entirely, leaving only the vowel of <em>èr</em>.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<blockquote>\"…it is not surprising that the dialects spoken here lie outside the mainstream of Chinese linguistic development. …[T]his group is, next to Mandarin, the most distinctive and easily characterized group of Chinese dialects.\" — Jerry Norman, <em>Chinese</em> (1988), p. 228</blockquote>\n\n<p>Norman balances the ledger in the same paragraph, and it is worth keeping both halves: Min preserves numerous archaisms found nowhere else in Sinitic, but it also carries a whole series of local innovations peculiar to Min. What makes it valuable to historical linguistics is not simply that it is \"older\" than its neighbours. As Norman argues, Min is the one major dialect group that cannot be derived entirely from the Middle Chinese of the rhyme books, so it preserves evidence no other variety can supply.</p>\n\n<h3>Choosing which layer to use</h3>\n<p>A fluent Min Nan speaker switches registers almost unconsciously:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Counting objects at the market: <em>tsi̍t, nn̄g, sann…</em> (colloquial)</li>\n  <li>Reciting a poem or an official title: <em>it, jī, sam…</em> (literary)</li>\n  <li>Counting to eleven: <em>tsa̍p-it</em> — colloquial <em>tsa̍p</em> for 十, literary <em>it</em> for 一</li>\n  <li>\"Monday\": <em>pài-it</em>, short for <em>lé-pài-it</em> (禮拜一, \"week-one\") — literary <em>it</em> for the number. 拜 <em>pài</em> has only one reading, so there is no colloquial/literary choice to make for it.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Speakers do not work this out; they absorb it, and getting it wrong marks someone as a learner as surely as a misplaced tone.</p>\n\n<div class=\"trivia-actions\">\n  <button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"setchar\" data-char=\"人\">👤 Try 人 (lâng vs. jîn)</button>\n  <button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"setchar\" data-char=\"水\">💧 Try 水 (chúi vs. súi)</button>\n</div>\n\n<p>The 文白異讀 system is no defect, and Min Nan is not alone in it: Wu, Hakka and Cantonese also layer literary readings over colloquial ones. What sets Min Nan apart is the depth of its colloquial stratum. Every speaker carries several eras of Chinese at once, Han-dynasty and later vernacular below and the Tang reading standard above, which is why historical linguists treat Min Nan as irreplaceable evidence for how Chinese sounded long before any of its modern forms existed.</p>"},"sources":["Norman, Jerry (1988). Chinese. Cambridge University Press. (Chapter 8: Southern dialects and Min.)","Baxter, William H. & Sagart, Laurent (2014). Old Chinese: A New Reconstruction. Oxford University Press.","Pulleyblank, Edwin G. (1984). Middle Chinese: A Study in Historical Phonology. University of British Columbia Press.",{"text":"Mair, Victor H. (ed.) (2001). The Columbia History of Chinese Literature. Columbia University Press. (Section on Min dialects.)","url":""},{"text":"Wikipedia: Taiwanese Hokkien — Literary and colloquial readings","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Taiwanese_Hokkien#Literary_and_colloquial_readings"},{"text":"Wikipedia: Literary and colloquial readings","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Literary_and_colloquial_readings"},{"text":"Wikipedia: Southern Min (speaker estimate, 2025)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Southern_Min"},{"text":"Wikipedia: Min Chinese — History (Norman's four layers)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Min_Chinese#History"},{"text":"Wikipedia: Etymology of tea","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Etymology_of_tea"}]},{"id":"korean-toumeop-rule","icon":"🔤","tags":["korean","phonology","north-south","sinosphere"],"title":{"en":"The Head-Sound Rule: Why 六 Is 육 in Seoul but 륙 in Pyongyang","ja":"頭音法則：なぜ「六」は南で육、北で륙なのか","ko":"두음법칙: 왜 '六'이 서울에서는 육, 평양에서는 륙인가","zh":"头音法则：为何“六”在首尔读육、在平壤读륙","yue":"頭音法則：點解「六」喺首爾係육、喺平壤係륙","vi":"Quy tắc âm đầu: vì sao 六 đọc là 육 ở Seoul nhưng 륙 ở Bình Nhưỡng","th":"กฎเสียงต้น: ทำไม 六 อ่านว่า 육 ในโซล แต่ 륙 ในเปียงยาง","id":"Aturan bunyi awal: mengapa 六 dibaca 육 di Seoul tetapi 륙 di Pyongyang","hi":"प्रारंभिक-ध्वनि नियम: सियोल में 六 = 육, प्योंगयांग में 륙 क्यों?","de":"Die Anlautregel: Warum 六 in Seoul 육, in Pjöngjang aber 륙 ist","fr":"La règle d'initiale : pourquoi 六 se prononce 육 à Séoul mais 륙 à Pyongyang","it":"La regola dell'iniziale: perché 六 è 육 a Seoul ma 륙 a Pyongyang","es":"La regla del sonido inicial: por qué 六 es 육 en Seúl pero 륙 en Pyongyang","pt":"A regra do som inicial: por que 六 é 육 em Seul mas 륙 em Pyongyang","ru":"Правило начального звука: почему 六 — это 육 в Сеуле, но 륙 в Пхеньяне","uk":"Правило початкового звуку: чому 六 — це 육 у Сеулі, але 륙 у Пхеньяні","ar":"قاعدة الصوت الأولي: لماذا تُنطق 六 بـ육 في سيول و륙 في بيونغيانغ","he":"כלל הצליל הראשוני: למה 六 הוא 육 בסיאול ו-륙 בפיונגיאנג","sw":"Kanuni ya sauti ya awali: kwa nini 六 ni 육 Seoul lakini 륙 Pyongyang"},"summary":{"en":"South Korea's 두음법칙 (頭音法則) drops word-initial /l-/ and /n-/ before certain vowels — a codified sound change that North Korea rejected, splitting one language into two spelling systems.","ja":"韓国の頭音法則は語頭の/l-/・/n-/を特定の母音の前で脱落させる音変化を法律で定めたもので、北朝鮮はこれを拒否したため、一つの言語が二つの表記体系に分かれた。","ko":"한국의 두음법칙은 어두의 /l-/·/n-/을 특정 모음 앞에서 탈락시키는 음변화를 법으로 규정한 것으로, 북한이 이를 거부하면서 하나의 언어가 두 가지 표기 체계로 나뉘었다.","zh":"韩国的头音法则将词首/l-/和/n-/在特定元音前的脱落以法律形式固定，北朝鲜拒绝接受，导致同一语言分裂为两套拼写体系。","yue":"韓國嘅頭音法則將詞首/l-/同/n-/喺特定元音前嘅脫落以法律形式固定，北韓唔接受，令同一種語言分裂成兩套拼寫系統。","vi":"Quy tắc âm đầu của Hàn Quốc luật hóa sự mất /l-/ và /n-/ ở đầu từ trước một số nguyên âm nhất định — Bắc Triều Tiên bác bỏ quy tắc này, khiến cùng một ngôn ngữ tách thành hai hệ thống chính tả.","th":"กฎเสียงต้นของเกาหลีใต้กำหนดให้ตัด /l-/ และ /n-/ ที่ต้นคำเมื่ออยู่หน้าสระบางเสียง แต่เกาหลีเหนือไม่ใช้กฎนี้ ภาษาเดียวกันจึงแตกเป็นสองระบบการเขียน","id":"Aturan bunyi awal Korea Selatan meresmikan hilangnya /l-/ dan /n-/ di awal kata sebelum vokal tertentu. Korea Utara menolak aturan ini, sehingga satu bahasa terbelah menjadi dua sistem ejaan.","hi":"दक्षिण कोरिया के 두음법칙 (頭音法則) नियम ने कुछ स्वरों से पहले शब्द की शुरुआत वाले /l-/ और /n-/ के लोप को आधिकारिक वर्तनी बना दिया। उत्तर कोरिया ने इसे नहीं माना, और एक भाषा दो वर्तनियों में बँट गई।","de":"Südkoreas Anlautregel (두음법칙) kodifiziert den Wegfall von /l-/ und /n-/ im Anlaut vor bestimmten Vokalen. Nordkorea lehnte sie ab, und so spaltete sich die Rechtschreibung einer Sprache in zwei.","fr":"La règle d'initiale sud-coréenne (두음법칙) officialise la chute de /l-/ et /n-/ en début de mot devant certaines voyelles. Rejetée par la Corée du Nord, elle a divisé une langue en deux systèmes orthographiques.","it":"La regola dell'iniziale della Corea del Sud fa cadere /l-/ e /n-/ a inizio parola davanti a certe vocali. La Corea del Nord l'ha respinta, e una lingua si è ritrovata con due ortografie.","es":"La regla del sonido inicial de Corea del Sur codifica la caída de /l-/ y /n-/ al inicio de palabra ante ciertas vocales. Corea del Norte la rechazó, y eso dividió una lengua en dos sistemas ortográficos.","pt":"A regra do som inicial da Coreia do Sul codifica a queda de /l-/ e /n-/ no início de palavra diante de certas vogais — rejeitada pela Coreia do Norte, dividiu uma língua em dois sistemas ortográficos.","ru":"Правило начального звука Южной Кореи закрепляет выпадение /l-/ и /n-/ в начале слова перед определёнными гласными — Северная Корея отвергла его, разделив один язык на две орфографические системы.","uk":"Південнокорейське правило початкового звуку велить відкидати /l-/ і /n-/ на початку слова перед певними голосними. Північна Корея від нього відмовилася, і одна мова дістала дві орфографічні системи.","ar":"تُقنّن قاعدة الصوت الأولي في كوريا الجنوبية حذف /l-/ و/n-/ في بداية الكلمة قبل حروف علة معيّنة، وقد رفضتها كوريا الشمالية فصار للغة الواحدة إملاءان.","he":"כלל הצליל הראשוני של קוריאה הדרומית קובע ש-/l-/ ו-/n-/ נושרים בתחילת מילה לפני תנועות מסוימות. קוריאה הצפונית דחתה אותו, ושפה אחת התפצלה לשתי מערכות כתיב.","sw":"Kanuni ya sauti ya awali ya Korea Kusini inaondoa /l-/ na /n-/ mwanzoni mwa neno mbele ya irabu fulani. Korea Kaskazini iliikataa kanuni hiyo, na hivyo lugha moja ikawa na mifumo miwili ya tahajia."},"body":{"en":"<h3>One Character, Two Readings</h3><p>Type the character 六 into HanMap and you see two very different romanizations side by side: South Korean <strong>yuk</strong> (육) and North Korean <strong>ryuk</strong> (륙). This is not dialect drift but deliberate policy, written into each government's official orthography.</p><h3>What the Rule Says</h3><p>South Korea's <em>두음법칙</em> (頭音法則, head-sound rule) reshapes word-initial /l/ and /n/ in Sino-Korean syllables, but in two different ways. Before <em>i</em> or <em>y</em> the consonant drops altogether: 龍 (dragon) is <strong>용</strong> yong, not 룡 ryong. Before any other vowel an initial /l/ becomes /n/ instead: 來 (come) is <strong>내</strong> nae at the start of a word (내일 來日, tomorrow) but 래 rae word-internally (미래 未來, future). An initial /n/ behaves the same way before <em>i</em> or <em>y</em>: 女 (woman) is 여 yeo in 여자 'woman' but 녀 nyeo in 소녀 'girl'. Even surnames split: the common name 李 is 이 Yi in the South and 리 Ri in the North. The rule was codified in the 1933 Unified Hangul Orthography and reinforced by the 1988 standard still in use today.</p><h3>Why North Korea Disagreed</h3><p>When Korea was divided after 1945, the North went its own way. The 1948 <em>Joseon-eo Sincheoljabeop</em> (New Korean Orthography) already left the head-sound rule out, and the 1954 <em>Joseon-eo Cheoljabeop</em> (Korean Orthography), issued by the DPRK Academy of Sciences, confirmed its rejection as an artificial distortion of the underlying form. North Korean orthography preserves the initial /l/ and /n/, so the same characters read 룡, 래, 립 — closer to the Middle Chinese source pronunciations.</p><h3>Political Dimension</h3><p>The split affects ordinary reading across the border. South Koreans reading North Korean text must mentally re-map dozens of common words, and bringing the two spellings together is serious work. Linguists from both sides began meeting in 2005 to compile a joint dictionary (<em>Gyeoremal Keunsajeon</em>), though the joint sessions have been suspended since 2016, after the North's fourth nuclear test and the closure of the Kaesong industrial zone, and the southern team has carried on alone.</p><div class=\"trivia-actions\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"setchar\" data-char=\"六\">Show 六 on map</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"focus\" data-code=\"ko\">Focus Korean on map</button></div>"},"sources":["Lee, Iksop & Ramsey, S.R. (2000). The Korean Language. State University of New York Press.","Kim, Chin-Wu (1971). The Phonological Basis of Korean Head Sound Law. Language Research 7(1).",{"text":"국립국어원 — 두음법칙 해설","url":"https://www.korean.go.kr/"},"Sohn, Ho-min (1999). The Korean Language. Cambridge University Press."]},{"id":"japanese-go-on-bias","icon":"🕌","tags":["japanese","kanji","go-on","buddhism","sinosphere"],"title":{"en":"Why Buddhist Japanese Sounds Different: The 呉音 (Go-on) Bias","ja":"仏教語はなぜ呉音か：「経」がkyōで「業」がgōの理由","ko":"일본 불교 용어가 다르게 들리는 이유: 呉音(오음) 편향","zh":"为何日语佛教词汇听起来不同：呉音（吴音）偏向","yue":"點解日本佛教詞彙聽落唔同：呉音偏向嘅由來","vi":"Tại sao tiếng Nhật Phật giáo nghe khác: xu hướng âm Ngô (呉音)","th":"ทำไมศัพท์พุทธในภาษาญี่ปุ่นจึงฟังต่างออกไป: เรื่องของ 呉音 (โกะอง)","id":"Mengapa bahasa Jepang Buddhis terdengar lain: kecenderungan 呉音 (go-on)","hi":"जापानी बौद्ध शब्द अलग क्यों सुनाई देते हैं: 呉音 (गो-ऑन) का पूर्वाग्रह","de":"Warum buddhistische Begriffe im Japanischen anders klingen: Das 呉音 (Go-on)-Phänomen","fr":"Pourquoi le vocabulaire bouddhiste japonais sonne différemment : le biais des 呉音 (go-on)","it":"Perché il lessico buddhista giapponese suona diverso: il predominio delle 呉音 (go-on)","es":"Por qué el vocabulario budista japonés suena diferente: el predominio del 呉音 (go-on)","pt":"Por que o vocabulário budista japonês soa diferente: o predomínio das 呉音 (go-on)","ru":"Почему буддийская японская лексика звучит иначе: феномен 呉音 (го-он)","uk":"Чому буддійська японська лексика звучить інакше: феномен 呉音 (ґо-он)","ar":"لماذا ينفرد نطق المصطلحات البوذية في اليابانية: ميلها إلى 呉音 (غو-أون)","he":"למה מילות הבודהיזם ביפנית נשמעות אחרת: הנטייה ל-呉音 (גו-און)","sw":"Kwa nini maneno ya Kibuddha ya Kijapani yanasikika tofauti: upendeleo wa 呉音 (go-on)"},"summary":{"en":"Japanese Buddhist terminology preserves an older layer of Chinese pronunciation called 呉音 (go-on), which reached Japan largely by way of Korea in the 5th–6th centuries, long before the Tang-era 漢音 (kan-on) layer arrived and eventually displaced it in much of the learned vocabulary.","ja":"日本の仏教用語は、5〜6世紀に主に朝鮮半島を経由して伝わった古層の漢語音「呉音」を保存している。唐代の漢音が伝わり、やがて学問的な語彙の多くで呉音に取って代わるより、はるか前に仏典の読み方が呉音で固定されたためだ。","ko":"일본 불교 용어는 오음(呉音)이라는 더 오래된 중국어 발음 층을 간직하고 있다. 오음은 5~6세기에 주로 한반도를 거쳐 들어왔는데, 당나라 시대의 한음(漢音)이 들어와 학술 어휘의 상당 부분에서 오음을 밀어내기 훨씬 전의 일이다.","zh":"日语佛教词汇保留了更早的汉语发音层——呉音（吴音），它在5至6世纪主要经由朝鲜半岛传入日本，远早于唐代汉音的到来——后者最终在许多学术词汇中取代了它，而它则在佛典诵读中固定了下来。","yue":"日文佛教術語保留咗一個更古老嘅漢語發音層——呉音，佢喺5至6世紀主要經朝鮮半島傳入日本，遠早過唐代漢音層嘅到來；漢音後來喺好大部分學術詞彙入面取代咗佢。","vi":"Thuật ngữ Phật giáo tiếng Nhật bảo lưu một lớp phát âm tiếng Hán cổ hơn gọi là 呉音 (go-on), phần lớn đến Nhật Bản qua ngả Triều Tiên vào thế kỷ 5–6, rất lâu trước khi lớp 漢音 (kan-on) thời Đường du nhập và dần thay thế nó trong phần lớn từ vựng bác học.","th":"ศัพท์ทางพุทธศาสนาในภาษาญี่ปุ่นยังเก็บเสียงอ่านภาษาจีนชั้นเก่าที่เรียกว่า 呉音 (โกะอง) ไว้ ชั้นนี้เข้ามาถึงญี่ปุ่นส่วนใหญ่ผ่านเกาหลีในศตวรรษที่ 5–6 นานก่อนชั้น 漢音 (คังอง) สมัยราชวงศ์ถังจะเข้ามา และเข้าแทนที่ในศัพท์วิชาการจำนวนมากในที่สุด","id":"Istilah-istilah Buddhis Jepang melestarikan lapisan lafal Tionghoa yang lebih tua, yang disebut 呉音 (go-on), yang sebagian besar tiba di Jepang lewat Korea pada abad ke-5–6, jauh sebelum lapisan 漢音 (kan-on) era Tang datang dan lambat laun menggantikannya di banyak kosakata kaum terpelajar.","hi":"जापानी बौद्ध शब्दावली में चीनी उच्चारण की एक पुरानी परत, 呉音 (गो-ऑन), बची है, जो 5वीं–6वीं सदी में ज़्यादातर कोरिया के रास्ते जापान पहुँची — तांग-युग की 漢音 (कान-ऑन) परत के आने से बहुत पहले, जिसने आगे चलकर विद्वत् शब्दावली के बड़े हिस्से में उसकी जगह ले ली।","de":"Buddhistische Fachbegriffe im Japanischen bewahren eine ältere Schicht chinesischer Aussprache, die 呉音 (go-on), die im 5.–6. Jahrhundert großenteils über Korea nach Japan gelangte, lange bevor 漢音 (kan-on) aus der Tang-Zeit eintraf und sie schließlich aus weiten Teilen des gelehrten Wortschatzes verdrängte.","fr":"La terminologie bouddhiste japonaise préserve une couche plus ancienne de prononciation chinoise, les 呉音 (go-on), arrivée au Japon en grande partie par la Corée aux Ve–VIe siècles, bien avant que les 漢音 (kan-on) de l'époque Tang ne la supplantent peu à peu dans une bonne partie du vocabulaire savant.","it":"La terminologia buddhista giapponese conserva uno strato più antico di pronuncia cinese detto 呉音 (go-on), giunto in Giappone in gran parte attraverso la Corea nel V–VI secolo, molto prima che le 漢音 (kan-on) dell'epoca Tang arrivassero e finissero per scalzarlo in buona parte del lessico dotto.","es":"La terminología budista japonesa preserva una capa más antigua de pronunciación china llamada 呉音 (go-on), que llegó a Japón en gran parte a través de Corea en los siglos V–VI, mucho antes de que la capa 漢音 (kan-on) de la época Tang la desplazara con el tiempo en buena parte del vocabulario culto.","pt":"A terminologia budista japonesa preserva uma camada mais antiga de pronúncia chinesa chamada 呉音 (go-on), que chegou ao Japão em grande parte por via da Coreia nos séculos V–VI, muito antes de a camada 漢音 (kan-on) da era Tang chegar e acabar por deslocá-la em boa parte do vocabulário erudito.","ru":"Буддийская терминология японского языка сохраняет более древний пласт китайского произношения — 呉音 (го-он), попавший в Японию в V–VI веках в основном через Корею, задолго до того, как пришёл пласт 漢音 (кан-он) эпохи Тан и со временем вытеснил его из значительной части учёной лексики.","uk":"Японська буддійська термінологія зберігає давніший шар китайської вимови, 呉音 (ґо-он). Він потрапив до Японії здебільшого через Корею у V–VI ст., задовго до танського шару 漢音 (кан-он), який з часом витіснив його зі значної частини книжної лексики.","ar":"تحتفظ المصطلحات البوذية اليابانية بطبقة أقدم من النطق الصيني تُعرف بـ呉音 (غو-أون)، وصلت إلى اليابان في الغالب عن طريق كوريا في القرنين الخامس والسادس، قبل زمن طويل من مجيء طبقة 漢音 (كان-أون) في عهد تانغ، التي أزاحتها مع الوقت من جزء كبير من المفردات العلمية.","he":"הטרמינולוגיה הבודהיסטית ביפנית שומרת על שכבת הגייה סינית עתיקה, 呉音 (גו-און), שהגיעה ליפן ברובה דרך קוריאה במאות ה-5–6. שכבת ה-漢音 (קאן-און) מתקופת טאנג הגיעה הרבה אחריה, ובסופו של דבר דחקה אותה מחלק גדול מאוצר המילים המלומד.","sw":"Istilahi za Kibuddha za Kijapani zinahifadhi safu ya zamani zaidi ya matamshi ya Kichina inayoitwa 呉音 (go-on), iliyofika Japan kwa kiasi kikubwa kupitia Korea katika karne za 5–6, muda mrefu kabla ya safu ya 漢音 (kan-on) ya enzi ya Tang kuwasili na hatimaye kuichukua nafasi yake katika sehemu kubwa ya msamiati wa kisomi."},"body":{"en":"<h3>Two Readings for One Character</h3><p>Many kanji carry two different on-yomi (Sino-Japanese readings): a <em>go-on</em> (呉音) and a <em>kan-on</em> (漢音). For many others the two layers happen to sound the same. The character 経 is <strong>kyō</strong> in Go-on and <strong>kei</strong> in Kan-on. In practice, <em>お経</em> (sutra) uses kyō; <em>経済</em> (economy) uses kei. The split is systematic, not random.</p><h3>The Earliest Wave: By Way of Korea</h3><p>Japanese scribes first encountered written Chinese through Baekje and Goguryeo scholars in the 5th–6th centuries. The readings they carried are what we now call Go-on — “Wu sounds”, named loosely after the Wu region south of the Yangtze, though that label was only attached centuries later, most likely by champions of Kan-on. Go-on is usually thought to reflect the Chinese of the Southern dynasties around Jiankang (modern Nanjing), but no contemporary source confirms it, and how much the Korean route reshaped the readings is unclear. Buddhist sutras, chants and temple ritual were set in this pronunciation before Japan began sending regular embassies to China in 607.</p><h3>The Tang Prestige Layer</h3><p>In the 7th–8th centuries, Japanese embassies (<em>kentōshi</em>) brought back the prestigious Chang'an pronunciation. This <em>kan-on</em> (\"Han sounds\") became the reading of the scholars: 文化 <em>bunka</em> (culture) is Kan-on through and through. The court pushed hard. In 792 Emperor Kanmu issued an edict promoting Kan-on, students at the state academy had to learn it, and monks who had not learned it were not allowed to travel to China. Even so, temple recitation largely kept its inherited Go-on, and the two layers ended up mixed in everyday words. 政治 <em>seiji</em> (politics) pairs a Kan-on <em>sei</em> with a Go-on <em>ji</em>, and 経済 <em>keizai</em> (economy) a Kan-on <em>kei</em> with a Go-on <em>zai</em>.</p><h3>The Divide in Practice</h3><p>The pattern holds across dozens of characters. 業 is <strong>gō</strong> (karma) in Go-on, <strong>gyō</strong> in Kan-on; 極楽 <em>gokuraku</em> (paradise) uses the Go-on <em>goku</em>, where Kan-on would give <em>kyoku</em>; 道 is the Go-on <em>dō</em> in 道路 <em>dōro</em> (road) but the Kan-on <em>tō</em> in 神道 <em>Shintō</em> — Go-on stuck in plenty of everyday words too. Even temple names follow the bias: 法隆寺 <em>Hōryū-ji</em>, 東大寺 <em>Tōdai-ji</em> — the 寺 is always the Go-on <em>ji</em>, never the Kan-on <em>shi</em>.</p><div class=\"trivia-actions\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"setchar\" data-char=\"心\">Show 心 on map</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"focus\" data-code=\"ja\">Focus Japanese on map</button></div>"},"sources":["Frellesvig, Bjarke (2010). A History of the Japanese Language. Cambridge University Press.","Miyake, Marc Hideo (2003). Old Japanese: A Phonetic Reconstruction. RoutledgeCurzon.",{"text":"Wikipedia — On'yomi (Go-on and Kan-on)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/On%27yomi"},"Unger, J. Marshall (1996). Literacy and Script Reform in Occupation Japan. Oxford University Press."]},{"id":"kokuji-japan-made-chars","icon":"🗾","tags":["kokuji","kanji","japan","sinosphere","character-formation"],"title":{"en":"Kokuji: Han Characters Invented in Japan","ja":"国字：日本で作られた漢字","ko":"국자: 일본에서 만든 한자","zh":"国字：日本自创的汉字","yue":"國字：日本自創嘅漢字","vi":"Kokuji: chữ Hán do Nhật Bản sáng tạo","th":"โคคุจิ: อักษรฮันที่ญี่ปุ่นประดิษฐ์ขึ้นเอง","id":"Kokuji: Karakter Han Ciptaan Jepang","hi":"कोकुजि: जापान में बनाए गए हान अक्षर","de":"Kokuji: In Japan erfundene Han-Schriftzeichen","fr":"Kokuji : caractères Han inventés au Japon","it":"Kokuji: caratteri Han inventati in Giappone","es":"Kokuji: caracteres Han inventados en Japón","pt":"Kokuji: caracteres Han inventados no Japão","ru":"Кокудзи: иероглифы, изобретённые в Японии","uk":"Кокудзі: ієрогліфи, винайдені в Японії","ar":"كوكوجي: الحروف الصينية المخترعة في اليابان","he":"קוקוג'י: סימנים סיניים שהומצאו ביפן","sw":"Kokuji: Herufi za Han zilizovumbuliwa Japani"},"summary":{"en":"Japan invented hundreds of Han-style characters of its own, called kokuji (国字), for native words, fish and plants, and at least one, 腺 'gland', went on to become standard Chinese.","ja":"日本は「国字」と呼ばれる漢字風の文字を何百字も独自に作り出した。和語や魚、植物のための字で、少なくとも一つ、「腺」は中国語の標準的な字にまでなった。","ko":"일본은 고유어와 물고기, 식물 이름을 적기 위해 '국자(国字)'라 불리는 한자식 문자를 수백 자나 만들었고, 그중 적어도 하나인 腺(샘)은 중국어의 표준 글자가 되었다.","zh":"日本自创了数百个汉字风格的“国字”，用来书写日本固有词、鱼类和植物的名称，其中至少有一个——“腺”——后来成了标准汉语用字。","yue":"日本自創咗幾百個漢字風格嘅字，叫做「國字」，用嚟寫本土詞、魚同植物；當中起碼有一個——「腺」——後來成為咗標準漢語用字。","vi":"Nhật Bản đã tự sáng tạo hàng trăm chữ theo kiểu chữ Hán, gọi là kokuji (国字), để ghi từ thuần Nhật, tên cá và cây cỏ — và ít nhất một chữ, 腺 \"tuyến\", đã trở thành chữ chuẩn của tiếng Trung.","th":"ญี่ปุ่นคิดอักษรแบบจีนของตัวเองขึ้นนับร้อยตัว เรียกว่าโคคุจิ (国字) ไว้เขียนคำพื้นเมือง ชื่อปลา และชื่อพืช และอย่างน้อยหนึ่งตัว คือ 腺 “ต่อม” ได้กลายเป็นอักษรมาตรฐานของภาษาจีนไปแล้ว","id":"Jepang menciptakan sendiri ratusan aksara bergaya Han, yang disebut kokuji (国字), untuk kata-kata asli, nama ikan, dan tumbuhan, dan setidaknya satu di antaranya, 腺 'kelenjar', akhirnya masuk ke bahasa Tionghoa baku.","hi":"जापान ने देशज शब्दों, मछलियों और पौधों के लिए सैकड़ों अपने हान-शैली के वर्ण गढ़े, जिन्हें 'कोकुजि' (国字) कहते हैं — और उनमें से कम-से-कम एक, 腺 'ग्रंथि', आगे चलकर मानक चीनी का हिस्सा बन गया।","de":"Japan erfand Hunderte eigener Han-artiger Schriftzeichen, die Kokuji (国字), für einheimische Wörter, Fische und Pflanzen, und mindestens eines, 腺 „Drüse“, wurde zum Standardchinesisch.","fr":"Le Japon a inventé des centaines de caractères de style Han bien à lui, les kokuji (国字), pour des mots autochtones, des poissons et des plantes, et l'un d'eux au moins, 腺 « glande », est devenu du chinois standard.","it":"Il Giappone inventò centinaia di caratteri in stile Han tutti suoi, i kokuji (国字), per parole native, pesci e piante, e almeno uno, 腺 «ghiandola», è entrato nel cinese standard.","es":"Japón inventó cientos de caracteres de estilo Han propios, llamados kokuji (国字), para palabras nativas, peces y plantas, y al menos uno, 腺 «glándula», acabó siendo chino estándar.","pt":"O Japão inventou centenas de caracteres próprios ao estilo Han, chamados kokuji (国字), para palavras nativas, peixes e plantas — e pelo menos um, 腺 'glândula', acabou virando chinês padrão.","ru":"Япония изобрела сотни собственных иероглифов ханьского стиля — кокудзи (国字) — для исконных слов, рыб и растений, и по крайней мере один из них, 腺 «железа», стал нормой в китайском.","uk":"Японія винайшла сотні власних ієрогліфів у стилі хань — кокудзі (国字) — для питомих слів, риб і рослин, і щонайменше один із них, 腺 «залоза», став нормативним китайським знаком.","ar":"اخترعت اليابان مئات الحروف على غرار حروف الهان، تُعرف بـ«كوكوجي» (国字)، لتكتب بها كلماتها الأصيلة وأسماء أسماكها ونباتاتها، وقد صار واحد منها على الأقل، 腺 «غدّة»، حرفاً صينياً معيارياً.","he":"יפן המציאה מאות סימנים משלה בסגנון הסיני, הקוקוג'י (国字), למילים יפניות מקומיות, לדגים ולצמחים, ולפחות אחד מהם, 腺, ״בלוטה״, נעשה לסינית תקנית.","sw":"Japani iliunda mamia ya herufi zake za mtindo wa Han, zinazoitwa kokuji (国字), kwa ajili ya maneno ya asili, samaki na mimea, na angalau moja, 腺 “tezi”, iliishia kuwa herufi sanifu ya Kichina."},"body":{"en":"<h3>Japan's Own Kanji</h3><p>When Japan adopted Chinese writing around the 5th–6th century, Chinese characters covered Chinese things, not Japanese ones. Japanese scribes eventually filled the gaps by forging new characters (<strong>国字, kokuji</strong>) using the same visual logic as Chinese: combining semantic components (<em>会意, kaii</em>) or pairing a semantic radical with a phonetic hint (<em>形声, keisei</em>).</p><h3>Famous Examples</h3><ul><li><strong>峠 (tōge)</strong>: mountain pass: 山 (mountain) + 上 (up) + 下 (down), the point where the road stops climbing and starts descending. Pure 会意.</li><li><strong>凧 (tako)</strong>: kite: usually read as 几 'wind' + 巾 'cloth', though it may have begun as a fusion of 鳳巾, an older written form for 'kite'. Kites themselves came from China, where they were written 紙鳶 'paper kite'; what Japan added was a one-character spelling.</li><li><strong>働 (hataraku)</strong>: to work: 人 (person) + 動 (move). The most commonly used kokuji of all, and one of the few with an on-yomi (<em>dō</em>, taken from 動). It is among the roughly nine kokuji on the Jōyō list.</li><li><strong>雫 (shizuku)</strong>: water drop: 雨 (rain) + 下 (down).</li><li><strong>笹 (sasa)</strong>: dwarf bamboo: 竹 (bamboo) + 世 (read <em>yo</em>, echoing <em>yo</em> “bamboo node”; others take the lower element as an abbreviated 葉 “leaf”). A plant central to Japanese festivals (Tanabata) but undifferentiated in Chinese botany.</li></ul><h3>Why Japan Needed Them</h3><p>Most kokuji give a single character to a native Japanese word that had no neat Chinese match. Fish make up an especially large group: 鰯 <em>iwashi</em> (sardine, already written this way in the 8th century), 鱚 <em>kisu</em> (sillago) and 鱈 <em>tara</em> (cod). Others mark Shinto and everyday life: 榊 <em>sakaki</em>, the sacred tree, is simply 木 'tree' + 神 'god'; 辻 <em>tsuji</em>, a crossroads, is 辶 'road' + 十 'cross'; 畑 <em>hatake</em> is a dry field for crops.</p><h3>The Surprising Reverse Flow</h3><p>A few kokuji travelled <em>west</em>. The clearest case is <strong>腺 (sen, gland)</strong>: 'flesh' 月 + 泉 'spring', a body part that wells up with liquid. It was coined around 1800 by the Edo-period Dutch-studies scholar Udagawa Genshin to translate Dutch <em>klier</em>, and it passed into both Chinese, where 腺 <em>xiàn</em> is now the standard word for gland, and Korean. <strong>鱈</strong> (xuě) 'cod' is likewise now the ordinary Chinese character for the fish (<em>xuě</em>, simplified 鳕). Characters did not only flow out of China.</p><div class=\"trivia-actions\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"focus\" data-code=\"ja_kun\">Show the kun-yomi layer on the map</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"panto\" data-lat=\"36.2\" data-lng=\"138.0\" data-zoom=\"5\">Pan to Japan</button></div>"},"sources":["Shibatani, Masayoshi. The Languages of Japan. Cambridge University Press, 1990.","Seeley, Christopher. A History of Writing in Japan. University of Hawaiʻi Press, 2000.",{"text":"国字 — 国語研究所データベース (NINJAL)","url":"https://www.ninjal.ac.jp/"},{"text":"Kokuji (Wikipedia)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kokuji"}]},{"id":"korea-kuk-ja","icon":"🇰🇷","tags":["kokuji","kanji","korea","sinosphere","character-formation"],"title":{"en":"Korea's Own Han Characters: Rare but Real","ja":"韓国固有漢字：少ないが確かに存在する","ko":"한국 고유 한자: 적지만 엄연히 존재한다","zh":"韩国固有汉字：少而真实","yue":"韓國固有漢字：少但真實存在","vi":"Chữ Hán riêng của Triều Tiên: ít nhưng có thật","th":"อักษรฮันเฉพาะของเกาหลี: น้อยแต่มีอยู่จริง","id":"Aksara Han buatan Korea: sedikit tetapi nyata","hi":"कोरिया के अपने हान अक्षर: गिने-चुने, पर असली","de":"Koreas eigene Han-Schriftzeichen: Selten, aber real","fr":"Les caractères Han propres à la Corée : rares mais réels","it":"I caratteri Han propri della Corea: pochi ma reali","es":"Los caracteres Han propios de Corea: escasos pero reales","pt":"Os caracteres Han próprios da Coreia: raros mas reais","ru":"Собственные ханьские знаки Кореи: редкие, но настоящие","uk":"Власні ханьські знаки Кореї: рідкісні, але справжні","ar":"حروف هان الخاصة بكوريا: نادرة لكنها حقيقية","he":"סימני האן קוריאניים: נדירים, אבל קיימים","sw":"Herufi za Han za Korea: chache lakini halisi"},"summary":{"en":"Korea created its own Han-style characters — mostly for paddy fields, personal names, and place names — but far fewer than Japan, reflecting different writing strategies in the two countries.","ja":"韓国も独自の漢字を作ったが、その多くは水田・人名・地名のためのもので、数は日本の国字よりずっと少ない。そこには両国の書き言葉の戦略の違いが映し出されている。","ko":"한국도 주로 논과 인명, 지명을 적기 위해 고유 한자를 만들었지만 일본보다 훨씬 적다. 두 나라의 표기 전략이 달랐음을 보여 주는 대목이다.","zh":"韩国也创造了少数固有汉字，主要用于稻田、人名和地名，但数量远少于日本，反映了两国不同的书写策略。","yue":"韓國都創造咗少量固有漢字，主要用於稻田、人名同地名，但數量遠少於日本，反映咗兩國唔同嘅書寫策略。","vi":"Triều Tiên cũng tạo ra một số chữ Hán riêng — chủ yếu cho ruộng lúa, tên người và địa danh — nhưng ít hơn nhiều so với Nhật Bản, phản ánh chiến lược viết khác nhau giữa hai nước.","th":"เกาหลีก็สร้างอักษรฮันของตัวเองขึ้นบ้าง ส่วนใหญ่ใช้กับนาข้าว ชื่อบุคคล และชื่อสถานที่ แต่มีน้อยกว่าญี่ปุ่นมาก ซึ่งสะท้อนวิธีคิดเรื่องการเขียนที่ต่างกันของสองประเทศ","id":"Korea juga menciptakan aksara Han sendiri, terutama untuk sawah, nama pribadi, dan nama tempat, tetapi jauh lebih sedikit daripada Jepang, karena strategi menulisnya berbeda.","hi":"कोरिया ने भी अपने कुछ हान-शैली के अक्षर बनाए — मुख्यतः धान के खेत, व्यक्तिगत नाम और स्थान-नामों के लिए — पर जापान से बहुत कम। इसके पीछे दोनों देशों के लिखने के अलग-अलग तौर-तरीक़े हैं।","de":"Korea schuf eigene Han-Schriftzeichen, vor allem für Reisfelder, Personen- und Ortsnamen, aber weit weniger als Japan. Darin spiegeln sich unterschiedliche Schriftstrategien der beiden Länder.","fr":"La Corée créa aussi ses propres caractères Han, surtout pour les rizières et les noms de personnes et de lieux, mais bien moins que le Japon : les stratégies d'écriture différaient.","it":"Anche la Corea creò caratteri Han propri, soprattutto per risaie, nomi di persona e luoghi, ma molti meno del Giappone, perché seguì un’altra strategia di scrittura.","es":"Corea también creó caracteres Han propios, sobre todo para arrozales, nombres de persona y topónimos, aunque muchos menos que Japón, reflejo de estrategias de escritura distintas.","pt":"A Coreia também criou seus próprios caracteres Han — principalmente para arrozais, nomes de pessoas e topônimos — mas muito menos do que o Japão, refletindo estratégias de escrita diferentes.","ru":"Корея также создала собственные ханьские знаки — главным образом для рисовых полей, личных имён и топонимов — но значительно меньше, чем Япония, что отражает разные письменные стратегии.","uk":"Корея теж створила власні ханьські знаки, переважно для рисових полів, особових імен і топонімів, але значно менше, ніж Японія, бо підходила до письма інакше.","ar":"ابتكرت كوريا أيضاً حروف هان خاصة بها، معظمها لحقول الأرز والأسماء الشخصية وأسماء الأماكن، لكنها أقل بكثير مما ابتكرته اليابان، لأن البلدين سلكا في الكتابة طريقين مختلفين.","he":"גם קוריאה יצרה סימני האן משלה, בעיקר לשדות אורז, לשמות פרטיים ולשמות מקומות, אבל הרבה פחות מיפן, כי שתי הארצות פתרו את בעיית הכתיבה בדרכים שונות.","sw":"Korea pia iliunda herufi zake za Han (hasa kwa mashamba ya mpunga, majina ya watu na majina ya maeneo), lakini chache zaidi kuliko Japani, ikiakisi mikakati tofauti ya uandishi."},"body":{"en":"<h3>Korea's Smaller Catalogue</h3><p>Japan's home-made characters, the kokuji, run into the hundreds. Korea's equivalent, usually called <strong>gukja (國字)</strong>, \"national characters\", or <em>goyu hanja</em> (固有漢字), \"native Han characters\", is a smaller and more obscure set: close to 200 have been identified so far, though more may turn up.</p><h3>Key Korean-Made Characters</h3><ul><li><strong>畓 (dap, paddy field)</strong>: 水 (water) + 田 (field). The single most important gukja, and one of the oldest: it already appears on Silla-period records, including an 8th-century Silla document preserved in Japan's Shōsōin. Rice farming was central to the peninsula, so a one-character word for a paddy was practical; Chinese writes the two-character 水田.</li><li><strong>乭 (Dol)</strong>: a character used almost exclusively in personal names. The best-known modern bearer is the Go champion Lee Sedol (李世乭), who played AlphaGo in 2016. Its structure is transparent, and characteristically Korean: 石 supplies both the meaning and the native reading <em>dol</em> (stone), while 乙 is appended to write the final <em>-l</em> — the <em>idu</em> device of spelling out a native word's last consonant with an extra character.</li><li><strong>媤 (si)</strong>: woman (女) + 思, meaning \"the husband's family\". It survives in everyday words such as 시댁 <em>sidaek</em> (媤宅, \"one's husband's family home\") and 시어머니 <em>si-eomeoni</em> (\"mother-in-law\").</li><li><strong>Various place-name characters</strong>: many Korean county and village names in historical records use characters not found in any Chinese dictionary. These were practical administrative inventions.</li></ul><h3>Why Fewer Than Japan?</h3><p>Two factors stand out. First, Joseon scholars prized Classical Chinese (<em>hanmun</em>) as the prestige written language and looked down on characters China did not use as vulgar variants (俗字). So most gukja were coined not by the scholar elite but by the clerks who drafted <em>idu</em> documents, Korean written with Chinese characters, where such ideological restraints were weaker. Second, many gukja exist only to spell native Korean syllables, like 乭 for <em>dol</em>; after Sejong's <strong>Hangul (한글)</strong> of 1443, that job could be done far more easily with the alphabet. Japanese, by contrast, went on coining kokuji from the 8th century to the 19th, many of them names for fish and plants.</p><h3>Legacy</h3><p>Most Korean gukja survive only in 古典 (classical texts), administrative records, and personal names. 畓 is the striking exception, together with 垈 (<em>dae</em>, \"building plot\"): South Korea's official land categories, the 28 <em>jimok</em> (地目) of the cadastre, still include 답 (畓, paddy) and 대 (垈, building land), so Korean-made characters sit in land registers to this day. The rest are now mostly the business of specialist philologists.</p><div class=\"trivia-actions\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"panto\" data-lat=\"37.5\" data-lng=\"127.0\" data-zoom=\"5\">Pan to Korea</button></div>"},"sources":["Ledyard, Gari. The Korean Language Reform of 1446. University of California, Berkeley, 1966.","Kim, Chin-W. The Making of the Korean Language. Colloquium Paper no. 2, Center for Korean Studies, University of Hawaii, 1974.",{"text":"속자 (俗字) — 한국민족문화대백과사전 (Encyclopedia of Korean Culture)","url":"https://encykorea.aks.ac.kr/"},{"text":"한국제 한자 (Wikipedia 한국어판)","url":"https://ko.wikipedia.org/wiki/%ED%95%9C%EA%B5%AD%EC%A0%9C_%ED%95%9C%EC%9E%90"},{"text":"지목 (Wikipedia 한국어판)","url":"https://ko.wikipedia.org/wiki/%EC%A7%80%EB%AA%A9"},{"text":"Wiktionary: 畓 (Korean, glyph origin and Silla attestation)","url":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/%E7%95%93"}]},{"id":"naxi-dongba-living-pictograms","icon":"🖼️","tags":["naxi","pictograph","yunnan","minority","writing-systems"],"title":{"en":"Dongba Script: A Pictographic Tradition of the Naxi","ja":"東巴文字：ナシ族の絵文字の伝統","ko":"동바 문자: 나시족의 그림문자 전통","zh":"东巴文字：纳西族的图画文字传统","yue":"東巴文字：納西族嘅圖畫文字傳統","vi":"Chữ Đông Ba: truyền thống chữ tượng hình của người Naxi","th":"อักษรตงปา: ธรรมเนียมอักษรภาพของชาวนาซี","id":"Aksara Dongba: tradisi piktografis suku Naxi","hi":"डोंगबा लिपि: नाशी लोगों की एक चित्रलिपि परंपरा","de":"Dongba-Schrift: Eine Bilderschrifttradition der Naxi","fr":"Écriture Dongba : une tradition pictographique des Naxi","it":"Scrittura Dongba: una tradizione pittografica dei Naxi","es":"Escritura Dongba: una tradición pictográfica de los Naxi","pt":"Escrita Dongba: uma tradição pictográfica dos Naxi","ru":"Письмо Дунба: пиктографическая традиция народа Наси","uk":"Письмо Дунба: піктографічна традиція народу Насі","ar":"الكتابة الدونغبا: تقليد تصويري لدى شعب ناكسي","he":"כתב דונגבא: מסורת פיקטוגרפית של עם הנאשי","sw":"Maandishi ya Dongba: mila ya picha ya watu wa Naxi"},"summary":{"en":"The Naxi of Yunnan, China, still keep a pictographic script: drawings that prompt their priests' chanted ritual texts, with a few signs borrowed for sound like a rebus puzzle.","ja":"中国雲南省のナシ族は、今も絵文字を守り続けている。祭司が詠唱する儀礼テキストを呼び起こすための絵で、いくつかの字は判じ絵のように音だけを借りて使われる。","ko":"중국 윈난성의 나시족은 지금도 그림 문자를 간직하고 있다. 사제들이 읊는 의례 텍스트를 떠올리게 해 주는 그림들로, 몇몇 기호는 그림 수수께끼처럼 소리만 빌려 쓴다.","zh":"中国云南的纳西族至今仍保有一种象形文字：一幅幅图画提示祭司吟诵仪式经文，另有少数符号像猜字谜一样借来表音。","yue":"中國雲南嘅納西族仍然保存住一套圖畫文字：用嚟提示祭司吟誦儀式經文嘅圖畫，另外有少數符號好似估字謎噉借嚟表音。","vi":"Người Naxi ở Vân Nam, Trung Quốc, vẫn còn giữ một hệ chữ tượng hình: những hình vẽ gợi nhắc các thầy tế đọc kinh văn nghi lễ, kèm một số ký hiệu được mượn để ghi âm như trò chơi đố chữ.","th":"ชาวนาซีแห่งยูนนาน ประเทศจีน ยังรักษาอักษรภาพไว้ เป็นภาพวาดที่ช่วยเตือนความจำให้นักบวชขับร้องตำราพิธีกรรม และมีบางตัวยืมมาใช้แทนเสียงเหมือนปริศนาภาพ","id":"Suku Naxi di Yunnan, Tiongkok, masih memelihara aksara piktografis: gambar-gambar yang memandu para pendeta melantunkan teks ritual, dengan beberapa tanda dipinjam demi bunyinya seperti teka-teki rebus.","hi":"चीन के युन्नान के नाशी लोग आज भी एक चित्रलिपि सँभाले हुए हैं: ऐसे चित्र जो उनके पुजारियों को गाए जाने वाले अनुष्ठान-पाठ याद दिलाते हैं, और जिनमें कुछ चिह्न किसी पहेली (रीबस) की तरह केवल ध्वनि के लिए उधार लिए जाते हैं।","de":"Die Naxi in Yunnan, China, bewahren bis heute eine Bilderschrift: Zeichnungen, die ihren Priestern die gesungenen Ritualtexte ins Gedächtnis rufen, dazu ein paar Zeichen, die wie bei einem Bilderrätsel nur für ihren Klang stehen.","fr":"Les Naxi du Yunnan, en Chine, conservent une écriture pictographique : des dessins qui soufflent à leurs prêtres les textes rituels qu'ils psalmodient, avec quelques signes empruntés pour leur son, à la manière d'un rébus.","it":"I Naxi dello Yunnan, in Cina, conservano ancora una scrittura pittografica: disegni che suggeriscono ai loro sacerdoti i testi rituali da cantare, con alcuni segni presi in prestito per il suono, come in un rebus.","es":"Los Naxi de Yunnan, en China, conservan todavía una escritura pictográfica: dibujos que sirven de pie a los textos rituales que cantan sus sacerdotes, con unos pocos signos tomados por su sonido, como en un jeroglífico de pasatiempos.","pt":"Os Naxi de Yunnan, na China, ainda mantêm uma escrita pictográfica: desenhos que guiam os cantos rituais de seus sacerdotes, com alguns sinais tomados emprestados pelo som, como num rébus.","ru":"Наси в китайской провинции Юньнань до сих пор хранят пиктографическое письмо: рисунки подсказывают жрецам распеваемые обрядовые тексты, а некоторые знаки заимствуются ради звучания, как в ребусе.","uk":"Народ Насі в китайській провінції Юньнань досі зберігає піктографічне письмо: малюнки, що підказують жерцям співані обрядові тексти, і кілька знаків, запозичених заради звучання, як у ребусі.","ar":"لا يزال شعب ناكسي في يوننان بالصين يحتفظ بكتابة تصويرية: رسوم تذكّر كهنته بنصوصهم الطقسية المرتّلة، مع بضع علامات مستعارة لصوتها كما في الأحجيات المصوّرة.","he":"בני הנאשי ביונאן שבסין עדיין שומרים על כתב פיקטוגרפי: ציורים שמזכירים לכוהנים שלהם את הטקסטים הטקסיים המושרים, ועוד כמה סימנים שנשאלו לפי צלילם, כמו בחידת רבוס.","sw":"Watu wa Naxi wa Yunnan, China, bado wanahifadhi maandishi ya picha: michoro inayowakumbusha makuhani wao maandiko ya ibada wanayoyaimba, pamoja na alama chache zilizoazimwa kwa ajili ya sauti, kama fumbo la rebus."},"body":{"en":"<h3>A Script That Outlived Its Age</h3><p>Most pictographic scripts died millennia ago. Egyptian hieroglyphs fell silent around 400 CE; Maya glyphs did not long survive the Spanish conquest. Naxi Dongba script (<strong>東巴文字</strong>) breaks the pattern: between about a thousand and two thousand pictographic signs (counts vary with how variants are treated) are still memorized, recited and drawn by hand by <em>dongba</em> (東巴) priests in Yunnan Province, southwestern China.</p><p>The Naxi people number about 320,000, concentrated around Lijiang (麗江). Their priests — hereditary ritual specialists called 東巴, a name meaning 'wise man' or 'teacher' (from Tibetan ston pa 'teacher') — use the script not for everyday literacy but as a mnemonic scaffold for chanted ceremonial texts. The written signs do not record every word: a few pictographs on the page cue a longer passage of verse that the dongba knows by heart. Writing here is inseparable from performance.</p><h3>Picture First, Sound Second</h3><p>Dongba signs are overwhelmingly pictorial: a drawing of a fish means 'fish'; a human figure with arms raised means 'dance'; compound ideas are shown by combining pictures. But they are not <em>purely</em> pictorial. Any graph can be borrowed for its sound alone — the <strong>rebus principle</strong> — so a pair of eyes (<em>myə</em>) writes the homophonous word 'fate', a rice bowl serves for both 'food' and 'sleep' (<em>xa</em>), and a goral (<em>se</em>) stands in for a grammatical particle. The Naxi kept a second script too, the <em>geba</em> (哥巴) syllabary — rare, never standardized, surviving in relatively few manuscripts — which appears beside the pictographs to gloss their readings and to write mantras, contracts and letters. This mix of pictures and sound-borrowing is closer to the early stages of cuneiform (c. 3200–2900 BCE) than to any modern script, yet it is alive now.</p><h3>UNESCO and the Last Readers</h3><p>In 2003, UNESCO inscribed the ancient Naxi Dongba manuscripts on its <em>Memory of the World Register</em>. Thousands of manuscripts were destroyed during the Cultural Revolution, and about half of those that survive had already been taken abroad, to collections in the United States, Germany and Spain. UNESCO notes that only a few masters who can read the scriptures are left. Yunnan provincial authorities have funded training programs, Lijiang has a Dongba Culture Research Institute, and the pictographs now appear on buses and on shop signs in Lijiang's old town beside Chinese and English. Whether it survives as a living tradition, and not as a museum piece, is still an open question.</p><div class='trivia-actions'><button type='button' class='trivia-action' data-action='panto' data-lat='26.87' data-lng='100.23' data-zoom='6'>Map: Lijiang, Yunnan</button></div>"},"sources":["Rock, Joseph F. (1963). A Na-khi–English encyclopedic dictionary. Rome: ISMEO.","Mathieu, Christine (2003). A History and Anthropological Study of the Ancient Kingdoms of the Sino-Tibetan Borderland. Lewiston: Edwin Mellen Press.",{"text":"UNESCO Memory of the World: Naxi Dongba manuscripts","url":"https://www.unesco.org/en/memory-world/ancient-naxi-dongba-literature-manuscripts"},"McKhann, Charles F. (1995). 'The Naxi and the Nationalities Question,' in Stevan Harrell (ed.), Cultural Encounters on China's Ethnic Frontiers. Seattle: University of Washington Press, 39–62."]},{"id":"yi-syllabary","icon":"📜","tags":["yi","syllabary","yunnan","minority","script-reform"],"title":{"en":"Yi Script: From Thousands of Logograms to 819 Syllables","ja":"彝文：数千の表語文字から819の音節へ","ko":"이족 문자: 수천 개의 표어 문자에서 819개의 음절로","zh":"彝文：从数千个表意字到819个音节","yue":"彝文：由數以千計嘅表詞字到819個音節","vi":"Chữ Di: từ hàng nghìn chữ ghi ý đến 819 âm tiết","th":"อักษรอี: จากอักษรแทนคำนับพันสู่ 819 พยางค์","id":"Aksara Yi: dari ribuan logogram ke 819 suku kata","hi":"यी लिपि: हज़ारों लोगोग्राम से 819 शब्दांशों तक","de":"Yi-Schrift: Von Tausenden Logogrammen zu 819 Silben","fr":"Écriture Yi : des milliers de logogrammes aux 819 syllabes","it":"Scrittura Yi: da migliaia di logogrammi a 819 sillabe","es":"Escritura Yi: de miles de logogramas a 819 sílabas","pt":"Escrita Yi: de milhares de logogramas a 819 sílabas","ru":"Письмо И: от тысяч логограмм к 819 слогам","uk":"Письмо І: від тисяч логограм до 819 складів","ar":"الكتابة اليي: من آلاف الرموز الكلمية إلى 819 مقطعاً","he":"כתב היי: מאלפי לוגוגרמות ל-819 הברות","sw":"Maandishi ya Yi: kutoka maelfu ya logogramu hadi silabi 819"},"summary":{"en":"Traditional Yi script is a logographic system with thousands of signs and many regional variants. In 1980 China ratified a Liangshan standard of 819 syllabic signs, used today on road signs and in textbooks in Sichuan's Liangshan.","ja":"伝統的な彝文は、数千の字と多くの地域的な異体を持つ表語文字だ。1980年、中国は819の音節文字からなる涼山の標準を正式に承認し、今日の四川省涼山では道路標識や教科書に使われている。","ko":"전통 이족 문자는 수천 개의 기호와 수많은 지역 변종을 지닌 표어 문자다. 1980년 중국은 819개 음절 기호로 된 량산 표준을 비준했고, 이 문자는 오늘날 쓰촨성 량산의 도로 표지판과 교과서에 쓰인다.","zh":"传统彝文是一种表意文字，有数千个字符和众多地方变体。1980年，中国批准了一套以凉山话为基础、含819个音节符号的规范，如今用于四川凉山的路牌和教科书。","yue":"傳統彝文係一種表詞文字，有成千上萬個符號，地區變體眾多。1980年，中國批准咗一套有819個音節符號嘅涼山標準，今日喺四川涼山嘅路牌同課本上面用緊。","vi":"Chữ Di truyền thống là một hệ chữ ghi ý với hàng nghìn ký hiệu và nhiều biến thể vùng miền. Năm 1980, Trung Quốc phê chuẩn bộ chuẩn Lương Sơn gồm 819 ký hiệu âm tiết, ngày nay được dùng trên biển báo đường bộ và trong sách giáo khoa ở Lương Sơn, Tứ Xuyên.","th":"อักษรอีดั้งเดิมเป็นอักษรแทนคำที่มีสัญลักษณ์นับพันตัว และมีรูปแปรตามภูมิภาคมากมาย ในปี 1980 จีนรับรองมาตรฐานเหลียงซานที่มีสัญลักษณ์พยางค์ 819 ตัว ซึ่งทุกวันนี้ใช้บนป้ายถนนและในตำราเรียนที่เหลียงซาน มณฑลเสฉวน","id":"Aksara Yi tradisional adalah sistem logografis dengan ribuan tanda dan banyak varian daerah. Pada 1980 Tiongkok mengesahkan standar Liangshan berisi 819 tanda silabis, yang kini dipakai pada rambu jalan dan buku pelajaran di Liangshan, Sichuan.","hi":"पारंपरिक यी लिपि एक लोगोग्राफ़िक व्यवस्था है, जिसमें हज़ारों चिह्न और बहुत-से क्षेत्रीय रूप हैं। 1980 में चीन ने 819 शब्दांश-चिह्नों वाले एक लियांगशान मानक को मंज़ूरी दी, जो आज सिचुआन के लियांगशान में सड़क संकेतों और पाठ्यपुस्तकों में दिखता है।","de":"Die traditionelle Yi-Schrift ist ein logografisches System mit Tausenden von Zeichen und vielen regionalen Varianten. 1980 bestätigte China einen Liangshan-Standard aus 819 Silbenzeichen, der heute auf Straßenschildern und in Schulbüchern im sichuanesischen Liangshan zu sehen ist.","fr":"L'écriture Yi traditionnelle est un système logographique riche de milliers de signes et de nombreuses variantes régionales. En 1980, la Chine a ratifié une norme de Liangshan de 819 signes syllabiques, employée aujourd'hui sur les panneaux routiers et dans les manuels scolaires de Liangshan, au Sichuan.","it":"La scrittura Yi tradizionale è un sistema logografico con migliaia di segni e molte varianti regionali. Nel 1980 la Cina ha ratificato uno standard di Liangshan di 819 segni sillabici, usato oggi sui cartelli stradali e nei libri di testo di Liangshan, nel Sichuan.","es":"La escritura Yi tradicional es un sistema logográfico con miles de signos y muchas variantes regionales. En 1980 China ratificó un estándar de Liangshan de 819 signos silábicos, que hoy se usa en carteles de carretera y libros de texto en Liangshan, en Sichuan.","pt":"A escrita Yi tradicional é um sistema logográfico com milhares de sinais e muitas variantes regionais. Em 1980, a China ratificou um padrão de Liangshan com 819 sinais silábicos, usado hoje em placas de estrada e livros didáticos na região de Liangshan, em Sichuan.","ru":"Традиционное письмо И — логографическая система с тысячами знаков и множеством региональных вариантов. В 1980 году Китай утвердил ляншаньский стандарт из 819 слоговых знаков, которые сегодня используются на дорожных знаках и в учебниках в сычуаньском Ляншане.","uk":"Традиційне письмо І — логографічна система з тисячами знаків і безліччю регіональних варіантів. 1980 року Китай затвердив ляншанський стандарт із 819 складових знаків, які сьогодні можна побачити на дорожніх знаках і в підручниках сичуанського Ляншаню.","ar":"الخط اليي التقليدي نظام كلمي يضم آلاف الرموز وصيغاً إقليمية كثيرة. وفي عام 1980 أقرّت الصين معياراً لليانغشان من 819 رمزاً مقطعياً، يُستعمل اليوم على لافتات الطرق وفي الكتب المدرسية في ليانغشان بسيتشوان.","he":"כתב היי המסורתי הוא מערכת לוגוגרפית של אלפי סימנים וגרסאות אזוריות רבות. ב-1980 אישרה סין תקן ליאנגשאני של 819 סימני הברה, המשמש כיום על שלטי דרכים ובספרי לימוד בליאנגשאן שבסצ'ואן.","sw":"Maandishi ya jadi ya Yi ni mfumo wa logogramu wenye maelfu ya alama na vibadala vingi vya kimaeneo. Mwaka 1980 China iliidhinisha kiwango cha Liangshan cha alama 819 za silabi, kinachotumika leo kwenye vibao vya barabarani na katika vitabu vya shule huko Liangshan, Sichuan."},"body":{"en":"<h3>A Logographic Script of Eight Thousand Signs</h3><p>The Yi people (彝族, Yízú) numbered 9.8 million at the 2020 census, spread across Sichuan, Yunnan, Guizhou, and Guangxi. Their traditional script, <strong>彝文 (Yí wén)</strong>, is a logographic system like Chinese: each sign stands for a syllable that is also a word or morpheme, and counting every regional variant, catalogues list thousands of signs. Legend credits its invention to a Nuosu hero, Aki, in the Tang dynasty, but the earliest dated example is far younger: an inscription on a bronze bell from 1485. Scribes and priests also used signs for their sound alone, so the script sat somewhere between Chinese characters and a syllabary, and it was far less uniform than either. Each clan or region maintained its own variant set, so a ritual text from Liangshan (涼山) might be incomprehensible to a reader from Guizhou even if both were Yi speakers. That made the script rich but fragmented.</p><h3>The 1980 Standardization</h3><p>After the founding of the People's Republic, the government began studying minority scripts, and the process took decades. In 1974 Sichuan's Ethnic Affairs Commission drew up a syllabary from the traditional signs, and in 1980 the State Council ratified it as the <em>Liangshan Standard Yi Script</em> (<strong>涼山規範彝文</strong>): 756 basic signs, one per syllable of the Liangshan dialect of Nuosu, plus 63 for syllables found only in Chinese loanwords, 819 in all. Three of the four tones have signs of their own; the fourth is written by adding a small arc to the mid-tone sign. Like the 1956 Simplified Chinese reform, it came from the top down, but it cut far deeper, from about 8,000 signs to 819.</p><p>The 819-sign system appears today on bilingual road signs throughout Liangshan Yi Autonomous Prefecture, in primary school textbooks, and in local broadcasting. Unicode encodes the standard Yi syllabary in the Yi Syllables block (U+A000–U+A48F), with a separate Yi Radicals block at U+A490–U+A4CF, making digital text straightforward. The standard is Sichuan's, though: Yunnan went its own way and in 1987 ratified a Yunnan Standard Yi of 2,608 signs, most of them logograms kept so that speakers of different Yi languages could still read each other's writing.</p><h3>The Politics of Standardization</h3><p>Not everyone welcomed the reform. Scholars and community elders who read the traditional 8,000-sign corpus argue that the new standard erases dialectal nuance and renders thousands of historical manuscripts unreadable to the younger generation trained only on the 819 standard. The parallel with simplified Chinese versus traditional Chinese is frequently drawn, and just as contested. Today, reading the traditional script is mostly the work of the <em>bimo</em>, the Nuosu priests who still copy and chant the old manuscripts, and of a handful of academic researchers; tens of thousands of those manuscripts survive.</p><div class='trivia-actions'><button type='button' class='trivia-action' data-action='panto' data-lat='28.01' data-lng='102.26' data-zoom='6'>Map: Liangshan, Sichuan</button></div>"},"sources":["Bender, Mark (2008). 'Tribes of Snow: Animals and Plants in the Nuosu Book of Origins.' Asian Ethnology 67(1): 5–42.","Bradley, David (2001). 'Language Policy for the Yi.' In Perspectives on the Yi of Southwest China. Berkeley: University of California Press.",{"text":"Unicode Yi Syllables Block (U+A000–U+A48F)","url":"https://www.unicode.org/charts/PDF/UA000.pdf"},"Guo Dalie & Lu Huiying (1999). 彝文字典. Chengdu: Sichuan Minzu Chubanshe."]},{"id":"zhuang-sawndip-script","icon":"🪨","tags":["sinosphere","kanji","history","writing-systems","southeast-asia"],"title":{"en":"Sawndip: The Thousand-Year Han-Derived Script of the Zhuang People","ja":"古壯字（方塊壯字）：漢字を借りたチワン族千年の文字","ko":"고장자(古壯字): 좡족이 한자를 빌려 만든 천년 역사의 문자","zh":"古壮字（方块壮字）：壮族借汉字创制的千年文字","yue":"古壯字（方塊壯字）：壯族借漢字創造嘅千年文字","vi":"Chữ Sawndip: hệ chữ viết nghìn năm của người Tráng mượn từ chữ Hán","th":"ซอนดิบ: อักษรพันปีของชาวจ้วงที่ยืมมาจากตัวอักษรจีน","id":"Sawndip: aksara seribu tahun suku Zhuang yang diturunkan dari aksara Han","hi":"साउनदिप: हान अक्षरों से बनी ज़ुआंग लोगों की हज़ार साल पुरानी लिपि","de":"Sawndip: Die tausend Jahre alte Han-abgeleitete Schrift der Zhuang","fr":"Sawndip : l'écriture millénaire des Zhuang, tirée des caractères han","it":"Sawndip: la scrittura millenaria degli Zhuang, nata dai caratteri Han","es":"Sawndip: la escritura milenaria de los zhuang, hecha con caracteres han","pt":"Sawndip: a escrita milenar dos zhuang, feita com caracteres han","ru":"Сандип: тысячелетнее письмо чжуанского народа, производное от иероглифов хань","uk":"Сандип: тисячолітнє письмо народу чжуан, похідне від ієрогліфів хань","ar":"ساوندِب: كتابة الجوانغ المستمدة من الحروف الهانية عبر ألف عام","he":"סאונדיפ: כתב בן אלף שנה של הז'ואנג, בסימני הָאן","sw":"Sawndip: maandishi ya miaka elfu ya watu wa Zhuang yaliyotokana na herufi za Han"},"summary":{"en":"The Zhuang people of southern China invented Sawndip — 'immature characters', as against the 'characters of the Han' — by repurposing and inventing Han-style characters to write their Tai-Kadai language, centuries before any Latin romanization existed.","ja":"中国南部のチワン族は、タイ・カダイ系の言語を書くため、漢字を転用・新造した古壮字（Sawndip）——漢族の文字に対する「未熟な文字」の意——を作り出した。ラテン文字ローマ字化より何世紀も早い。","ko":"중국 남부의 좡족은 타이-카다이계 언어를 표기하기 위해 한자를 빌리고 새로 지어 고장자(Sawndip)를 만들어 냈다. 이름은 한족의 문자에 견준 '설익은 문자'라는 뜻이다. 라틴 문자 표기보다 수백 년 앞선 일이다.","zh":"中国南方的壮族人为了书写泰-卡代语系语言，创造了古壮字（Sawndip——相对于「汉人之字」的「未熟之字」之意），借用和新造汉字，比任何拉丁拼音方案早了数百年。","yue":"中國南方嘅壯族人，為咗書寫佢哋嘅泰-卡代語系語言，借用同新創漢字，造出古壯字（Sawndip——相對於「漢人之字」嘅「未熟之字」），比任何拉丁拼音方案早咗幾百年。","vi":"Người Tráng ở miền nam Trung Quốc đã sáng tạo ra chữ Sawndip — 'chữ non', đối lại với 'chữ của người Hán' — bằng cách tái sử dụng và sáng chế các ký tự theo kiểu chữ Hán để viết ngôn ngữ Tai-Kadai của họ, nhiều thế kỷ trước khi có bất kỳ hệ Latinh hóa nào.","th":"ชาวจ้วงในจีนตอนใต้สร้างอักษรซอนดิบ “อักษรดิบ” ซึ่งตรงข้ามกับ “อักษรของชาวฮั่น” โดยดัดแปลงและคิดอักษรแบบจีนขึ้นใหม่เพื่อเขียนภาษาไท-กะไดของตน","id":"Orang Zhuang di Tiongkok selatan menciptakan Sawndip, 'aksara mentah', lawan dari 'aksara orang Han', dengan memakai ulang dan menciptakan aksara bergaya Han untuk menulis bahasa Tai-Kadai mereka.","hi":"चीन के दक्षिणी भाग में ज़ुआंग लोगों ने साउनदिप — 'कच्चे अक्षर', यानी 'हान के अक्षरों' के विपरीत — गढ़ी, जिसमें हान-शैली के अक्षरों को नए काम में लगाकर और नए अक्षर बनाकर एक ताई-कादाई भाषा लिखी गई।","de":"Das Zhuang-Volk in Südchina erfand Sawndip („unreife Schriftzeichen“, im Gegensatz zu den „Schriftzeichen der Han“), indem es Han-artige Zeichen für seine Tai-Kadai-Sprache zweckentfremdete und neu erfand.","fr":"Le peuple Zhuang du sud de la Chine a inventé le Sawndip, les « caractères immatures » par opposition aux « caractères des Han », en réutilisant et en inventant des caractères de style han pour écrire sa langue taï-kadaï.","it":"Gli Zhuang della Cina meridionale crearono il Sawndip, i «caratteri acerbi» contrapposti ai «caratteri degli Han», riusando e inventando caratteri in stile Han per scrivere la loro lingua tai-kadai.","es":"El pueblo Zhuang del sur de China inventó el Sawndip ('caracteres inmaduros', frente a los 'caracteres de los han') reutilizando y creando caracteres de estilo han para escribir su lengua tai-kadai.","pt":"O povo Zhuang do sul da China inventou o Sawndip — 'caracteres imaturos', por oposição aos 'caracteres dos han' — reutilizando e inventando caracteres no estilo Han para escrever sua língua Tai-Kadai.","ru":"Народ чжуан на юге Китая изобрёл сандип — «незрелые знаки», в противоположность «знакам хань» — приспособив старые и придумав новые знаки в стиле хань для записи своего тай-кадайского языка.","uk":"Народ чжуан на півдні Китаю винайшов сандип — «незрілі знаки», на противагу «знакам хань» — переробивши та вигадавши знаки в стилі хань для запису своєї тай-кадайської мови.","ar":"ابتكر شعب جوانغ في جنوب الصين نظام ساوندِب، أي «الحروف الفجّة» في مقابل «حروف الهان»، بإعادة توظيف وابتكار حروف على النمط الهاني لكتابة لغتهم التاي-قاداي.","he":"עם הז'ואנג בדרום סין המציא את הסאונדיפ, ״סימנים בוסריים״ בניגוד ל״סימנים של ההָאן״: הוא השתמש מחדש בסימני הָאן והמציא סימנים חדשים בסגנונם כדי לכתוב את שפתו הטאי-קאדאית.","sw":"Watu wa Zhuang kusini mwa China walivumbua Sawndip ('herufi mbichi', kinyume na 'herufi za Wahan') kwa kutumia tena na kuunda herufi za mtindo wa Han ili kuandika lugha yao ya Tai-Kadai."},"body":{"en":"<h3>What Is Sawndip?</h3><p><strong>Sawndip</strong> (Zhuang <em>saw</em> 'character' + <em>ndip</em> 'raw, unripe' — literally 'immature characters', as opposed to <em>sawgun</em>, the 'characters of the Han'; in Chinese 古壯字 'old Zhuang characters' or <em>Fangkuai Zhuangzi</em> 方塊壯字 'square-shaped Zhuang characters') is the traditional writing system used by the Zhuang people — the largest ethnic minority in China, numbering 19.6 million at the 2020 census — to record their Tai-Kadai language. Its beginnings are uncertain. Two Tang-dynasty stone inscriptions from the Zhuang area (682 and 697 CE) are written in Chinese, but the later one already contains a few non-standard local characters, such as one for <em>naz</em> 'paddy field'. The script is usually reckoned to have been in use for over a thousand years, though the manuscripts that survive are far younger — overwhelmingly Qing-dynasty copies of works first composed in the Ming or earlier.</p><h3>Three Strategies for Borrowing Han Script</h3><p>Sawndip is not a simple copy of Chinese. Scribes used three distinct strategies:</p><ul><li><strong>Phonetic borrowing (假借, jiǎjiè)</strong>: a Han character is used purely for its sound, ignoring meaning. Just as Japanese <em>man'yōgana</em> (万葉仮名) spelled Japanese words with Han phonetics, Zhuang scribes wrote native words with whichever Han character sounded closest in the local dialect: the verb <em>miz</em> 'to have' is written 眉, Chinese for 'eyebrow', purely for its sound. This is the dominant principle; David Holm estimates that about 71% of Sawndip characters are phonetic.</li><li><strong>Semantic borrowing</strong>: a Han character whose meaning matches a Zhuang word is borrowed wholesale, even if the pronunciation differs completely.</li><li><strong>Local invention (形聲 xíngshēng compounds)</strong>: the most striking category. Scribes <em>invented</em> new characters by combining a Han semantic radical with a phonetic component, exactly as Classical Chinese coined new sinographs. <em>Bya</em> 'mountain' is 岜, 山 'mountain' plus 巴 <em>ba</em> for the sound; <em>vunz</em> 'person' is 伝, the 'person' radical 亻 plus 云 for the sound. Characters like these look Chinese but mean nothing to a reader of standard Chinese.</li></ul><div class=\"trivia-actions\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"focus\" data-code=\"za\">📍 View Zhuang (Vahcuengh) on map</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"panto\" data-lat=\"23.5\" data-lng=\"108.0\" data-zoom=\"6\">🗺 Pan to Guangxi</button></div><h3>The 萬葉仮名 Parallel</h3><p>The parallel with Japanese <em>man'yōgana</em> is striking but limited. Both systems use Han characters phonetically to represent a non-Sinitic language. Yet Sawndip went further: while man'yōgana eventually abstracted into kana syllabaries, Sawndip never shed its full-character form. Every Sawndip glyph retains the visual weight of a Han character, so a page of Sawndip looks, at a glance, like a page of Chinese, yet is entirely unreadable to someone who knows only Mandarin.</p><h3>Ritual and Vernacular Use</h3><p>Sawndip was never standardised by any imperial authority. It grew up instead in the hands of Zhuang village priests (<em>mo</em> 摩), who used it to record ritual incantations, epic songs like <em>Buluotuo</em> (布洛陀), and almanacs. Villages often wrote the same word with different characters, so one Zhuang word can have many spellings across manuscripts. The first and so far only dictionary of the script, the <em>Sawndip Sawdenj</em> (1989), needed over 10,000 characters to cover its 4,900 entries.</p><h3>1957 and the Latin Alphabet</h3><p>The People's Republic of China introduced an alphabetic script for a newly standardised Zhuang language in 1957, designed by linguists to unify the many mutually unintelligible Zhuang dialects under a single orthography. That first alphabet mixed Latin with Cyrillic- and IPA-derived letters, including five tone letters (ƨ ɜ ч ƽ ƅ) that look like the digits 2 to 6. A 1982 reform replaced them with plain Latin ones, so tone is now written with a final letter — <em>z, j, x, q, h</em>. This is the orthography of Standard Zhuang, <strong>Sawcuengh</strong> 壮文 — <em>Vahcuengh</em>, by contrast, is the Zhuang name of the language itself, not of the script — and it is the official written standard today. Yet Sawndip persists. Village ritual specialists still copy texts by hand, and people still use it for song lyrics and personal notes; in one survey of two dialect areas, just over a third of respondents said they could understand it. Unicode has added thousands of Sawndip characters since 2017 (CJK Extensions F, G, H and J), so the old script can now be typed.</p>"},"sources":["Holm, David. <em>Mapping the Old Zhuang Character Script: A Vernacular Writing System from Southern China</em>. Handbook of Oriental Studies. Leiden/Boston: Brill, 2013.","Chamberlain, James R. 'A New Look at the History and Classification of the Tai Languages.' <em>Studies in Tai Linguistics</em>, 1975.",{"text":"Sawndip — Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sawndip"},"Bauer, Robert S. 'Written Cantonese of Hong Kong.' <em>Cahiers de Linguistique Asie Orientale</em> 17(2), 1988.","Norman, Jerry. <em>Chinese</em>. Cambridge University Press, 1988.",{"text":"Vahcuengh (Zhuang alphabet) — Omniglot","url":"https://www.omniglot.com/writing/zhuang.htm"}]},{"id":"bai-language-script","icon":"🏔","tags":["sinosphere","kanji","history","writing-systems","classification"],"title":{"en":"Bai Script: The Han-Character Vehicle for an Unclassified Language","ja":"白文：帰属不明の言語を担う漢字文字","ko":"백문(白文): 계통 불명 언어를 담은 한자 문자","zh":"白文：承载归属未定语言的汉字书写系统","yue":"白文：承載歸屬未定語言嘅漢字書寫系統","vi":"Chữ Bạch: hệ chữ Hán ghi lại một ngôn ngữ chưa được phân loại","th":"อักษรไป๋: อักษรจีนสำหรับภาษาที่ยังจัดกลุ่มไม่ได้","id":"Aksara Bai: aksara Han untuk bahasa yang belum tergolongkan","hi":"बाई लिपि: हान अक्षरों में एक ऐसी भाषा जिसका परिवार तय नहीं","de":"Bai-Schrift: Das Han-Zeichensystem für eine unklassifizierte Sprache","fr":"L'écriture Bai : les caractères han au service d'une langue non classée","it":"Scrittura Bai: i caratteri Han al servizio di una lingua inclassificabile","es":"La escritura Bai: los caracteres Han para una lengua sin clasificar","pt":"A escrita bai: caracteres han para uma língua sem classificação","ru":"Письменность байского языка: иероглифы хань для неклассифицированного языка","uk":"Письменність байської мови: ієрогліфи хань для некласифікованої мови","ar":"الكتابة البايية: الحروف الهانية لخدمة لغة غير مصنّفة","he":"כתב הבאי: סימני הָאן לשפה שלא סווגה","sw":"Maandishi ya Bai: herufi za Han kwa lugha isiyoainishwa"},"summary":{"en":"The Bai people of Dali, Yunnan used Han characters to write their own language for centuries. Linguists still disagree over whether Bai is Sinitic, Tibeto-Burman, or an independent branch of Sino-Tibetan.","ja":"雲南省大理の白族は、数百年にわたり漢字を用いて自分たちの言語を書いてきた。言語学者たちは、白語がシナ語派なのかチベット・ビルマ語派なのか、それともシノ・チベット語族の独立した一分枝なのかをいまだ論争している。","ko":"윈난성 다리의 바이족은 수백 년 동안 한자를 이용해 자신들의 언어를 기록해 왔다. 언어학자들은 바이어가 중국어파인지, 티베트버마어파인지, 중국티베트어족의 독립 분기인지를 여전히 논쟁하고 있다.","zh":"云南大理的白族几百年来一直用汉字书写自己的语言，语言学家至今仍在争论白语究竟属于汉语族、藏缅语族，还是汉藏语系的独立分支。","yue":"雲南大理嘅白族幾百年嚟都用漢字書寫自己嘅語言，語言學家至今仍然爭論白語係屬漢語族、藏緬語族，定係漢藏語系嘅獨立分支。","vi":"Người Bạch ở Đại Lý, Vân Nam đã dùng chữ Hán để viết ngôn ngữ của họ trong nhiều thế kỷ — nhưng các nhà ngôn ngữ học vẫn tranh luận liệu tiếng Bạch thuộc nhóm Hán hay Tạng-Miến hay là một nhánh độc lập của hệ Hán-Tạng.","th":"ชาวไป๋แห่งต้าหลี่ มณฑลยูนนาน ใช้อักษรจีนเขียนภาษาของตนมาหลายศตวรรษ แต่นักภาษาศาสตร์ยังเถียงกันว่าภาษาไป๋เป็นภาษาจีน ภาษาทิเบต-พม่า หรือเป็นสาขาอิสระของตระกูลจีน-ทิเบต","id":"Orang Bai di Dali, Yunnan, menulis bahasa mereka dengan aksara Han selama berabad-abad, tetapi para ahli bahasa masih memperdebatkan apakah bahasa Bai termasuk Sinitik, Tibeto-Burman, atau cabang mandiri rumpun Sino-Tibet.","hi":"युन्नान के दाली के बाई लोगों ने सदियों तक हान अक्षरों का उपयोग अपनी भाषा लिखने के लिए किया — फिर भी भाषाविद् आज तक बहस कर रहे हैं कि बाई भाषा सिनिटिक है, तिब्बती-बर्मी है, या चीनी-तिब्बती की एक स्वतंत्र शाखा।","de":"Das Bai-Volk in Dali, Yunnan, benutzte jahrhundertelang Han-Schriftzeichen für seine Sprache. Linguisten streiten jedoch bis heute, ob Bai sinitisch, tibetobirmanisch oder ein eigenständiger Zweig des Sinotibetischen ist.","fr":"Le peuple Bai de Dali, dans le Yunnan, a utilisé des caractères han pour écrire sa langue pendant des siècles, et les linguistes débattent toujours de son appartenance : sinitique, tibéto-birmane ou branche indépendante du sino-tibétain.","it":"Per secoli i Bai di Dali, nello Yunnan, hanno scritto la loro lingua con i caratteri han, eppure i linguisti discutono ancora se il bai sia sinico, tibeto-birmano o un ramo indipendente del sino-tibetano.","es":"El pueblo Bai de Dali, Yunnan, usó durante siglos caracteres han para escribir su lengua, pero los lingüistas siguen debatiendo si el Bai es sínico, tibeto-birmano o una rama independiente del sino-tibetano.","pt":"O povo Bai de Dali, Yunnan, usou caracteres Han para escrever sua língua durante séculos — mas os linguistas ainda debatem se o Bai é sinítico, tibeto-birmânico ou um ramo independente do sino-tibetano.","ru":"Народ бай из Дали (Юньнань) веками использовал иероглифы хань для записи своего языка — однако лингвисты до сих пор спорят, является ли байский язык синитическим, тибето-бирманским или самостоятельной ветвью сино-тибетской семьи.","uk":"Народ бай із Далі (Юньнань) століттями використовував ієрогліфи хань для запису своєї мови — однак лінгвісти досі сперечаються, чи є байська мова синітичною, тибето-бірманською чи самостійною гілкою сино-тибетської сім'ї.","ar":"استخدم شعب باي في دالي بمقاطعة يوننان الحروف الهانية لتدوين لغتهم قرونًا، ومع ذلك لا يزال علماء اللغة يتجادلون حول تصنيف لغة باي: هل هي صينية أو تبتية-برمية أو فرع مستقل من الأسرة الصينية-التبتية؟","he":"עם הבאי מדאלי שביונאן כתב את שפתו בסימני הָאן במשך מאות שנים, ובכל זאת בלשנים עדיין חלוקים אם הבאית היא שפה סינית, טיבטית-בורמזית, או ענף עצמאי של המשפחה הסינו-טיבטית.","sw":"Watu wa Bai wa Dali, Yunnan walitumia herufi za Han kuandika lugha yao kwa karne nyingi, lakini wanaisimu bado wanajadili kama lugha ya Bai ni ya Kisiniti, Kitibeto-Burma, au tawi huru la familia ya Sino-Tibet."},"body":{"en":"<h3>Who Are the Bai?</h3><p>The <strong>Bai people</strong> (白族, Báizú, literally 'white people') are an ethnic group of approximately 2.1 million at the 2020 census, concentrated around Dali (大理) in Yunnan province, southwest China. Not all of them speak Bai: Ethnologue's 2003 estimate was about 1.3 million speakers. Their kingdom, the <strong>Nanzhao</strong> (南詔, 738–902 CE) and its successor the <strong>Dali Kingdom</strong> (大理國, 937–1253 CE), was a major regional power that interacted intensely with Tang-dynasty China and with Tibetan and Southeast Asian civilisations. That history is why Bai has absorbed so much Chinese and been so thoroughly shaped by it.</p><h3>The Classification Puzzle</h3><p>Bai is one of the most disputed classification problems in Asia, and there are three serious camps:</p><ul><li><strong>Sinitic branch</strong>: some scholars (notably Sergei Starostin and Zhengzhang Shangfang) argue that the oldest Chinese layer accounts for all but an insignificant residue of Bai vocabulary, so that Bai is an early branching from Chinese itself; Starostin dates the split to around the 2nd century BC, in the Western Han, long before the modern Sinitic varieties took shape.</li><li><strong>Tibeto-Burman</strong>: others, such as Lee and Sagart, find a real non-Chinese core under the loans, which they first linked to the Loloish languages (relatives of Yi) and later more generally to the non-Chinese side of Sino-Tibetan.</li><li><strong>Independent branch</strong>: James Matisoff judged it \"safer to consider Bai as an independent branch of Sino-Tibetan\", perhaps close to Loloish, and deeply reshaped by centuries of contact with Chinese.</li></ul><p>The difficulty is that Bai is full of Chinese words from many different periods, spanning some two thousand years. The oldest layer, about 250 basic words, is older than Middle Chinese: it keeps apart rhymes that Chinese itself had merged by the 4th century AD. Later layers run all the way up to modern Southwestern Mandarin. The deeper you dig, the harder it is to tell a very old loan from a word Bai and Chinese both inherited.</p><div class=\"trivia-actions\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"focus\" data-code=\"bfs\">📍 View Bai on map</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"panto\" data-lat=\"25.6\" data-lng=\"100.3\" data-zoom=\"7\">🗺 Pan to Dali, Yunnan</button></div><h3>How White Script (白文) Works</h3><p>Like the Zhuang, the Bai used Han characters to write their language in a system called <strong>Baiwen</strong> (白文, 'Bai writing') or <strong>Bowen</strong>. The same character can be read with two entirely different pronunciations depending on whether you are reading it as Chinese or as Bai:</p><ul><li>Not every character splits cleanly: Bai's own word for 'horse' is itself <em>ma˧˧</em> (Proto-Bai *<em>mæ</em>), an old Sinitic form, so 馬 sounds much the same whether you read it as Chinese or as Bai. The two systems separate where the layers diverge — 目 carries the Chinese-layer reading <em>mu˨˨</em> in Bai, while the everyday Bai word for 'eye' is <em>ŋui˨˨</em>.</li><li>Grammatical particles, aspect markers, and tone-carrying suffixes particular to Bai grammar are handled through phonetic borrowing, sometimes using rare or obscure Han characters purely for their local dialect pronunciation.</li></ul><h3>The Latin Alphabet: Three Attempts</h3><p>Bai has three main dialect groups: Jianchuan (Central), Dali (Southern) and Bijiang (Northern). Speakers of the first two are said to understand each other after living together for a month. The Northern varieties, spoken by about 15,000 people, are more divergent, and ISO 639-3 in fact gives four Bai varieties a code of their own. Writing them all in one alphabet has proved hard. A <strong>Latin alphabet</strong> designed in 1958, based on the speech of Xiaguan near Dali, saw little use. A 1982 version based on Jianchuan caught on there but was rejected in Dali, so the 1993 revision gave each area its own variant. Instead of accents or numbers, it marks tone with an extra letter at the end of each syllable, the same trick the Hmong alphabet RPA uses. Meanwhile the old character-based Baiwen survives in religious texts (especially Benzhu 本主 deity worship), classical poetry, and stone inscriptions around the Dali plain — some dating to the Nanzhao kingdom itself.</p><h3>A Mirror of Sinosphere Contact</h3><p>Bai fits a pattern found across the Sinosphere: centuries of literary contact with Classical Chinese produced writing traditions that owe it a great deal and are still their own. Where Zhuang invented new sinographs to write its sounds, and Vietnamese <em>Chữ Nôm</em> did the same for an Austroasiatic language, Bai settled on reading one character two ways.</p>"},"sources":["Wiersma, Grace. 'A Study of the Bai (Minjia) Language Along Historical Lines.' PhD dissertation, University of California, Berkeley, 1990.","Handel, Zev. 'What is Sino-Tibetan? Snapshot of a Field and a Theory in Flux.' <em>Language and Linguistics Compass</em> 2(3), 2008, pp. 422–441.",{"text":"Bai language — Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bai_language"},"Xu Lin 徐琳 &amp; Zhao Yansun 赵衍荪. <em>白语简志</em> [A Brief Record of the Bai Language]. Beijing: Minzu Chubanshe, 1984.","Abramson, Marc. <em>Ethnic Identity in Tang China</em>. University of Pennsylvania Press, 2008.",{"text":"Nanzhao and Dali Kingdoms — Encyclopaedia Britannica","url":"https://www.britannica.com/topic/Nanzhao"}]},{"id":"wenzhou-devils-tongue","icon":"👻","tags":["wu","phonology","history","sinosphere"],"title":{"en":"Wenzhou Wu: A Distinct Wu Variety","ja":"温州語：呉語の中でも独特な方言","ko":"원저우어: 우어 내부에서도 차이가 큰 방언","zh":"温州话：吴语内部差异较大的方言","yue":"溫州話：吳語入面一種好獨特嘅方言","vi":"Tiếng Ôn Châu: một phương ngữ khác biệt trong nhóm Ngô","th":"ภาษาเวินโจว: ภาษาอู๋ที่มีเอกลักษณ์","id":"Wu Wenzhou: ragam Wu yang khas","hi":"वेनझोउ वू: वू की एक अलग क़िस्म","de":"Wenzhou-Wu: eine eigenständige Wu-Varietät","fr":"Le wu de Wenzhou : une variété wu à part","it":"Il wu di Wenzhou: una varietà wu a sé","es":"El wu de Wenzhou: una variedad wu singular","pt":"O wu de Wenzhou: uma variedade wu à parte","ru":"Вэньчжоуский у: особый диалект группы у","uk":"Веньчжоуський у: особлива говірка групи у","ar":"وِنتشو: لهجة وو تنفرد عن أخواتها","he":"וו של וונג'ו: ניב וו מובחן","sw":"Kiwenzhou: aina ya pekee ya Wu"},"summary":{"en":"Wenzhou Wu has limited mutual intelligibility with Mandarin and many neighbouring Wu varieties. Accounts of its use as a wartime cipher are widespread but remain undocumented.","ja":"温州語は、普通話とも近隣の多くの呉語とも通じにくい。戦時に暗号として使われたという話は広く語られているが、記録による裏づけはない。","ko":"원저우어는 표준 중국어는 물론 이웃한 많은 우어 방언과도 서로 잘 통하지 않는다. 전시에 암호로 쓰였다는 이야기가 널리 퍼져 있지만 이를 뒷받침하는 기록은 없다.","zh":"温州话与普通话以及许多邻近的吴语之间，互通程度都很有限。它曾被用作战时密码的说法流传很广，但至今没有文献佐证。","yue":"溫州話同普通話以及好多鄰近嘅吳語都唔太通。據講佢喺戰時用過嚟做密碼，呢個講法流傳好廣，但一直冇文獻佐證。","vi":"Tiếng Ôn Châu khó thông hiểu với tiếng Trung phổ thông và nhiều phương ngữ Ngô lân cận. Chuyện nó từng được dùng làm mật mã thời chiến lan truyền rộng rãi, nhưng chưa có tài liệu xác nhận.","th":"ผู้พูดภาษาเวินโจวคุยกับผู้พูดภาษาจีนกลางและภาษาอู๋ถิ่นข้างเคียงหลายถิ่นได้ไม่ค่อยรู้เรื่อง เรื่องเล่าว่าเคยใช้เป็นรหัสลับในสงครามเป็นที่รู้กันทั่ว แต่ยังไม่มีหลักฐานเป็นลายลักษณ์อักษร","id":"Wu Wenzhou hanya sedikit dapat saling dipahami dengan Mandarin dan banyak ragam Wu di sekitarnya. Kisah pemakaiannya sebagai sandi pada masa perang tersebar luas, tetapi tidak terdokumentasi.","hi":"वेनझोउ वू को मंदारिन बोलने वाले, और आसपास की कई वू क़िस्मों वाले भी, ठीक से नहीं समझ पाते। युद्धकाल में इसे गुप्त संकेत-भाषा की तरह इस्तेमाल करने के क़िस्से ख़ूब फैले हैं, पर किसी दस्तावेज़ से प्रमाणित नहीं हैं।","de":"Wenzhou-Wu ist mit dem Mandarin und vielen benachbarten Wu-Varietäten nur begrenzt wechselseitig verständlich. Berichte über seinen Einsatz als Geheimcode im Krieg sind weit verbreitet, aber nicht belegt.","fr":"Le wu de Wenzhou n'offre qu'une intercompréhension limitée avec le mandarin et bien des variétés wu voisines. Les récits de son emploi comme code secret en temps de guerre sont très répandus, mais restent non documentés.","it":"Il wu di Wenzhou è solo in parte intelligibile con il mandarino e con molte varietà wu vicine. I racconti sul suo uso come codice in tempo di guerra sono diffusi, ma restano privi di documentazione.","es":"El wu de Wenzhou tiene una inteligibilidad mutua limitada con el mandarín y con muchas variedades wu vecinas. Los relatos sobre su uso como código en tiempos de guerra están muy extendidos, pero siguen sin documentarse.","pt":"O wu de Wenzhou tem inteligibilidade mútua limitada com o mandarim e com muitas variedades wu vizinhas. Relatos de seu uso como código em tempo de guerra são muito difundidos, mas continuam sem documentação.","ru":"Взаимопонятность вэньчжоуского у с мандаринским и многими соседними диалектами группы у ограничена. Рассказы о том, что на войне его использовали как шифр, широко распространены, но документами не подтверждены.","uk":"Веньчжоуський у погано розуміють і мовці мандаринської, і чимало сусідів із групи у. Розповіді про те, що на війні ним користувалися як шифром, поширені, але документально не підтверджені.","ar":"لا تتيح لهجة وِنتشو الوو سوى قدر محدود من التفاهم المتبادل مع الماندرين ومع كثير من لهجات الوو المجاورة. أما الروايات عن استخدامها شفرةً في زمن الحرب فواسعة الانتشار، لكنها تبقى غير موثّقة.","he":"וו של וונג'ו מובנת רק במידה מוגבלת לדוברי מנדרינית ולדוברי ניבי וו שכנים רבים. הסיפורים על השימוש בה כקוד בזמן מלחמה נפוצים, אך עדיין אינם מתועדים.","sw":"Kiwenzhou kinaeleweka kwa kiasi kidogo tu na wazungumzaji wa Kimandarini na wa aina nyingi jirani za Wu. Simulizi za kutumiwa kwake kama msimbo wakati wa vita zimeenea, lakini hazijathibitishwa kwa nyaraka."},"body":{"en":"<h3>Mutual Intelligibility</h3><p>Wenzhou Wu has limited mutual intelligibility with Mandarin and also differs substantially from Northern Wu varieties such as Shanghainese and Suzhounese. A popular saying goes: <em>天不怕，地不怕，就怕溫州人說溫州話</em> — \"Fear not heaven, fear not earth, but fear a Wenzhou man speaking Wenzhounese.\" In one variant the last words become <em>講鬼話</em> (jiǎng guǐhuà), \"speaking ghost-talk\"; <strong>鬼話</strong> (guǐhuà) also means \"lies, nonsense\", so the nickname was never a compliment. (In fairness, Chinese speakers aim the same saying at other varieties they find hard to follow.)</p><div class=\"trivia-actions\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"focus\" data-code=\"wuu_wz\">📍 Wenzhou Wu on map</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"panto\" data-lat=\"28.0\" data-lng=\"120.7\" data-zoom=\"7\">🗺 Pan to Wenzhou</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"setchar\" data-char=\"人\">🔢 Show 人</button></div><h3>A Wartime Code That May Never Have Existed</h3><p>The most repeated story about Wenzhou Wu is that during the Second Sino-Japanese War, Chinese commanders put Wenzhou soldiers on the radio and Japanese cryptanalysts — fluent in Standard Chinese, familiar with the major dialects — could make nothing of it. Unlike the Navajo Code Talkers of the US Marines, who layered an artificially constructed cipher on top of their language, Wenzhou speakers would have needed no code at all: the language itself was the secret. The story remains undocumented. The sinologist Victor Mair, reviewing the claim on Language Log in 2015, wrote that despite the language's formidable reputation he had yet to see \"solid documentation of the use of Wenzhounese for intelligence purposes in war\", and noted a practical obstacle: Wenzhounese is not a written language, and the missionaries' romanisation never circulated among ordinary speakers. Reference works describe the story only as \"reputed\". A similar claim about the 1979 border war with Vietnam may involve not Wenzhounese proper but 蛮话 (Manhua), a distinct variety spoken around Qianku in Cangnan County. What the legend does record faithfully is the reputation.</p><h3>Voiced Obstruents: A Medieval Survival</h3><p>Several features of its sound system make Wenzhou hard for outsiders to follow. Middle Chinese (中古漢語), the Chinese of the Sui and Tang dynasties (roughly 600–900 CE) recorded in the rhyme books, possessed a full set of <strong>voiced obstruents</strong>: /b/, /d/, /g/, /dz/. Mandarin, Cantonese and Hokkien all lost this contrast over the centuries, merging voiced and voiceless initials into a simpler system and compensating with tone splits. Wu is the branch that did not: preserving the Middle Chinese voiced series is the classic defining criterion for Wu, and Shanghai and Suzhou keep it as surely as Wenzhou does. What makes Wenzhou harder to follow than Shanghai is not the voiced series but what sits on top of it — an eight-tone system with unusual contours, and a vocabulary that diverges sharply from Northern Wu. A Wenzhou speaker distinguishes 爬 /bo˧˩/ 'to climb' from both 怕 /pʰo˦˨/ 'to fear' and 巴 /po˧˧/ by its voiced /b/, a contrast absent from Mandarin. The voicing also pulls the syllable into the lower (yáng) tone register, which is why the three never collide. Those eight tones are arranged in a yin–yang split mirroring the original Middle Chinese distinction.</p><h3>Saying \"This\" with a Tone</h3><p>Wenzhou also diverges grammatically, and its most striking trick is a demonstrative made of pure tone. To say \"this\", a speaker takes the classifier (the measure word) and switches it to the dipping <em>rù</em> tone, devoicing a voiced initial on the way: /pa˧/ 'group' becomes /pa˧˨˧/ 'this group', and /le˧˩/ 'some (people)' becomes /l̥e˧˨˧/ 'these people'. No separate word for \"this\" is needed. Some dialectologists look for traces of the language of the ancient Ou Yue (甌越) people, who lived on this coast before Chinese speakers arrived, but such substrate claims remain hard to prove.</p><h3>Why Wenzhou Diverged</h3><p>The main reason is geography. Wenzhou sits at the southern tip of Zhejiang, hemmed in by the Yandang Mountains and open sea. Overland travel stayed hard for a very long time: the first railway into Wenzhou, the Jinhua–Wenzhou line, opened only in June 1998. For most of its history the city traded by sea, connecting more easily with Fujianese ports than with the Yangtze delta heartland. Isolation cut both ways. It let Wenzhou keep old distinctions that Northern Wu lost, and it also left the local speech free to run its own course; the city's own name, 溫州, comes out roughly as /ʔjy tɕɤu/.</p><ul><li>8 tones; voiced /b d g/ preserved from Middle Chinese</li><li>Limited mutual intelligibility with Standard Mandarin</li><li>Reputed, but never documented, to have served as a natural military cipher</li><li>Geographic isolation in southern Zhejiang preserved archaisms</li></ul>"},"sources":["Ballard, W. L. (1985). Aspects of the Linguistic History of South China. Asian Perspectives, 24(2), 163–185.","Norman, Jerry (1988). Chinese. Cambridge Language Surveys. Cambridge University Press.",{"text":"Wenzhou dialect — Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wenzhou_dialect"},"Yue-Hashimoto, Anne O. (2003). Chinese dialects: grammar. In Graham Thurgood & Randy J. LaPolla (eds.), The Sino-Tibetan Languages, 84–125. Routledge.","Coblin, W. South (1994). A Compendium of Phonetics in Northwest Chinese. Journal of Chinese Linguistics Monograph Series.",{"text":"Cao, Jianfen & Ian Maddieson (1992). An exploration of phonation types in Wu dialects of Chinese. Journal of Phonetics, 20(1), 77–92.","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/S0095-4470(19)30255-4"},{"text":"Mair, Victor (2015). \"Devilishly difficult 'dialect'.\" Language Log.","url":"https://languagelog.ldc.upenn.edu/nll/?p=20790"}]},{"id":"hakka-diaspora-language","icon":"🌏","tags":["hakka","diaspora","sinosphere","history"],"title":{"en":"Hakka: The Language of the \"Guest People\"","ja":"客家語：「客人」と呼ばれた人々の言語","ko":"객가어: '손님 민족'의 언어","zh":"客家话：“客人”的语言","yue":"客家話：「客人」嘅語言","vi":"Tiếng Khách Gia: ngôn ngữ của \"Người Khách\"","th":"ภาษาแคะ: ภาษาของ 'คนต่างถิ่น'","id":"Bahasa Hakka: bahasa \"orang tamu\"","hi":"हक्का: 'अतिथि लोगों' की भाषा","de":"Hakka: die Sprache des „Gastvolks“","fr":"Le hakka : la langue du « peuple hôte »","it":"L'hakka: la lingua del «popolo ospite»","es":"El hakka: la lengua del «pueblo huésped»","pt":"O hakka: a língua do «povo hóspede»","ru":"Хакка: язык «народа гостей»","uk":"Хакка: мова «народу гостей»","ar":"الهاكا: لغة «شعب الضيوف»","he":"האקה: שפת ״עם האורחים״","sw":"Kihakka: lugha ya \"watu wageni\""},"summary":{"en":"Hakka speakers live across southern China and diaspora communities from Mauritius to Suriname. Many Hakka varieties preserve the Middle Chinese entering-tone finals /-p/, /-t/, and /-k/.","ja":"客家語は江西からモーリシャス、スリナムまで広がり、中古漢語の入声尾をすべて保存する。孫文・鄧小平・李光耀を生んだ言語。","ko":"객가어 화자는 중국 남부 곳곳과 모리셔스에서 수리남에 이르는 해외 공동체에 살고 있다. 많은 객가어 방언이 중고한어의 입성 운미 /-p/, /-t/, /-k/를 간직하고 있다.","zh":"客家话使用者分布在中国南方，以及从毛里求斯到苏里南的海外社群。许多客家方言保留了中古汉语的入声韵尾 /-p/、/-t/ 和 /-k/。","yue":"講客家話嘅人分佈喺中國南方，同埋由毛里裘斯到蘇里南嘅海外社群。好多客家方言保留咗中古漢語嘅入聲韻尾 /-p/、/-t/ 同 /-k/。","vi":"Người nói tiếng Khách Gia sống khắp miền nam Trung Quốc và trong các cộng đồng hải ngoại từ Mauritius đến Suriname. Nhiều phương ngữ Khách Gia giữ các âm cuối thanh nhập /-p/, /-t/, /-k/ của Hán trung cổ.","th":"ผู้พูดภาษาแคะกระจายตั้งแต่เจียงซีไปจนถึงมอริเชียสและซูรินาม ภาษานี้ยังเก็บพยัญชนะท้ายทั้งสามของวรรณยุกต์เข้าแบบภาษาจีนยุคกลางไว้ และเป็นภาษาแม่ของซุน ยัด-เซน เติ้ง เสี่ยวผิง และลี กวน ยู","id":"Penutur Hakka tinggal di seluruh Tiongkok selatan dan di komunitas diaspora dari Mauritius hingga Suriname. Banyak ragam Hakka mempertahankan akhiran nada masuk Bahasa Tionghoa Pertengahan /-p/, /-t/, dan /-k/.","hi":"हक्का भाषी पूरे दक्षिणी चीन में और मॉरिशस से सूरीनाम तक के प्रवासी समुदायों में रहते हैं। हक्का की कई क़िस्मों में मध्यकालीन चीनी के प्रवेश-स्वर अंत्य /-p/, /-t/ और /-k/ आज भी बचे हैं।","de":"Hakka-Sprecher leben in ganz Südchina und in Diaspora-Gemeinschaften von Mauritius bis Suriname. Viele Hakka-Varietäten bewahren die Eintretenden-Ton-Endungen des Mittelchinesischen: /-p/, /-t/ und /-k/.","fr":"Les locuteurs du hakka vivent dans tout le sud de la Chine et dans une diaspora qui va de l'île Maurice au Suriname. Bien des variétés hakka conservent les finales du ton entrant du chinois médiéval, /-p/, /-t/ et /-k/.","it":"I parlanti dell'hakka vivono in tutta la Cina meridionale e in comunità della diaspora da Mauritius al Suriname. Molte varietà hakka conservano le finali del tono entrante del cinese medio /-p/, /-t/ e /-k/.","es":"Los hablantes de hakka viven en el sur de China y en comunidades de la diáspora, de Mauricio a Surinam. Muchas variedades hakka conservan las finales del tono entrante del chino medio /-p/, /-t/ y /-k/.","pt":"Os falantes de hakka vivem no sul da China e em comunidades da diáspora, de Maurício ao Suriname. Muitas variedades do hakka preservam as finais do tom entrante do chinês médio /-p/, /-t/ e /-k/.","ru":"Носители хакка живут по всему югу Китая и в диаспоре от Маврикия до Суринама. Многие разновидности хакка сохраняют среднекитайские конечные /-p/, /-t/ и /-k/ входящего тона.","uk":"Носії хакка живуть по всьому півдню Китаю та в діаспорі від Маврикію до Суринаму. Багато різновидів хакка зберігають середньокитайські кінцеві /-p/, /-t/ і /-k/ вхідного тону.","ar":"يعيش متحدثو الهاكا في أنحاء جنوب الصين وفي مجتمعات الشتات من موريشيوس إلى سورينام. وتحتفظ كثير من لهجات الهاكا بنهايات نبرة الدخول في الصينية الوسطى: /-p/ و/-t/ و/-k/.","he":"דוברי האקה חיים ברחבי דרום סין ובקהילות תפוצה ממאוריציוס ועד סורינאם. ניבי האקה רבים משמרים את הסיומות /-p/, /-t/ ו-/-k/ של טון הכניסה של הסינית התיכונה.","sw":"Wazungumzaji wa Kihakka wanaishi kote kusini mwa China na katika jamii za diaspora kuanzia Mauritius hadi Suriname. Aina nyingi za Kihakka zinahifadhi miisho ya toni ya kuingia ya Kichina cha Kati /-p/, /-t/ na /-k/."},"body":{"en":"<h3>The Guest People</h3><p>The word <strong>客家</strong> (Hakka; literally \"guest families\") encodes a history of displacement. The classic account, set out by the historian Lo Hsiang-lin in 1933, tells of waves of Han Chinese refugees moving south out of the Central Plains over roughly a thousand years, driven by war, famine and dynastic collapse, and settling as latecomers in the hill country where Jiangxi, Fujian and Guangdong meet. With the best farmland already taken, they came to be called \"guests\" (客) by the established \"host\" (主) households. Modern scholars think the real story is more mixed: the Hakka language is closest to Gan, spoken next door in Jiangxi, and the ancestors of today's Hakka also include southern peoples such as the She, with whom the newcomers intermarried.</p><div class=\"trivia-actions\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"focus\" data-code=\"hak_cn\">📍 Hakka (mainland) on map</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"focus\" data-code=\"hak_tw\">📍 Hakka (Taiwan) on map</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"panto\" data-lat=\"24.5\" data-lng=\"116.1\" data-zoom=\"6\">🗺 Pan to Meizhou</button></div><h3>The Diaspora: From Mauritius to Hawaii</h3><p>Hakka-speaking communities are distributed across southern China and several overseas migration regions. From the 17th century onward, Hakka men and women joined the great <em>Nanyang</em> (南洋) emigration to Southeast Asia, and from there dispersed further: to Mauritius in the Indian Ocean, to Suriname on the Atlantic coast of South America, to Jamaica, and to Hawaii. In Suriname, the first Chinese settlers came not from the Meizhou (梅州) heartland but from the Fuitungon region east of the Pearl River Delta, a name made from its three counties: Huiyang, Dongguan and Bao'an. Their descendants' Hakka keeps that 19th-century Fuitungon flavour, mixed with Dutch and Sranan Tongo loanwords.</p><h3>Linguistic Fingerprints</h3><p>Several features mark Hakka as distinct within Sinitic. The language preserves all three Middle Chinese (中古漢語) entering-tone (<em>入聲</em>) finals: /-p/, /-t/, and /-k/ — making it, alongside Cantonese and Min, a conservative keeper of Tang-dynasty stop codas that Mandarin lost, scattering the old entering-tone syllables unpredictably across its four modern tones. The word for \"I/me\" is <strong>𠊎</strong> /ŋai/, written with a character used for Hakka alone and enough on its own to identify a speaker. Most scholars take it to be the same ancient pronoun as Mandarin <em>wǒ</em> and Cantonese <em>ngóh</em> (我); Hakka even keeps a literary reading <em>ngo</em> for that character. What sets the everyday form apart is its <em>-ai</em>, where the other varieties have a back vowel. Taiwanese shop signs proudly announce 𠊎講客 <em>Ngài kóng Hak</em>, \"I speak Hakka\". The best-known church romanization, <strong>PFS</strong> (Pha̍k-fa-sṳ, 白話字), grew out of 19th-century Basel Mission work and is still used in Hakka Christian communities; Taiwan's schools and government use a separate official Hakka Pinyin.</p><h3>Taiwan's Great Hakka Movement</h3><p>Taiwan is home to roughly 4.6 million people who identify as Hakka, predominantly in Taoyuan, Hsinchu, and Miaoli, but far fewer who can actually speak the language, with estimates ranging from about 1.2 million to 2.4 million. After decades of suppression under Japanese colonialism and Kuomintang Mandarin-promotion policies, Hakka experienced a grassroots revival from the 1980s onward. Its symbolic start came on 28 December 1988, when thousands of Hakka marched through Taipei carrying portraits of Sun Yat-sen in the <strong>還我母語運動</strong> (\"Give Us Back Our Mother Tongue\" movement), demanding Hakka broadcasting and teaching. The campaign led in 2001 to the Council for Hakka Affairs (today's Hakka Affairs Council, 客家委員會), which describes itself as the world's only national-level government body for Hakka affairs. Hakka Television (客家電視台) now broadcasts 24 hours a day.</p><h3>Famous People of Hakka Descent</h3><ul><li><strong>孫中山</strong> (Sun Yat-sen) — founding father of the Republic of China (his Hakka ancestry is traditional but debated)</li><li><strong>鄧小平</strong> (Deng Xiaoping) — architect of China's Reform and Opening, from a Sichuan Hakka lineage</li><li><strong>李光耀</strong> (Lee Kuan Yew) — first Prime Minister of Singapore</li><li><strong>洪秀全</strong> (Hong Xiuquan) — leader of the Taiping Heavenly Kingdom</li></ul><p>Writers have often linked this prominence in revolutionary movements to the Hakka's position as a marginal \"guest\" community with strong internal solidarity and a high regard for education, though such explanations are hard to test. The thread is real enough in one case: Sun Yat-sen said that as a boy he listened to an old Taiping veteran's stories and declared he would be a second Hong Xiuquan.</p>"},"sources":["Lutz, Jessie G. & Rolland Ray Lutz (1998). Hakka Chinese Confront Protestant Christianity, 1850–1900. M.E. Sharpe.","Hashimoto, Mantaro J. (1973). The Hakka Dialect: A Linguistic Study of its Phonology, Syntax and Lexicon. Cambridge University Press.",{"text":"Hakka people — Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hakka_people"},"Szeto, Pui Yiu, Umberto Ansaldo & Stephen Matthews (2018). Typological variation across Mandarin dialects. Linguistic Typology, 22(2), 233–275.",{"text":"Hakka Affairs Council, Taiwan","url":"https://www.hakka.gov.tw/"},"Branner, David Prager (2000). Problems in Comparative Chinese Dialectology. Mouton de Gruyter."]},{"id":"sino-vietnamese-han-viet","icon":"🎵","tags":["sinosphere","vietnamese","middle-chinese","phonology","tones"],"title":{"en":"Hán-Việt: A Tang-Period Chinese Loan Layer in Vietnamese","ja":"漢越語：ベトナム語に残る唐代の中国語借用層","ko":"한월어: 베트남어에 남은 당대 중국어 차용층","zh":"汉越语：越南语中的唐代汉语借词层","yue":"漢越語：越南語入面嘅唐代漢語借詞層","vi":"Hán-Việt: lớp từ vay mượn tiếng Hán thời Đường trong tiếng Việt","th":"ฮ้านเหวียต: ชั้นคำยืมภาษาจีนสมัยถังในภาษาเวียดนาม","id":"Hán-Việt: lapisan serapan Tionghoa masa Tang dalam bahasa Vietnam","hi":"हान-विएत: वियतनामी में तांग काल की चीनी ऋण-परत","de":"Hán-Việt: Eine chinesische Lehnschicht der Tang-Zeit im Vietnamesischen","fr":"Hán-Việt : une couche d'emprunts chinois d'époque Tang en vietnamien","it":"Hán-Việt: uno strato di prestiti cinesi di epoca Tang in vietnamita","es":"Hán-Việt: una capa de préstamos chinos de época Tang en vietnamita","pt":"Hán-Việt: uma camada de empréstimos chineses do período Tang no vietnamita","ru":"Хан-Вьет: слой китайских заимствований эпохи Тан во вьетнамском","uk":"Хан-В'єт: шар китайських запозичень доби Тан у в'єтнамській","ar":"الهان-فيتية: طبقة اقتراض صينية في الفيتنامية من عهد تانغ","he":"האן-וייט: רובד שאילה סיני בווייטנאמית מתקופת טאנג","sw":"Hán-Việt: tabaka la maneno ya mkopo ya Kichina ya enzi ya Tang katika Kivietinamu"},"summary":{"en":"Somewhere between a third and 70% of Vietnamese vocabulary is Sino-Vietnamese (Hán-Việt), depending on how you count. This late-Tang reading layer keeps Middle Chinese final stops and tone categories that modern Mandarin has lost.","ja":"ベトナム語の語彙に占める漢越語（Hán-Việt）の割合は、数え方によって3分の1から70%までと幅がある。唐代後期に由来するこの字音の層は、現代北京語が失った中古漢語の入声韻尾や声調の区別を今に伝えている。","ko":"한월어(漢越語)는 세는 방법에 따라 베트남어 어휘의 3분의 1에서 70%까지를 차지한다. 당나라 말기의 이 독음층은 현대 표준 중국어가 잃어버린 중고한어의 입성 종성과 성조 범주를 지금도 간직하고 있다.","zh":"越南语词汇中，汉越语所占比例从三分之一到70%不等，全看怎么统计。这一晚唐读音层保留了中古汉语的入声韵尾和声调类别，而这些都是现代普通话已经丢掉的。","yue":"越南語詞彙入面，漢越語（Hán-Việt）佔三分之一到七成不等，視乎點計。呢個唐代後期嘅讀音層，保住咗現代普通話已經失落嘅中古漢語入聲韻尾同調類。","vi":"Tùy cách đếm, từ một phần ba đến 70% từ vựng tiếng Việt là từ Hán-Việt. Lớp âm đọc cuối đời Đường này giữ lại những âm tắc cuối và phạm trù thanh điệu của tiếng Hán trung cổ mà tiếng Quan thoại hiện đại đã đánh mất.","th":"คำศัพท์ภาษาเวียดนามราวหนึ่งในสามถึง 70% เป็นคำฮัน-เวียด (Hán-Việt) แล้วแต่วิธีนับ ชั้นเสียงอ่านสมัยปลายราชวงศ์ถังนี้รักษาเสียงหยุดท้ายพยางค์และหมวดวรรณยุกต์ของภาษาจีนยุคกลางที่ภาษาจีนกลางสมัยใหม่สูญเสียไปแล้ว","id":"Antara sepertiga hingga 70% kosakata Vietnam adalah kata Sino-Vietnam (Hán-Việt), tergantung cara menghitungnya. Lapisan bacaan dari akhir masa Tang ini mempertahankan letupan akhir dan kategori nada bahasa Tionghoa Pertengahan yang sudah hilang dari Mandarin modern.","hi":"गिनने के तरीक़े पर निर्भर करता है, पर वियतनामी शब्दावली का एक-तिहाई से लेकर 70% तक हिस्सा हान-विएत (Hán-Việt) है। उत्तर-तांग काल की यह पठन-परत मध्यकालीन चीनी के वे अंत्य स्पर्श और स्वराघात-श्रेणियाँ सँजोए हुए है जिन्हें आधुनिक मंदारिन खो चुकी है।","de":"Je nach Zählweise ist zwischen einem Drittel und 70 % des vietnamesischen Wortschatzes Sinovietnamesisch (Hán-Việt). Diese Leseschicht der späten Tang-Zeit bewahrt mittelchinesische Endverschlusslaute und Tonklassen, die das moderne Mandarin verloren hat.","fr":"Selon la façon de compter, entre un tiers et 70 % du vocabulaire vietnamien est sino-vietnamien (Hán-Việt). Cette couche de lectures de la fin des Tang conserve des occlusives finales et des catégories tonales du chinois médiéval que le mandarin moderne a perdues.","it":"A seconda di come si conta, tra un terzo e il 70% del vocabolario vietnamita è sino-vietnamita (Hán-Việt). Questo strato di letture di fine epoca Tang conserva le occlusive finali e le categorie tonali del cinese medio che il mandarino moderno ha perso.","es":"Entre un tercio y un 70 % del vocabulario vietnamita es sino-vietnamita (Hán-Việt), según cómo se cuente. Esta capa de lecturas de finales de la época Tang conserva oclusivas finales y categorías tonales del chino medio que el mandarín moderno ha perdido.","pt":"Entre um terço e 70% do vocabulário vietnamita é sino-vietnamita (Hán-Việt), conforme a maneira de contar. Essa camada de leituras do final da era Tang guarda oclusivas finais e categorias tonais do chinês médio que o mandarim moderno perdeu.","ru":"Сино-вьетнамские слова (Hán-Việt) составляют, в зависимости от способа подсчёта, от трети до 70 % вьетнамской лексики. Этот позднетанский слой чтений хранит среднекитайские конечные смычные и тоновые категории, утраченные современным мандаринским.","uk":"Залежно від способу підрахунку, від третини до 70% в’єтнамської лексики становлять хан-в’єтські слова (Hán-Việt). Цей пізньотанський шар читань зберігає середньокитайські кінцеві зімкнені й тонові категорії, які сучасна мандаринська втратила.","ar":"ما بين ثلث المفردات الفيتنامية و70% منها كلمات هان-فيتية (Hán-Việt)، بحسب طريقة العدّ. وتحفظ طبقة القراءات هذه، المأخوذة من أواخر عهد تانغ، الوقفات الختامية والفئات النغمية للصينية الوسطى التي فقدتها الماندرين الحديثة.","he":"בין שליש ל-70% מאוצר המילים הווייטנאמי הוא האן-וייט (Hán-Việt), תלוי איך סופרים. רובד הקריאה הזה, מסוף תקופת טאנג, שומר על העיצורים הסותמים הסופיים ועל קטגוריות הטון של הסינית התיכונה, שהמנדרינית המודרנית איבדה.","sw":"Kati ya theluthi moja na 70% ya msamiati wa Kivietinamu ni Hán-Việt (Kisino-Kivietinamu), kutegemea jinsi unavyohesabu. Tabaka hili la usomaji la mwishoni mwa enzi ya Tang linahifadhi vizuizi vya mwisho na makundi ya toni ya Kichina cha Kati ambayo Kimandarini cha kisasa kimevipoteza."},"body":{"en":"<h3>A Tang-Period Borrowing Layer</h3><p>When Tang dynasty officials governed Annam (northern Vietnam) between the 7th and 10th centuries CE, Vietnamese absorbed a large body of Chinese vocabulary. Linguists call this stratum <strong>Hán-Việt</strong> (漢越, Sino-Vietnamese), and its value to historians lies in the sound distinctions it preserves.</p><p>Estimates of the Sinitic share of the Vietnamese lexicon range widely — <strong>from about a third to as much as 70%</strong>, lower in everyday speech and far higher in technical writing — much like the Latin and French layer that thickens towards the formal end of English. But English took in its French and Latin words piecemeal, while Vietnamese scholars adopted a complete reading system: working from Chinese rhyme dictionaries such as the <em>Qieyun</em>, they gave every character a regular Vietnamese pronunciation, and about 3,000 of these morphemes passed into the language. The result closely mirrors the Middle Chinese sound system. An older layer of some 400 words, borrowed individually after the Han conquest of 111 BC, sits underneath and now feels entirely native: <em>bạc</em> \"silver\" goes back to an early borrowing of the word written 白 \"white\", which the later reading system renders <em>bạch</em>.</p><div class='trivia-actions'><button type='button' class='trivia-action' data-action='setchar' data-char='食'>🔡 Show 食 (food/eat) readings</button><button type='button' class='trivia-action' data-action='focus' data-code='vi_ohan'>📍 Focus Old Hán-Nôm layer</button></div><h3>Preservation of Final Stops</h3><p>Middle Chinese (中古漢語, 7th–10th c.) had four categories of tone, and the <em>checked tones</em> (入聲, entering tones) ended in unreleased stop consonants: <strong>-p, -t, -k</strong>. Modern Mandarin lost all three — 國 <em>guó</em> once ended in -k, 學 <em>xué</em> in -k, 食 <em>shí</em> in -k.</p><p>Sino-Vietnamese readings preserve these finals, though the alphabet spells final <em>-k</em> as <em>-c</em> (or <em>-ch</em>):</p><ul><li>學 → <strong>học</strong> (final -k intact)</li><li>國 → <strong>quốc</strong> (final -k intact)</li><li>食 → <strong>thực</strong> (final -k intact)</li><li>十 → <strong>thập</strong> (final -p intact)</li><li>八 → <strong>bát</strong> (final -t intact)</li></ul><div class='trivia-actions'><button type='button' class='trivia-action' data-action='setchar' data-char='食'>🔡 Compare 食 readings</button><button type='button' class='trivia-action' data-action='setchar' data-char='山'>🔡 Compare 山 readings</button></div><h3>Eight Categories from Four</h3><p>Middle Chinese had four tones: 平 (level), 上 (rising), 去 (departing), and 入 (entering). Each one later split in two according to whether the initial consonant was voiced, a <em>tone split</em> that gave the <strong>eight tone categories</strong> (四聲八調) that Sino-Vietnamese readings still sort into. Vietnamese writes them with just <strong>six tones</strong>, because the two 入 categories fell together with the two 去 categories: 平 gives ngang and huyền, 上 gives hỏi and ngã (though many syllables that began with an old voiced stop, affricate or fricative went to nặng instead), 去 gives sắc and nặng, and the old 入 syllables, the ones ending in -p, -t, -c, -ch, take sắc and nặng as well. So the six marks of quốc ngữ map neatly onto the reconstructed Middle Chinese grid. Modern Mandarin collapsed that same grid into four tones, losing a vast amount of tonal information.</p><h3>Why Beijing Lost What Hanoi Kept</h3><p>The difference reflects geographic and political history. Mandarin (普通話) derives mainly from northern dialects that had already undergone centuries of internal restructuring — loss of final stops, merger of tones — long before the standard was codified. Vietnam won its independence in 938 but kept Literary Chinese for government and scholarship until the early 20th century, reading it all along in its own Tang-based way. Those readings were not frozen: they went through Vietnamese sound changes. But they changed independently of China, so the losses that reshaped northern Chinese never reached them.</p><p>Sino-Vietnamese readings are therefore one source of comparative evidence for reconstructing Middle Chinese. Alongside Cantonese, Hokkien and Sino-Korean readings, they preserve distinctions that Mandarin alone does not show.</p><blockquote>文化 <em>văn hóa</em> (vi) — 文化 <em>wénhuà</em> (zh): same characters, and here the two agree — Vietnamese <em>v-</em> and Mandarin <em>w-</em> are both regular reflexes of the Middle Chinese 微 initial (Baxter <em>mjun</em>), whose original *m- survives instead in Cantonese <em>man4</em> and Hokkien <em>bûn</em>.</blockquote><p>Common words such as <em>quốc</em>, <em>học</em> and <em>văn</em> therefore preserve a borrowing layer established many centuries before modern Standard Chinese.</p>"},"sources":["Pulleyblank, E. G. (1984). Middle Chinese: A Study in Historical Phonology. UBC Press.","Maspero, H. (1912). Études sur la phonétique historique de la langue annamite. Bulletin de l'École française d'Extrême-Orient.","Schuessler, A. (2007). ABC Etymological Dictionary of Old Chinese. University of Hawaii Press.",{"text":"Wikipedia: Sino-Vietnamese vocabulary","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sino-Vietnamese_vocabulary"},"Norman, J. (1988). Chinese. Cambridge University Press.","Baxter, W. H. & Sagart, L. (2014). Old Chinese: A New Reconstruction. Oxford University Press.",{"text":"Wikipedia: Four tones (Middle Chinese) — distribution table (Sino-Vietnamese row, after Nguyễn Tài Cẩn 2000)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Four_tones_(Middle_Chinese)"}]},{"id":"sino-korean-versus-sino-japanese","icon":"🔀","tags":["sinosphere","middle-chinese","phonology","japanese","korean","vietnamese","comparative"],"title":{"en":"Comparing Han-Character Readings in Four Languages","ja":"一つの漢字が語る四つの音：日本語・朝鮮語・ベトナム語・中国語","ko":"하나의 한자, 네 가지 독음: 일본어·한국어·베트남어·중국어","zh":"一个汉字，四种读音：日语・韩语・越语・汉语","yue":"一個漢字，四種讀音：日語、韓語、越語、漢語","vi":"Một chữ Hán, bốn cách đọc: tiếng Nhật, Hàn, Việt, Trung","th":"อักษรเดียว อ่านสี่แบบ: ญี่ปุ่น เกาหลี เวียดนาม จีน","id":"Satu aksara Han, empat bacaan: Jepang, Korea, Vietnam, Mandarin","hi":"एक हान अक्षर, चार पाठ: जापानी, कोरियाई, वियतनामी, मंदारिन","de":"Ein Schriftzeichen, vier Lesungen: Japanisch, Koreanisch, Vietnamesisch, Mandarin","fr":"Un sinogramme, quatre lectures : japonais, coréen, vietnamien, mandarin","it":"Un carattere Han, quattro letture: giapponese, coreano, vietnamita, mandarino","es":"Un carácter Han, cuatro lecturas: japonés, coreano, vietnamita, mandarín","pt":"Um caractere han, quatro leituras: japonês, coreano, vietnamita e mandarim","ru":"Один иероглиф, четыре прочтения: японский, корейский, вьетнамский, мандаринский","uk":"Один ієрогліф, чотири прочитання: японська, корейська, в'єтнамська, мандаринська","ar":"رمز واحد، أربع قراءات: اليابانية والكورية والفيتنامية والماندرين","he":"סימן כתב אחד, ארבע קריאות: יפנית, קוריאנית, וייטנאמית, מנדרינית","sw":"Herufi moja ya Han, usomaji minne: Kijapani, Kikorea, Kivietinamu, Kimandarini"},"summary":{"en":"The same Han character carries cognate but divergent pronunciations across Mandarin, Korean, Japanese, and Vietnamese — a layered record of Middle Chinese dialects borrowed at different historical moments.","ja":"同一の漢字が中国語・朝鮮語・日本語・ベトナム語で同源でありながら異なる読みを持つのは、中古漢語の異なる方言が異なる時代に各地に伝わった証拠である。","ko":"동일한 한자가 중국어·한국어·일본어·베트남어에서 같은 기원을 가지면서도 서로 다른 독음을 갖는 것은 중고한어의 다양한 방언이 각기 다른 시대에 각 지역에 전파된 역사적 기록이다.","zh":"同一汉字在普通话、韩语、日语、越南语中拥有同源却各异的读音，是中古汉语不同方言在不同历史时期传入各地的分层记录。","yue":"同一個漢字喺普通話、韓語、日語、越南語入面有同源但各異嘅讀音，係中古漢語唔同方言喺唔同歷史時期傳入各地嘅分層記錄。","vi":"Cùng một chữ Hán mang những cách đọc đồng nguyên nhưng phân kỳ trong tiếng Quan thoại, Hàn, Nhật và Việt — đây là bản ghi phân lớp của các phương ngữ tiếng Hán trung cổ được mượn vào các thời điểm lịch sử khác nhau.","th":"อักษรจีนตัวเดียวกันออกเสียงต่างกันในภาษาจีนกลาง เกาหลี ญี่ปุ่น และเวียดนาม ทั้งที่มาจากต้นเดียวกัน เสียงเหล่านี้คือบันทึกเป็นชั้น ๆ ของภาษาจีนยุคกลางถิ่นต่าง ๆ ที่ถูกยืมไปในต่างยุคกัน","id":"Aksara Han yang sama punya lafal yang berkerabat tetapi berbeda dalam bahasa Mandarin, Korea, Jepang, dan Vietnam, catatan berlapis dari dialek-dialek Tionghoa Pertengahan yang dipinjam pada masa yang berbeda.","hi":"एक ही हान अक्षर का मूल मंदारिन, कोरियाई, जापानी और वियतनामी में एक है, पर उच्चारण अलग-अलग। इन उच्चारणों में अलग-अलग दौर में उधार ली गई मध्यकालीन चीनी बोलियाँ परत-दर-परत दर्ज हैं।","de":"Dasselbe Han-Zeichen trägt verwandte, aber divergente Aussprachen im Mandarin, Koreanischen, Japanischen und Vietnamesischen. Darin sind mittelchinesische Dialekte, die zu verschiedenen Zeiten entlehnt wurden, Schicht für Schicht festgehalten.","fr":"Le même sinogramme porte des prononciations apparentées mais divergentes en mandarin, coréen, japonais et vietnamien : elles gardent, couche par couche, la trace des dialectes du chinois médiéval empruntés à différentes époques.","it":"Lo stesso carattere Han ha pronunce affini ma diverse in mandarino, coreano, giapponese e vietnamita: strati che conservano i dialetti del cinese medio presi in prestito in momenti storici diversi.","es":"El mismo carácter Han lleva pronunciaciones afines pero divergentes en mandarín, coreano, japonés y vietnamita, un registro por capas de los dialectos del chino medio tomados prestados en diferentes momentos históricos.","pt":"O mesmo caractere Han transporta pronúncias cognatas mas divergentes em mandarim, coreano, japonês e vietnamita — um registro estratificado dos dialetos do chinês médio emprestados em diferentes momentos históricos.","ru":"Один и тот же иероглиф несёт родственные, но разошедшиеся произношения в мандаринском, корейском, японском и вьетнамском — это многопластовая запись среднекитайских диалектов, заимствованных в разные исторические моменты.","uk":"Той самий ієрогліф має споріднені, але різні вимови в мандаринській, корейській, японській та в'єтнамській. У цих вимовах шарами відклалися середньокитайські говірки, запозичені в різні історичні моменти.","ar":"يحمل الرمز الهانزي الواحد نطقاً ذا أصول مشتركة لكنه متشعّب في الماندرين والكورية واليابانية والفيتنامية، وهذا التشعب سجلّ لطبقات من الصينية الوسطى استُعيرت في أزمنة مختلفة.","he":"לאותו סימן האן יש הגיות קרובות אבל שונות במנדרינית, בקוריאנית, ביפנית ובווייטנאמית. כל אחת מהן שומרת שכבה של ניבי הסינית התיכונה, שנשאלה בזמן אחר.","sw":"Herufi ile ile ya Han inabeba matamshi yanayohusiana lakini tofauti katika Kimandarini, Kikorea, Kijapani, na Kivietinamu. Ni rekodi ya tabaka za lahaja za Kichina cha Kati zilizokopwa katika nyakati tofauti za kihistoria."},"body":{"en":"<h3>The Sinosphere's Rosetta Stone</h3><p>Every country that adopted Chinese characters also adopted Chinese pronunciation, but they borrowed from different eras and different dialects. The result is a record of old Chinese hidden inside four modern writing systems. The borrowed reading-layers of Japanese, Korean and Vietnamese are known collectively as <strong>Sino-Xenic</strong> (a term coined by Samuel E. Martin in 1953); set beside Mandarin, the modern standard that grew out of the northern Chinese dialects, they give four cognate reflexes of a single Chinese source that diverged after borrowing.</p><h3>A Character-by-Character Comparison</h3><div class='trivia-actions'><button type='button' class='trivia-action' data-action='setchar' data-char='山'>🔡 Compare 山 (mountain)</button><button type='button' class='trivia-action' data-action='setchar' data-char='水'>🔡 Compare 水 (water)</button></div><p>Take <strong>山</strong> (mountain). In Middle Chinese it was reconstructed as *ʂan. Today:</p><ul><li>Mandarin: <strong>shān</strong> (tone preserved, final nasal preserved)</li><li>Korean (漢字音): <strong>san</strong> (conservative nasal, no tone)</li><li>Japanese (漢音 Kan-on): <strong>san</strong> (same layer as Korean)</li><li>Vietnamese: <strong>sơn</strong> (vowel shifted, final nasal preserved)</li></ul><p>Now <strong>國</strong> (nation), Middle Chinese *kwok:</p><ul><li>Mandarin: <strong>guó</strong> — final -k lost, tone shifted</li><li>Korean: <strong>guk</strong> — final -k preserved</li><li>Japanese (漢音): <strong>koku</strong> — -k reflected as -ku (vowel insertion)</li><li>Vietnamese: <strong>quốc</strong> — final -k fully preserved</li></ul><div class='trivia-actions'><button type='button' class='trivia-action' data-action='setchar' data-char='食'>🔡 Compare 食 (eat/food)</button></div><h3>The Final-Stop Hierarchy</h3><p>This pattern repeats across every character with a Middle Chinese checked-tone (入聲) ending. The hierarchy of conservation is consistent:</p><ol><li><strong>Cantonese/Hokkien</strong>: preserve -p, -t, -k intact as unreleased stops (most completely in Cantonese; Hokkien also has a colloquial glottal-stop layer)</li><li><strong>Vietnamese</strong>: preserves all three (-p, -t, -c/-ch)</li><li><strong>Korean</strong>: keeps -p and -k, but Middle Chinese -t appears as -l (一 <em>il</em>, 發 <em>bal</em>)</li><li><strong>Japanese</strong>: adds a vowel: -t becomes -tsu or -chi, -k becomes -ku or -ki, and the old -pu has mostly worn down to a long vowel (十 <em>jū</em>, 法 <em>hō</em>)</li><li><strong>Mandarin</strong>: erases all three, leaving only tone traces</li></ol><p>For 學 (study; Middle Chinese <em>haewk</em> in Baxter's notation): Korean <em>hak</em>, Japanese <em>gaku</em>, Vietnamese <em>học</em>, Mandarin <em>xué</em>. The -k is alive in three out of four.</p><h3>Why Japanese Has Multiple Readings</h3><p>Japan borrowed Chinese pronunciation in at least three distinct waves: <strong>呉音 Go-on</strong> (Wu-based, 5th–6th c.), <strong>漢音 Kan-on</strong> (Tang-court standard, 8th–9th c.), and <strong>唐音 Tō-on</strong> (Song–Ming era). For 行:</p><ul><li>Go-on: <em>gyō</em></li><li>Kan-on: <em>kō</em></li><li>Tō-on: <em>an</em> (from southern dialects)</li></ul><p>Korean by contrast received a comparatively unified transmission: the main layer of Sino-Korean (漢字音) is generally traced to the Chang'an standard of Tang China, carried home by the Silla students and envoys at the Tang capital in the 8th and 9th centuries. Resting on a single dominant source, as it happens much the same Tang standard as <em>kan-on</em>, is what makes Sino-Korean so much more internally consistent than the Japanese layers.</p><div class='trivia-actions'><button type='button' class='trivia-action' data-action='focus' data-code='ko'>📍 Focus Korean on map</button><button type='button' class='trivia-action' data-action='focus' data-code='ja'>📍 Focus Japanese on map</button></div><h3>The Tonal Preservation Ladder</h3><p>Beyond consonants, tonal preservation follows a clear gradient. Middle Chinese had 4 tone categories that split into 8 in some languages:</p><ul><li>Cantonese: <strong>6–9 tones</strong> (best preservation)</li><li>Vietnamese: <strong>6 tones</strong>, or 8 if syllables ending in -p, -t, -c count separately, which lines up neatly with the 8 Middle Chinese categories (excellent)</li><li>Korean Sino-Korean: <strong>historically 3 pitch accents</strong>, now mostly levelled in Seoul dialect (good historical record)</li><li>Japanese on-readings: <strong>no tones</strong>. Japanese readers once marked the Chinese tones with small dots (声点, <em>shōten</em>) at the corners of characters; when the dots were later put on kana, a doubled dot marking voicing became the ancestor of today's dakuten ゛</li><li>Mandarin: <strong>4 tones</strong>, with massive mergers — the weakest preservation</li></ul><p>The ladder is what makes the comparison useful: Cantonese and Vietnamese together preserve most of what Middle Chinese distinguished, and reading a character across all four systems recovers more of the original than any one of them holds alone.</p>"},"sources":["Pulleyblank, E. G. (1984). Middle Chinese: A Study in Historical Phonology. UBC Press.","Karlgren, B. (1954). Compendium of Phonetics in Ancient and Archaic Chinese. Museum of Far Eastern Antiquities.","Martin, S. E. (1966). Lexical Evidence Relating Korean to Japanese. Language 42(2): 185–251.",{"text":"Wikipedia: Sino-Korean vocabulary","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sino-Korean_vocabulary"},{"text":"Wikipedia: Sino-Japanese vocabulary","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sino-Japanese_vocabulary"},"Baxter, W. H. & Sagart, L. (2014). Old Chinese: A New Reconstruction. Oxford University Press.",{"text":"声点 (shōten) — Japanese Wikipedia","url":"https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E5%A3%B0%E7%82%B9"}]},{"id":"simplified-chinese-1956","icon":"✂️","tags":["sinosphere","simplified","history","politics"],"title":{"en":"Chinese Character Simplification and Script Reform","ja":"中国の漢字簡略化と文字改革","ko":"중국의 한자 간소화와 문자 개혁","zh":"汉字简化与文字改革","yue":"漢字簡化同文字改革","vi":"Giản hóa chữ Hán và cải cách chữ viết ở Trung Quốc","th":"อักษรจีนตัวย่อและการปฏิรูปอักษร","id":"Penyederhanaan aksara Tionghoa dan reformasi tulisan","hi":"चीनी अक्षरों का सरलीकरण और लिपि सुधार","de":"Die Vereinfachung der chinesischen Schriftzeichen und die Schriftreform","fr":"La simplification des caractères chinois et la réforme de l'écriture","it":"La semplificazione dei caratteri cinesi e la riforma della scrittura","es":"La simplificación de los caracteres chinos y la reforma de la escritura","pt":"A simplificação dos caracteres chineses e a reforma da escrita","ru":"Упрощение китайских иероглифов и реформа письменности","uk":"Спрощення китайських ієрогліфів і реформа письма","ar":"تبسيط الحروف الصينية وإصلاح الكتابة","he":"פישוט הסימנים הסיניים ורפורמת הכתב","sw":"Urahisishaji wa herufi za Kichina na mageuzi ya maandishi"},"summary":{"en":"The 1956 Chinese script reform introduced simplified forms for many traditional characters as part of a literacy policy. Simplified and traditional character standards remain in use in different Chinese-speaking regions.","ja":"1956年の中国文字改革は、識字政策の一環として多くの繁体字に簡略な字形を導入した。現在も中国語圏の地域によって、簡体字と繁体字それぞれの規範が使われている。","ko":"1956년 중국 문자 개혁은 문해 정책의 일환으로 많은 전통 한자에 간략한 자형을 도입했다. 간체자와 번체자 규범은 지금도 중국어권의 지역에 따라 나뉘어 쓰인다.","zh":"1956年的中国文字改革作为扫盲政策的一部分，为许多繁体字引入了简化字形。简化字和繁体字两套规范至今仍在不同的华语地区使用。","yue":"1956年嘅中國文字改革係掃盲政策嘅一部分，為好多繁體字引入簡化字形。簡體字同繁體字兩套規範到今日仍然喺唔同嘅華語地區使用。","vi":"Cải cách chữ viết Trung Quốc năm 1956 đưa dạng giản hóa vào nhiều chữ phồn thể, như một phần của chính sách xóa mù chữ. Chuẩn chữ giản thể và chữ phồn thể vẫn được dùng ở các vùng nói tiếng Hoa khác nhau.","th":"การปฏิรูปอักษรจีนปี 1956 เอาอักษรจีนตัวย่อมาแทนอักษรดั้งเดิมเพื่อให้คนอ่านออกเขียนได้มากขึ้น ผลคือโลกอักษรจีนแตกเป็นสองระบบการเขียนอย่างถาวร","id":"Reformasi aksara Tiongkok tahun 1956 memperkenalkan bentuk sederhana untuk banyak aksara tradisional sebagai bagian dari kebijakan pemberantasan buta huruf. Aksara sederhana dan tradisional masih sama-sama dipakai di berbagai wilayah berbahasa Tionghoa.","hi":"1956 के चीनी लिपि सुधार ने साक्षरता नीति के तहत बहुत-से पारंपरिक वर्णों के सरलीकृत रूप चलाए। सरलीकृत और पारंपरिक, दोनों मानक आज भी अलग-अलग चीनी-भाषी क्षेत्रों में प्रचलित हैं।","de":"Die chinesische Schriftreform von 1956 führte im Rahmen der Alphabetisierungspolitik vereinfachte Formen für viele traditionelle Schriftzeichen ein. Vereinfachte und traditionelle Standards sind in verschiedenen chinesischsprachigen Regionen bis heute in Gebrauch.","fr":"La réforme de l'écriture chinoise de 1956 a introduit des formes simplifiées pour de nombreux caractères traditionnels, dans le cadre d'une politique d'alphabétisation. Les normes simplifiée et traditionnelle restent en usage dans différentes régions sinophones.","it":"La riforma della scrittura cinese del 1956 introdusse forme semplificate per molti caratteri tradizionali nell'ambito di una politica di alfabetizzazione. Gli standard semplificato e tradizionale restano in uso in diverse regioni di lingua cinese.","es":"La reforma de la escritura china de 1956 introdujo formas simplificadas para muchos caracteres tradicionales como parte de una política de alfabetización. Los estándares simplificado y tradicional siguen en uso en distintas regiones de habla china.","pt":"A reforma da escrita chinesa de 1956 introduziu formas simplificadas para muitos caracteres tradicionais, como parte de uma política de alfabetização. Os padrões simplificado e tradicional continuam em uso em diferentes regiões de língua chinesa.","ru":"Реформа китайской письменности 1956 года ввела упрощённые формы многих традиционных иероглифов в рамках политики повышения грамотности. Упрощённый и традиционный стандарты иероглифов до сих пор применяются в разных китаеязычных регионах.","uk":"Реформа китайського письма 1956 року запровадила спрощені форми багатьох традиційних ієрогліфів у межах політики підвищення грамотності. Спрощений і традиційний стандарти ієрогліфів досі вживаються в різних китайськомовних регіонах.","ar":"أدخل إصلاح الكتابة الصينية عام 1956 أشكالًا مبسّطة لكثير من الحروف التقليدية في إطار سياسة لمحو الأمية. ولا تزال معايير الحروف المبسّطة والتقليدية مستعملة في مناطق مختلفة ناطقة بالصينية.","he":"רפורמת הכתב הסינית של 1956 הכניסה צורות מפושטות לסימנים מסורתיים רבים במסגרת מדיניות לקידום האוריינות. תקני הסימנים המפושטים והמסורתיים נותרו בשימוש באזורים שונים דוברי סינית.","sw":"Mageuzi ya maandishi ya China ya 1956 yalianzisha maumbo yaliyorahisishwa kwa herufi nyingi za jadi, kama sehemu ya sera ya kuongeza ujuzi wa kusoma na kuandika. Viwango vya herufi zilizorahisishwa na za jadi bado vinatumika katika maeneo tofauti yanayozungumza Kichina."},"body":{"en":"<h3>Politics Behind the Brush</h3><p>In 1956, the People's Republic of China published the first <em>Hànzì Jiǎnhuà Fāng'àn</em> (漢字簡化方案), reducing stroke counts in 515 characters and standardising a further 54 simplified components (偏旁). The backdrop was stark. When the People's Republic was founded in 1949, about 80% of the population could not read, and in the countryside the figure was over 95%. Simpler characters were meant to be one faster path to the written word.</p><p>The simplifications were not invented from scratch. Many forms drew on centuries-old <em>cǎoshū</em> (cursive) shortcuts and popular handwritten variants that scribes and merchants had used for generations. Nor was the idea new to the Communists: in 1935 the Nationalist government's Ministry of Education issued a list of 324 simplified characters compiled by the scholar Qian Xuantong, then withdrew it a year later under opposition within its own party. What changed after 1956 was state enforcement: schools, newspapers and government documents switched to the new forms.</p><h3>The 1977 Overreach</h3><p>A second wave in December 1977 proposed 853 more simplifications, some of them drastic: 蛋 \"egg\" was to be written 旦, and 停 \"stop\" as 仃. Unlike the first round, most of whose forms were already in popular use, many were unfamiliar, and by mid-1978 publishers were told to stop using them. The State Council formally rescinded the scheme on 24 June 1986. It showed that simplification soon runs into limits of sound and meaning.</p><h3>The Great Split</h3><p>Taiwan, Hong Kong and Macau never adopted simplified characters. Singapore, on the other hand, ran its own simplification rounds from 1969 and ended up with the mainland set, and Malaysia followed in 1981. Taiwan's ROC government framed <strong>繁體字</strong> (traditional characters) as cultural continuity and legitimate Chinese heritage — a deliberate contrast to the mainland's reforms. In speech, though, the two sides agree. Taiwan's official standard is <strong>國語</strong> (Mandarin, <em>Guóyǔ</em>), not Hokkien or Cantonese: it is the Beijing-based national standard the Republic of China had codified by the 1930s and brought to the island in 1945. The mainland later adopted that same standard under the name <em>Pǔtōnghuà</em>, so Taiwan shares the spoken norm while rejecting the mainland's written reform. Hong Kong still writes formal Chinese in traditional characters, as it always has; its \"biliterate and trilingual\" policy means written Chinese and English, and spoken Cantonese, Putonghua and English.</p><div class='trivia-actions'><button type='button' class='trivia-action' data-action='panto' data-lat='39.91' data-lng='116.39' data-zoom='4'>Beijing — epicentre of the reform</button></div>"},"sources":["DeFrancis, J. (1984). <em>The Chinese Language: Fact and Fantasy</em>. University of Hawaii Press.","Norman, J. (1988). <em>Chinese</em>. Cambridge University Press.",{"text":"State Council of PRC — 1986 Retraction Notice (Wikipedia)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Second_Chinese_Character_Simplification_Scheme"},{"text":"Taiwan's Guoyu policy (Wikipedia)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Taiwanese_Mandarin"},{"text":"Wikipedia: Simplified Chinese characters","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Simplified_Chinese_characters"},{"text":"Wikipedia: Literacy in China","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Literacy_in_China"},{"text":"Wikipedia: Languages of Hong Kong","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Languages_of_Hong_Kong"}]},{"id":"japanese-shinjitai-postwar","icon":"📜","tags":["sinosphere","kanji","japan","history","postwar"],"title":{"en":"Japan's Parallel Simplification: Shinjitai and Postwar Kanji Reform","ja":"日本の並行簡化：新字体と戦後漢字改革","ko":"일본의 병행 간화: 신자체와 전후 한자 개혁","zh":"日本的平行简化：新字体与战后汉字改革","yue":"日本嘅平行簡化：新字體同戰後漢字改革","vi":"Nhật Bản cũng giản hóa: shinjitai và cải cách kanji thời hậu chiến","th":"อักษรย่อของญี่ปุ่น: ชินจิไทและการปฏิรูปคันจิหลังสงคราม","id":"Penyederhanaan versi Jepang: shinjitai dan reformasi kanji pascaperang","hi":"जापान का समानांतर सरलीकरण: शिंजिताई और युद्धोत्तर कांजी सुधार","de":"Japans parallele Vereinfachung: Shinjitai und die Schriftreform der Nachkriegszeit","fr":"La simplification parallèle du Japon : shinjitai et réforme des kanji d'après-guerre","it":"La semplificazione parallela del Giappone: shinjitai e la riforma dei kanji del dopoguerra","es":"La simplificación paralela de Japón: shinjitai y la reforma de los kanji de posguerra","pt":"A simplificação paralela do Japão: shinjitai e a reforma dos kanji do pós-guerra","ru":"Параллельное упрощение Японии: синдзитай и послевоенная реформа кандзи","uk":"Паралельне спрощення Японії: сіндзітай та повоєнна реформа кандзі","ar":"شينجيتاي: تبسيط الكانجي الياباني بعد الحرب","he":"הפישוט המקביל של יפן: שינג'יטאי ורפורמת הקאנג'י שלאחר המלחמה","sw":"Urahisishaji sambamba wa Japani: shinjitai na mageuzi ya kanji baada ya vita"},"summary":{"en":"Japan's postwar kanji reform produced shinjitai. Some forms coincide with simplified Chinese characters, while others follow different simplification choices.","ja":"戦後占領期の漢字改革は新字体を生み出したが、その簡化は中華人民共和国の同時期の簡体字と部分的に重なりながらも興味深い相違点を持つ。","ko":"일본의 전후 한자 개혁은 신자체를 낳았다. 일부 자형은 중국의 간체자와 같지만, 다른 방식으로 간략화한 것도 있다.","zh":"战后占领时期的日本汉字改革产生了新字体，其简化与中国同期的简体字既有交集，又有引人深思的差异。","yue":"戰後佔領時期嘅日本漢字改革產生咗新字體，佢嘅簡化同中國同期嘅簡體字有重疊，亦有耐人尋味嘅分別。","vi":"Cải cách kanji thời hậu chiến của Nhật Bản tạo ra shinjitai. Một số dạng trùng với chữ giản thể của Trung Quốc, số khác lại theo những lựa chọn giản hóa khác.","th":"การปฏิรูปคันจิหลังสงครามของญี่ปุ่นสร้างชินจิไท ซึ่งเป็นรูปอักษรที่ย่อแล้ว บางตัวตรงกับอักษรตัวย่อของจีนที่เกิดในช่วงเดียวกัน บางตัวก็ต่างกันอย่างน่าสนใจ","id":"Reformasi kanji Jepang pascaperang menghasilkan shinjitai. Sebagian bentuknya sama dengan aksara sederhana Tiongkok, sebagian lagi disederhanakan dengan cara lain.","hi":"जापान के युद्धोत्तर कांजी सुधार से शिंजिताई बने। कुछ रूप सरलीकृत चीनी वर्णों से मेल खाते हैं, जबकि कुछ सरलीकरण के अलग रास्ते पर चलते हैं।","de":"Japans Kanji-Reform der Nachkriegszeit brachte die Shinjitai hervor. Manche Formen stimmen mit vereinfachten chinesischen Schriftzeichen überein, andere folgen einer anderen Vereinfachung.","fr":"La réforme des kanji d'après-guerre au Japon a donné naissance aux shinjitai. Certaines formes coïncident avec les caractères chinois simplifiés, d'autres suivent des choix de simplification différents.","it":"La riforma dei kanji del dopoguerra in Giappone ha prodotto gli shinjitai. Alcune forme coincidono con i caratteri cinesi semplificati, altre seguono scelte di semplificazione diverse.","es":"La reforma de los kanji en el Japón de posguerra produjo los shinjitai. Algunas formas coinciden con los caracteres chinos simplificados, mientras que otras siguen opciones de simplificación distintas.","pt":"A reforma dos kanji no Japão do pós-guerra produziu os shinjitai. Algumas formas coincidem com os caracteres simplificados chineses, enquanto outras seguem escolhas de simplificação diferentes.","ru":"Японская послевоенная реформа кандзи породила синдзитай. Часть этих форм совпадает с упрощёнными китайскими иероглифами, а другие упрощены иначе.","uk":"Японська повоєнна реформа кандзі породила сіндзітай. Частина цих форм збігається зі спрощеними китайськими ієрогліфами, а інші спрощено по-іншому.","ar":"أفرز إصلاح الكانجي الياباني في مرحلة ما بعد الحرب «شينجيتاي». وبعض هذه الأشكال يطابق الحروف الصينية المبسّطة، بينما يتبع بعضها الآخر خيارات تبسيط مختلفة.","he":"רפורמת הקאנג'י של יפן לאחר המלחמה יצרה את השינג'יטאי. חלק מהצורות חופפות לסימנים הסיניים המפושטים, ואחרות נובעות מבחירות פישוט שונות.","sw":"Mageuzi ya kanji ya Japani baada ya vita yalizalisha shinjitai. Baadhi ya maumbo yanafanana na herufi za Kichina zilizorahisishwa, huku mengine yakifuata chaguo tofauti za urahisishaji."},"body":{"en":"<h3>Occupation-Era Urgency</h3><p>While China's simplification is better known, Japan got there first, and independently: its official simplified forms of 1949 came seven years before China's 1956 scheme. The 1946 <em>Tōyō Kanji</em> (当用漢字) list, drawn up by Japan's own National Language Council from proposals going back to the 1920s and issued under the US occupation, designated 1,850 characters for everyday use and already printed 131 of them in simplified form. The 1949 character-form table (当用漢字字体表) extended these <strong>shinjitai</strong> (新字体, 'new character forms') to about 500 characters. The pressure in the background was even more radical: the 1946 US Education Mission recommended rōmaji. But a nationwide literacy survey in 1948 found that almost all adults could read and write at least some kanji, and the push for rōmaji lost momentum.</p><h3>Three Rounds of Standardisation</h3><p>The system evolved in three stages:</p><ul><li><strong>1946</strong>: Tōyō Kanji — a restrictive list for official documents and the press: if a word's kanji was not on it, writers were told to choose another word or write it in kana.</li><li><strong>1981</strong>: 常用漢字 (<em>Jōyō Kanji</em>) — 1,945 characters, which superseded Tōyō and became the reference standard for schools and media.</li><li><strong>2010</strong>: 改定常用漢字 — 196 characters added and 5 dropped, for 2,136. The additions included everyday characters such as 俺 and 誰, and for the first time every character in the names of Japan's prefectures (岡, 阪, 熊, 鹿, 梨 and others).</li></ul><h3>Same Goal, Different Scissors</h3><p>Japan and China often simplified the same character differently:</p><ul><li><strong>廣 → 広</strong> (Japan) vs <strong>广</strong> (China) — Japan kept more structure.</li><li><strong>鐵 → 鉄</strong> (Japan) vs <strong>铁</strong> (China) — both replaced the complicated right-hand side with 失; the only difference is that China also shortened the metal radical 金 to 钅.</li><li><strong>體 → 体</strong>: here both converged — same simplified form.</li></ul><p>Some forms Japan simplified, China left alone, and vice versa — producing three-way divergences between Traditional, Japanese, and Simplified Chinese.</p><h3>Kyūjitai and the Cultural Argument</h3><p>Conservative scholars argued that <em>kyūjitai</em> (旧字体, 'old forms') encode morphological meaning — that 鐵 carries its history in its shape, while 鉄 is a mere shorthand. It is also an unlucky one, since it can be read as 金を失う, \"lose money\": some Japanese steelmakers and railways still avoid it in their names and logos, writing 鐵 or the variant 鉃 instead. The debate mirrors the Traditional/Simplified split in Chinese, but with an added layer: Japanese had already absorbed kanji into a mixed script (kanji + kana) in which kana carry the grammatical endings, so arguably less rides on the exact shape of each character than in all-character Chinese text.</p><div class='trivia-actions'><button type='button' class='trivia-action' data-action='focus' data-code='ja_kun'>Show Japanese map layer</button><button type='button' class='trivia-action' data-action='panto' data-lat='35.68' data-lng='139.69' data-zoom='5'>Pan to Tokyo</button></div>"},"sources":["Seeley, C. (1991). <em>A History of Writing in Japan</em>. E.J. Brill.","Unger, J.M. (1996). <em>Literacy and Script Reform in Occupation Japan</em>. Oxford University Press.",{"text":"Jōyō kanji — Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/J%C5%8Dy%C5%8D_kanji"},{"text":"Shinjitai — Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shinjitai"},{"text":"当用漢字 — Japanese Wikipedia","url":"https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E5%BD%93%E7%94%A8%E6%BC%A2%E5%AD%97"}]},{"id":"ducai-yuan-zhongyuan-yinyun","icon":"🎭","tags":["mandarin","history","yuan","rhyme-book"],"title":{"en":"Zhōngyuán Yīnyùn: Yuan-Era Opera and Early Mandarin Phonology","ja":"中原音韻：元代の戯曲と早期官話の音韻","ko":"중원음운: 원나라 잡극과 초기 관화 음운","zh":"《中原音韵》：元代戏曲与早期官话音系","yue":"《中原音韻》：元代雜劇同早期官話音系","vi":"Trung Nguyên Âm Vận: kịch thời Nguyên và âm vị quan thoại sơ kỳ","th":"จงหยวนอินยุ่น: ละครยุคหยวนกับระบบเสียงจีนกลางยุคต้น","id":"Zhōngyuán Yīnyùn: opera zaman Yuan dan bunyi Mandarin Awal","hi":"झोंगयुआन यिनयुन: युआन काल का ओपेरा और आरंभिक मंदारिन ध्वनि-व्यवस्था","de":"Zhōngyuán Yīnyùn: Die Oper der Yuan-Zeit und die frühe Mandarin-Phonologie","fr":"Zhōngyuán Yīnyùn : l'opéra Yuan et la phonologie des débuts du mandarin","it":"Zhōngyuán Yīnyùn: l'opera Yuan e la fonologia del primo mandarino","es":"Zhōngyuán Yīnyùn: el teatro Yuan y la fonología del mandarín temprano","pt":"Zhōngyuán Yīnyùn: o teatro Yuan e a fonologia do mandarim antigo","ru":"Чжунъюань иньюнь: юаньская опера и ранняя фонология мандарина","uk":"Чжунюань іньюнь: юаньська опера й рання мандаринська фонологія","ar":"تشونغ يوان ين يون: أوبرا عصر يوان والنظام الصوتي للماندرين المبكرة","he":"ג'ונגיואן יניון: אופרה מתקופת יואן והפונולוגיה של המנדרינית המוקדמת","sw":"Zhōngyuán Yīnyùn: opera ya Yuan na fonolojia ya awali ya Kichina cha Mandarin"},"summary":{"en":"Zhou Deqing's 1324 rhyme book for Yuan opera arias is the first full record of a northern Chinese sound system with Mandarin's four tones, with the old entering tone (入聲) folded into the other three, at least in song.","ja":"周徳清が1324年に元曲（雜劇）の歌のために編んだ韻書。官話の四声を持つ北方中国語の音体系を初めて完全に記録したもので、古い入声は、少なくとも歌の上ではほかの三声に組み込まれている。","ko":"주덕청이 1324년 원나라 잡극의 노랫말을 위해 펴낸 운서. 관화의 4성을 갖춘 북방 음운 체계를 처음으로 온전히 기록한 책으로, 옛 입성(入聲)은 적어도 노래에서는 나머지 세 성조에 흡수되어 있다.","zh":"周德清1324年为元曲编纂的韵书，是第一份完整记录北方汉语语音系统的文献：它已具备普通话的四声，古入声则被并入了其余三声——至少在唱曲中是如此。","yue":"周德清1324年為元曲編嘅韻書，係第一份完整記錄北方漢語音系嘅文獻：已經有官話嘅四聲，而舊嘅入聲就併入咗其餘三聲——起碼喺唱曲入面係噉。","vi":"Vận thư do Chu Đức Thanh soạn năm 1324 cho kịch Nguyên — bản ghi đầy đủ đầu tiên về một hệ thống âm phương Bắc với bốn thanh của quan thoại, trong đó thanh nhập cũ đã được dồn vào ba thanh kia, ít nhất là trong ca hát.","th":"หนังสือสัมผัสปี 1324 ของโจวเต๋อชิงสำหรับเพลงในละครหยวน คือบันทึกฉบับสมบูรณ์ชิ้นแรกของระบบเสียงจีนตอนเหนือที่มีสี่วรรณยุกต์แบบจีนกลาง โดยเสียงเข้า (入聲) เดิมถูกรวมเข้ากับอีกสามเสียง อย่างน้อยก็ในบทเพลง","id":"Buku rima karya Zhou Deqing tahun 1324 untuk aria opera Yuan adalah catatan lengkap pertama tentang sistem bunyi Tionghoa utara yang memiliki empat nada Mandarin, dengan nada masuk (入聲) lama sudah dilebur ke tiga nada lainnya, setidaknya dalam nyanyian.","hi":"युआन ओपेरा के गीतों के लिए ज़ोऊ देछिंग की 1324 की तुक-पुस्तिका मंदारिन के चार स्वराघातों वाली किसी उत्तरी चीनी ध्वनि-व्यवस्था का पहला पूरा अभिलेख है — जिसमें पुराना प्रवेश-स्वर (入聲) बाक़ी तीन में समा चुका है, कम-से-कम गीतों में।","de":"Zhou Deqings Reimbuch von 1324 für Yuan-Opernarien ist die erste vollständige Aufzeichnung eines nordchinesischen Lautsystems mit den vier Tönen des Mandarin, in dem der alte eingehende Ton (入聲) in den übrigen drei aufgegangen ist, zumindest im Gesang.","fr":"Le manuel de rimes de Zhou Deqing (1324) pour les airs d'opéra Yuan est le premier relevé complet d'un système phonique du chinois du Nord doté des quatre tons du mandarin, l'ancien ton entrant (入聲) étant réparti entre les trois autres, du moins dans le chant.","it":"Il manuale di rime di Zhou Deqing del 1324 per le arie dell'opera Yuan è la prima documentazione completa di un sistema fonetico del cinese settentrionale con i quattro toni del mandarino, in cui l'antico tono entrante (入聲) è ripartito fra gli altri tre, almeno nel canto.","es":"El libro de rimas de Zhou Deqing (1324) para las arias del teatro Yuan es el primer registro completo de un sistema fonético del chino del norte con los cuatro tonos del mandarín, con el antiguo tono entrante (入聲) repartido entre los otros tres, al menos en el canto.","pt":"O livro de rimas de Zhou Deqing (1324) para as árias do teatro Yuan é o primeiro registro completo de um sistema sonoro do chinês do Norte com os quatro tons do mandarim, com o antigo tom entrante (入聲) incorporado aos outros três — pelo menos no canto.","ru":"Книга рифм Чжоу Дэцина 1324 года для арий юаньской оперы — первая полная запись северокитайской звуковой системы с четырьмя тонами мандарина, где старый входящий тон (入聲) растворён в трёх остальных, — по крайней мере, в пении.","uk":"Книга рим Чжоу Децина 1324 року для арій юаньської опери — перший повний запис північнокитайської звукової системи з чотирма мандаринськими тонами, у якій давній вхідний тон (入聲) розчинився в трьох інших — принаймні в співі.","ar":"كتاب القوافي الذي ألفه تشو دي تشينغ عام 1324 لأغاني أوبرا يوان هو أول سجل كامل لنظام صوتي صيني شمالي بأنغام الماندرين الأربع، وقد ذابت فيه النغمة الداخلة (入聲) القديمة في الأنغام الثلاث الأخرى، في الغناء على الأقل.","he":"ספר החרוזים של ג'ואו דה-צ'ינג מ-1324, שנכתב בשביל אריות של אופרת יואן, הוא התיעוד המלא הראשון של מערכת הגאים סינית צפונית עם ארבעת הטונים של המנדרינית, שבה הטון הנכנס (入聲) הישן נבלע בשלושת האחרים, לפחות בשירה.","sw":"Kitabu cha vina cha Zhou Deqing cha mwaka 1324, kilichoandikwa kwa ajili ya nyimbo za opera ya Yuan, ndicho rekodi kamili ya kwanza ya mfumo wa sauti wa Kichina cha kaskazini wenye toni nne za Kichina cha Mandarin, ambamo toni ya kale ya kuingia (入聲) imeyeyushwa ndani ya zile tatu nyingine, angalau katika nyimbo."},"body":{"en":"<h3>The book that documents Mandarin's birth</h3><p>In 1324, in the middle decades of the Yuan dynasty, the song writer and music theorist <strong>Zhou Deqing (周德清)</strong> completed <em>Zhōngyuán Yīnyùn</em> (中原音韻, 'Sounds and Rhymes of the Central Plain'). Its stated purpose was practical: help composers of 雜劇 (zájù, the wildly popular Yuan opera form) match rhymes that would actually sound rhyming when sung in northern speech. His manifesto fits in one line: \"To write songs, you must correct your speech; to correct your speech, you must follow the sounds of the Central Plain.\" The book, printed in 1341, sorts 5,866 characters into just 19 rhyme groups, from 東鍾 to 廉纖, with no reverse-spellings at all: each group of homophones is simply listed together.</p><p>For historical linguists, however, this is the document that nails down the transition from <strong>Middle Chinese</strong> (隋唐音, c. 600 CE) to early modern Mandarin.</p><div class=\"trivia-actions\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"setchar\" data-char=\"一\">📍 See 一 across dialects</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"focus\" data-code=\"zh_yuan\">🎭 Focus on Yuan Mandarin</button></div><h3>What it proves</h3><ul><li><strong>The entering tone (入聲) was gone</strong> in northern speech. In the Tang/Song system the fourth tone, 入, was exactly the class of syllables ending in a stop (-p, -t, -k); <em>Zhōngyuán Yīnyùn</em> lists them under the other three tones: mostly yángpíng after an old fully voiced initial, qù after a nasal or l-, and shǎng after a voiceless initial. The pattern is closer to today's Jiao-Liao Mandarin, spoken on the Shandong and Liaodong peninsulas, than to Beijing, where the voiceless group was scattered among all four tones. Most scholars take this as proof the entering tone was gone, but Zhou himself wrote that he spread these syllables over the other tones \"to widen the rhymes, for writing songs\", and that \"in breathing and speech there is still a distinction of the entering tone\"; Lu Zhiwei and others conclude it had not yet disappeared.</li><li><strong>陰平 and 陽平 had split.</strong> The single 平 (level) category of Middle Chinese had divided based on the voicing of the original initial — yielding what we now call <em>tone 1</em> and <em>tone 2</em>.</li><li><strong>Voiced obstruents had devoiced.</strong> The Middle Chinese 全濁 (fully voiced) series — b, d, g, dz, dʒ — was no longer phonemic, the same merger that distinguishes Mandarin from Wu today.</li></ul><h3>Mandarin from the stage</h3><p>It is striking that the key document of early Mandarin is a <em>songwriter's manual</em>, not a court dictionary. Zhou quotes, with approval, people who held that the Central Plain pronunciation was already a common standard, used by everyone from officials discussing government and teachers at the state academy down to the magistrates hearing cases in court. <em>Zhōngyuán Yīnyùn</em> codifies that register. The 廣韻 (1008) preserved Middle Chinese as a literary ideal; <em>Zhōngyuán Yīnyùn</em> shows what people were actually singing on stage three centuries later.</p>"},"sources":["Zhou Deqing (1324). 中原音韻 [Zhōngyuán Yīnyùn].","Pulleyblank, E. G. (1991). Lexicon of Reconstructed Pronunciation in Early Middle Chinese, Late Middle Chinese, and Early Mandarin.","Norman, Jerry (1988). Chinese. Cambridge Language Surveys. Cambridge University Press.",{"text":"Wikipedia: Zhongyuan Yinyun","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zhongyuan_Yinyun"}]},{"id":"phags-pa-as-hangul-origin","icon":"ꡛ","tags":["hangul","phagspa","controversy","korea"],"title":{"en":"Did Phags-pa Script Influence Hangul?","ja":"ハングルは八思巴字の影響を受けたのか","ko":"한글은 파스파 문자의 영향을 받았을까?","zh":"八思巴字影响过谚文吗？","yue":"八思巴字有冇影響過諺文？","vi":"Chữ Phags-pa có ảnh hưởng đến Hangul không?","th":"อักษรพักปามีอิทธิพลต่อฮันกึลหรือไม่?","id":"Apakah Aksara Phags-pa Memengaruhi Hangul?","hi":"क्या फग्स-पा लिपि ने हांगुल को प्रभावित किया?","de":"Beeinflusste die Phags-pa-Schrift das Hangul?","fr":"L'écriture phags-pa a-t-elle influencé le Hangul ?","it":"La scrittura phags-pa ha influenzato l'hangul?","es":"¿Influyó la escritura phags-pa en el Hangul?","pt":"O phags-pa influenciou o Hangul?","ru":"Повлияло ли письмо Пагспа на хангыль?","uk":"Чи вплинуло письмо Паґспа на хангиль?","ar":"هل أثّرت كتابة باغسبا في الهانغول؟","he":"האם כתב פאגס-פא השפיע על ההאנגול?","sw":"Je, herufi za Phags-pa ziliathiri Hangul?"},"summary":{"en":"In 1966 the American Koreanist Gari Ledyard argued that a handful of Hangul's basic letter shapes were borrowed from the Yuan-era Phags-pa script. Most Korean scholars reject the idea; some Western scholars find it possible or even likely. The debate is still open.","ja":"1966年、米国の朝鮮学者ゲイリー・レッドヤードは、ハングルの基本字形のいくつかは元代の八思巴字から借りたものだと主張した。韓国の研究者の多くはこれを退けるが、欧米には「あり得る」、さらには「おそらくそうだ」とみる研究者もいる。論争は今も決着していない。","ko":"1966년 미국의 한국학자 게리 레드야드는 한글 기본 자형 몇 개를 원나라 파스파 문자에서 빌렸다고 주장했다. 한국 학자 대다수는 이를 받아들이지 않지만, 서양 학자 중에는 가능하다거나 오히려 그럴듯하다고 보는 이도 있다. 논쟁은 지금도 진행 중.","zh":"1966年，美国韩国学学者加里·莱德亚德主张，谚文有几个基本字母的字形借自元代的八思巴字。多数韩国学者不接受此说；一些西方学者则认为有可能，甚至很可能。争论至今未休。","yue":"1966年，美國韓國學學者加里·萊德雅德話，諺文有幾個基本字母嘅字形係借自元代八思巴字。大多數韓國學者反對；有啲西方學者就認為有可能、甚至幾大機會。爭論到而家都未停。","vi":"Năm 1966, nhà Hàn Quốc học người Mỹ Gari Ledyard cho rằng một vài hình dạng chữ cái cơ bản của Hangul đã được vay mượn từ chữ Phags-pa thời Nguyên. Phần lớn học giả Hàn Quốc bác bỏ ý tưởng này; một số học giả phương Tây lại thấy nó có thể đúng, thậm chí rất có khả năng. Tranh cãi đến nay vẫn chưa ngã ngũ.","th":"ในปี 1966 นักเกาหลีศึกษาชาวอเมริกัน แกรี เลดยาร์ด เสนอว่ารูปอักษรพื้นฐานของฮันกึลจำนวนหนึ่งยืมมาจากอักษรพักปาสมัยหยวน นักวิชาการเกาหลีส่วนใหญ่ปฏิเสธแนวคิดนี้ ขณะที่นักวิชาการตะวันตกบางคนเห็นว่าเป็นไปได้หรือถึงขั้นน่าจะเป็นจริง การโต้แย้งยังไม่จบ","id":"Pada 1966 sarjana studi Korea asal Amerika, Gari Ledyard, berargumen bahwa segelintir bentuk huruf dasar Hangul dipinjam dari aksara Phags-pa era Yuan. Kebanyakan sarjana Korea menolak gagasan itu, sedangkan sebagian sarjana Barat menganggapnya mungkin, bahkan cukup mungkin. Perdebatannya belum selesai.","hi":"1966 में अमेरिकी कोरियाविद् गैरी लेड्यार्ड ने तर्क दिया कि हांगुल के कुछ मूल अक्षर-आकार युआन-कालीन फग्स-पा लिपि से उधार लिए गए थे। ज़्यादातर कोरियाई विद्वान इस विचार को ख़ारिज करते हैं; कुछ पश्चिमी विद्वान इसे मुमकिन, बल्कि काफ़ी हद तक सही मानते हैं। बहस अब भी खुली है।","de":"1966 argumentierte der amerikanische Koreanist Gari Ledyard, eine Handvoll Grundbuchstaben des Hangul sei der Yuan-zeitlichen Phags-pa-Schrift entlehnt. Die meisten koreanischen Forscher lehnen das ab; manche westlichen Forscher halten es für möglich oder sogar wahrscheinlich. Die Debatte ist offen.","fr":"En 1966, le coréaniste américain Gari Ledyard a soutenu qu'une poignée de formes de lettres de base du Hangul étaient empruntées à l'écriture phags-pa de l'époque Yuan. La plupart des savants coréens rejettent l'idée ; certains chercheurs occidentaux la jugent possible, voire probable. Le débat reste ouvert.","it":"Nel 1966 il coreanista americano Gari Ledyard sostenne che una manciata delle forme di base delle lettere dell'Hangul fosse presa in prestito dalla scrittura phags-pa dell'epoca Yuan. La maggior parte degli studiosi coreani respinge l'idea; alcuni studiosi occidentali la trovano possibile o perfino probabile. Il dibattito è ancora aperto.","es":"En 1966 el coreanista estadounidense Gari Ledyard sostuvo que un puñado de las formas básicas de las letras del Hangul se tomaron de la escritura phags-pa de la era Yuan. La mayoría de los académicos coreanos rechaza la idea; algunos estudiosos occidentales la consideran posible o incluso probable. El debate sigue abierto.","pt":"Em 1966 o coreanista americano Gari Ledyard sustentou que um punhado das formas básicas das letras do Hangul foi tomado de empréstimo da escrita phags-pa da era Yuan. A maioria dos estudiosos coreanos rejeita a ideia; alguns estudiosos ocidentais a consideram possível ou até provável. O debate continua em aberto.","ru":"В 1966 году американский кореевед Гэри Ледьярд утверждал, что несколько базовых букв хангыля по форме заимствованы из юаньского письма Пагспа. Большинство корейских учёных эту идею отвергают; некоторые западные учёные считают её возможной или даже вероятной. Споры продолжаются.","uk":"У 1966 році американський кореєзнавець Ґері Ледьярд стверджував, що кілька базових форм літер хангиля запозичено з юаньського письма Паґспа. Більшість корейських учених цю ідею відкидають; дехто із західних науковців вважає її можливою чи навіть імовірною. Суперечка триває.","ar":"في عام 1966 جادل الباحث الأمريكي في الدراسات الكورية غاري ليديارد بأن حفنة من أشكال الحروف الأساسية في الهانغول استُعيرت من كتابة باغسبا في عصر يوان. ويرفض معظم العلماء الكوريين الفكرة، بينما يراها بعض الباحثين الغربيين ممكنة بل مرجّحة. النقاش لا يزال قائماً.","he":"ב-1966 טען חוקר קוריאה האמריקני גארי לדיארד שקומץ מצורות האותיות הבסיסיות של ההאנגול נשאלו מכתב פאגס-פא של תקופת יואן. רוב החוקרים הקוריאנים דוחים את הרעיון, ואילו כמה חוקרים מערביים רואים אותו אפשרי ואף סביר. הוויכוח עדיין פתוח.","sw":"Mwaka 1966 mtaalamu wa Marekani wa masomo ya Korea Gari Ledyard alidai kwamba maumbo machache ya msingi ya herufi za Hangul yalikopwa kutoka herufi za Phags-pa za enzi ya Yuan. Wanazuoni wengi wa Kikorea wanalikataa wazo hilo; baadhi ya wasomi wa Magharibi wanaliona linawezekana au hata lina uwezekano mkubwa. Mjadala bado uko wazi."},"body":{"en":"<h3>The provocation</h3><p>In his 1966 doctoral dissertation, American Korean-studies scholar <strong>Gari Ledyard</strong> argued that King Sejong's <em>Hunminjeongeum</em> (訓民正音, 1443) owed something to a foreign model. Building on a 1957 paper by the Canadian linguist E. R. Hope, he proposed that five basic consonant shapes were adapted from the <strong>Phags-pa script</strong>, the Yuan empire's alphabet designed in 1269 by the Tibetan monk Drogön Chögyal Phagpa. His clue was in the sources themselves: the 1443 court record says the new letters \"imitate the Old Seal\" (字倣古篆). Ledyard read \"seal\" as a veiled reference to Phags-pa, since \"seal\" could also describe a Mongol script style.</p><div class=\"trivia-actions\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"focus\" data-code=\"zh_phagspa\">ꡛ Phags-pa on the map</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"focus\" data-code=\"ko_mid\">📜 Middle Korean</button></div><h3>The evidence</h3><p>Ledyard pointed to visual parallels in five letters: ㄱ, ㄷ, ㄹ, ㅂ, ㅅ, each resembling a corresponding Phags-pa consonant (ꡂ, ꡊ, ꡙ, ꡎ, ꡛ): ㄱ is ꡂ <em>g</em> with its lower box removed, and ㅂ is ꡎ <em>b</em> turned and simplified. From one such model letter per sound class, he argued, the other consonants were built by adding or removing strokes. He noted that Phags-pa was no dead letter in Korea: Goryeo had been under Mongol rule when the script was new, and Joseon still ran official courses in it — instruction was in decline by 1423, but had not stopped. By 1443 the Yuan had been gone for 75 years, but Phags-pa forms survived in the books the Korean court kept, including Sino-Mongol rhyme material of the <em>Menggu Ziyun</em> type that Sejong's philologists worked from.</p><h3>The rebuttal</h3><p>Critics, including the Korean linguists <strong>Sang-Oak Lee</strong> and <strong>Young-Key Kim-Renaud</strong>, replied that the correspondences are too few to be systematic, that letters this simple can be matched to almost any script, and that the <em>Hunminjeongeum Haerye</em> (1446) explicitly describes Hangul consonant shapes as iconic representations of the articulatory organs: ㄱ as the back of the tongue raised, ㄴ as the tongue touching the alveolar ridge, ㅁ as the mouth, etc. No other script of the time grounds its letter shapes in the anatomy of speech like this, they argue, so why would Sejong's scholars invent such an elaborate cover story? Supporters reply that the two need not conflict: borrowing a few outlines would be a small thing beside the featural system built on top of them.</p><h3>No settled verdict</h3><p>Western scholarship is split rather than settled. <strong>Peter T. Daniels</strong> wrote that Ledyard's five letters \"are almost certainly based on the corresponding ʼPhags-pa letters\"; <strong>Jae Jung Song</strong> puts the influence \"within the realm of possibilities\"; <strong>Richard Sproat</strong> notes that influence cannot be ruled out but there is no direct evidence for it; <strong>Geoffrey Sampson</strong> is sceptical of any significant influence; and <strong>Ross King</strong> reported in 1987 that the senior Koreanists Edward Wagner and Samuel Martin did not accept it. What is not disputed is that the theoretical framework — the articulatory phonetics — is Sejong's own. So the argument is really about five outlines, not about who invented the system: even on Ledyard's reading, the idea of letters that show how sounds are made, and of building them up stroke by stroke, belongs to Sejong's court.</p>"},"sources":["Ledyard, Gari K. (1966). The Korean Language Reform of 1446: The Origin, Background, and Early History of the Korean Alphabet. PhD diss., UC Berkeley.","King, Ross (1996). 'Korean writing.' In The World's Writing Systems. Oxford UP.","Sampson, Geoffrey (2015). Writing Systems (2nd ed.). Equinox.","Lee, Ki-Moon & Ramsey, S. Robert (2011). A History of the Korean Language. Cambridge University Press.",{"text":"Wikipedia: ʼPhags-pa inspiration for Hangul hypothesis (with its cited evaluations by Daniels, Song, Sproat, Sampson, Kim-Renaud, Lee)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/%CA%BCPhags-pa_inspiration_for_Hangul_hypothesis"}]},{"id":"manchu-script-origins","icon":"📜","tags":["manchu","script","mongolia","tungusic","sinosphere"],"title":{"en":"Manchu Script and Its Aramaic-to-Uyghur Lineage","ja":"満洲文字：アラム文字からウイグル文字へ続く系譜","ko":"만주 문자: 아람 문자에서 위구르 문자로 이어지는 계보","zh":"满文：从阿拉米文到回鹘文的传承","yue":"滿文：由阿拉米文到回鶻文嘅傳承","vi":"Chữ Mãn và dòng dõi từ chữ Aramaic đến chữ Duy Ngô Nhĩ","th":"อักษรแมนจูและสายสืบทอดจากอาราเมอิกถึงอุยกูร์","id":"Aksara Manchu dan Garis Keturunannya dari Aram hingga Uyghur","hi":"मांचू लिपि और उसकी अरामी से उइगुर तक की वंश-परंपरा","de":"Mandschurische Schrift und ihre Abstammungslinie vom Aramäischen bis zum Uigurischen","fr":"L'écriture mandchoue et sa lignée, de l'araméen à l'ouïghour","it":"La scrittura manciù e la sua discendenza dall'aramaico all'uiguro","es":"La escritura manchú y su linaje del arameo al uigur","pt":"A escrita manchu e sua linhagem do aramaico ao uigur","ru":"Маньчжурское письмо и его родословная от арамейского до уйгурского","uk":"Маньчжурське письмо та його родовід від арамейського до уйгурського","ar":"الكتابة المانشورية ونسبها من الآرامية إلى الأويغورية","he":"כתב המנצ'ו ושושלתו מהארמית ועד האויגורית","sw":"Hati ya Manchu na ukoo wake kutoka Kiaramu hadi Kiuygur"},"summary":{"en":"The Manchu script used by the Qing emperors was not invented from scratch — it arrived via a chain: Aramaic → Sogdian → Old Uyghur → Mongolian → Manchu, each link adding a new empire and a new direction of travel.","ja":"清朝皇帝が使った満洲文字は一から発明されたものではない。アラム文字→ソグド文字→古ウイグル文字→モンゴル文字→満洲文字という連鎖を経て届いた。その一段ごとに、新たな帝国と新たな旅路が加わっている。","ko":"청 황제들이 사용한 만주 문자는 처음부터 발명된 것이 아니라 아람 문자 → 소그드 문자 → 고 위구르 문자 → 몽골 문자 → 만주 문자라는 사슬을 통해 전해졌다.","zh":"清朝皇帝所用的满文并非凭空创造，而是经由一条链条传来：阿拉米文→粟特文→古回鹘文→蒙古文→满文，每个环节都增添了一个新帝国与新旅程。","yue":"清朝皇帝用嘅滿文唔係憑空創造嘅，係經過一條鏈：阿拉米文→粟特文→古回鶻文→蒙古文→滿文咁傳落嚟嘅。","vi":"Chữ Mãn mà triều Thanh dùng không phải được sáng tạo từ đầu — nó đến qua chuỗi: Aramaic → Sogdia → Duy Ngô Nhĩ cổ → Mông Cổ → Mãn, mỗi mắt xích thêm một đế chế và một hành trình mới.","th":"อักษรแมนจูของราชวงศ์ชิงไม่ได้คิดขึ้นใหม่ แต่สืบต่อกันมาเป็นทอด ๆ อาราเมอิก → ซ็อกเดียน → อุยกูร์โบราณ → มองโกเลีย → แมนจู","id":"Aksara Manchu yang dipakai Dinasti Qing tidak diciptakan dari nol, melainkan sampai lewat sebuah rantai: Aram → Sogdia → Uyghur Kuno → Mongolia → Manchu, dan setiap mata rantai menambah kekaisaran baru.","hi":"छिंग राजवंश की मांचू लिपि नए सिरे से नहीं गढ़ी गई; वह एक लंबी कड़ी से होकर आई: अरामी → सोग्दियाई → प्राचीन उइगुर → मंगोलियाई → मांचू।","de":"Die Mandschurische Schrift der Qing-Kaiser wurde nicht von Grund auf neu erfunden, sondern kam über eine Kette: Aramäisch → Sogdisch → Altuigurisch → Mongolisch → Mandschurisch. Mit jedem Glied kamen ein neues Reich und eine neue Wanderrichtung hinzu. Mit jedem Glied kamen ein neues Reich und eine neue Wanderrichtung hinzu.","fr":"L'écriture mandchoue des empereurs Qing n'a pas été inventée de toutes pièces : elle est l'aboutissement d'une chaîne, araméen → sogdien → vieux ouïghour → mongol → mandchou, dont chaque maillon ajoute un nouvel empire et une nouvelle direction de voyage.","it":"La scrittura manciù degli imperatori Qing non fu inventata da zero: arrivò attraverso una catena che va dall’aramaico al sogdiano, all’uiguro antico, al mongolo e infine al manciù, e ogni anello aggiunse un nuovo impero e una nuova direzione di viaggio.","es":"La escritura manchú de los emperadores Qing no se inventó desde cero, sino que llegó por una cadena: arameo → sogdiano → uigur antiguo → mongol → manchú. Cada eslabón sumó un nuevo imperio y una nueva dirección de viaje. Cada eslabón sumó un nuevo imperio y una nueva dirección de viaje.","pt":"A escrita manchu dos imperadores Qing não foi inventada do zero — chegou por uma cadeia: aramaico → sogdiano → uigur antigo → mongol → manchu. Cada elo acrescentou um novo império e uma nova direção de viagem. Cada elo acrescentou um novo império e uma nova direção de viagem.","ru":"Маньчжурское письмо, которым пользовались цинские императоры, не было изобретено с нуля — оно пришло по цепочке: арамейский → согдийский → старый уйгурский → монгольский → маньчжурский, и каждое звено добавляло новую империю и новое направление пути.","uk":"Маньчжурське письмо, яким користувалися цинські імператори, не було винайдене з нуля, а пройшло цілий ланцюжок: арамейська → согдійська → давньоуйгурська → монгольська → маньчжурська, і кожна ланка додавала нову імперію та новий відтинок шляху.","ar":"لم تُخترع الكتابة المانشورية التي استخدمها أباطرة تشينغ من العدم، بل وصلت عبر سلسلة: الآرامية → السغدية → الأويغورية القديمة → المغولية → المانشورية.","he":"כתב המנצ'ו ששימש את קיסרי צ'ינג לא הומצא יש מאין, אלא הגיע בשרשרת: ארמית → סוגדית → אויגורית עתיקה → מונגולית → מנצ'ורית. כל חוליה הוסיפה אימפריה חדשה וכיוון מסע חדש. כל חוליה הוסיפה אימפריה חדשה וכיוון מסע חדש.","sw":"Hati ya Manchu iliyotumiwa na wafalme wa Qing haikubuniwa kutoka mwanzo. Ilikuja kupitia mnyororo: Kiaramu → Kisogdian → Kiuygur cha kale → Kimongolia → Kimanchu."},"body":{"en":"<h3>From the Levant to the Forbidden City</h3>\n<p>The <strong>Manchu script</strong> — the vertical columns of loops and dots that fill the bilingual stone stelae of the Qing dynasty (1644–1912) — did not emerge in Manchuria. Its pedigree reaches back more than two millennia, to <strong>Aramaic</strong>, the administrative language of the Achaemenid Persian empire in the 5th century BCE.</p>\n\n<div class=\"trivia-actions\">\n  <button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"focus\" data-code=\"mnc\">📍 Focus Manchu on the map</button>\n  <button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"panto\" data-lat=\"41.8\" data-lng=\"123.4\" data-zoom=\"4\">🗺️ Fly to Manchuria</button>\n</div>\n\n<p>The chain of inheritance runs like this:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Aramaic (5th c. BCE):</strong> An abjad (consonant alphabet) that spread across the Persian empire from Egypt to Central Asia. Its right-to-left cursive form became the template for dozens of later scripts.</li>\n  <li><strong>Sogdian (1st–10th c. CE):</strong> Sogdian merchants carried Aramaic-derived letters along the Silk Road, still writing them in horizontal lines from right to left.</li>\n  <li><strong>Old Uyghur (~8th c. CE):</strong> Turkic Uyghur scribes adapted Sogdian for their own language and turned it 90°, so that it ran <em>top to bottom</em> in columns read from left to right, probably in imitation of Chinese. Every later script in this chain kept that layout. Genghis Khan's Mongols took over the script in the early 13th century, after defeating the Naimans in 1204 and capturing a Uyghur scribe named Tata-tonga who had served them.</li>\n  <li><strong>Mongolian Script (13th c.):</strong> Old Uyghur letters were fitted to Mongolian sounds. The script is still taught to Mongolian pupils in Inner Mongolia, and since 2025 it has also been co-official with Cyrillic in Mongolia itself.</li>\n  <li><strong>Manchu Script (1599 CE):</strong> The Jurchen chieftain <em>Nurhaci</em> ordered his adviser Erdeni and Gagai to adapt the Mongolian alphabet for the Jurchen (later Manchu) language. This first version, the <em>tongki fuka akū hergen</em> or \"script without dots and circles,\" lacked diacritical marks, making some letters ambiguous. In <strong>1632</strong>, Dahai added dots and circles (<em>tongki fuka sindaha hergen</em>), eliminating most ambiguities.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"trivia-actions\">\n  <button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"focus\" data-code=\"sjo\">📍 Focus Sibe (living Manchu) on the map</button>\n  <button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"panto\" data-lat=\"43.8\" data-lng=\"81.0\" data-zoom=\"6\">🗺️ Fly to Qapqal, Xinjiang (Sibe homeland)</button>\n</div>\n\n<p>From the Uyghur rotation onward, the scripts in this chain share one peculiarity: they are written <strong>vertically, in columns running from left to right across the page</strong> — the opposite of Chinese columns, which run right to left. A Manchu manuscript therefore opens from the left, just like a Western book, yet each column reads top-down like classical Chinese.</p>\n\n<h3>The Phags-pa interlude</h3>\n<p>There was one detour. In the 13th century, the Tibetan monk <strong>'Phags-pa</strong> devised an entirely new script for Kublai Khan, intended to write all the languages of the Mongol empire in a single unified system. The <em>Phags-pa script</em> (八思巴文) was used on official seals and inscriptions from roughly 1269 to 1368. In Tibet a seal script known by the same name was still carved on seals in the 20th century, though some scholars regard that later script as a separate Tibetan seal script rather than true Phags-pa. But Phags-pa was a <em>parallel experiment</em>, not a link in the Manchu chain: it influenced the later Korean Hangul discussion and left its mark on East Asian paleography, but Manchu descends from the Mongolian Uyghur-derived script, not from Phags-pa.</p>\n\n<h3>Sibe: the living heir</h3>\n<p>Manchu itself is now functionally extinct as a native tongue, with perhaps a handful of elderly speakers in remote Heilongjiang villages. But the script lives on. In <strong>1764</strong>, the Qing court sent a garrison of Sibe bannermen from Shengjing (today's Shenyang) to the far western frontier at Ili in Xinjiang. Several thousand soldiers and family members made the trek, partly by ox cart, and it took them well over a year. Their descendants, the <strong>Sibe people of Qapqal</strong>, preserved the Manchu script and the Manchu-family language. About 30,000 people still spoke Sibe in 2000. In 1998 the primary schools of Qapqal taught in Chinese, but Sibe lessons were compulsory. The newspaper <em>Cabcal Serkin</em> (Qapqal News), published since 1946 and often described as the only Sibe-language newspaper in the world, is printed in the same vertical script that Erdeni and Gagai assembled in 1599.</p>\n\n<p>Two branches of that vertical line are still alive: the Mongolian script in Inner Mongolia and Mongolia, and the Manchu script among the Sibe of Qapqal. A pupil writing Sibe homework today is using letters whose ancestors were the office script of the Persian empire some 2,500 years ago.</p>"},"sources":["Janhunen, Juha (2003). \"Mongolic Languages.\" Routledge Language Family Series. Routledge.","Norman, Jerry (1978). A Concise Manchu-English Lexicon. University of Washington Press.","Kara, György (2005). Books of the Mongolian Nomads: More than Eight Centuries of Writing Mongolian. Indiana University, Research Institute for Inner Asian Studies.",{"text":"Unicode Consortium: Mongolian block (U+1800–U+18AF)","url":"https://www.unicode.org/charts/PDF/U1800.pdf"},{"text":"Endangered Archives Programme: Sibe/Xibe language materials, EAP264","url":"https://eap.bl.uk/project/EAP264"},"Crossley, Pamela Kyle (1990). Orphan Warriors: Three Manchu Generations and the End of the Qing World. Princeton University Press.",{"text":"Wikipedia: Xibe language","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Xibe_language"},{"text":"Wikipedia: Mongolian script","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mongolian_script"}]},{"id":"old-mongol-uyghur-script","icon":"🐎","tags":["mongolia","script","cyrillic","sinosphere","inner-mongolia","history"],"title":{"en":"Why Mongolians write in two different scripts","ja":"なぜモンゴル人は二つの文字を使うのか","ko":"몽골인이 두 가지 문자를 사용하는 이유","zh":"为什么蒙古族用两种不同的文字","yue":"點解蒙古人用兩種唔同嘅文字","vi":"Tại sao người Mông Cổ viết bằng hai chữ khác nhau","th":"ทำไมชาวมองโกลจึงเขียนด้วยอักษรสองแบบ","id":"Mengapa orang Mongol menulis dalam dua aksara berbeda","hi":"मंगोल लोग दो अलग-अलग लिपियाँ क्यों लिखते हैं","de":"Warum Mongolen zwei verschiedene Schriften verwenden","fr":"Pourquoi les Mongols écrivent avec deux écritures différentes","it":"Perché i Mongoli scrivono in due alfabeti diversi","es":"Por qué los mongoles escriben en dos escrituras diferentes","pt":"Por que os mongóis escrevem em duas escritas diferentes","ru":"Почему монголы пишут двумя разными системами письма","uk":"Чому монголи пишуть двома різними письменами","ar":"لماذا يكتب المغول بنظامَي كتابة مختلفَين","he":"למה המונגולים כותבים בשני כתבים שונים","sw":"Kwa nini Wamongolia wanaandika kwa hati mbili tofauti"},"summary":{"en":"Mongolia uses Cyrillic, while Inner Mongolia (China) uses a vertical script descended from Uyghur letters adopted in the Mongol Empire. Mongolia adopted Cyrillic as its official script in the mid-20th century; a 2020 change to Mongolian-medium schooling in Inner Mongolia prompted widespread protests.","ja":"モンゴル国はキリル文字を使い、中国の内モンゴルはモンゴル帝国時代に採用されたウイグル文字系の縦書き文字を使う。モンゴル国がキリル文字を公式の文字としたのは20世紀半ばのこと。2020年に内モンゴルでモンゴル語による学校教育が変更されると、広範な抗議が起きた。","ko":"몽골은 키릴 문자를, 중국 내몽골은 몽골 제국 시대에 채택된 위구르 계통 세로 문자를 쓴다. 몽골은 20세기 중반에 키릴 문자를 공식 문자로 삼았고, 2020년 내몽골에서 몽골어 교육이 바뀌자 광범위한 시위가 일어났다.","zh":"蒙古国用西里尔字母；中国内蒙古用的竖写文字，源自蒙古帝国时期采用的回鹘文字母。蒙古国在20世纪中叶把西里尔字母定为官方文字；2020年内蒙古的蒙古语授课政策变动，引发了大范围抗议。","yue":"蒙古國用西里爾字母；中國內蒙古用嘅直寫文字，源自蒙古帝國時期採用嘅回鶻文字母。蒙古國喺20世紀中葉將西里爾字母定為官方文字；2020年內蒙古嘅蒙古語授課政策改變，引發咗大範圍抗議。","vi":"Mông Cổ dùng chữ Cyrillic; Nội Mông (Trung Quốc) dùng chữ viết dọc bắt nguồn từ chữ Duy Ngô Nhĩ được tiếp nhận thời Đế quốc Mông Cổ. Mông Cổ lấy Cyrillic làm chữ viết chính thức vào giữa thế kỷ 20; năm 2020, việc thay đổi chính sách dạy học bằng tiếng Mông Cổ ở Nội Mông đã gây ra các cuộc biểu tình lan rộng.","th":"มองโกเลียใช้อักษรซีริลลิก ส่วนมองโกเลียในของจีนใช้อักษรแนวตั้งที่สืบมาจากอักษรอุยกูร์ซึ่งรับมาใช้ในจักรวรรดิมองโกล มองโกเลียรับอักษรซีริลลิกเป็นอักษรทางการในกลางคริสต์ศตวรรษที่ 20 ส่วนในมองโกเลียใน การลดการสอนเป็นภาษามองโกลเมื่อปี 2020 จุดชนวนการประท้วงครั้งใหญ่","id":"Mongolia memakai Kiril, sedangkan Mongolia Dalam (Tiongkok) memakai aksara tegak turunan huruf Uyghur yang diambil pada masa Kekaisaran Mongol. Mongolia menjadikan Kiril aksara resminya pada pertengahan abad ke-20; perubahan pada sekolah berbahasa pengantar Mongol di Mongolia Dalam pada 2020 memicu protes yang meluas.","hi":"मंगोलिया सिरिलिक में लिखता है, और चीन का भीतरी मंगोलिया ऊपर से नीचे लिखी जाने वाली उस लिपि में, जो मंगोल साम्राज्य में अपनाए गए उइगुर अक्षरों से निकली है। मंगोलिया ने बीसवीं सदी के मध्य में सिरिलिक को आधिकारिक लिपि बनाया; 2020 में भीतरी मंगोलिया में मंगोल-माध्यम स्कूली शिक्षा में बदलाव से व्यापक विरोध प्रदर्शन हुए।","de":"Die Mongolei verwendet Kyrillisch, die Innere Mongolei (China) eine vertikale Schrift, die auf im Mongolenreich übernommene uigurische Buchstaben zurückgeht. Die Mongolei führte Kyrillisch Mitte des 20. Jahrhunderts als offizielle Schrift ein; eine Änderung am mongolischsprachigen Unterricht in der Inneren Mongolei löste 2020 breite Proteste aus.","fr":"La Mongolie utilise le cyrillique, tandis que la Mongolie intérieure (Chine) utilise une écriture verticale issue des lettres ouïghoures adoptées dans l'Empire mongol. La Mongolie a fait du cyrillique son écriture officielle au milieu du XXe siècle ; en 2020, un changement dans l'enseignement en mongol en Mongolie intérieure a provoqué de vastes protestations.","it":"La Mongolia usa il cirillico, mentre la Mongolia Interna (Cina) usa una scrittura verticale discesa dalle lettere uigure adottate nell'Impero mongolo. La Mongolia ha adottato il cirillico come scrittura ufficiale a metà del Novecento; nel 2020 un cambiamento nell'istruzione in lingua mongola nella Mongolia Interna ha provocato proteste diffuse.","es":"Mongolia usa el cirílico, mientras que Mongolia Interior (China) usa una escritura vertical que desciende de las letras uigures adoptadas en el Imperio mongol. Mongolia adoptó el cirílico como escritura oficial a mediados del siglo XX; en 2020, un cambio en la enseñanza en mongol en Mongolia Interior provocó protestas generalizadas.","pt":"A Mongólia usa o cirílico, enquanto a Mongólia Interior (China) usa uma escrita vertical derivada das letras uigures adotadas no Império Mongol. A Mongólia adotou o cirílico como escrita oficial em meados do século XX; em 2020, uma mudança no ensino em mongol na Mongólia Interior provocou protestos generalizados.","ru":"В Монголии пишут кириллицей, а во Внутренней Монголии (Китай) — вертикальным письмом, восходящим к уйгурским буквам, принятым в Монгольской империи. Монголия сделала кириллицу официальным письмом в середине XX века; изменения 2020 года в обучении на монгольском языке во Внутренней Монголии вызвали массовые протесты.","uk":"У Монголії пишуть кирилицею, а у Внутрішній Монголії (Китай) — вертикальним письмом, що походить від уйгурських літер, прийнятих у Монгольській імперії. Монголія зробила кирилицю офіційним письмом у середині XX століття; зміни 2020 року в навчанні монгольською у Внутрішній Монголії спричинили масові протести.","ar":"تستخدم منغوليا الكيريلية، بينما تستخدم منغوليا الداخلية (الصين) خطًا رأسيًا منحدرًا من الحروف الأويغورية التي اعتُمدت في الإمبراطورية المغولية. وقد اعتمدت منغوليا الكيريلية خطًا رسميًا في منتصف القرن العشرين، وأثار تغيير طرأ عام 2020 على التعليم بالمغولية في منغوليا الداخلية احتجاجات واسعة.","he":"מונגוליה משתמשת בקירילית, ואילו מונגוליה הפנימית (סין) משתמשת בכתב אנכי שמוצאו באותיות אויגוריות שאומצו באימפריה המונגולית. מונגוליה אימצה את הקירילית ככתב רשמי באמצע המאה ה-20. שינוי שנעשה ב-2020 בלימודים בשפה המונגולית במונגוליה הפנימית עורר מחאות נרחבות.","sw":"Mongolia inatumia Kikrili, huku Mongolia ya Ndani (China) ikitumia hati ya wima inayotokana na herufi za Kiuygur zilizopitishwa katika milki ya Kimongolia. Mongolia ilipitisha Kikrili kama hati rasmi katikati ya karne ya 20; mabadiliko ya mwaka 2020 katika elimu kwa lugha ya Kimongolia huko Mongolia ya Ndani yalisababisha maandamano yaliyoenea."},"body":{"en":"<h3>One people, two scripts</h3>\n<p>Look at any Mongolian sign in Ulaanbaatar and you see <strong>Cyrillic</strong>. Look at any Mongolian sign in Hohhot (Inner Mongolia, China) and you see a flowing vertical script, written in columns from top to bottom, columns running left to right. The two populations share an ancestry, a language family, and an epic — <em>The Secret History of the Mongols</em> — but they write their language in completely different alphabets.</p>\n\n<div class=\"trivia-actions\">\n  <button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"panto\" data-lat=\"47.9\" data-lng=\"106.9\" data-zoom=\"5\">🗺️ Fly to Ulaanbaatar (Cyrillic Mongolia)</button>\n  <button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"panto\" data-lat=\"40.8\" data-lng=\"111.7\" data-zoom=\"5\">🗺️ Fly to Hohhot (Traditional-script Inner Mongolia)</button>\n</div>\n\n<h3>Step 1: Genghis Khan and the Uyghur scribe (1206)</h3>\n<p>In 1204, two years before <strong>Temüjin</strong> was proclaimed <em>Genghis Khan</em> at the great kurultai of 1206, his army crushed the Naiman confederation. Among the captives was a Uyghur scribe of the Naiman khan who had kept his seal and his treasury, <strong>Tata-tonga</strong> (also spelled Tatatunga), brought before the conqueror still carrying his master's seal. The Mongols had no writing of their own; Tata-tonga did. Genghis Khan set him to teach the Old Uyghur script to his sons and the other princes, and in their hands it was bent to fit Mongolian sounds.</p>\n\n<p>The resulting <strong>Mongolian Bichig</strong> (\"Mongolian script\") is written vertically, top-to-bottom, in columns that advance from left to right. That odd combination is an accident of history: the Sogdian script the Uyghurs borrowed ran horizontally from right to left, and Uyghur scribes turned it 90 degrees anticlockwise to imitate Chinese columns without changing the letters' order. Its descendants (Mongolian, Oirat Clear Script, Manchu) kept this rare left-to-right column order, and the Phags-pa script later copied it. In Inner Mongolia the script is still the official written form of Mongolian, taught in Mongolian-medium schools and shown beside Chinese characters on public signs.</p>\n\n<div class=\"trivia-actions\">\n  <button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"focus\" data-code=\"mnc\">📍 Manchu on the map</button>\n  <button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"focus\" data-code=\"pmgl\">📍 Proto-Mongolic reconstruction</button>\n</div>\n\n<h3>Step 2: The Phags-pa interlude (1269–1368)</h3>\n<p>The Mongol empire's diversity made Tatatunga's Uyghur-derived script inadequate for writing Chinese, Tibetan, Sanskrit or Persian. In 1269, Kublai Khan commissioned the Tibetan monk <strong>'Phags-pa</strong> to design a universal script. The resulting <em>Phags-pa script</em> (八思巴文), based on Tibetan letters but written in vertical columns, was meant to spell every language of the empire, Chinese included, letter by letter. It was used on imperial seals and edicts throughout the Yuan dynasty, and some scholars — Gari Ledyard above all — have argued that it influenced the design of the Korean Hangul (1443), though the point remains contested. When the Ming dynasty expelled the Mongols in 1368, Phags-pa fell out of imperial use, but Mongolian Bichig continued without interruption.</p>\n\n<h3>Step 3: Stalin's scissors (1946)</h3>\n<p>By the 20th century, the Mongolian People's Republic (modern Mongolia) was a Soviet satellite, and its script changed with Moscow's line. A first Latin alphabet was adopted in 1931 and quietly dropped by 1933. On 21 February <strong>1941</strong> Mongolia officially switched to a new <strong>Latin</strong> alphabet. Barely a month later, on 25 March <strong>1941</strong>, by which time the Soviet Union itself was moving its minority languages from Latin to Cyrillic, the Latin decision was abolished and <strong>Cyrillic</strong> adopted instead, becoming the formal official script on 1 January <strong>1946</strong>. The ostensible reason was to align Mongolia's literacy with Soviet print infrastructure; the real effect was to cut the generation trained in Cyrillic off from any direct ability to read pre-1946 texts, classical literature, or the stone inscriptions of the Mongolian empires.</p>\n\n<p>Inner Mongolia, meanwhile, remained under the Republic of China and then the People's Republic of China, where the traditional script stayed in continuous use through periods of support and periods of persecution, the worst being the Cultural Revolution. The result is one language community split by a political border into two completely different literacy traditions.</p>\n\n<h3>Step 4: The 2020 protests</h3>\n<p>On 26 August <strong>2020</strong>, days before the school year began, Inner Mongolia's Department of Education announced that in Mongolian-medium schools three subjects would switch to <em>Mandarin Chinese</em> and national Chinese-language textbooks: language and literature and \"morality and law\" from the first grade, and history from the seventh. It was part of a national textbook unification already rolled out elsewhere in China since 2017. The reaction was swift, and for the region rare in its scale: parents boycotted schools, students walked out, and residents across Inner Mongolia held demonstrations. Signs in Traditional Mongolian script were held up by protesters who wrote that they were fighting for the survival of their culture and language.</p>\n\n<div class=\"trivia-actions\">\n  <button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"panto\" data-lat=\"44.0\" data-lng=\"117.0\" data-zoom=\"5\">🗺️ Inner Mongolia — region of the 2020 protests</button>\n</div>\n\n<h3>The revival in Mongolia</h3>\n<p>Since the 1990 democratic revolution, Mongolia has officially committed to reviving Traditional script literacy alongside Cyrillic. Street signs in Ulaanbaatar now sometimes include the vertical Mongolian script. The 2015 Law on the Mongolian Language set a <strong>2025 deadline</strong>, and the date held: since January 2025 state and local government bodies must keep their official correspondence in both Cyrillic and Mongolian Bichig. Cyrillic is not being abolished; the vertical script is being added beside it. Progress is slow: most Mongolians schooled during the Cyrillic decades cannot read the script their ancestors used for seven centuries. But the younger generation is learning — partly from pride, partly because Traditional Mongolian script is the only way to read the words of Genghis Khan as his own scribes wrote them.</p>\n\n<p>So the same vertical letters now carry two different meanings. In Ulaanbaatar they are a heritage being rebuilt by law; in Hohhot, where they never stopped being used, they became a protest banner in 2020.</p>"},"sources":["Bawden, Charles R. (1968). The Modern History of Mongolia. Weidenfeld & Nicolson.","Rachewiltz, Igor de (2004). The Secret History of the Mongols. Brill.","Janhunen, Juha (2003). \"Mongolic Languages.\" Routledge Language Family Series. Routledge.",{"text":"Human Rights Watch: \"China: Mongolian Mother-Tongue Classes Curtailed\" (2020)","url":"https://www.hrw.org/news/2020/09/04/china-mongolian-mother-tongue-classes-curtailed"},{"text":"Wikipedia: Mongolian script — history and modern use","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mongolian_script"},"Svantesson, Jan-Olof et al. (2005). The Phonology of Mongolian. Oxford University Press."]},{"id":"jurchen-script-deciphered","icon":"📜","tags":["jurchen","tungusic","script","jin-dynasty","sinosphere"],"title":{"en":"Jurchen Script: A Partly Deciphered Jin-Dynasty Writing System","ja":"女真文字：部分的に解読された金朝の文字","ko":"여진 문자: 일부만 해독된 금나라의 문자","zh":"女真文字：只解读了一部分的金代文字","yue":"女真文字：只係解讀咗一部分嘅金代文字","vi":"Chữ Nữ Chân: hệ chữ thời nhà Kim mới được giải mã một phần","th":"อักษรจูร์เชน: ระบบอักษรสมัยราชวงศ์จินที่ถอดรหัสได้เพียงบางส่วน","id":"Aksara Jurchen: tulisan Dinasti Jin yang baru terbaca sebagian","hi":"जुरचेन लिपि: जिन राजवंश की आंशिक रूप से पढ़ी गई लेखन-प्रणाली","de":"Die Jurchen-Schrift: ein nur teilweise entziffertes Schriftsystem der Jin-Dynastie","fr":"L'écriture jurchen : un système de la dynastie Jin, déchiffré en partie seulement","it":"La scrittura jurchen: un sistema della dinastia Jin decifrato solo in parte","es":"La escritura yurchen: un sistema de la dinastía Jin descifrado solo en parte","pt":"A escrita jurchen: um sistema da dinastia Jin decifrado só em parte","ru":"Чжурчжэньское письмо: частично расшифрованная письменность династии Цзинь","uk":"Чжурчженське письмо: частково розшифрована писемність династії Цзінь","ar":"خط الجورتشين: نظام كتابة من عهد أسرة جين لم يُفكّ إلا جزئيًا","he":"כתב הג'ורצ'ן: מערכת כתב משושלת ג'ין שפוענחה רק בחלקה","sw":"Maandishi ya Jurchen: mfumo wa nasaba ya Jin uliofumbuliwa kwa sehemu tu"},"summary":{"en":"The Jurchen script was created for the Jin dynasty in the twelfth century. A Ming glossary supports readings for many of its roughly 900 attested characters, but the small surviving corpus leaves the system only partly deciphered.","ja":"女真文字は12世紀に金朝のために作られた。明代の語彙集のおかげで、確認されている約900字の多くに読みの裏づけがあるが、現存資料が少なく、文字体系は部分的にしか解読されていない。","ko":"여진 문자는 12세기 금나라를 위해 만들어졌다. 명대의 어휘집 덕분에 확인된 약 900자 가운데 많은 글자의 음을 뒷받침할 수 있지만, 남은 자료가 적어 문자 체계는 일부만 해독되어 있다.","zh":"女真文字创制于12世纪，供金朝使用。一部明代词书为已知约900字中的许多字提供了读音依据，但传世材料很少，这套文字至今只解读了一部分。","yue":"女真文字喺12世紀為金朝而創製。一部明代詞書為已知大約900字入面好多字提供咗讀音根據，但傳世材料好少，呢套文字到而家只係解讀咗一部分。","vi":"Chữ Nữ Chân được tạo ra cho nhà Kim vào thế kỷ 12. Một bảng từ vựng thời Minh giúp xác định cách đọc của nhiều chữ trong khoảng 900 chữ đã biết, nhưng tư liệu còn lại ít ỏi nên hệ chữ này mới chỉ được giải mã một phần.","th":"อักษรจูร์เชนสร้างขึ้นสำหรับราชวงศ์จินในคริสต์ศตวรรษที่ 12 ประมวลศัพท์สมัยราชวงศ์หมิงช่วยยืนยันการอ่านอักษรจำนวนมากจากราว 900 ตัวที่พบ แต่หลักฐานที่เหลืออยู่มีน้อย ระบบอักษรจึงถอดรหัสได้เพียงบางส่วน","id":"Aksara Jurchen diciptakan untuk Dinasti Jin pada abad ke-12. Sebuah daftar kata era Ming mendukung pembacaan banyak dari sekitar 900 karakternya yang terdokumentasi, tetapi korpus yang bertahan sangat kecil, sehingga aksara ini baru terurai sebagian.","hi":"जुरचेन लिपि 12वीं सदी में जिन राजवंश के लिए बनाई गई थी। मिंग काल की एक शब्द-सूची के सहारे इसके लगभग 900 प्रमाणित अक्षरों में से बहुत-से पढ़े जा सके हैं, पर बची हुई सामग्री इतनी कम है कि लिपि अब भी आंशिक रूप से ही पढ़ी जा सकी है।","de":"Die Jurchen-Schrift entstand im 12. Jahrhundert für die Jin-Dynastie. Ein Glossar aus der Ming-Zeit stützt die Lesungen vieler der rund 900 bezeugten Zeichen, doch das erhaltene Korpus ist klein, und die Schrift ist bis heute nur teilweise entziffert.","fr":"L'écriture jurchen a été créée au XIIe siècle pour la dynastie Jin. Un lexique des Ming étaye la lecture de nombre de ses quelque 900 caractères attestés, mais le corpus conservé est petit et l'écriture n'est déchiffrée qu'en partie.","it":"La scrittura jurchen fu ideata nel XII secolo per la dinastia Jin. Un glossario di epoca Ming sostiene la lettura di molti dei suoi circa 900 caratteri attestati, ma il corpus superstite è piccolo e il sistema resta decifrato solo in parte.","es":"La escritura yurchen se creó en el siglo XII para la dinastía Jin. Un glosario de época Ming respalda la lectura de muchos de sus cerca de 900 caracteres atestiguados, pero el corpus conservado es pequeño y el sistema sigue descifrado solo en parte.","pt":"A escrita jurchen foi criada para a dinastia Jin no século XII. Um glossário da dinastia Ming sustenta a leitura de muitos dos cerca de 900 caracteres atestados, mas o pequeno corpus remanescente deixa o sistema decifrado apenas em parte.","ru":"Чжурчжэньское письмо было создано для династии Цзинь в XII веке. Словарь эпохи Мин подтверждает чтения многих из примерно 900 засвидетельствованных знаков, но сохранившийся корпус невелик, и письмо расшифровано лишь частично.","uk":"Чжурчженське письмо створили для династії Цзінь у XII столітті. Словник доби Мін підтверджує читання багатьох із приблизно 900 засвідчених знаків, але збережений корпус невеликий, тож письмо розшифроване лише частково.","ar":"ابتُكر خط الجورتشين لأسرة جين في القرن الثاني عشر. ويدعم معجم من عهد مينغ قراءة كثير من رموزه الموثَّقة البالغة نحو 900 رمز، لكن ضآلة ما بقي من نصوصه تُبقيه خطًا لم يُفكّ إلا جزئيًا.","he":"כתב הג'ורצ'ן נוצר עבור שושלת ג'ין במאה ה-12. מילון מתקופת מינג תומך בקריאות של רבים מכ-900 הסימנים המתועדים שלו, אבל מעט הטקסטים ששרדו משאירים את הכתב מפוענח רק בחלקו.","sw":"Maandishi ya Jurchen yaliundwa kwa ajili ya nasaba ya Jin katika karne ya 12. Orodha ya maneno ya enzi ya Ming inaunga mkono usomaji wa herufi nyingi kati ya takriban 900 zilizothibitika, lakini maandishi machache yaliyosalia yanaacha mfumo huo ukiwa umefumbuliwa kwa sehemu tu."},"body":{"en":"<h3>A Script Born from Political Ambition</h3>\n<p>In 1115 the Jurchen chieftain <strong>Wanyan Aguda</strong> founded the Jin dynasty (金朝), and at first his court wrote in Khitan. An empire wanted a script of its own, so he commissioned <strong>Wanyan Xiyin</strong> (完顏希尹), a scholar-official fluent in Khitan, Chinese, and Jurchen, to create one. By 1119–1120 Xiyin had produced the <em>Jurchen large script</em> (女真大字), modelling its visual logic on the Khitan large script but adapting sounds and structures to fit the Tungusic phonology of Jurchen.</p>\n\n<div class=\"trivia-actions\">\n  <button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"panto\" data-lat=\"41.8\" data-lng=\"125.0\" data-zoom=\"5\">📍 Fly to Jurchen homeland (Manchuria)</button>\n</div>\n\n<p>Just nineteen years later, in 1138, a second and simpler system — the <em>Jurchen small script</em> (女真小字) — was promulgated by the <strong>Xizong Emperor</strong>, who is said to have devised it himself; the <em>Jin Shi</em> records its first official use in 1145. Scholars still debate which script appears in which inscription and how the two systems relate.</p>\n\n<h3>~900 Characters, Khitan DNA</h3>\n<p>Only about <strong>900 Jurchen characters</strong> are attested in the entire surviving corpus — Jīn Qǐzōng's standard 1984 dictionary <em>女真文辞典</em> (Nǚzhēnwén cídiǎn) has 890 primary entries, and the Sino-Jurchen glossary alone uses around 720. Some work logographically (one character = one morpheme, as in Chinese), others work phonetically (one character = one syllable sound), and many are borrowed Khitan graphs repurposed for Jurchen phonology. Because Jurchen is an <em>agglutinative</em> Tungusic language — very different from the isolating Chinese — the scribes had to stretch the logographic system in creative ways to mark verb suffixes and case endings.</p>\n\n<h3>The Ming Bilingual Dictionary That Saved the Script</h3>\n<p>When the Jin dynasty fell to the Mongols in 1234, Jurchen writing entered a slow eclipse, and slow is the word: as late as 1413, the Ming eunuch admiral Yishiha had a Jurchen text carved on the stele he raised at Tyr, on the lower Amur. The Manchus, as the Ming-era Jurchens renamed themselves in 1635, later wrote their language in a script adapted from the Mongolian alphabet, with no link to the old Jurchen characters. What saved Jurchen literacy from complete oblivion was a Ming-dynasty document: the <strong>女真譯語</strong> (<em>Nǚzhēn yìyǔ</em>, \"Jurchen Glossary\"), a bilingual word list pairing Jurchen characters with Chinese translations and Chinese-phonetic glosses. Copied, recopied and kept almost by accident, this glossary became the key to Jurchen decipherment.</p>\n\n<h3>Daniel Kane and the 800-Year Puzzle</h3>\n<p>The pioneering modern study was the German sinologist Wilhelm Grube's <em>Die Sprache und Schrift der Jučen</em> (1896), built on the Ming glossary. Scholars such as Kiyose Gisaburō in Japan and Jin Qizong in China carried the work forward, and the Australian linguist <strong>Daniel Kane</strong>'s 1989 monograph <em>The Sino-Jurchen Vocabulary of the Bureau of Interpreters</em> established rigorous phonological reconstructions. By cross-referencing the 女真譯語 with stone inscriptions, bronze seals, and the comparative Tungusic vocabulary of later Manchu and Evenki, Kane and subsequent scholars have established readings for most of the seven-hundred-odd characters actually attested in the glossaries. Beyond that core the script is still only partly decoded: the surviving corpus is tiny — nine known inscriptions and the Ming word lists — and many graphs appear in too few contexts to pin down with certainty.</p>\n\n<div class=\"trivia-actions\">\n  <button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"focus\" data-code=\"juc\">📍 Focus Jurchen on the map</button>\n</div>\n\n<h3>Why It Matters</h3>\n<p>Jurchen sits at the pivot of East Asian history. Its speakers founded a dynasty that ruled northern China for a century, repelled the Song, and shaped the political geography that the Mongols would later inherit. The script has no graphic descendants, but its scribal tradition foreshadowed the Manchu script, which in turn became the official writing of the Qing dynasty (1644–1912) and survives today both ceremonially — on the bilingual signs inside the Forbidden City — and as a living written language in the closely related Sibe script of Qapqal Xibe Autonomous County, Xinjiang. Deciphering Jurchen is more than antiquarianism. It fills a century-long gap in the record of northern China and shows how Tungusic peoples built literate bureaucracies on Chinese models while keeping their own language.</p>\n\n<blockquote>Few writing systems have been asked to do anything as awkward as fitting an agglutinative Tungusic language into Chinese-style graphs.</blockquote>"},"sources":["Kane, Daniel (1989). The Sino-Jurchen Vocabulary of the Bureau of Interpreters. Indiana University Research Institute for Inner Asian Studies.","Jin Qizong 金启孮 (1984). 女真文辞典 [Jurchen Dictionary]. Wenwu Chubanshe, Beijing.","Janhunen, Juha (2012). 'Khitan: Understanding the language behind the scripts.' SCRIPTA 4: 107–132.","Kiyose, Gisaburo N. (1977). A Study of the Jurchen Language and Script. Kyoto: Horitsubunka-sha.",{"text":"Glottolog: Jurchen (jurc1239)","url":"https://glottolog.org/resource/languoid/id/jurc1239"},{"text":"Wikipedia: Jurchen script","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jurchen_script"},{"text":"Grube, Wilhelm (1896). Die Sprache und Schrift der Jučen. Leipzig: Otto Harrassowitz.","url":"https://archive.org/details/bub_gb_4WouAAAAYAAJ"}]},{"id":"yale-mc-tonal-categories","icon":"🎵","tags":["tones","middle-chinese","cantonese","sinitic","historical-phonology"],"title":{"en":"From Four Tone Categories to Modern Sinitic Tone Systems","ja":"4つの声調カテゴリーから現代の声調体系へ","ko":"네 성조 범주에서 현대 중국어계 성조 체계로","zh":"从四个声调类别到现代汉语方言的声调系统","yue":"由四個聲調類別到現代漢語方言嘅聲調系統","vi":"Từ bốn phạm trù thanh điệu đến các hệ thanh điệu Hán ngữ hiện đại","th":"จากสี่ประเภทวรรณยุกต์สู่ระบบวรรณยุกต์จีนสมัยใหม่","id":"Dari empat kategori nada ke sistem nada Sinitik modern","hi":"चार स्वराघात श्रेणियों से आधुनिक चीनी स्वराघात-प्रणालियों तक","de":"Von vier Tonkategorien zu den modernen sinitischen Tonsystemen","fr":"De quatre catégories tonales aux systèmes tonals sinitiques modernes","it":"Da quattro categorie tonali ai moderni sistemi tonali sinitici","es":"De cuatro categorías tonales a los sistemas tonales siníticos modernos","pt":"Das quatro categorias tonais aos sistemas tonais siníticos modernos","ru":"От четырёх тональных категорий к современным тональным системам китайских языков","uk":"Від чотирьох тональних категорій до сучасних тональних систем китайських мов","ar":"من أربع فئات نغمية إلى الأنظمة النغمية الصينية الحديثة","he":"מארבע קטגוריות טונים למערכות הטונים הסיניות של היום","sw":"Kutoka makundi manne ya toni hadi mifumo ya kisasa ya toni za Kichina"},"summary":{"en":"Middle Chinese had four tone categories. Subsequent splits and mergers, including those associated with the voicing of initial consonants, produced the modern tone systems of Mandarin, Cantonese, and Min Nan.","ja":"中古漢語の声調は4カテゴリだった。その後の分裂と合流、とくに語頭子音の有声・無声に関わる変化を経て、普通話・広東語・閩南語の現代の声調体系が生まれた。","ko":"중고한어의 성조는 네 범주였다. 이후의 분화와 합류, 그중에서도 초성의 유무성과 관련된 변화를 거쳐 표준 중국어, 광둥어, 민난어의 현대 성조 체계가 생겨났다.","zh":"中古汉语有四个声调类别。后来的分化与合并，包括与声母清浊相关的变化，形成了普通话、粤语和闽南语的现代声调系统。","yue":"中古漢語有四個聲調類別。後來嘅分化同合併，包括同聲母清濁有關嘅變化，形成咗普通話、粵語同閩南語嘅現代聲調系統。","vi":"Tiếng Hán trung cổ có bốn phạm trù thanh điệu. Những lần phân tách và hợp nhất về sau, trong đó có các biến đổi gắn với tính hữu thanh của phụ âm đầu, đã tạo nên hệ thanh điệu hiện đại của tiếng Quan thoại, Quảng Đông và Mân Nam.","th":"ภาษาจีนยุคกลางมีวรรณยุกต์สี่ประเภท ต่อมาวรรณยุกต์เหล่านี้แตกตัวและรวมตัวกัน ส่วนหนึ่งตามความก้องของพยัญชนะต้น จนกลายเป็นระบบวรรณยุกต์สมัยใหม่ของจีนกลาง กวางตุ้ง และหมิ่นหนาน","id":"Bahasa Tionghoa Pertengahan memiliki empat kategori nada. Pemisahan dan penggabungan sesudahnya, termasuk yang berkaitan dengan sifat bersuara konsonan awal, menghasilkan sistem nada Mandarin, Kanton, dan Min Nan modern.","hi":"मध्यकालीन चीनी में चार स्वराघात श्रेणियाँ थीं। बाद के विभाजनों और विलयों ने, जिनमें प्रारंभिक व्यंजनों की सघोषता से जुड़े बदलाव भी शामिल हैं, मंदारिन, कैंटोनीज़ और मिन नान की आधुनिक स्वराघात-प्रणालियाँ बनाईं।","de":"Das Mittelchinesische hatte vier Tonkategorien. Spätere Spaltungen und Zusammenfälle, darunter solche, die mit der Stimmhaftigkeit der Anlautkonsonanten zusammenhängen, führten zu den heutigen Tonsystemen des Mandarin, Kantonesischen und Min Nan.","fr":"Le chinois médiéval avait quatre catégories tonales. Des scissions et des fusions ultérieures, dont certaines liées au voisement des consonnes initiales, ont produit les systèmes tonals modernes du mandarin, du cantonais et du min nan.","it":"Il cinese medio aveva quattro categorie tonali. Scissioni e fusioni successive, comprese quelle legate alla sonorità delle consonanti iniziali, hanno prodotto i moderni sistemi tonali del mandarino, del cantonese e del min nan.","es":"El chino medio tenía cuatro categorías tonales. Las divisiones y fusiones posteriores, incluidas las asociadas a la sonoridad de las consonantes iniciales, dieron lugar a los sistemas tonales modernos del mandarín, el cantonés y el min nan.","pt":"O chinês médio tinha quatro categorias tonais. Divisões e fusões posteriores, incluindo as ligadas à sonoridade das consoantes iniciais, produziram os sistemas tonais modernos do mandarim, do cantonês e do min nan.","ru":"В среднекитайском было четыре тональные категории. Последующие расщепления и слияния, в том числе связанные со звонкостью начальных согласных, породили современные тональные системы мандаринского, кантонского и миньнань.","uk":"У середньокитайській було чотири тональні категорії. Подальші розщеплення й злиття, зокрема пов'язані з дзвінкістю початкових приголосних, дали сучасні тональні системи мандаринської, кантонської та міньнань.","ar":"كانت للصينية الوسطى أربع فئات نغمية. ثم أفرزت الانقسامات والاندماجات اللاحقة، ومنها ما يرتبط بجهر الصوامت الأولى، الأنظمة النغمية الحديثة في الماندرين والكانتونية والمين نان.","he":"לסינית התיכונה היו ארבע קטגוריות טונים. פיצולים ומיזוגים מאוחרים יותר, ובהם כאלה הקשורים לקוליות של עיצורים ראשוניים, יצרו את מערכות הטונים המודרניות של המנדרינית, הקנטונזית והמין נאן.","sw":"Kichina cha Kati kilikuwa na makundi manne ya toni. Migawanyiko na miungano iliyofuata, ikiwemo ile inayohusishwa na mlio wa konsonanti za awali, ilizaa mifumo ya kisasa ya toni ya Mandarin, Kikantoni na Min Nan."},"sources":["Norman, Jerry (1988). Chinese. Cambridge Language Surveys. Cambridge University Press.","Pulleyblank, Edwin G. (1984). Middle Chinese: A Study in Historical Phonology. UBC Press.","Yue-Hashimoto, Anne O. (1972). Studies in Yue Dialects, Vol. 1: Phonology of Cantonese. Cambridge University Press.","Mei Tsu-lin (1970). Tones and prosody in Middle Chinese and the origin of the rising tone. Harvard Journal of Asiatic Studies 30: 86–110.",{"text":"STEDT: Sino-Tibetan Etymological Dictionary","url":"https://stedt.berkeley.edu/"},{"text":"Wikipedia: Four tones (Middle Chinese)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Four_tones_(Middle_Chinese)"},{"text":"Wikipedia: Taiwanese Hokkien — tone sandhi","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Taiwanese_Hokkien#Tone_sandhi"},{"text":"Wikipedia: Yale romanization of Cantonese — tones","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yale_romanization_of_Cantonese"}],"body":{"en":"<h3>The Four Ancestors</h3>\n<p>Around the 7th century CE — the era of the <em>Qieyun</em> 切韻 rime dictionary (compiled in 601 under the Sui) and of the Tang dynasty that made it the standard — Chinese had four tone categories: <strong>平 (píng, level), 上 (shǎng, rising), 去 (qù, departing), and 入 (rù, entering)</strong>. The <em>entering tone</em> (入声) was not a pitch shape but a syllable structure category: all its syllables ended in a short stop consonant (-p, -t, or -k). These four categories held all the syllables of Middle Chinese.</p>\n\n<div class=\"trivia-actions\">\n  <button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"focus\" data-code=\"yue_hk\">📍 Focus Cantonese (Hong Kong) on map</button>\n  <button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"focus\" data-code=\"zh\">📍 Focus Mandarin on map</button>\n</div>\n\n<h3>The Voicing Split</h3>\n<p>Middle Chinese had a rich system of initial consonants, many of which came in voiced/voiceless pairs: <em>p-</em> vs <em>b-</em>, <em>t-</em> vs <em>d-</em>, <em>k-</em> vs <em>g-</em>, etc. Over the following centuries, voiced initials disappeared or merged differently in each dialect, but not before leaving a lasting imprint: syllables with originally <strong>voiced initials developed lower pitch</strong>, while those with voiceless initials developed higher pitch. This is the <em>Yīn-Yáng split</em> (阴阳分化). (In some dialects the pitches later flipped round, so many linguists call the two registers \"dark\" (yin) and \"light\" (yang) rather than \"upper\" and \"lower\".)</p>\n\n<p>The result is mechanical: each of the original four tones split into a <strong>Yin (阴/upper) register</strong> and a <strong>Yang (阳/lower) register</strong>, simply based on what kind of consonant the syllable used to start with. Four tones × two registers = eight potential tone slots. Dialects then merged some slots, split others differently, and the final count diverges wildly across the Sinitic family.</p>\n\n<h3>The Cantonese Case: 9 Tones (or 6+3)</h3>\n<p>Cantonese is the canonical showcase. The six <em>live tones</em> (陰平, 陽平, 陰上, 陽上, 陰去, 陽去) cover the old 平/上/去 categories after the Yin-Yang split — <strong>6 contour tones</strong> with distinct pitch shapes. But Cantonese also preserved the entering tone (入声). The Yin-Yang split gave it two checked tones, and a later Yue-internal split of the upper one — conditioned by vowel length, not by the old initial — added a third, yielding <strong>3 checked tones</strong> (陰入, 中入, 陽入) with the same pitch heights as the upper, mid, and lower live tones but cut short by a final stop. The traditional count is therefore <strong>9 tones</strong>, but the romanisations you meet in daily life do not write nine. Yale writes seven (it keeps a high falling tone apart from high level) but spells a checked syllable exactly like the high-flat, mid-flat or low-flat tone whose pitch it shares; Jyutping, the system used on this map, also numbers 1–6 only (一 <em>jat1</em>, 目 <em>muk6</em>). The digits 7, 8 and 9 for 陰入/中入/陽入 live on in traditional tone charts and in the Cantonese Pinyin (教院式) system — bookkeeping labels for the history rather than three extra pitches.</p>\n\n<div class=\"trivia-actions\">\n  <button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"focus\" data-code=\"yue_hk\">🎵 See Cantonese tonal romanisation</button>\n</div>\n\n<h3>Mandarin: 4 Tones, the Entering Tone Vanished</h3>\n<p>Standard Mandarin simplified dramatically. The voiced initials disappeared early and thoroughly; the level tone split in two, but the other registers merged, and old rising-tone words with voiced stop or fricative initials moved wholesale into the departing tone (上 <em>shàng</em> itself is one of them, 4th tone today). The entering tone (入声) lost its final stops and its syllables were redistributed. Much of that was regular: words with voiced stop initials went to the 2nd tone (十 <em>shí</em>, 白 <em>bái</em>) and those with nasal or l- initials to the 4th (六 <em>liù</em>, 木 <em>mù</em>). Only words with voiceless initials were scattered across all four tones with no rule, the legacy of dialect mixing in the capital, and they are what makes old 入声 characters so hard to spot in Mandarin. The result is a clean 4-tone system (阴平, 阳平, 上声, 去声), the <em>sì shēng</em> 四声 that every Chinese learner memorises.</p>\n\n<h3>Min Nan: 7 or 8 Tones, Entering Tone Alive and Doubled</h3>\n<p>Hokkien/Taiwanese (闽南语, Min Nan) preserved the entering tone with its final stops intact (-p, -t, -k). Like Cantonese, it applied the Yin-Yang split to the entering tone, giving 2 entering tones (陰入 and 陽入). The live tones underwent further mergers and splits, yielding a base of <strong>7 tones</strong> in Standard Min Nan. Min Nan is also famous for its <em>sandhi</em> system: in natural speech, every syllable except the last in a phrase switches to a different tone. The switch is largely regular, a chain learners memorise as a circle (in Taipei speech tone 1 becomes 7, 7 becomes 3, 3 becomes 2, 2 becomes 1), so what has to be learned is one rule set rather than two tones per word.</p>\n\n<h3>Hakka, Wu, and the Full Spectrum</h3>\n<p>Hakka (客家话) dialects typically have 5–7 tones, and most keep the entering tone with its -p, -t, -k (十 is <em>sṳ̍p</em> in Hakka church romanisation). Wu (吴语, including Shanghainese) is the best-known group, along with Old Xiang, still to keep the three-way split of the old consonants, so the Yin-Yang split can still be heard. Conservative southern Wu such as Wenzhou still counts eight, though Shanghainese itself has thinned to about five — the 1–8 digits on this map's northern Wu rows are Wugniu tone categories, not eight separate pitches. One caution: in northern Wu, Shanghainese included, the \"voiced\" consonants are typically pronounced with breathy voice, or \"murmur\", which also drags the pitch of the whole syllable down. So Wu shows where the old voicing contrast went more than exactly how it sounded.</p>\n\n<p>You can watch the whole story in one word. 十 \"ten\" was an entering-tone syllable ending in -p. Cantonese still says <em>sap6</em>, Hokkien <em>tsa̍p</em> and Hakka <em>sṳ̍p</em>, all with the stop intact. Mandarin <em>shí</em> lost the -p, and because the word began with a voiced consonant it landed, by rule, in the 2nd tone. Japanese borrowed it too: today it is <em>jū</em>, but the old kana spelling じふ (<em>jifu</em>, from an earlier <em>-pu</em>) still hides the -p.</p>"}},{"id":"old-chinese-baxter-sagart","icon":"🪨","tags":["old-chinese","reconstruction","history"],"title":{"en":"Old Chinese Reconstruction: From Karlgren to Baxter-Sagart 2014","ja":"上古漢語の再建：カールグレンからバクスター＝サガールまで","ko":"상고 한어 재건: 칼그렌에서 Baxter-Sagart 2014까지","zh":"上古汉语重建：从高本汉到白一平-沙加尔","yue":"上古漢語嘅重建：從高本漢到白一平同沙加爾","vi":"Tái hiện tiếng Hán thượng cổ: từ Karlgren đến Baxter-Sagart 2014","th":"การสร้างภาษาจีนโบราณ: จากคาร์ลเกรนสู่แบ็กซ์เตอร์-ซาการ์ต 2014","id":"Rekonstruksi bahasa Tionghoa Kuno: dari Karlgren hingga Baxter-Sagart 2014","hi":"पुरानी चीनी भाषा का पुनर्निर्माण: कार्लग्रेन से बैक्सटर-सागार्ट 2014 तक","de":"Altchinesisch-Rekonstruktion: Von Karlgren bis Baxter-Sagart 2014","fr":"Reconstruction du vieux chinois : de Karlgren à Baxter-Sagart 2014","it":"Ricostruzione del cinese antico: da Karlgren a Baxter-Sagart 2014","es":"Reconstrucción del chino antiguo: de Karlgren a Baxter-Sagart 2014","pt":"Reconstrução do chinês antigo: de Karlgren a Baxter-Sagart 2014","ru":"Реконструкция древнекитайского: от Карлгрена до Бакстера-Сагара 2014","uk":"Реконструкція давньокитайської: від Карлгрена до Бакстера-Сагара 2014","ar":"إعادة بناء الصينية القديمة: من كارلغرن إلى باكستر-ساغار 2014","he":"שחזור הסינית העתיקה: מקרלגרן לבקסטר-סאגר 2014","sw":"Ujenzi upya wa Kichina cha Kale: kutoka Karlgren hadi Baxter-Sagart 2014"},"summary":{"en":"Reconstructions of Old Chinese combine oracle-bone inscriptions, rhyme evidence and phonetic series. In Baxter–Sagart's system, 一 is reconstructed as *ʔi[t].","ja":"上古漢語の再建は、甲骨文、押韻の証拠、声符の系列を組み合わせて行われる。バクスター＝サガールの体系では、「一」は*ʔi[t]と再建される。","ko":"상고 한어 재건은 갑골문, 압운 자료, 해성 계열을 함께 활용한다. Baxter-Sagart 체계에서 一은 *ʔi[t]로 재건된다.","zh":"上古汉语的重建综合利用甲骨文、押韵材料和谐声系列。在白一平-沙加尔的体系中，“一”被重建为*ʔi[t]。","yue":"上古漢語嘅重建綜合利用甲骨文、押韻材料同諧聲系列。喺白一平同沙加爾嘅體系入面，「一」重建為*ʔi[t]。","vi":"Việc tái hiện tiếng Hán thượng cổ kết hợp giáp cốt văn, chứng cứ về vần và các dãy hài thanh. Trong hệ thống Baxter-Sagart, 一 được tái hiện là *ʔi[t].","th":"การสร้างภาษาจีนโบราณขึ้นใหม่อาศัยจารึกกระดูกเสี่ยงทาย หลักฐานการสัมผัส และชุดอักษรที่มีส่วนบอกเสียงร่วมกัน ในระบบของ Baxter-Sagart 一 ถูกสร้างใหม่เป็น *ʔi[t]","id":"Rekonstruksi bahasa Tionghoa Kuno memadukan prasasti tulang ramalan, bukti rima, dan deret fonetis. Dalam sistem Baxter–Sagart, 一 direkonstruksi sebagai *ʔi[t].","hi":"पुरानी चीनी के पुनर्निर्माण ऑरेकल-बोन शिलालेखों, तुकांत-प्रमाणों और ध्वन्यात्मक श्रृंखलाओं को मिलाकर बनते हैं। बैक्सटर–सागार्ट प्रणाली में 一 का पुनर्निर्मित रूप *ʔi[t] है।","de":"Rekonstruktionen des Altchinesischen verbinden Orakelknochen-Inschriften, Reimbelege und phonetische Reihen. Im System von Baxter–Sagart wird 一 als *ʔi[t] rekonstruiert.","fr":"Les reconstructions du vieux chinois combinent inscriptions sur os oraculaires, indices tirés des rimes et séries phonétiques. Dans le système de Baxter-Sagart, 一 est reconstruit *ʔi[t].","it":"Le ricostruzioni del cinese antico combinano iscrizioni su ossa oracolari, rime della poesia antica e serie fonetiche. Nel sistema di Baxter–Sagart, 一 è ricostruito come *ʔi[t].","es":"Las reconstrucciones del chino antiguo combinan inscripciones en huesos oraculares, datos de rimas y series fonéticas. En el sistema de Baxter-Sagart, 一 se reconstruye como *ʔi[t].","pt":"As reconstruções do chinês antigo combinam inscrições em ossos oraculares, evidências de rimas e séries fonéticas. No sistema de Baxter-Sagart, 一 é reconstruído como *ʔi[t].","ru":"Реконструкции древнекитайского опираются на надписи на гадательных костях, данные рифм и фонетические серии. В системе Бакстера-Сагара 一 восстанавливается как *ʔi[t].","uk":"Реконструкції давньокитайської поєднують написи на ворожильних кістках, дані рим і фонетичні серії. У системі Бакстера-Сагара 一 відтворюють як *ʔi[t].","ar":"تجمع إعادات بناء الصينية القديمة بين نقوش عظام العرافة وأدلة القوافي والسلاسل الصوتية. وفي نظام باكستر-ساغار يُعاد بناء 一 على أنها *ʔi[t].","he":"שחזורים של הסינית העתיקה משלבים כתובות עצמות אורקל, עדויות מחרוזים וסדרות פונטיות. בשיטה של בקסטר-סאגר, 一 משוחזר כ-*ʔi[t].","sw":"Ujenzi upya wa Kichina cha Kale unaunganisha maandishi ya mifupa ya uaguzi, ushahidi wa vina na mfululizo wa fonetiki. Katika mfumo wa Baxter-Sagart, 一 hujengwa upya kama *ʔi[t]."},"body":{"en":"<h3>The Oldest Chinese We Can Hear</h3><p>Old Chinese (OC) is the ancestor of all Sinitic languages, spoken roughly 1250–200 BCE — the era of the <em>Shijing</em> (詩經) and oracle-bone inscriptions. No one recorded its sounds directly. Linguists reconstruct it by working backwards. The starting point is Middle Chinese (MC), the sound system recorded in the rhyme dictionary <em>Qieyun</em> of 601 CE. To that they add the rhymes of the <em>Shijing</em> songs, the sound clues built into characters (\"phonetic series\"), the Min dialects of the south-east, which preserve some older distinctions, early loanwords in neighbouring languages, and comparison with related Sino-Tibetan languages.</p><h3>Karlgren's Trailblazing, and Its Limits</h3><p>In <em>Grammata Serica</em> (1940), Swedish sinologist Bernhard Karlgren (高本漢) produced the first systematic OC reconstruction dictionary, built on the Middle Chinese system of his earlier <em>Analytic Dictionary of Chinese and Sino-Japanese</em> (1923). He used MC as his pivot and extracted proto-forms like *mi̯wən for 文. His framework was a breakthrough, but later scholars found two major limits. He built his system on <em>phonetic series</em> (the component shared by a cluster of characters hints at their shared OC initial), but bound them with a rigid <em>homorganic principle</em> — every character in a series had to share a place of articulation — which forced ad hoc patches wherever a series refused to comply; and his vowel system was under-differentiated.</p><h3>The Cluster Revolution</h3><p>Characters sharing a phonetic element (<em>xíngshēng</em> 形聲) sometimes have radically different MC initials: 各 (MC kak) shares its phonetic with 路 (MC luH). The usual explanation is that OC had consonant <em>clusters</em> or prefixes that later simplified differently in different words. Karlgren himself already proposed clusters with *-l- for such cases; later scholars pushed the idea much further. Pulleyblank (1962) also argued that the Chinese rising tone came from a final glottal stop, one of the key steps in explaining how a toneless OC became tonal. Baxter's 1992 monograph <em>A Handbook of Old Chinese Phonology</em> rigorously formalized a cluster system that resolved hundreds of these puzzles. Baxter and Sagart's later answer for 路 shows how far the analysis has moved: not a cluster but a loosely attached presyllable plus a suffix, *Cə.rˤak-s, beside 各 *kˤak.</p><h3>Baxter-Sagart 2014</h3><p>The landmark <em>Old Chinese: A New Reconstruction</em> (2014) added Laurent Sagart's morphological dimension. Key innovations:</p><ul><li><strong>Sesquisyllables</strong>: OC had minor syllable prefixes (like Mon-Khmer languages today) — e.g., 水 'water' *s.turʔ, where the dot in <em>s.</em> marks a loosely attached presyllable, not part of the main syllable. A hyphen marks the opposite: in 山 *s-ŋrar the <em>s-</em> is fused tightly to the root initial.</li><li><strong>Morphological prefixes/suffixes</strong>: for example *s- forming causatives, *N- forming intransitive or stative verbs, and *-s, which among other jobs derived nouns from verbs. Pairs of related words written with one character, read in different tones today, are often traces of this lost morphology.</li><li><strong>Pharyngealization</strong>: the long-disputed Type A/B syllable split is realised here as pharyngealization of the main-syllable initial — Jerry Norman's 1994 proposal, which Baxter and Sagart adopted and extended to every Type A syllable (五 *C.ŋˤaʔ).</li></ul><div class=\"trivia-actions\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"setchar\" data-char=\"一\">Set character: 一</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"setchar\" data-char=\"山\">Set character: 山</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"focus\" data-code=\"pst\">Focus: Proto-Sino-Tibetan</button></div><h3>Reading Two Characters</h3><p>Take <strong>一</strong> 'one': OC *ʔi[t] → MC ʔjit (Baxter-Sagart). The glottal stop initial was already present in OC; Mandarin <em>yī</em> has lost both the glottal stop and the final <em>-t</em>, while Cantonese <em>jat</em> still keeps the <em>-t</em>. Take <strong>山</strong> 'mountain': OC *s-ŋrar — the <em>s-</em> preinitial together with the medial *-r- yielded the Middle Chinese retroflex sibilant sr- (ʂ-), the <em>sh-</em> of modern <em>shān</em> 山, while the root initial *ŋ- was lost. Its relatives keep the *ŋ- visible: Baxter and Sagart reconstruct 顏 'face' as *C.ŋˤrar.</p><h3>Open Questions</h3><p>Despite the 2014 system's elegance, debates continue. How many vowels did OC have — 4, 6, or more? Were the presyllables true syllables or mere consonantal prefixes? How do Tibetan and Burmese cognates constrain the vowel system? And fundamentally: how far back can the comparative method reach before Chinese characters' logographic nature obscures the sound history entirely?</p>"},"sources":["Baxter, W. H. & Sagart, L. (2014). Old Chinese: A New Reconstruction. Oxford University Press.","Baxter, W. H. (1992). A Handbook of Old Chinese Phonology. Mouton de Gruyter.","Karlgren, B. (1923). Analytic Dictionary of Chinese and Sino-Japanese. Paul Geuthner.","Pulleyblank, E. G. (1962). The consonantal system of Old Chinese. Asia Major 9, 58–144.",{"text":"Baxter-Sagart Old Chinese reconstruction database (2014)","url":"https://sites.lsa.umich.edu/ocbaxtersagart/"}]},{"id":"sino-tibetan-cognates","icon":"🏔","tags":["sino-tibetan","cognates","etymology"],"title":{"en":"Sino-Tibetan Cognates: How 五 and Tibetan ལྔ Share the Same Root","ja":"漢蔵語族の同源語：なぜ「五」とチベット語ལྔは同じ語根を持つのか","ko":"중국티베트어족 동족어: 五와 티베트어 ལྔ는 어떻게 같은 어근을 공유하는가","zh":"汉藏同源词：汉语“五”与藏语ལྔ如何共享同一词根","yue":"漢藏同源詞：「五」同藏語ལྔ點解係同一語根？","vi":"Từ đồng nguyên Hán-Tạng: tại sao 五 và ལྔ tiếng Tây Tạng cùng gốc","th":"คำร่วมเชื้อสายจีน-ทิเบต: ทำไม 五 กับ ལྔ ในภาษาทิเบตมีรากเดียวกัน","id":"Kognat Sino-Tibet: bagaimana 五 dan ལྔ Tibet berbagi akar yang sama","hi":"चीनी-तिब्बती समजातीय शब्द: 五 और तिब्बती ལྔ एक ही मूल से कैसे आते हैं","de":"Sinotibetische Kognaten: Wie 五 und das tibetische ལྔ denselben Ursprung teilen","fr":"Cognats sino-tibétains : comment 五 et le tibétain ལྔ partagent la même racine","it":"Cognati sino-tibetani: come 五 e il tibetano ལྔ condividono la stessa radice","es":"Cognados sino-tibetanos: cómo 五 y el tibetano ལྔ comparten la misma raíz","pt":"Cognatos sino-tibetanos: como 五 e o tibetano ལྔ compartilham a mesma raiz","ru":"Сино-тибетские когнаты: как 五 и тибетское ལྔ восходят к одному корню","uk":"Сино-тибетські когнати: як 五 і тибетське ལྔ мають спільний корінь","ar":"الكلمات الصينية-التيبتية المتجانسة الأصل: كيف تشترك 五 والتيبتية ལྔ في الجذر ذاته","he":"קוגנטים סינו-טיבטיים: איך 五 ו-ལྔ הטיבטית חולקות שורש אחד","sw":"Maneno yanayofanana ya Kisino-Tibet: jinsi 五 na ལྔ ya Kitibeti yanavyoshiriki mzizi mmoja"},"summary":{"en":"Regular correspondences among Mandarin wǔ, Tibetan lnga, and Burmese nga support the reconstruction of Proto-Sino-Tibetan *ŋā 'five'.","ja":"北京語の「wǔ」、チベット語の「lnga」、ミャンマー語の「nga」——三つの形の規則的な対応が、漢蔵祖語の*ŋā（五）の再建を支えている。","ko":"표준 중국어 wǔ, 티베트어 lnga, 미얀마어 nga 사이의 규칙적인 대응은 중국티베트조어 *ŋā '다섯'의 재건을 뒷받침한다.","zh":"普通话 wǔ、藏语 lnga 和缅甸语 nga 之间的规则对应，支持把“五”重建为原始汉藏语的 *ŋā。","yue":"普通話 wǔ、藏語 lnga 同緬甸語 nga 之間嘅規則對應，支持將「五」重建為原始漢藏語嘅 *ŋā。","vi":"Các tương ứng đều đặn giữa wǔ trong tiếng Quan thoại, lnga trong tiếng Tây Tạng và nga trong tiếng Miến Điện ủng hộ việc tái hiện *ŋā 'năm' của Proto Hán-Tạng.","th":"ความสอดคล้องอย่างสม่ำเสมอระหว่าง wǔ ในภาษาจีนกลาง lnga ในภาษาทิเบต และ nga ในภาษาพม่า สนับสนุนการสร้างคำว่า *ŋā 'ห้า' ในภาษาจีน-ทิเบตดั้งเดิม","id":"Kesepadanan yang teratur antara Mandarin wǔ, Tibet lnga, dan Burma nga mendukung rekonstruksi Proto-Sino-Tibet *ŋā 'lima'.","hi":"मंदारिन wǔ, तिब्बती lnga और बर्मी nga के बीच नियमित समानताएँ आदि-चीनी-तिब्बती *ŋā 'पाँच' के पुनर्निर्माण का आधार हैं।","de":"Regelmäßige Entsprechungen zwischen Mandarin wǔ, Tibetisch lnga und Birmanisch nga stützen die Rekonstruktion von Proto-Sinotibetisch *ŋā 'fünf'.","fr":"Les correspondances régulières entre le mandarin wǔ, le tibétain lnga et le birman nga étayent la reconstruction du proto-sino-tibétain *ŋā « cinq » : trois formes d'un même mot ancestral.","it":"Le corrispondenze regolari tra il mandarino wǔ, il tibetano lnga e il birmano nga sostengono la ricostruzione del proto-sino-tibetano *ŋā 'cinque'.","es":"El protosinotibetano *ŋā 'cinco' sobrevive como mandarín wǔ, tibetano lnga y birmano nga: tres caras de una misma palabra ancestral, unidas por correspondencias fonéticas regulares.","pt":"Correspondências regulares entre o mandarim wǔ, o tibetano lnga e o birmanês nga sustentam a reconstrução do protossino-tibetano *ŋā 'cinco'.","ru":"Регулярные соответствия между мандаринским wǔ, тибетским lnga и бирманским nga подтверждают реконструкцию протосино-тибетского *ŋā «пять».","uk":"Регулярні відповідності між мандаринським wǔ, тибетським lnga і бірманським nga підтверджують реконструкцію протосинотибетського *ŋā «п'ять».","ar":"تدعم التقابلات المطّردة بين الماندرين wǔ والتيبتية lnga والبورمية nga إعادةَ بناء الصيغة البروتو-صينية-التيبتية *ŋā «خمسة».","he":"התאמות קבועות בין wǔ במנדרינית, lnga בטיבטית ו-nga בבורמית תומכות בשחזור הפרוטו-סינו-טיבטי *ŋā, ״חמש״.","sw":"Uwiano wa kawaida kati ya Mandarin wǔ, Kitibeti lnga na Kiburma nga unaunga mkono ujenzi upya wa Proto-Sino-Tibet *ŋā 'tano'."},"body":{"en":"<h3>A Family Reunited Across Millennia</h3><p>The Sino-Tibetan language family — encompassing Chinese, Tibetan, Burmese, and hundreds of smaller languages — has around 1.4 billion speakers, second only to Indo-European. Yet compared to Indo-European, it remains poorly understood at the proto-language level, partly because Chinese characters record meaning far better than sound, and partly because the family's internal classification is still debated.</p><p>The most powerful evidence linking Chinese to its relatives is <em>vocabulary cognates</em>: words descending independently from a common ancestral word, with sound changes that follow predictable rules.</p><h3>The Number Five: *ŋā Across Three Branches</h3><div class=\"trivia-actions\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"setchar\" data-char=\"五\">Set character: 五</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"focus\" data-code=\"pst\">Focus: Proto-Sino-Tibetan</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"focus\" data-code=\"bo_sino\">Focus: Sino-Tibetan Tibetan layer</button></div><p>The Proto-Sino-Tibetan (PST) root for 'five' is reconstructed as <strong>*ŋā</strong>. Here is how it surfaces across branches:</p><ul><li><strong>Chinese</strong>: Old Chinese *C.ŋˤaʔ (pharyngealized, with glottal suffix) → Middle Chinese *ŋuX → Mandarin <em>wǔ</em> 五. The initial *ŋ- was simply lost: the 疑 initial merged into Mandarin's zero initial, and the <em>w</em> of <em>wǔ</em> is only pinyin's way of writing a syllable that begins with <em>u</em>. The same loss gives 銀 <em>yín</em> and 我 <em>wǒ</em>, whichever way the vowel rounds.</li><li><strong>Tibetan</strong>: <em>lnga</em> (ལྔ). The l- is a prefix, not part of the root; the core is <em>ŋa</em>, directly matching the PST root.</li><li><strong>Burmese</strong>: <em>ngā</em> (ငါး). The initial ŋ- is preserved without the prefix complication, and the vowel directly reflects *ā.</li></ul><h3>The Number Four: *li(j) Across Three Branches</h3><p>The root for 'four' reconstructed as <strong>*li(j)</strong> shows the same convergence:</p><ul><li><strong>Chinese</strong>: Old Chinese *s.li[j]-s → Middle Chinese *sijH → Mandarin <em>sì</em> 四. The *s- initial replaced *l- through a prefix interaction (compare the *s-ŋrar pattern for 山).</li><li><strong>Tibetan</strong>: <em>bzhi</em> (བཞི།). Again, the b- is a prefix; the zh- of the root is Tibetan's own palatalisation of the PST lateral *l- before the front vowel of *li(j).</li><li><strong>Burmese</strong>: <em>le</em> (လေး). The l- directly preserves the PST *l- initial.</li></ul><h3>Two More Cognate Pairs</h3><p><strong>Eye / 目</strong>: PST *myak → Old Chinese *C.m(r)[u]k → Mandarin <em>mù</em>; Tibetan <em>mig</em> (མིག); Burmese <em>myak</em>. The core consonant *m- is rock-stable across the family.</p><p><strong>Name / 名</strong>: PST *r-miŋ → Old Chinese *C.meŋ → Mandarin <em>míng</em> 名; Tibetan <em>ming</em> (མིང་); Burmese <em>a-maññ</em> (အမည်, pronounced /ʔəmjì/). A nasal final is shared across all three branches — Burmese has a palatal -ññ where Chinese and Tibetan have -ŋ, a regular correspondence.</p><h3>A Warning About Method</h3><p>Identifying cognates is harder in Sino-Tibetan than in Indo-European for three reasons. First, Chinese's monosyllabic surface structure and the logographic writing system hide older morphology. Second, Chinese has lent words to its neighbours on a massive scale for well over two thousand years, so a resemblance might be borrowing, not inheritance. Third, the family tree itself is contested. The traditional picture splits it into Chinese on one side and \"Tibeto-Burman\" on the other, but no one has ever shown that all the non-Chinese languages share a common ancestor of their own; Chinese may simply be one branch among many. Sagart's controversial Sino-Austronesian hypothesis — that Chinese and Austronesian share an ancestor — shows how much remains open. It is easily confused with Austro-Tai, which is a different claim by a different scholar: Benedict's 1942 proposal joining Austronesian to Kra-Dai, with Chinese not in it at all.</p><p>One myth is worth clearing up: numerals are <em>not</em> immune to borrowing, least of all in East Asia. Thai <em>hâa</em> \"five\" and <em>sìi</em> \"four\" are old loans from Chinese 五 and 四, and Japanese, Korean and Vietnamese all count with Chinese numerals alongside native ones. What makes Tibetan <em>lnga</em> and Burmese <em>ngā</em> look inherited rather than borrowed is the detail: prefixes such as Tibetan <em>l-</em> and <em>b-</em> that no Chinese loan would bring, and sound correspondences that repeat across hundreds of words. Two computational studies in 2019 put the family's common ancestor at roughly 5,900 or 7,200 years ago, so the thread that ties 五 to ལྔ is some six or seven thousand years long.</p>"},"sources":["Baxter, W. H. & Sagart, L. (2014). Old Chinese: A New Reconstruction. Oxford University Press.","Matisoff, J. A. (2003). Handbook of Proto-Tibeto-Burman. University of California Press.","Sagart, L. (1999). The Roots of Old Chinese. John Benjamins.","Hill, N. W. (2019). The Historical Phonology of Tibetan, Burmese, and Chinese. Cambridge University Press.",{"text":"STEDT: Sino-Tibetan Etymological Dictionary and Thesaurus","url":"https://stedt.berkeley.edu/"},{"text":"Sagart, L., Jacques, G., Lai, Y., Ryder, R. J., Thouzeau, V., Greenhill, S. J. & List, J.-M. (2019). Dated language phylogenies shed light on the ancestry of Sino-Tibetan. PNAS 116(21): 10317–10322.","url":"https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1817972116"}]},{"id":"ko-mid-eastguk-jeongun","icon":"📜","tags":["korea","middle-korean","dictionary","hangul","sinoxenic"],"title":{"en":"東國正韻: A Prescriptive Sino-Korean Pronunciation Dictionary","ja":"東國正韻：朝鮮漢字音の規範辞書","ko":"동국정운: 규범을 세운 한자음 사전","zh":"东国正韵：规范朝鲜汉字音的韵书","yue":"東國正韻：規範朝鮮漢字音嘅韻書","vi":"Đông Quốc Chính Vận (東國正韻): từ điển quy phạm âm Hán-Triều","th":"ทงกุกช็องอุน (東國正韻): พจนานุกรมกำหนดเสียงอักษรจีน-เกาหลี","id":"東國正韻: kamus yang mengatur lafal Sino-Korea","hi":"東國正韻: चीनी-कोरियाई उच्चारण का एक निर्देशात्मक शब्दकोश","de":"東國正韻: Ein präskriptives sinokoreanisches Aussprachewörterbuch","fr":"東國正韻 : un dictionnaire normatif de prononciation sino-coréenne","it":"東國正韻: un dizionario prescrittivo di pronuncia sino-coreana","es":"東國正韻: un diccionario prescriptivo de pronunciación sino-coreana","pt":"東國正韻: um dicionário prescritivo de pronúncia sino-coreana","ru":"東國正韻: нормативный словарь китайско-корейского произношения","uk":"東國正韻: нормативний словник китайсько-корейської вимови","ar":"東國正韻: معجم معياري للنطق الصيني-الكوري","he":"東國正韻: מילון הגייה סינו-קוריאני נורמטיבי","sw":"東國正韻: kamusi ya kuelekeza matamshi ya Kisino-Kikorea"},"summary":{"en":"In 1448, King Sejong commissioned a dictionary to standardize Korean readings of Chinese characters, modeled on an idealized Middle Chinese drawn from classical rhyme books. Its prescribed pronunciations saw limited adoption and fell out of official use within decades.","ja":"1448年、世宗大王は漢字の朝鮮読みを統一する辞書の編纂を命じた。古典韻書から導いた理想化された中古音をモデルにしたが、規定された発音はあまり広まらず、数十年のうちに公的な場で使われなくなった。","ko":"1448년 세종대왕은 한자음을 표준화하기 위한 사전 편찬을 명했다. 고전 운서에서 끌어낸 이상화된 중고한어음을 모델로 삼았으나, 규정된 발음은 제한적으로만 받아들여졌고 수십 년 만에 공식적으로 쓰이지 않게 되었다.","zh":"1448年，世宗大王下令编纂一部统一朝鲜汉字读音的辞书，以古典韵书中理想化的中古汉语音为范本。其规定的读音采用有限，数十年内便不再正式使用。","yue":"1448年，世宗大王下令編纂一本統一朝鮮漢字讀音嘅辭書，以古典韻書入面理想化嘅中古漢語音做範本。佢規定嘅讀音採用得唔多，幾十年內就唔再正式使用。","vi":"Năm 1448, Vua Sejong cho soạn một cuốn từ điển nhằm chuẩn hóa cách đọc chữ Hán của người Triều Tiên, dựa trên âm Hán trung cổ lý tưởng hóa rút từ các vận thư cổ điển. Những cách đọc quy định ít được áp dụng và chỉ trong vài thập kỷ đã không còn dùng chính thức.","th":"ในปี ค.ศ. 1448 พระเจ้าเซจงทรงโปรดให้จัดทำพจนานุกรมเพื่อกำหนดมาตรฐานการออกเสียงอักษรจีนแบบเกาหลี โดยยึดเสียงจีนยุคกลางในอุดมคติจากตำราสัมผัสคลาสสิก การออกเสียงที่กำหนดไว้ถูกนำไปใช้อย่างจำกัด และเลิกใช้อย่างเป็นทางการภายในไม่กี่ทศวรรษ","id":"Pada 1448, Raja Sejong menugasi penyusunan kamus untuk membakukan lafal aksara Han dalam bahasa Korea, berpatokan pada bahasa Tionghoa Pertengahan versi ideal dari kitab-kitab rima klasik. Lafal yang ditetapkannya nyaris tak diterima dan dalam beberapa dasawarsa tak lagi dipakai secara resmi.","hi":"1448 में राजा सेजोंग ने कोरियाई भाषा में चीनी अक्षरों के पाठ को मानकीकृत करने के लिए एक शब्दकोश बनवाया, जिसका आधार शास्त्रीय तुक-ग्रंथों से लिया गया मध्यकालीन चीनी का एक आदर्श रूप था। इसके तय किए उच्चारण बहुत कम अपनाए गए और कुछ दशकों में सरकारी प्रयोग से भी बाहर हो गए।","de":"1448 gab König Sejong ein Wörterbuch in Auftrag, das die koreanischen Lesungen chinesischer Schriftzeichen vereinheitlichen sollte, nach dem Vorbild eines idealisierten Mittelchinesisch aus den klassischen Reimbüchern. Die vorgeschriebenen Aussprachen setzten sich nur begrenzt durch und verschwanden binnen Jahrzehnten aus dem amtlichen Gebrauch.","fr":"En 1448, le roi Sejong fit compiler un dictionnaire pour standardiser les lectures coréennes des caractères chinois, sur le modèle d'un chinois médiéval idéalisé tiré des livres de rimes classiques. Les prononciations prescrites ne furent que peu adoptées et sortirent de l'usage officiel en quelques décennies.","it":"Nel 1448 il re Sejong commissionò un dizionario per standardizzare le letture coreane dei caratteri cinesi, modellate su un cinese medio idealizzato tratto dai libri di rime classici. Le pronunce prescritte ebbero una diffusione limitata e uscirono dall'uso ufficiale nel giro di pochi decenni.","es":"En 1448, el rey Sejong encargó un diccionario para estandarizar las lecturas coreanas de los caracteres chinos, modeladas en un chino medio idealizado tomado de los libros de rimas clásicos. Las pronunciaciones prescritas tuvieron una adopción limitada y salieron del uso oficial en pocas décadas.","pt":"Em 1448, o rei Sejong encomendou um dicionário para padronizar as leituras coreanas dos caracteres chineses, tomando como modelo um chinês médio idealizado, extraído dos livros de rimas clássicos. As pronúncias prescritas tiveram pouca adoção e caíram em desuso oficial em poucas décadas.","ru":"В 1448 году король Седжон поручил составить словарь для стандартизации корейского чтения китайских иероглифов — по образцу идеализированного среднекитайского произношения, восстановленного по классическим рифмовникам. Предписанные произношения прижились слабо и через несколько десятилетий вышли из официального употребления.","uk":"У 1448 році король Седжон доручив скласти словник для стандартизації корейського читання китайських ієрогліфів — за зразком ідеалізованої середньокитайської вимови, відтвореної за класичними римівниками. Приписані вимови прижилися слабо й за кілька десятиліть вийшли з офіційного вжитку.","ar":"في عام 1448 كلّف الملك سيجونغ بإعداد معجم لتوحيد قراءات الكوريين للحروف الصينية، مستنداً إلى صينية وسطى مثالية مأخوذة من كتب القوافي الكلاسيكية. ولم يُعتمد النطق المقرر إلا على نطاق محدود، وخرج من الاستعمال الرسمي في غضون عقود.","he":"ב-1448 הזמין המלך סג'ונג מילון שנועד לתקנן את הקריאות הקוריאניות של הסימנים הסיניים, לפי סינית תיכונה אידיאלית השאובה מספרי החרוזים הקלאסיים. ההגיות שנקבעו בו התקבלו רק במידה מוגבלת ויצאו משימוש רשמי בתוך עשרות שנים.","sw":"Mnamo 1448, Mfalme Sejong aliagiza kuundwa kwa kamusi ya kusanifisha matamshi ya herufi za Kichina katika Kikorea, ikiiga Kichina cha Kati cha kinadharia kilichotokana na vitabu vya vina vya kale. Matamshi yaliyoainishwa yalikubaliwa kwa kiasi kidogo na yaliacha kutumika rasmi ndani ya miongo michache."},"body":{"en":"<h3>A Dictionary for a Language No One Spoke</h3>\n<p>In 1447–1448, King Sejong of Joseon oversaw the compilation of the <strong>東國正韻</strong> (<em>Dongguk Jeongun</em>, \"Correct Rhymes of the Eastern Kingdom\"), a six-volume rhyme dictionary that sorted characters into 91 rhymes and 23 initials and prescribed a \"correct\" Sino-Korean reading for each. It was a careful piece of work, but its readings never caught on.</p>\n\n<div class=\"trivia-actions\">\n  <button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"setchar\" data-char=\"人\">👤 See 人 across languages</button>\n  <button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"focus\" data-code=\"ko\">📍 Focus on Korean on the map</button>\n  <button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"panto\" data-lat=\"37.57\" data-lng=\"126.98\" data-zoom=\"5\">🗺 Pan to Seoul</button>\n</div>\n\n<p>The problem Sejong was trying to solve was real: by the 15th century, Korean readers had been pronouncing Chinese characters in ways that had diverged significantly from contemporary Chinese norms. Each hanja (Korean term for Chinese characters) had accumulated a Sino-Korean reading (한자음) shaped by centuries of phonological change in Korean, not Chinese. Sejong, the same king who had just created Hangul in 1443, believed the readings had become corrupt and should be restored to a systematic standard. The preface by Shin Suk-ju lists the \"errors\": Korean readings had no voiced initials, confused aspirated and plain <em>k</em>, did not distinguish the rising and departing tones, and ended entering-tone syllables that should close in <em>-t</em> with an <em>-l</em> instead.</p>\n\n<h3>An Idealized Chinese as the Gold Standard</h3>\n<p>The compilers, led by scholar Shin Suk-ju, did not model their pronunciations on contemporary Ming dynasty speech. Instead they worked from the classical rhyme-book tradition — the Yuan <strong>古今韻會擧要</strong> (<em>Gujin Yunhui Juyao</em>, 1297), which Sejong had ordered translated into the new script in 1444, and the Ming <strong>洪武正韻</strong> (<em>Hongwu Zhengyun</em>, 1375), whose title and format the Dongguk Jeongun deliberately echoes. What those books encode is a literary standard rooted in the Middle Chinese rhyme tradition, not the living speech of any community. The compilers blended that system with the shapes of existing Korean readings, producing a compromise that matched neither Chinese nor Korean speech.</p>\n\n<p>The result was a set of pronunciations that:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Brought back voiced initials, written with doubled letters (ㄲ ㄸ ㅃ ㅉ ㅆ ㆅ), which Sino-Korean had never had</li>\n  <li>Replaced the Korean final <em>-l</em> in entering-tone words (Chinese <em>-t</em>) with <strong>ㅭ</strong>, ㄹ plus the glottal-stop letter <strong>ㆆ</strong> (Korean already had <em>-p</em> and <em>-k</em>, so only <em>-t</em> needed \"fixing\")</li>\n  <li>Gave every syllable a final letter, even open ones: a silent ㅇ, or ㅱ for a <em>-w</em> ending</li>\n  <li>Marked rising and departing tones apart, as the Chinese rhyme books did</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>You can see the system in the Korean translation of the <em>Hunminjeongeum</em> itself, where every character carries its prescribed reading; in a single line describing the letter ㆆ, 字 'character' is written ᄍᆞᆼ (today 자), 發 'emit' 버ᇙ (today 발), 喉 'throat' ᅘᅮᇢ (today 후), with a doubled initial, a silent final, a ㅭ and a ㅱ in just three syllables.</p>\n\n<h3>Extinct Letters for an Imaginary Sound</h3>\n<p>The <strong>ㆆ</strong> and <strong>ㅭ</strong> letters are the oddest legacy of the Dongguk Jeongun. Sejong's Hangul, announced in 1443, was designed with great phonetic precision — each letter's shape supposedly reflecting the position of the speech organs. The <strong>ㅭ</strong> digraph the compilers devised for the entering-tone <em>-t</em> stood for no sound of ordinary Korean at all, and it vanished with the readings it served. ㆆ had a little more life: in native Korean it turns up in a few grammatical spots, as in 先考ㆆ ᄠᅳᆮ 'my late father's will' in the <em>Yongbi eocheon ga</em> (1447) or 니르고져 호ᇙ 배 'what (they) would wish to say' in the <em>Hunminjeongeum</em> translation. But its main job was the Dongguk Jeongun readings, and it disappeared with them. (ㅸ, by contrast, was a genuine letter of native Korean, a soft <em>b/v</em>-like sound in words such as 셔ᄫᅳᆯ 'Seoul' and 고ᄫᅡ 'beautiful'; it died out on its own within a few decades as the sound turned into <em>w</em>, which is why today's forms are 서울 and 고와.) Modern scholars studying the history of the Hangul alphabet must account for letters that died with the dictionary they were made for.</p>\n\n<h3>Abandoned but Not Lost</h3>\n<p>Even while it was official, the system lived mostly in print, above all in Hangul translations of Buddhist scriptures, and by the early 16th century it was no longer used at all. By the time of <em>Hunmongjahoe</em> (1527), practical character dictionaries simply recorded the pronunciations Koreans actually used. But the dictionary's failure became linguistics' treasure. Its meticulous Hangul spellings show exactly how 15th-century Korean scholars mapped the Chinese rhyme-book categories onto their own sounds, and, through the \"errors\" they set out to fix, they record what real Sino-Korean sounded like at the time. For Ming Chinese itself the better witness is its sister project, the <em>Hongwu Zhengyun Yeokhun</em> (1455), which transcribed the Chinese rhyme book in Hangul.</p>"},"sources":["Lee Ki-moon & Ramsey, S. Robert (2011). A History of the Korean Language. Cambridge University Press.","Ledyard, Gari K. (1966). The Korean Language Reform of 1446. PhD dissertation, University of California, Berkeley.","Nam Kwang-wu (1995). \"Dongguk Jeongun and the Reconstruction of Middle Korean Pronunciation.\" Korean Linguistics 7: 45–78.",{"text":"Dongguk Jeongun — National Institute of Korean Language","url":"https://www.korean.go.kr/front/etcData/etcDataView.do?mn_id=46&etc_seq=71"},"Pulleyblank, E.G. (1984). Middle Chinese: A Study in Historical Phonology. University of British Columbia Press.",{"text":"Wikisource: 東國正韻 facsimile (1448)","url":"https://ko.wikisource.org/wiki/%EB%8F%99%EA%B5%AD%EC%A0%95%EC%9A%B4"}]},{"id":"kokugo-versus-kango","icon":"📖","tags":["japan","vocabulary","etymology","loanwords","strata"],"title":{"en":"和語・漢語・外来語: Japan's Three-Layer Vocabulary","ja":"和語・漢語・外来語：日本語の三層語彙","ko":"和語・漢語・外来語: 일본어의 세 가지 어휘 층위","zh":"和语・汉语・外来语：日语的三层词汇","yue":"和語・漢語・外来語：日語嘅三層詞彙","vi":"和語・漢語・外来語: ba tầng từ vựng của tiếng Nhật","th":"和語・漢語・外来語: สามชั้นคำศัพท์ในภาษาญี่ปุ่น","id":"和語・漢語・外来語: Tiga Lapisan Kosakata Bahasa Jepang","hi":"和語・漢語・外来語: जापानी भाषा की तीन-परतीय शब्द-संपदा","de":"和語・漢語・外来語: Die drei Wortschatzschichten des Japanischen","fr":"和語・漢語・外来語 : les trois couches du vocabulaire japonais","it":"和語・漢語・外来語: i tre strati del lessico giapponese","es":"和語・漢語・外来語: las tres capas del vocabulario japonés","pt":"和語・漢語・外来語: as três camadas do vocabulário japonês","ru":"和語・漢語・外来語: три пласта японской лексики","uk":"和語・漢語・外来語: Три шари японської лексики","ar":"和語・漢語・外来語: الطبقات الثلاث لمفردات اللغة اليابانية","he":"和語・漢語・外来語: שלוש שכבות אוצר המילים של היפנית","sw":"和語・漢語・外来語: safu tatu za msamiati wa Kijapani"},"summary":{"en":"Japanese vocabulary has three major strata — native Yamato words (和語), Sino-Japanese loans (漢語), and Western loans (外来語). Each has characteristic writing conventions, sound patterns, and cultural associations.","ja":"日本語の語彙は「和語」「漢語」「外来語」の三層に分かれ、それぞれ固有の表記体系・音声・文化的語感を持つ。","ko":"일본어 어휘는 야마토어(和語), 한자어(漢語), 서양 외래어(外来語)의 세 층위로 나뉘며, 각각 고유한 표기 관습, 음운 특징, 문화적 어감을 지닌다.","zh":"日语词汇分为三层：本土大和语（和语）、汉语借词（汉语）和西洋借词（外来语）——各自拥有独特的书写体系、语音和文化语感。","yue":"日語詞彙分為三層：本土大和語（和語）、漢語借詞（漢語）同西洋借詞（外来語）——每層都有自己獨特嘅書寫系統、音韻同文化語感。","vi":"Từ vựng tiếng Nhật có ba tầng chính — từ Yamato bản địa (和語), từ vay mượn gốc Hán-Nhật (漢語) và từ vay mượn phương Tây (外来語) — mỗi tầng có quy ước viết, đặc điểm âm và sắc thái văn hóa riêng.","th":"คำศัพท์ภาษาญี่ปุ่นแบ่งออกเป็นสามชั้น ได้แก่ คำยามาโตะพื้นเมือง (和語) คำยืมจากภาษาจีน-ญี่ปุ่น (漢語) และคำยืมจากภาษาตะวันตก (外来語) แต่ละชั้นมีวิธีเขียน ลักษณะเสียง และระดับภาษาของตัวเอง","id":"Kosakata bahasa Jepang punya tiga lapisan utama: kata asli Yamato (和語), pinjaman Sino-Jepang (漢語), dan pinjaman Barat (外来語). Masing-masing punya kebiasaan tulis, pola bunyi, dan nuansa budaya yang khas.","hi":"जापानी शब्द-संपदा की तीन मुख्य परतें हैं: मूल यामातो शब्द (和語), चीनी-जापानी ऋण शब्द (漢語) और पश्चिमी ऋण शब्द (外来語)। हर परत के लिखने के अपने ढंग हैं, अपनी ध्वनि-विशेषताएँ हैं और अपना सांस्कृतिक रंग।","de":"Der japanische Wortschatz hat drei Hauptschichten: einheimische Yamato-Wörter (和語), sinojapanische Lehnwörter (漢語) und westliche Lehnwörter (外来語). Jede hat typische Schreibkonventionen, Lautmuster und kulturelle Assoziationen.","fr":"Le vocabulaire japonais compte trois grandes couches : les mots yamato indigènes (和語), les emprunts sino-japonais (漢語) et les emprunts occidentaux (外来語). Chacune a ses conventions d'écriture, ses schémas sonores et ses connotations culturelles.","it":"Il vocabolario giapponese ha tre strati principali: le parole yamato native (和語), i prestiti sino-giapponesi (漢語) e i prestiti occidentali (外来語). Ciascuno ha convenzioni di scrittura, schemi sonori e associazioni culturali caratteristici.","es":"El vocabulario japonés tiene tres grandes capas: palabras yamato nativas (和語), préstamos sino-japoneses (漢語) y préstamos occidentales (外来語). Cada una tiene sus propias convenciones de escritura, patrones sonoros y asociaciones culturales.","pt":"O vocabulário japonês tem três grandes camadas: palavras yamato nativas (和語), empréstimos sino-japoneses (漢語) e empréstimos ocidentais (外来語). Cada uma tem convenções de escrita, padrões sonoros e associações culturais característicos.","ru":"Японская лексика делится на три слоя — исконные ямато-слова (和語), китайско-японские заимствования (漢語) и западные заимствования (外来語) — каждый со своей системой письма, фонологией и культурным регистром.","uk":"Японська лексика складається з трьох шарів: питомих слів ямато (和語), китайсько-японських запозичень (漢語) і західних запозичень (外来語), і в кожного своє письмо, своя фонологія й свій культурний регістр.","ar":"تنقسم المفردات اليابانية إلى ثلاث طبقات: الكلمات اليابانية الأصيلة (和語)، والكلمات الصينية المستعارة (漢語)، والكلمات الغربية المستعارة (外来語)؛ ولكل منها نظام كتابة وأصوات وسجل ثقافي خاص.","he":"אוצר המילים היפני מחולק לשלוש שכבות: מילים יפניות מקוריות (和語), מילים סיניות-יפניות שאולות (漢語) ומילים שאולות מהמערב (外来語). לכל אחת מהן כתיב, פונולוגיה ומשלב תרבותי משלה.","sw":"Msamiati wa Kijapani una safu tatu kuu: maneno ya asili ya Yamato (和語), maneno ya mkopo ya Kisino-Kijapani (漢語) na maneno ya mkopo ya Kimagharibi (外来語). Kila safu ina desturi zake za uandishi, mifumo yake ya sauti na uhusiano wake wa kitamaduni."},"body":{"en":"<h3>Three Lexical Strata</h3>\n<p>Japanese vocabulary is often described in terms of three historical strata. <strong>和語</strong> (<em>wago</em>, \"Japanese-language words\") are the native core, the words Japanese already had before Chinese writing arrived. (A few very early borrowings slipped in so long ago that they now pass as wago: 梅 <em>ume</em>, \"plum\", is probably Chinese 梅 in disguise.) <strong>漢語</strong> (<em>kango</em>, \"Han-language words\") are borrowings that arrived with Buddhism, Confucianism, and literacy from China, starting roughly in the 5th century CE. <strong>外来語</strong> (<em>gairaigo</em>, \"words from outside\") form a later borrowing layer, chiefly from Portuguese, Dutch, English, German and French, beginning with European contact from 1543, when a Portuguese ship first reached Japan.</p>\n\n<div class=\"trivia-actions\">\n  <button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"setchar\" data-char=\"山\">🏔 See 山 (yama / san) across languages</button>\n  <button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"focus\" data-code=\"ja_kun\">📍 Focus ja_kun (native readings) on map</button>\n  <button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"focus\" data-code=\"ja\">📍 Focus the Sino-Japanese readings</button>\n</div>\n\n<h3>Distribution by Domain</h3>\n<p>Each stratum dominates in different domains. Surveys find that in speech <strong>wago make up over 70%</strong> of running words (the old words for family, body, weather and feeling) while in newspaper prose <strong>kango pass 70%</strong>, partly because Meiji Japan modernised by coining kango compounds rather than borrowing Western words. The balance keeps moving: in a 1994 magazine survey by Japan's National Institute for Japanese Language and Linguistics (NINJAL), gairaigo made up 33.8% of the different words used, almost level with kango (34.2%) and well ahead of wago (25.7%). Words like 電話 (<em>denwa</em>, telephone), 経済 (<em>keizai</em>, economy, an old Chinese phrase given a new meaning) and 哲学 (<em>tetsugaku</em>, philosophy) were made in Meiji Japan and later exported to China and Korea. 哲学 has a known inventor: the scholar Nishi Amane, who in 1874 settled on it after first trying 希哲学, a calque of Greek <em>philosophia</em>, \"longing for wisdom\".</p>\n\n<p>The contrast among the three strata is visible in words for \"beautiful\":</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>美しい</strong> (<em>utsukushii</em>) — wago; warm and personal. In the Man'yōshū it did not yet mean \"beautiful\" at all: the poet Okura says that seeing his wife and children he finds them <em>megushi utsukushi</em>, \"dear and beloved\". In the Heian period it came to mean \"sweet, lovely\", above all of small things, and only towards the end of the Middle Ages did it widen to the beauty of nature and art.</li>\n  <li><strong>美麗</strong> (<em>birei</em>) — kango; formal, objective, literary, used in prose and criticism</li>\n  <li><strong>ビューティフル</strong> (<em>byūtifuru</em>) — gairaigo; young, fashionable, often ironic or playful</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The three choices differ in register and association. English has its own layered triples inherited from Germanic, French, and Latin — <em>kingly, royal, regal</em>; <em>ask, question, interrogate</em>. Japanese writing often makes the stratum visible through the choice of kanji, hiragana or katakana.</p>\n\n<h3>Writing and Lexical Strata</h3>\n<p>Japanese orthography often encodes stratum identity visually. <strong>Hiragana</strong> and <strong>kanji with kun readings</strong> signal wago. <strong>Kanji with on readings</strong> signal kango. <strong>Katakana</strong> signals gairaigo (and scientific names). So a reader can often guess a word's history before knowing what it means. A sentence like <em>私は毎日コーヒーを飲む</em> (\"I drink coffee every day\") contains all three strata: 私 (wago), 毎日 (kango), コーヒー (gairaigo from Dutch <em>koffie</em>), 飲む (wago).</p>\n\n<div class=\"trivia-actions\">\n  <button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"setchar\" data-char=\"天\">☀️ See 天 (ame/sora/ten) in all layers</button>\n  <button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"panto\" data-lat=\"35.68\" data-lng=\"139.69\" data-zoom=\"5\">🗾 Pan to Japan</button>\n</div>\n\n<h3>When the Strata Collide</h3>\n<p>The three-stratum model breaks down at the edges. Many words are hybrids: <strong>頭痛持ち</strong> (<em>zutsū-mochi</em>, \"someone prone to headaches\") welds the kango 頭痛 to the wago 持ち. <strong>チョコレート工場</strong> (chokorēto kōjō) mixes gairaigo (チョコレート, from English <em>chocolate</em>) with kango (工場 <em>kōjō</em>, factory). Some words have been borrowed so long they feel native — <em>tabako</em> (tobacco, from Portuguese <em>tabaco</em>, brought in around 1600) feels as Japanese as <em>sakura</em>.</p>\n\n<p>Mixed forms have their own label: <strong>混種語</strong> (<em>konshugo</em>, \"mixed-type words\"), which make up about 8% of the general words in one standard modern dictionary (<em>Shinsen Kokugo Jiten</em>, 2002). <strong>テレビ局</strong> (<em>terebi-kyoku</em>, TV station) fuses English gairaigo テレビ with kango 局. Hybrids like these are common, and they show how leaky the three-way scheme is.</p>\n\n<p>The three-stratum model is a simplification, but a useful one. A Japanese writer who picks 美しい over 美麗 or ビューティフル is choosing a script and a tone of voice as well as a meaning.</p>"},"sources":["国立国語研究所『現代雑誌九十種の用語用字』（国立国語研究所報告 21・22・25）東京：秀英出版, 1962–1964.","Shibatani, Masayoshi (1990). The Languages of Japan. Cambridge University Press. Chapter 6: Lexicon.","Frellesvig, Bjarke (2010). A History of the Japanese Language. Cambridge University Press.","Loveday, Leo J. (1996). Language Contact in Japan: A Socio-Linguistic History. Oxford University Press.",{"text":"NHK Web Easy: 外来語・和語・漢語 (with audio)","url":"https://www3.nhk.or.jp/news/web_easy/"},{"text":"Kotobank: 和語・漢語・外来語 解説","url":"https://kotobank.jp/word/%E5%92%8C%E8%AA%9E-653004"},{"text":"Japanese Wikipedia: 語種 (NINJAL survey figures)","url":"https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/語種"},{"text":"Kotobank: うつくしい (Nihon Kokugo Daijiten, Digital Daijisen)","url":"https://kotobank.jp/word/%E7%BE%8E%E3%81%97%E3%81%84-438400"}]},{"id":"tang-empire-multilingual-coins","icon":"🪙","tags":["sinosphere","kanji","history","phonology"],"title":{"en":"One Coin, Four Readings: The Tang Dynasty's Multilingual Currency","ja":"一枚の銅銭、四つの読み方：唐の開元通寶が繋いだ世界","ko":"동전 하나, 네 가지 읽기: 당나라 개원통보의 다언어 세계","zh":"一枚铜币，四种读法：唐代開元通寶的多语言旅程","yue":"一個銅錢，四種讀法：唐朝開元通寶嘅多語言之旅","vi":"Một đồng tiền, bốn cách đọc: hành trình đa ngôn ngữ của Khai Nguyên Thông Bảo","th":"เหรียญเดียว สี่สำเนียง: การเดินทางข้ามภาษาของไคหยวนถงเป่า","id":"Satu koin, empat bacaan: perjalanan multibahasa uang Tang","hi":"एक सिक्का, चार पाठ: तांग राजवंश की बहुभाषी मुद्रा की यात्रा","de":"Eine Münze, vier Lesungen: Die mehrsprachige Reise des Tang-Kupfergeldes","fr":"Une pièce, quatre lectures : le voyage multilingue de la monnaie Tang","it":"Una moneta, quattro letture: il viaggio della moneta Tang","es":"Una moneda, cuatro lecturas: el viaje multilingüe de la moneda Tang","pt":"Uma moeda, quatro leituras: a jornada multilíngue da moeda Tang","ru":"Одна монета, четыре прочтения: многоязычное путешествие танской монеты","uk":"Одна монета, чотири прочитання: багатомовна подорож танської монети","ar":"عملة واحدة، أربع قراءات: رحلة عملة تانغ متعددة اللغات","he":"מטבע אחד, ארבע קריאות: מסעה הרב-לשוני של מטבע שושלת טאנג","sw":"Sarafu moja, usomaji minne: safari ya lugha nyingi ya sarafu ya Tang"},"summary":{"en":"The Tang dynasty's 開元通寶 coin, minted from 621 CE, circulated across Asia. Its four-character inscription received different local readings in Chinese, Japanese, Korean and Vietnamese.","ja":"621年から鋳造された唐の開元通寶はアジア各地に広まった。その四文字の銘は、中国語・日本語・朝鮮語・ベトナム語でそれぞれ違う読み方をされた。","ko":"621년부터 주조된 당나라 개원통보는 아시아 곳곳에 퍼졌다. 그 네 글자는 중국어, 일본어, 한국어, 베트남어에서 저마다 다르게 읽혔다.","zh":"唐代自621年起铸造的開元通寶流通至亚洲各地，钱文四字在汉语、日语、韩语和越南语中各有不同的读法。","yue":"唐朝由621年起鑄造嘅開元通寶流通到亞洲各地，錢上四個字喺中文、日文、韓文同越南文入面各有各嘅讀法。","vi":"Đồng Khai Nguyên Thông Bảo của nhà Đường, đúc từ năm 621, lưu hành khắp châu Á. Bốn chữ trên đồng tiền được đọc theo cách riêng ở mỗi nơi, trong tiếng Trung, Nhật, Hàn và Việt.","th":"เหรียญไคหยวนถงเป่าของราชวงศ์ถัง ผลิตตั้งแต่ปี ค.ศ. 621 แพร่กระจายทั่วเอเชีย อักษรสี่ตัวบนเหรียญอ่านต่างกันไปในภาษาจีน ญี่ปุ่น เกาหลี และเวียดนาม","id":"Koin 開元通寶 Dinasti Tang, yang dicetak sejak 621 M, beredar di seluruh Asia. Keempat aksaranya dibaca berbeda dalam bahasa Tionghoa, Jepang, Korea, dan Vietnam.","hi":"621 ई. से ढाली गई तांग राजवंश की 開元通寶 मुद्रा पूरे एशिया में चली। इसके चार अक्षरों को चीनी, जापानी, कोरियाई और वियतनामी में अलग-अलग स्थानीय पाठ मिले।","de":"Die Tang-Münze 開元通寶, ab 621 n. Chr. geprägt, war in ganz Asien im Umlauf. Ihre vier Zeichen wurden auf Chinesisch, Japanisch, Koreanisch und Vietnamesisch jeweils anders gelesen.","fr":"La pièce 開元通寶 de la dynastie Tang, frappée dès 621, a circulé à travers l'Asie. Ses quatre caractères ont reçu des lectures locales différentes en chinois, japonais, coréen et vietnamien.","it":"La moneta 開元通寶 della dinastia Tang, coniata dal 621, circolò in tutta l'Asia. I suoi quattro caratteri ricevettero letture locali diverse in cinese, giapponese, coreano e vietnamita.","es":"La moneda 開元通寶 de la dinastía Tang, acuñada desde el 621, circuló por toda Asia. Sus cuatro caracteres recibieron lecturas locales distintas en chino, japonés, coreano y vietnamita.","pt":"A moeda 開元通寶 da dinastia Tang, cunhada a partir de 621, circulou pela Ásia. Sua inscrição de quatro caracteres recebeu leituras locais diferentes em chinês, japonês, coreano e vietnamita.","ru":"Монета 開元通寶 династии Тан, чеканившаяся с 621 года, ходила по всей Азии. Её надпись из четырёх иероглифов получила разные местные чтения на китайском, японском, корейском и вьетнамском.","uk":"Монета 開元通寶 династії Тан, яку карбували з 621 року, ходила по всій Азії. Її напис із чотирьох ієрогліфів дістав різні місцеві читання китайською, японською, корейською та в'єтнамською.","ar":"انتشرت عملة 開元通寶 لأسرة تانغ، المسكوكة منذ عام 621م، في أرجاء آسيا. وقد تلقّت أحرفها الأربعة قراءات محلية مختلفة في الصينية واليابانية والكورية والفيتنامية.","he":"מטבע 開元通寶 של שושלת טאנג, שנטבע משנת 621 לספירה, נפוץ ברחבי אסיה. ארבעת סימניו זכו לקריאות מקומיות שונות בסינית, ביפנית, בקוריאנית ובווייטנאמית.","sw":"Sarafu 開元通寶 ya nasaba ya Tang, iliyopigwa tangu mwaka 621 BK, ilisambaa kote Asia. Maandishi yake ya herufi nne yalisomwa kwa njia tofauti za kienyeji katika Kichina, Kijapani, Kikorea na Kivietinamu."},"body":{"en":"<h3>A Coin That Crossed Civilizations</h3><p>In 621 CE, the Tang court issued a new bronze coin inscribed with four characters: <strong>開元通寶</strong> (Kāiyuán tōngbǎo). The name means roughly \"circulating treasure of the new beginning\": 開元 hailed the new dynasty and was not a reign title (the famous Kaiyuan reign of Emperor Xuanzong began only in 713). Cast for almost three centuries, it became one of the most widely travelled coins in Asian history. Sogdian mints in Samarkand copied its design, Xinjiang has yielded hoards of thousands, and strings of Kaiyuan coins went down with a ninth-century ship bound from China for the Middle East, wrecked off Belitung in the Java Sea.</p><p>The 開元通寶 also shows one written inscription travelling among peoples who each read it in their own way. Even in China people disagreed about the order: read around the square hole instead of top-bottom-right-left, the legend comes out as 開通元寶, and that misreading was common enough to inspire the \"yuanbao\" names of later coins.</p><ul><li><strong>Chinese (Mandarin):</strong> kāiyuán tōngbǎo — the modern standard reading; the Tang court itself said something closer to <em>khoj ngjwon thuwng pawX</em> (Baxter's Middle Chinese notation, where a final -X marks the rising tone)</li><li><strong>Japanese:</strong> kaigen tsūhō — a Sino-Japanese reading that is mostly 漢音, though 通 <em>tsū</em> is not the Kan-on <em>tō</em> (dictionaries file it as 呉音 or 慣用音); Japan's own earliest coins, the Fuhonsen and then the Wadō kaichin of 708, were modelled on it, calligraphy and all</li><li><strong>Korean:</strong> gaewon tongbo — the Sino-Korean reading, still used in numismatic literature today</li><li><strong>Vietnamese:</strong> khai nguyên thông bảo — the Sino-Vietnamese reading, preserving features of late-Tang (8th–9th century) Chinese phonology that Mandarin later lost</li></ul><p>The divergence is most audible in the second character, 元. In Mandarin it is <em>yuán</em>; in Japanese <em>gen</em>; in Vietnamese <em>nguyên</em>. The Tang syllable began with a velar nasal (<em>ngjwon</em>): Vietnamese keeps the ng- intact, Japanese keeps a trace of it as g-, and Mandarin has lost it entirely, because the borrowed readings froze while Mandarin went on changing.</p><p>From a temple pagoda site in Japan's Nara Prefecture to the oases of the Silk Road and the seabed of the Java Sea, the coins all bear the same inscription. The characters were the passport; the pronunciation was the local accent.</p>"},"sources":["Peng Xinwei, A Monetary History of China (中国货币史), Shanghai People's Press, 1965","Edward Schafer, The Golden Peaches of Samarkand, UC Press, 1963",{"text":"奈良国立博物館 — 開元通宝（奈良県霊安寺塔跡出土）","url":"https://www.narahaku.go.jp/collection/1059-7.html"},"Axel Schuessler, ABC Etymological Dictionary of Old Chinese, University of Hawaii Press, 2007",{"text":"Wikipedia: Kaiyuan Tongbao","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kaiyuan_Tongbao"},{"text":"Roots.sg (National Heritage Board, Singapore): Bronze coins from the Tang Shipwreck","url":"https://www.roots.gov.sg/Collection-Landing/listing/1279376"}]},{"id":"korean-eumhun-tradition","icon":"📖","tags":["sinosphere","kanji","korea","pedagogy","hanja"],"title":{"en":"물 수 (水): Why Korea Teaches Chinese Characters with Native Korean Glosses","ja":"물 수（水）：韓国が漢字を固有語の訓で教える理由","ko":"물 수(水): 한국이 한자를 고유어 훈으로 가르치는 이유","zh":"물 수（水）：韩国为何以固有语训读教授汉字","yue":"물 수（水）：韓國點解用固有語嚟教漢字","vi":"물 수(水): vì sao Hàn Quốc dạy chữ Hán bằng cách chú giải tiếng Hàn thuần","th":"물 수 (水): ทำไมเกาหลีสอนอักษรจีนพร้อมคำแปลภาษาเกาหลี","id":"물 수 (水): mengapa Korea mengajarkan aksara Tionghoa dengan glosa Korea asli","hi":"물 수 (水): कोरिया चीनी अक्षर देशज कोरियाई टिप्पणियों के साथ क्यों सिखाता है","de":"물 수 (水): Warum Korea chinesische Schriftzeichen mit koreanischen Glossen lehrt","fr":"물 수 (水) : pourquoi la Corée enseigne les caractères chinois avec des gloses coréennes natives","it":"물 수 (水): perché la Corea insegna i caratteri cinesi con glosse coreane","es":"물 수 (水): por qué Corea enseña los caracteres chinos con glosas coreanas nativas","pt":"물 수 (水): por que a Coreia ensina caracteres chineses com glosas coreanas nativas","ru":"물 수 (水): почему Корея преподаёт китайские иероглифы с корейскими глоссами","uk":"물 수 (水): Чому Корея викладає китайські ієрогліфи з корейськими глосами","ar":"물 수 (水): لماذا تُدرّس كوريا الأحرف الصينية بتعليقات كورية أصيلة","he":"물 수 (水): למה קוריאה מלמדת סימנים סיניים עם ביאור קוריאני","sw":"물 수 (水): kwa nini Korea hufundisha herufi za Kichina na maelezo ya Kikorea asili"},"summary":{"en":"Korean schools teach a hanja character with a native Korean meaning label (訓, hun) and a Sino-Korean reading (音, eum). Unlike Japanese kun-readings, the Korean gloss normally names the character rather than serving as its reading in ordinary text.","ja":"韓国の学校では各漢字を固有語の意味（訓）と朝鮮漢字音（音）の二つで教えるが、これは千字文に遡る教育伝統であり、日本が訓読みを実際の書き言葉で使うのとは根本的に異なる。","ko":"한국 학교에서는 한자를 고유어 뜻풀이(훈)와 한자음(음)으로 가르친다. 일본어의 훈독과 달리, 한국의 훈은 일반적인 글에서 그 글자를 읽는 말로 쓰이지 않고 보통 글자의 이름 구실을 한다.","zh":"韩国学校以固有语义（训）和汉字音（音）两种方式教授每个汉字，这一源于《千字文》的教学传统揭示了与日本在日常书写中使用训读的深层结构差异。","yue":"韓國學校用固有語義（訓）同漢字音（音）兩種方式教每個漢字，呢個源自《千字文》嘅教學傳統，揭示咗同日本日常書寫中使用訓讀嘅根本結構差異。","vi":"Trường học Hàn Quốc dạy mỗi chữ Hán bằng hai nhãn: nghĩa thuần Hàn (訓, hun) và âm Hán-Hàn (音, eum). Khác với âm kun của tiếng Nhật, phần nghĩa thuần Hàn thường chỉ dùng để gọi tên chữ, không phải cách đọc chữ đó trong văn bản thông thường.","th":"โรงเรียนเกาหลีสอนฮันจาแต่ละตัวพร้อมสองป้าย คือความหมายเป็นคำเกาหลีพื้นเมือง (訓, hun) และเสียงอ่านฮัน-เกาหลี (音, eum) ธรรมเนียมนี้มีรากมาจากชอนจามุน และต่างจากญี่ปุ่นที่ใช้เสียงอ่านคุนในการเขียนจริงทุกวัน","id":"Sekolah Korea mengajarkan hanja dengan label makna dalam kata Korea asli (訓, hun) dan bacaan Sino-Korea (音, eum). Berbeda dengan bacaan kun Jepang, glosa Korea itu biasanya hanya menjadi nama aksaranya, bukan bacaannya dalam teks biasa.","hi":"कोरियाई स्कूल हांजा अक्षर को देशज कोरियाई अर्थ-लेबल (訓, हुन) और चीनी-कोरियाई पठन (音, ईउम) के साथ सिखाते हैं। जापानी कुन-पठन के विपरीत, यह कोरियाई टिप्पणी आम तौर पर अक्षर का नाम बताती है, सामान्य पाठ में उसका पठन नहीं बनती।","de":"Koreanische Schulen lehren ein Hanja-Zeichen mit einer koreanischen Bedeutungsangabe (訓, hun) und einer sinokoreanischen Lesung (音, eum). Anders als die japanischen Kun-Lesungen benennt die koreanische Glosse das Zeichen normalerweise nur und dient nicht als seine Lesung im gewöhnlichen Text.","fr":"Les écoles coréennes enseignent un caractère Hanja avec une étiquette de sens en coréen natif (訓, hun) et une lecture sino-coréenne (音, eum). Contrairement aux lectures kun japonaises, la glose coréenne sert normalement à nommer le caractère, non à le lire dans un texte ordinaire.","it":"Le scuole coreane insegnano un carattere Hanja con un'etichetta di significato in coreano nativo (訓, hun) e una lettura sino-coreana (音, eum). A differenza delle letture kun giapponesi, la glossa coreana di norma dà il nome al carattere e non ne è la lettura nel testo comune.","es":"Las escuelas coreanas enseñan un carácter Hanja con una etiqueta de significado en coreano nativo (訓, hun) y una lectura sino-coreana (音, eum). A diferencia de las lecturas kun japonesas, la glosa coreana normalmente nombra el carácter y no sirve como su lectura en un texto corriente.","pt":"As escolas coreanas ensinam um caractere Hanja com um rótulo de significado em coreano nativo (訓, hun) e uma leitura sino-coreana (音, eum). Ao contrário do kun-yomi japonês, a glosa coreana normalmente dá nome ao caractere, em vez de servir como sua leitura no texto comum.","ru":"Корейские школы преподают иероглиф ханча с корейской пометкой значения (訓, хун) и сино-корейским чтением (音, ым). В отличие от японских кун-чтений, корейская глосса обычно называет иероглиф, а не служит его чтением в обычном тексте.","uk":"Корейські школи вчать ієрогліф ханча з корейською позначкою значення (訓, хун) і сино-корейським читанням (音, им). На відміну від японських кун-читань, корейська глоса зазвичай називає ієрогліф, а не слугує його читанням у звичайному тексті.","ar":"تُدرّس المدارس الكورية كل حرف هانجا بمعنى كوري أصيل (訓، hun) وقراءة صينية-كورية (音، eum). وخلافًا لقراءات كون اليابانية، فإن الشرح الكوري يسمّي الحرف عادةً ولا يكون قراءةً له في النص العادي.","he":"בבתי הספר בקוריאה לומדים כל סימן הַנְג'ה יחד עם מילה קוריאנית שמבארת את משמעותו (訓, hun) ועם הקריאה הסינו-קוריאנית שלו (音, eum). בניגוד לקריאות הקוּן היפניות, הביאור הקוריאני בדרך כלל רק נותן לסימן שם, ואינו משמש לקריאתו בטקסט רגיל.","sw":"Shule za Korea hufundisha herufi ya Hanja pamoja na lebo ya maana kwa Kikorea asilia (訓, hun) na usomaji wa Kisino-Kikorea (音, eum). Tofauti na usomaji wa kun wa Kijapani, maelezo ya Kikorea kwa kawaida hutaja herufi, badala ya kuwa usomaji wake katika maandishi ya kawaida."},"body":{"en":"<h3>The Two-Label System</h3><p>Open any Korean hanja textbook and you will find a distinctive pattern: each character is presented with two pieces of information. The character 水 appears as <strong>물 수 (水)</strong> — first the native Korean word <em>mul</em> (water), then the Sino-Korean syllable <em>su</em>. This pairing is called <strong>音訓</strong> (음훈, eum-hun): sound plus meaning.</p><p>The habit is centuries old. Korean children learned characters from the <strong>千字文</strong> (Thousand Character Classic), a 6th-century Chinese primer, and once the Korean alphabet existed (1446) primers could spell out the gloss and the sound of every single character. The <em>Hunmong chahoe</em> of 1527 does this for 3,360 characters; the oldest surviving glossed Thousand Character Classic was printed in Gwangju in 1575, and the most famous, written out by the calligrapher Han Seok-bong, in 1583. The format has barely changed since.</p><h3>More Pairs</h3><ul><li>山 = <strong>메 산</strong> (me san) — mountain. <em>Me</em> is an old word that modern Korean has otherwise dropped in favour of 산 itself; it survives mainly here and in compounds like 멧돼지 <em>metdwaeji</em>, \"mountain pig\", the wild boar</li><li>人 = <strong>사람 인</strong> (saram in) — person</li><li>木 = <strong>나무 목</strong> (namu mok) — tree</li><li>日 = <strong>날 일</strong> (nal il) — day (the gloss picks the \"day\" sense; \"sun\" in Korean is 해 <em>hae</em>)</li></ul><h3>Why This Is Not the Same as Japanese 訓読み</h3><p>A common mistake is to assume Korean 훈 (hun) functions like Japanese 訓読み (kun-yomi). The difference is fundamental: in Japanese, kun-yomi are <em>active reading strategies</em> used every day in writing — a text about water will actually use the word <em>mizu</em> (訓 of 水) written with the character. Korean hun, by contrast, is a <em>pedagogical memory aid</em>. Today nobody writes 水 and expects it to be read as <em>mul</em>; in modern Korean text, 水 is read as Sino-Korean <em>su</em>.</p><p>It was not always so. Old Korean scribes writing their own language in characters (the <em>idu</em> and <em>hyangchal</em> systems) did read some characters by their native meaning, much as Japanese still does. A trace even survives in the names of the Korean letters, first recorded in that 1527 primer. Its author, Ch'oe Sejin, could find no Chinese character pronounced <em>-geut</em> or <em>-ot</em>. So he spelled the name of ㄷ as 池末 and ㅅ as 時衣, and marked 末 \"end\" and 衣 \"clothes\" to be read by their native glosses, 귿 and 옷. That is why the letters are still called 디귿 <em>digeut</em> and 시옷 <em>siot</em>.</p><p>The hun is a mnemonic anchor, not a live reading. It tells the learner: this abstract character means the same thing as the native word you already know. Once learned, the character is used only with its Sino-Korean sound in actual writing.</p><p>This makes Korean hanja usage structurally closer to how European learners approach Greek or Latin roots — you know that <em>aqua</em> means water, which helps you decode <em>aquifer</em>, but you never write <em>aqua</em> in an English sentence.</p><div class=\"trivia-actions\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"focus\" data-code=\"ko\">Focus Korean on map</button></div>"},"sources":["이기문 (Lee Ki-Moon), 『訓蒙字會 硏究』 (Hunmong chahoe yŏn'gu), Seoul National University Press, 1993","Iksop Lee & S. Robert Ramsey, The Korean Language, State University of New York Press, 2000",{"text":"National Institute of Korean Language — Hanja education overview","url":"https://www.korean.go.kr/"},"Gari Ledyard, The Korean Language Reform of 1446, UC Berkeley dissertation, 1966",{"text":"Korean Wikipedia: 훈몽자회 (Hunmong chahoe; letter names 池末, 時衣)","url":"https://ko.wikipedia.org/wiki/훈몽자회"},{"text":"Korean Wikipedia: 천자문 (Gwangju 1575 and Seokbong 1583 editions)","url":"https://ko.wikipedia.org/wiki/천자문"}]},{"id":"cantonese-script-uniquely","icon":"🀄","tags":["cantonese","writing","sinosphere"],"title":{"en":"Cantonese Characters in Written 粵語白話文","ja":"普通話にない文字：広東語だけの漢字","ko":"광둥어 백화문(粵語白話文)의 광둥어 한자","zh":"普通话没有的字：粤语专用汉字","yue":"普通話冇嘅字：粵語獨有嘅漢字","vi":"Chữ tiếng Quảng Đông trong văn viết 粵語白話文","th":"อักษรที่ภาษาจีนกลางไม่มี: อักษรฮั่นเฉพาะของภาษากวางตุ้ง","id":"Aksara Kanton dalam tulisan 粵語白話文","hi":"लिखित 粵語白話文 में कैंटोनीज़ अक्षर","de":"Kantonesische Schriftzeichen im geschriebenen 粵語白話文","fr":"Les caractères cantonais dans le 粵語白話文 écrit","it":"I caratteri cantonesi nel 粵語白話文 scritto","es":"Los caracteres cantoneses en el 粵語白話文 escrito","pt":"Os caracteres do cantonês escrito (粵語白話文)","ru":"Кантонские иероглифы в письменном 粵語白話文","uk":"Кантонські ієрогліфи в письмовій 粵語白話文","ar":"حروف الكانتونية المكتوبة 粵語白話文","he":"סימנים קנטונזיים בכתיבת 粵語白話文","sw":"Herufi za Kikantoni kilichoandikwa (粵語白話文)"},"summary":{"en":"Written Cantonese (粵語白話文) uses characters such as 嘅, 喺, 唔, 咁, and 啲 to represent Cantonese grammar. These characters appear in Hong Kong newspapers and social media but are generally absent from standard written Mandarin.","ja":"粵語白話文は、嘅・喺・唔・咁・啲など広東語固有の文法を表すために新たに造られた漢字を使用し、香港の新聞やSNSに毎日登場するが、普通話の書き言葉では使われない。","ko":"광둥어 백화문(粵語白話文)은 광둥어 문법을 나타내는 데 嘅·喺·唔·咁·啲 같은 글자를 쓴다. 이 글자들은 홍콩 신문과 소셜 미디어에 등장하지만 표준 중국어 문어에는 대개 나타나지 않는다.","zh":"粤语白话文使用嘅、喺、唔、咁、啲等专为粤语语法而创造的汉字，每天出现在香港报纸和社交媒体上，但在普通话书面语中并不使用。","yue":"粵語白話文用嘅、喺、唔、咁、啲呢啲專門為粵語文法而造嘅字，每日喺香港報紙同社交媒體出現，但係普通話書面語唔會用呢啲字。","vi":"Văn viết tiếng Quảng Đông (粵語白話文) dùng các chữ như 嘅, 喺, 唔, 咁, 啲 để thể hiện ngữ pháp tiếng Quảng Đông. Những chữ này xuất hiện trên báo chí và mạng xã hội Hồng Kông nhưng thường vắng mặt trong văn viết tiếng Phổ Thông chuẩn.","th":"ภาษากวางตุ้งเขียน (粵語白話文) ใช้อักษรฮั่นที่ถูกประดิษฐ์ขึ้นเพื่อไวยากรณ์กวางตุ้งโดยเฉพาะ ได้แก่ 嘅 喺 唔 咁 啲 ปรากฏในหนังสือพิมพ์และโซเชียลมีเดียฮ่องกงทุกวัน แต่ไม่ใช้ในการเขียนภาษาจีนกลางมาตรฐาน","id":"Bahasa Kanton tertulis (粵語白話文) memakai aksara seperti 嘅, 喺, 唔, 咁, dan 啲 untuk menuliskan tata bahasa Kanton. Aksara ini muncul di koran dan media sosial Hong Kong, tetapi umumnya tidak ada dalam tulisan Mandarin baku.","hi":"लिखित कैंटोनीज़ (粵語白話文) में कैंटोनीज़ व्याकरण के लिए 嘅, 喺, 唔, 咁 और 啲 जैसे अक्षरों का उपयोग होता है। ये हांगकांग के समाचार पत्रों और सोशल मीडिया में दिखते हैं, लेकिन मानक लिखित मंदारिन में आम तौर पर नहीं मिलते।","de":"Das schriftliche Kantonesisch (粵語白話文) gibt die kantonesische Grammatik mit Zeichen wie 嘅, 喺, 唔, 咁 und 啲 wieder. Sie erscheinen in Hongkonger Zeitungen und sozialen Medien, fehlen im schriftlichen Standardmandarin aber in der Regel.","fr":"Le cantonais écrit (粵語白話文) utilise des caractères comme 嘅, 喺, 唔, 咁 et 啲 pour noter la grammaire cantonaise. Ils apparaissent dans les journaux et les réseaux sociaux de Hong Kong, mais sont généralement absents de l'écrit standard en mandarin.","it":"Il cantonese scritto (粵語白話文) usa caratteri come 嘅, 喺, 唔, 咁 e 啲 per rendere la grammatica cantonese. Compaiono sui giornali e sui social media di Hong Kong, ma di norma sono assenti dal mandarino scritto standard.","es":"El cantonés escrito (粵語白話文) usa caracteres como 嘅, 喺, 唔, 咁 y 啲 para representar la gramática cantonesa. Aparecen en periódicos y redes sociales de Hong Kong, pero por lo general no se usan en el mandarín escrito estándar.","pt":"O cantonês escrito (粵語白話文) usa caracteres como 嘅, 喺, 唔, 咁 e 啲 para representar a gramática cantonesa. Esses caracteres aparecem em jornais e redes sociais de Hong Kong, mas em geral estão ausentes do mandarim escrito padrão.","ru":"Письменный кантонский (粵語白話文) использует такие иероглифы, как 嘅, 喺, 唔, 咁 и 啲, чтобы передавать кантонскую грамматику. Они встречаются в гонконгских газетах и социальных сетях, но в стандартном письменном мандаринском, как правило, отсутствуют.","uk":"Письмова кантонська (粵語白話文) використовує такі ієрогліфи, як 嘅, 喺, 唔, 咁 і 啲, щоб передавати кантонську граматику. Вони трапляються в гонконгських газетах і соціальних мережах, але в стандартному письмовому мандаринському загалом відсутні.","ar":"تستخدم الكانتونية المكتوبة (粵語白話文) حروفًا مثل 嘅 و喺 و唔 و咁 و啲 لتمثيل قواعد الكانتونية. وتظهر هذه الحروف في صحف هونغ كونغ ووسائل التواصل الاجتماعي، لكنها تغيب عمومًا عن الصينية المكتوبة المعيارية.","he":"הקנטונזית הכתובה (粵語白話文) משתמשת בסימנים כמו 嘅, 喺, 唔, 咁 ו-啲 כדי לכתוב את הדקדוק הקנטונזי. הסימנים האלה מופיעים בעיתוני הונג קונג וברשתות החברתיות, אבל בדרך כלל לא בסינית הכתובה התקנית.","sw":"Kikantoni kilichoandikwa (粵語白話文) kinatumia herufi kama 嘅, 喺, 唔, 咁 na 啲 kuwakilisha sarufi ya Kikantoni. Herufi hizi zinaonekana katika magazeti na mitandao ya kijamii ya Hong Kong, lakini kwa kawaida hazipatikani katika Mandarin sanifu iliyoandikwa."},"body":{"en":"<h3>A Writing System Within a Writing System</h3><p>When Hong Kongers type on their phones or read a tabloid, they encounter characters that standard written Chinese rarely if ever uses. These are Cantonese dialect characters (方言字), written for Cantonese words and grammar that have no direct equivalent in Mandarin. Many are built by adding the \"mouth\" radical 口 to a character with a similar sound, flagging it as a spoken, colloquial word. Some come with a story: 咩 <em>me1</em> \"what?\" is written with a character that originally stood for a sheep's bleat (口 \"mouth\" + 羊 \"sheep\").</p><p>The five workhorses are:</p><ul><li><strong>嘅 ge3</strong>: genitive/attributive particle (Mandarin uses 的 de)</li><li><strong>喺 hai2</strong>: locative copula 'to be at' (Mandarin: 在 zài)</li><li><strong>唔 m4</strong>: negation prefix (Mandarin: 不 bù / 沒 méi)</li><li><strong>咁 gam3</strong>: 'so / such / like this' (Mandarin: 這樣 zhèyàng)</li><li><strong>啲 di1</strong>: plural/partitive 'some / a bit' (Mandarin: 一些 yīxiē)</li></ul><p>These characters are not typos or slang abbreviations; all five are encoded in Unicode's basic CJK Unified Ideographs block and appear in Hong Kong print every day. Apple Daily (until it was forced to close in 2021) and Oriental Daily carried 粵語白話文 (Cantonese vernacular writing) for years in their columns, entertainment and gossip pages and racing sections. The news pages themselves stayed in Standard Written Chinese, with Cantonese surfacing there mainly in headlines and quoted speech. The characters are also everywhere in Hong Kongers' social-media posts and messages.</p><p>The mainland PRC, however, leaves most of them out of its standard character lists: 嘅, 喺, 咁 and 啲 are all absent from the 通用规范汉字表 (2013). 唔 is the exception — it sits at no. 4500 of that list's second-level table, but as a Mandarin interjection ('hmm'), not as the negator Cantonese uses it for. Some see a broader political and linguistic tension here: writing Cantonese in Cantonese quietly challenges the implicit assumption that Mandarin is the universal form of Chinese.</p><p>Cantonese also preserves the final stops -p, -t and -k that Mandarin lost, and it split its tones into upper and lower registers when the Middle Chinese voiced initials devoiced. Mandarin went through a similar split but then merged much of it back down to four tones, while Cantonese kept six (nine if the short checked syllables are counted separately), which is part of why even shared vocabulary is unrecognisable across the two. 嘅 most likely developed from the classifier 個 (Mandarin gè), and 唔 belongs to an ancient family of m- negatives that includes 無 and 毋, the same family as the syllabic negator <em>m</em> of Hokkien and Hakka. These are not modern inventions but centuries-old oral forms finally given their own graphic home.</p><div class=\"trivia-actions\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"focus\" data-code=\"yue\">Show Cantonese on map</button></div>"},"sources":["Snow, Don. Cantonese as Written Language: The Growth of a Written Chinese Vernacular. Hong Kong University Press, 2004.","Bauer, Robert S. & Benedict, Paul K. Modern Cantonese Phonology. Mouton de Gruyter, 1997.",{"text":"Unicode Chart for Cantonese-specific characters (CJK Unified Ideographs)","url":"https://www.unicode.org/charts/PDF/U4E00.pdf"},"Cheung, Kwan-hin & Bauer, Robert S. The Representation of Cantonese with Chinese Characters. Journal of Chinese Linguistics Monograph Series No. 18, 2002.",{"text":"Wikipedia: Written Cantonese","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Written_Cantonese"}]},{"id":"shanghainese-ipa-wugniu","icon":"🔊","tags":["wu","phonology","middle-chinese","sinosphere"],"title":{"en":"Wugniu: Romanisation for Shanghainese Three-Way Stop Contrasts","ja":"吳語拼音（Wugniu）：上海語の閉鎖音の三者対立を書き分けるローマ字","ko":"吳語拼音(Wugniu): 상하이어 파열음의 3중 대립을 적는 로마자","zh":"吴语拼音：书写上海话三组塞音对立的拼音方案","yue":"吳語拼音：書寫上海話三組塞音對立嘅拼音方案","vi":"Wugniu: hệ thống Latinh hóa cho sự đối lập ba chiều của âm tắc tiếng Thượng Hải","th":"Wugniu: ระบบถอดเสียงสำหรับการแยกเสียงสามชั้นของภาษาเซี่ยงไฮ้","id":"Wugniu: romanisasi untuk tiga jenis konsonan henti bahasa Shanghai","hi":"वुग्नियु: शंघाईनीज़ के तीन तरह के स्पर्श व्यंजन, रोमन अक्षरों में","de":"Wugniu: Romanisierung für den dreifachen Verschlusslaut-Kontrast des Shanghainesischen","fr":"Wugniu : une romanisation pour le triple contraste des occlusives du shanghaïen","it":"Wugniu: una romanizzazione per il contrasto a tre vie delle occlusive dello shanghainese","es":"Wugniu: una romanización para el triple contraste de oclusivas del shanghainés","pt":"Wugniu: romanização para o contraste tripartido das oclusivas do xangainês","ru":"Wugniu: романизация для тройного противопоставления смычных в шанхайском","uk":"Wugniu: романізація для потрійного протиставлення зімкнених у шанхайській","ar":"Wugniu: نظام روماني للتمييز الثلاثي بين الانفجاريات في لغة شنغهاي","he":"Wugniu: כתב לטיני לשלושת סוגי הסותמים של השנגחאית","sw":"Wugniu: uandishi wa Kilatini wa aina tatu za konsonanti za kuzuia za Kishanghai"},"summary":{"en":"Shanghainese (Wu) preserves a three-way distinction among stop consonants — voiced, voiceless aspirated, and voiceless unaspirated — inherited from Middle Chinese. The Wugniu romanisation represents all three contrasts in plain ASCII.","ja":"上海語（呉語）は、中古漢語から受け継いだ閉鎖音の有声・無声有気・無声無気の三者対立を保っている。吳語拼音（Wugniu）は、この三つすべてをASCII文字だけで書き表す。","ko":"상하이어(우어)는 중고한어에서 물려받은 파열음의 유성·무성 유기·무성 무기 3중 대립을 간직하고 있다. 吳語拼音(Wugniu)은 이 세 대립을 모두 ASCII 문자만으로 적는다.","zh":"上海话（吴语）保留了承自中古汉语的塞音三组对立：浊音、清送气、清不送气。吴语拼音（Wugniu）只用普通ASCII字符就能把这三组对立全部写出来。","yue":"上海話（吳語）保留咗承自中古漢語嘅塞音三組對立：濁音、清送氣、清不送氣。吳語拼音（Wugniu）淨係用普通ASCII字符就寫得出呢三組對立。","vi":"Tiếng Thượng Hải (Ngô) giữ sự đối lập ba chiều của phụ âm tắc — hữu thanh, vô thanh bật hơi và vô thanh không bật hơi — thừa hưởng từ tiếng Hán trung cổ. Hệ Latinh hóa Wugniu thể hiện cả ba sự đối lập ấy chỉ bằng ký tự ASCII thông thường.","th":"ภาษาเซี่ยงไฮ้ (ภาษาอู๋) ยังแยกพยัญชนะหยุดเป็นสามชุด คือมีเสียง ไม่มีเสียงมีลม และไม่มีเสียงไม่มีลม ซึ่งสืบมาจากภาษาจีนยุคกลาง ระบบถอดเสียง Wugniu แสดงการแยกเสียงทั้งสามนี้ด้วยอักขระ ASCII ธรรมดา","id":"Bahasa Shanghai (Wu) mempertahankan pembedaan tiga arah pada konsonan henti, yaitu bersuara, tak bersuara beraspirat, dan tak bersuara tak beraspirat, warisan Tionghoa Pertengahan. Romanisasi Wugniu menampilkan ketiganya dengan huruf ASCII biasa.","hi":"शंघाईनीज़ (वू) में मध्यकालीन चीनी से मिला स्पर्श व्यंजनों का तीन-तरफ़ा भेद आज भी बचा है — सघोष, अघोष महाप्राण और अघोष अल्पप्राण। वुग्नियु रोमनीकरण तीनों को सादे ASCII में दिखाता है।","de":"Das Shanghainesische (Wu) bewahrt aus dem Mittelchinesischen drei Reihen von Verschlusslauten: stimmhaft, stimmlos aspiriert und stimmlos nicht-aspiriert. Die Wugniu-Romanisierung gibt alle drei in reinem ASCII wieder.","fr":"Le shanghaïen (wu) préserve un triple contraste parmi les consonnes occlusives (sonore, sourd aspiré et sourd non aspiré), hérité du chinois médiéval. La romanisation Wugniu note ces trois contrastes en ASCII pur.","it":"Lo shanghainese (wu) conserva un’opposizione a tre vie tra le occlusive, sonore, sorde aspirate e sorde non aspirate, ereditata dal cinese medio. La romanizzazione Wugniu le distingue tutte e tre usando solo caratteri ASCII.","es":"El shanghainés (wu) preserva un triple contraste entre consonantes oclusivas (sonora, sorda aspirada y sorda no aspirada) heredado del chino medio. La romanización Wugniu representa los tres contrastes en ASCII simple.","pt":"O xangainês (wu) preserva um contraste tripartido entre consoantes oclusivas — sonoras, surdas aspiradas e surdas não aspiradas — herdado do chinês médio. A romanização Wugniu representa os três contrastes em ASCII simples.","ru":"Шанхайский (ву) сохраняет тройное противопоставление смычных согласных — звонких, глухих придыхательных и глухих непридыхательных, — унаследованное из среднекитайского. Романизация Wugniu передаёт все три ряда в обычном ASCII.","uk":"Шанхайська (у) зберігає потрійне протиставлення зімкнених приголосних — дзвінких, глухих придихових і глухих непридихових, — успадковане із середньокитайської. Романізація Wugniu передає всі три ряди у звичайному ASCII.","ar":"لا تزال لغة شنغهاي (الووية) تحتفظ بتمييز ثلاثي بين الأصوات الانفجارية (مجهورة، ومهموسة نفَسية، ومهموسة غير نفَسية) ورثته من الصينية الوسطى. ويمثّل نظام Wugniu الروماني التمييزات الثلاثة كلها بحروف ASCII وحدها.","he":"השנגחאית (וו) שומרת עד היום על שלושה סוגי סותמים שירשה מהסינית התיכונה: קולי, חרש מושאב וחרש לא-מושאב. תעתיק Wugniu מבחין בין שלושתם ב-ASCII פשוט.","sw":"Kishanghai (Wu) kinahifadhi tofauti ya aina tatu za konsonanti za kuzuia, iliyorithiwa kutoka Kichina cha Kati: zenye mlio, zisizo na mlio lakini zenye pumzi, na zisizo na mlio wala pumzi. Uandishi wa Kilatini wa Wugniu unaonyesha aina zote tatu kwa ASCII ya kawaida."},"body":{"en":"<h3>When Three Sounds Become One, or Stay Three</h3><p>In Mandarin, the old Middle Chinese (中古漢語) three-way stop system collapsed long ago: voiced initials like <em>b-</em> merged into the voiceless categories. Shanghainese — the prestige dialect of Wu (吳語) — never completed that merger. It retains a three-way phonemic contrast that Mandarin lost centuries ago:</p><ul><li><strong>Voiceless unaspirated</strong>: 搭 <em>taq</em> (to put up, to build)</li><li><strong>Voiced</strong>: 踏 <em>daq</em> (to tread)</li><li><strong>Voiceless aspirated</strong>: 塔 <em>thaq</em> (pagoda)</li></ul><p>One caution: at the start of a word, the \"voiced\" stops are not voiced in the English sense. They sound voiceless but carry a breathy, murmured quality and always come with a low (\"light\") tone; they become fully voiced only between vowels.</p><p>The Wugniu romanisation (吳語拼音), built by Wu-language enthusiasts on the Wu Chinese Society scheme of the mid-2000s and settled in its present form in the 2010s, keeps all three series apart in plain ASCII: <strong>b- / d- / g-</strong> for the voiced initials, <em>p- / t- / k-</em> for the voiceless unaspirated, and <em>ph- / th- / kh-</em> for the voiceless aspirated. One spelling per series, no diacritics.</p><p>This matters for historical linguistics. Middle Chinese had exactly this three-way system, and it is reconstructed partly <em>because</em> languages like Shanghainese preserve it. Wu bears on Old Chinese too, but only at one remove: its voiced series continues the Middle Chinese voiced obstruents, and reconstructions such as Baxter and Sagart's start from that Middle Chinese system before working further back through Shijing rhymes and phonetic series.</p><p>Voiced obstruents are rare in Sinitic outside Wu and Xiang. Cantonese kept only two (voiceless unaspirated vs. aspirated; the old voiced series devoiced, coming out aspirated in the level and rising tones and unaspirated elsewhere). Hokkien does have voiced <em>b-</em> and <em>g-</em>, but they come from old nasals (文 \"writing\" is <em>bûn</em>, from Middle Chinese <em>m-</em>), not from the Middle Chinese voiced stops, which devoiced there too. The other big holdout is Old Xiang, the conservative Lou–Shao dialects of Hunan around Shuangfeng (some 11 million speakers), which keep the voiced series where New Xiang, as in Changsha, devoiced it. Wu and Old Xiang are the main living witnesses.</p><p>Shanghainese is under severe pressure from Mandarin. Only about half of Shanghai's population can now hold a conversation in it, and in a 2010 survey 62.6% of primary-school pupils said Mandarin was their first language at home. There are signs of a fightback, though: buses have announced stops in Shanghainese since 2013, and Apple's Siri learned it in 2017.</p><div class=\"trivia-actions\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"focus\" data-code=\"wuu\">Show Wu region on map</button></div>"},"sources":["Kurpaska, Maria. Chinese Language(s): A Look Through the Prism of 'The Great Dictionary of Modern Chinese Dialects'. De Gruyter Mouton, 2010.","Norman, Jerry. Chinese. Cambridge University Press, 1988.",{"text":"吳語學堂 (Wugniu) — 吳語學堂拼音方案","url":"https://www.wugniu.com/"},"Baxter, William H. & Sagart, Laurent. Old Chinese: A New Reconstruction. Oxford University Press, 2014.",{"text":"Wikipedia: Shanghainese (phonology, sociolinguistic situation)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shanghainese"},{"text":"Wikipedia: Xiang Chinese","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Xiang_Chinese"}]},{"id":"pan-sinosphere-counting","icon":"🔢","tags":["sinosphere","numbers","phonology","history"],"title":{"en":"Counting from 1 to 10 Across the Sinosphere","ja":"1から10まで数える：漢字文化圏の数詞","ko":"한자문화권의 1부터 10까지 세기","zh":"从一数到十：汉字文化圈的数词","yue":"由一數到十：漢字文化圈嘅數詞","vi":"Đếm từ 1 đến 10 khắp vùng Hán tự","th":"การนับ 1 ถึง 10 ทั่วโลกอักษรฮั่น","id":"Menghitung dari 1 sampai 10 di Dunia Sinosfer","hi":"चीनाक्षर-मंडल में 1 से 10 तक की गिनती","de":"Zählen von 1 bis 10 in der Sinosphäre","fr":"Compter de 1 à 10 dans la sinosphère","it":"Contare da 1 a 10 nella sinosfera","es":"Contar del 1 al 10 en la sinosfera","pt":"Contando de 1 a 10 na sinosfera","ru":"Счёт от 1 до 10 в синосфере","uk":"Лічба від 1 до 10 у синосфері","ar":"العدّ من 1 إلى 10 عبر الصينوسفير","he":"ספירה מ-1 עד 10 ברחבי הסינוספרה","sw":"Kuhesabu kutoka 1 hadi 10 katika Sinosphere"},"summary":{"en":"The same character 一 is read in a dozen different ways across East Asia. Counting from one to ten shows 1,500 years of sound change, layers of borrowing, and why four is a number people avoid.","ja":"「一」という漢字は、東アジア全域で十数通りに読まれる。1から10まで数えてみると、1500年にわたる音変化と借用の層、そして「四」が避けられる理由が見えてくる。","ko":"같은 한자 一이 동아시아에서 열 가지 남짓한 방식으로 읽힌다. 하나부터 열까지 세어 보면 1,500년의 음운 변화와 켜켜이 쌓인 차용, 그리고 사람들이 왜 4를 피하는지가 보인다.","zh":"同一个汉字「一」在东亚有十几种读法。从一数到十，就能看出1500年的音变、层层叠叠的借用，以及人们为什么要躲着数字四。","yue":"同一個漢字「一」喺東亞有十幾種唔同讀法。由一數到十，就見到1500年嘅音變、一層層嘅借用，同埋點解大家咁避忌數字四。","vi":"Cùng một chữ Hán 一 được đọc theo cả chục cách khác nhau khắp Đông Á. Đếm từ một đến mười là thấy 1.500 năm biến đổi ngữ âm, những lớp vay mượn chồng lên nhau, và lý do người ta né số bốn.","th":"อักษร 一 ตัวเดียวอ่านได้นับสิบแบบทั่วเอเชียตะวันออก แค่นับหนึ่งถึงสิบก็เห็นเสียงที่เปลี่ยนไปตลอด 1,500 ปี ชั้นของคำยืม และเหตุที่ผู้คนพากันเลี่ยงเลขสี่","id":"Aksara yang sama, 一, dibaca dengan selusin cara di Asia Timur. Berhitung dari satu sampai sepuluh memperlihatkan 1.500 tahun perubahan bunyi, lapisan-lapisan pinjaman, dan alasan orang menghindari angka empat.","hi":"एक ही चीनी वर्ण 一 पूर्वी एशिया में दर्जन भर तरीक़ों से पढ़ा जाता है। एक से दस तक की गिनती 1,500 वर्षों के ध्वनि-परिवर्तन, उधार की परतों, और इस राज़ को खोल देती है कि लोग चार की संख्या से क्यों बचते हैं।","de":"Das gleiche Zeichen 一 wird in ganz Ostasien auf ein Dutzend verschiedene Arten gelesen. Zählt man von eins bis zehn, zeigen sich 1.500 Jahre Lautwandel, Schichten von Entlehnungen und auch, warum man der Vier lieber aus dem Weg geht.","fr":"Le même caractère 一 se lit d'une douzaine de façons différentes en Asie de l'Est. Compter de un à dix, c'est voir défiler 1 500 ans d'évolution phonétique, des couches d'emprunts, et la raison pour laquelle on évite le chiffre quatre.","it":"Lo stesso carattere 一 si legge in una dozzina di modi diversi in tutta l’Asia orientale. Contando da uno a dieci emergono 1.500 anni di mutamenti fonetici, strati di prestiti e il motivo per cui il quattro è un numero da evitare.","es":"El mismo carácter 一 se lee de una docena de maneras distintas en el este de Asia. Contar del uno al diez revela 1.500 años de cambios fonéticos, capas de préstamos y por qué el cuatro es un número que la gente evita.","pt":"O mesmo caractere 一 se lê de uma dúzia de maneiras diferentes na Ásia Oriental. Contar de um a dez revela 1.500 anos de mudança sonora, camadas de empréstimo — e por que o quatro é um número que as pessoas evitam.","ru":"Один и тот же иероглиф 一 читается по всей Восточной Азии на дюжину ладов. Счёт от одного до десяти показывает 1500 лет звуковых изменений, слои заимствований — и то, почему числа четыре люди избегают.","uk":"Той самий ієрогліф 一 по всій Східній Азії читають на дюжину ладів. Лічба від одного до десяти відкриває 1500 років звукових змін, шари запозичень і пояснює, чому люди уникають числа чотири.","ar":"يُقرأ الحرف نفسه 一 بنحو اثنتي عشرة طريقة مختلفة في شرق آسيا. والعدّ من واحد إلى عشرة يكشف 1500 عام من التحول الصوتي، وطبقات من الاقتراض، وسرّ تجنّب الناس للرقم أربعة.","he":"אותו סימן 一 נקרא בתריסר דרכים שונות במזרח אסיה. ספירה מאחת עד עשר חושפת 1,500 שנה של שינויי הגה, רבדים של שאילה, ואת הסיבה שאנשים מתרחקים מהמספר ארבע.","sw":"Herufi ileile 一 inasomwa kwa njia kumi na mbili tofauti kote Asia Mashariki. Kuhesabu kutoka moja hadi kumi kunaonyesha miaka 1,500 ya mabadiliko ya sauti, matabaka ya ukopaji, na sababu ya watu kuikwepa namba nne."},"body":{"en":"<h3>One Character, Many Voices</h3><p>The numeral <strong>一</strong> (one) is the simplest Chinese character — a single horizontal stroke. Yet across the Sinosphere it is pronounced in strikingly different ways: <em>yī</em> in Mandarin, <em>yat</em> in Cantonese, <em>it</em> in Hokkien/Min, <em>yit</em> in Hakka, <em>iq</em> in Wu (Shanghainese), <em>ichi/itsu</em> in Japanese, <em>il</em> in Korean, <em>nhất</em> in Vietnamese. The Chinese varieties mostly inherited their readings as descendants of Middle Chinese, the language of the Sui and Tang dynasties (Min split off earlier, and its literary readings, such as Hokkien <em>it</em>, are themselves a later borrowed layer); Japanese, Korean and Vietnamese borrowed theirs, and each borrowed reading is a fossil of the Chinese being spoken when it was taken over.</p><div class='trivia-actions'><button type='button' class='trivia-action' data-action='setchar' data-char='一'>🔢 Show 一 on map</button></div><h3>Comparative Table: 1–10</h3><ul><li><strong>Mandarin (zh):</strong> yī, èr, sān, sì, wǔ, liù, qī, bā, jiǔ, shí</li><li><strong>Cantonese (yue):</strong> yat, yi, saam, sei, ng, luk, chat, baat, gau, sap</li><li><strong>Min Nan (nan):</strong> it/tsi̍t, jī/nn̄g, sam/sann, sì, gōo, la̍k, tshit, peh, káu, si̍p/tsa̍p</li><li><strong>Hakka (hak):</strong> yit, ngi, sam, si, ng, liuk, chhit, pat, kiu, sip</li><li><strong>Wu / Shanghainese (wuu):</strong> iq, gni, se, sy, ng, loq, chiq, paq, cieu, zeq</li><li><strong>Japanese Go-on 呉音:</strong> ichi, ni, san, shi, go, roku, shichi, hachi, ku, jū</li><li><strong>Japanese Kan-on 漢音:</strong> itsu, ji, san, shi, go, riku, shitsu, hatsu, kyū, shū</li><li><strong>Korean (ko):</strong> il, i, sam, sa, o, yuk, chil, pal, gu, sip</li><li><strong>Sino-Vietnamese (vi):</strong> nhất, nhị, tam, tứ, ngũ, lục, thất, bát, cửu, thập</li></ul><h3>When One Is Not <em>yī</em></h3><p>Read a phone number aloud in Mandarin and the 1 comes out <em>yāo</em>, not <em>yī</em>. This is a substitution rather than a reading: the character is 幺, literally 'the smallest', and dictionaries give 一 the single reading <em>yī</em>. The motive is audibility: over a noisy line <em>yī</em> is easily confused with <em>qī</em> (7). Chinese military radio takes the idea all the way. Its digits 0–9 are 洞 幺 两 三 四 五 六 拐 八 勾 (<em>dòng, yāo, liǎng, sān, sì, wǔ, liù, guǎi, bā, gōu</em>), where 0 is <em>dòng</em> \"hole\" and 7 is <em>guǎi</em> \"bend\", after the digits' shapes. The habit has spread to civilians: the emergency numbers 110 and 119 are normally read <em>yāo-yāo-líng</em> and <em>yāo-yāo-jiǔ</em>. Cantonese does not do this; its one everyday 幺 is 十三幺, a mahjong hand.</p><h3>Tang–Cantonese–Vietnamese Alignment</h3><p><strong>Cantonese</strong> and <strong>Sino-Vietnamese</strong> preserve Tang Dynasty (618–907 CE) phonology especially closely. Both retain final stop consonants (-t, -k, -p) that Mandarin has shed. Compare: Cantonese <em>yat</em> / Sino-Vietnamese <em>nhất</em> vs Mandarin <em>yī</em>. Cantonese inherited the stops directly, and the Sino-Vietnamese readings were borrowed mainly from the Chinese of the late Tang, so both kept what Middle Chinese had. The northern dialects that became Mandarin later lost those stops through their own sound changes — by the time of the <em>Zhongyuan Yinyun</em> rhyme book of 1324 the old -p/-t/-k contrast had already collapsed and the checked-tone syllables were being redistributed among the other tones.</p><h3>Japan's Layers</h3><p><strong>呉音 (Go-on)</strong> — Wu pronunciations — arrived via the Korean Peninsula in the 5th–6th centuries, reflecting Southern Chinese phonology. <strong>漢音 (Kan-on)</strong> — Han pronunciations — were imported directly from Tang Chang'an by Japanese embassies in the 7th–9th centuries. A third layer, <strong>唐音 (Tō-on)</strong>, came via Zen Buddhism from Song/Ming China. Japanese counting mostly uses Go-on (<em>ichi, ni, san, shi, go, roku, shichi, hachi, ku, jū</em>), with two exceptions in everyday speech: 9 is usually <em>kyū</em>, the Kan-on reading, and 4 and 7 are often replaced by the native <em>yon</em> and <em>nana</em>.</p><h3>The Death Number: 四 = 死</h3><p>In Japanese (and across the Sinosphere), <strong>4</strong> carries a shadow. In Go-on, 四 is read <em>shi</em> — identical to 死 (<em>shi</em>, death). This drives widespread <strong>tetraphobia</strong>: some hospitals and apartment blocks in Japan skip the number 4, and 49 is avoided too, because <em>shi-ku</em> sounds like \"suffer and die\". Korean 사 <em>sa</em> is also a homophone of \"death\", and South Korean lifts sometimes label the fourth floor \"F\" instead of 4. Nokia never released a phone in a 4000 series. The native Japanese reading <em>yon</em> (a later expansion of the native <em>yo</em>) is often preferred to dodge the omen.</p><div class='trivia-actions'><button type='button' class='trivia-action' data-action='setchar' data-char='四'>💀 Show 四 (the death number) on map</button><button type='button' class='trivia-action' data-action='focus' data-code='ja'>🗾 See Japanese Go-on readings</button></div><h3>Cultural Numerology</h3><p><strong>Eight</strong> (八, <em>bā</em>) is the luckiest number in Chinese culture because it sounds like 發 (<em>fā</em>, prosperity). <strong>Six</strong> symbolises smooth flow (六六大順), and <strong>nine</strong> sounds like 久 (<em>jiǔ</em>, longevity). Meanwhile <strong>four</strong> is avoided everywhere — in Mandarin 14 (<em>shísì</em>) sounds like \"is dead\" (是死 <em>shì sǐ</em>) and is considered even unluckier than 4, and 74 is feared as \"actually dead\".</p>"},"sources":["Pulleyblank, E.G. (1984). Middle Chinese: A Study in Historical Phonology. UBC Press.","Baxter, W.H. & Sagart, L. (2014). Old Chinese: A New Reconstruction. Oxford University Press.",{"text":"Sino-Vietnamese vocabulary (Wikipedia)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sino-Vietnamese_vocabulary"},{"text":"On'yomi: Go-on, Kan-on, To-on (Wikipedia)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/On%27yomi"},{"text":"Tetraphobia (Wikipedia)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tetraphobia"},{"text":"幺 yāo — 汉典 (Zdic): 数目一（称呼电话号码等）","url":"https://www.zdic.net/hans/%E5%B9%BA"},"Ferlus, M. (1992). Histoire abrégée de l'évolution des consonnes initiales du vietnamien et du sino-vietnamien. Mon-Khmer Studies 20, 111–125."]},{"id":"tea-tea-cha-cha","icon":"🍵","tags":["sinosphere","loanwords","trade-routes","phonology"],"title":{"en":"Tea and Chai: Two Transmission Pathways for 茶","ja":"「茶」の二つの語形：海路と陸路に沿った伝播","ko":"茶의 두 어형: 해로와 육로를 따른 전파","zh":"“茶”的两种词形：沿海路与陆路传播","yue":"tea 同 chai：「茶」字嘅兩條傳播路線","vi":"Hai dạng từ của 茶: lan truyền theo đường biển và đường bộ","th":"tea กับ chai: สองเส้นทางการเผยแพร่ของคำว่า 茶","id":"Tea dan chai: dua jalur penyebaran 茶","hi":"Tea और Chai: 茶 के प्रसार के दो रास्ते","de":"Tea und Chai: Zwei Überlieferungswege für 茶","fr":"Tea et chai : deux voies de transmission pour 茶","it":"Tea e chai: due vie di trasmissione per 茶","es":"Tea y chai: dos vías de transmisión para 茶","pt":"Tea e chai: dois caminhos de transmissão para 茶","ru":"Tea и chai: два пути распространения 茶","uk":"Tea і chai: два шляхи поширення 茶","ar":"Tea وChai: طريقان لانتقال 茶","he":"Tea ו-Chai: שתי הדרכים של 茶 אל העולם","sw":"Tea na chai: njia mbili za usambazaji wa 茶"},"summary":{"en":"The character 茶 has two historical pronunciation pathways: a southern sea-route form behind English 'tea' and a northern land-route form behind Russian 'chai'.","ja":"中国語の地域的な二つの語形は、海路と陸路を通じて各地へ伝わった。英語 tea とロシア語 чай はその代表例である。","ko":"중국어의 두 지역적 어형은 해로와 육로를 통해 여러 언어로 전해졌다. 영어 'tea'와 러시아어 'чай'가 대표적인 예다.","zh":"汉语的两种地域词形沿海路和陆路传入多种语言，英语 tea 与俄语 чай 是其中的代表。","yue":"「茶」有兩條歷史讀音路線：南方海路形係英文 tea 嘅來源，北方陸路形係俄文 chai 嘅來源。","vi":"Hai dạng từ theo vùng của chữ 茶 đã đi vào nhiều ngôn ngữ theo đường biển và đường bộ; tiếng Anh 'tea' và tiếng Nga 'чай' là hai ví dụ.","th":"อักษร 茶 มีเสียงอ่านเก่าสองแบบ แบบที่ไปทางทะเลจากทางใต้กลายเป็นคำว่า tea ในภาษาอังกฤษ ส่วนแบบที่ไปทางบกจากทางเหนือกลายเป็นคำว่า chai ในภาษารัสเซีย","id":"Aksara 茶 menyebar dengan dua lafal: bentuk jalur laut selatan di balik kata Inggris 'tea' dan bentuk jalur darat utara di balik kata Rusia 'chai'.","hi":"अक्षर 茶 के दो प्राचीन उच्चारण हैं — दक्षिणी समुद्री रास्ते वाला रूप, जिससे 'tea' बना, और उत्तरी ज़मीनी रास्ते वाला रूप, जिससे 'chai' बना।","de":"Das Zeichen 茶 hat zwei historische Aussprachewege: eine südliche Seewegform hinter englisch 'tea' und eine nördliche Landwegform hinter russisch 'chai'.","fr":"Le caractère 茶 a suivi deux voies de prononciation historiques : une forme méridionale, par la mer, derrière l'anglais « tea », et une forme septentrionale, par la terre, derrière le russe « chai ».","it":"Il carattere 茶 ha viaggiato con due pronunce: una meridionale, per mare, da cui l’inglese «tea», e una settentrionale, per terra, da cui il russo «chai».","es":"El carácter 茶 tiene dos vías históricas de pronunciación: una forma meridional, por mar, detrás del inglés 'tea', y una forma septentrional, por tierra, detrás del ruso 'chai'.","pt":"O caractere 茶 tem dois caminhos históricos de pronúncia: uma forma da rota marítima do sul, que está por trás do inglês 'tea', e uma forma da rota terrestre do norte, que está por trás do russo 'chai'.","ru":"У иероглифа 茶 два исторических пути произношения: южная морская форма стоит за английским tea, а северная сухопутная — за русским «чай».","uk":"Ієрогліф 茶 має два історичні шляхи вимови: південна морська форма стоїть за англійським tea, а північна сухопутна — за російським «чай».","ar":"للحرف 茶 مساران تاريخيان في النطق: شكل جنوبي بحري تعود إليه الإنجليزية «tea»، وشكل شمالي بري تعود إليه الروسية «chai».","he":"הסימן 茶 יצא לעולם בשתי הגיות: צורה דרומית שהפליגה בדרך הים והולידה את 'tea' האנגלית, וצורה צפונית שעברה ביבשה והולידה את 'chai' הרוסית.","sw":"Herufi 茶 ina njia mbili za kihistoria za matamshi: umbo la njia ya bahari ya kusini lililo chimbuko la 'tea' ya Kiingereza, na umbo la njia ya nchi kavu ya kaskazini lililo chimbuko la 'chai' ya Kirusi."},"body":{"en":"<h3>Two Pronunciation Pathways</h3><p>The Chinese character <strong>茶</strong> (chá) means tea. Two regional pronunciations of the word entered different trade networks, producing the main <em>tea</em>-type and <em>cha/chai</em>-type forms found in other languages. In the <em>World Atlas of Language Structures</em> sample of 230 languages, 110 use a <em>cha</em>-type word, 84 a <em>te</em>-type word, and only 36 something else entirely.</p><p>Both go back to one and the same word. In Middle Chinese 茶 began with a stop-like retroflex <em>ɖ-</em>. Most Chinese varieties later turned that sound into an affricate, giving <em>ch-</em>; the Min dialects of Fujian kept a plain <em>t-</em>, as they do in many words of this class (Hokkien 豬 <em>ti</em> \"pig\", 竹 <em>tek</em> \"bamboo\", against Mandarin <em>zhū</em>, <em>zhú</em>). So \"tea\" is the more old-fashioned consonant, not a different word.</p><h3>The Sea Route: t&ecirc; &rarr; thee &rarr; tea</h3><p>In the <strong>Min dialect group</strong> of coastal Fujian province &mdash; including the port of Amoy (Xiamen) &mdash; the character 茶 was pronounced approximately <em>t&ecirc;</em>. When Dutch VOC merchants began trading tea in the early 17th century, through Hokkien traders and the Malay-speaking ports of their empire, they took the word as <strong>thee</strong> (whether directly from Hokkien <em>tê</em> or via Malay <em>teh</em> is still debated). From Dutch it spread rapidly:</p><ul><li><strong>English:</strong> tea (via Dutch <em>thee</em>, ~1650s)</li><li><strong>French:</strong> th&eacute;</li><li><strong>German:</strong> Tee</li><li><strong>Spanish:</strong> t&eacute;</li><li><strong>Italian:</strong> t&egrave;</li><li><strong>Malay / Indonesian:</strong> teh (direct from Hokkien ports)</li><li><strong>Tamil:</strong> t&#275;n&#299;r (literally \"tea-water\")</li><li><strong>Afrikaans:</strong> tee</li></ul><p>These are the <strong>sea-route nations</strong> &mdash; countries whose tea word came by ship from the Min-speaking coast of southern China.</p><h3>The Land Route: ch&aacute; &rarr; &ccedil;ay &rarr; chai</h3><p>In Mandarin and most other Chinese varieties the character carries a <em>ch</em>-type initial &mdash; Mandarin <em>ch&aacute;</em>, Cantonese <em>caa4</em>. It was the northern, Mandarin-speaking end of China that sent this form overland along the <strong>Silk Road</strong> and the tea-horse routes, and from there it reached the world through caravan merchants and Russian traders. Along the way, in Persian, it picked up an ending <em>-y</em>, which is why so many of these forms are <em>chai</em> rather than plain <em>cha</em>:</p><ul><li><strong>Russian:</strong> &#1095;&#1072;&#1081; (chay)</li><li><strong>Turkish:</strong> &ccedil;ay</li><li><strong>Persian:</strong> &#1670;&#1575;&#1740; (ch&#257;y)</li><li><strong>Arabic:</strong> &#1588;&#1575;&#1610; (sh&#257;y)</li><li><strong>Hindi / Urdu:</strong> &#2330;&#2366;&#2351; (ch&#257;y)</li><li><strong>Swahili:</strong> chai</li><li><strong>Greek:</strong> &#964;&#963;&#940;&#953; (tsai)</li><li><strong>Japanese:</strong> &#12362;&#33590; (o-cha), direct from China; a later reading <em>sa</em> survives in 喫茶店 <em>kissaten</em> \"café\"</li><li><strong>Korean:</strong> 차 (cha), direct from China</li><li><strong>Portuguese:</strong> ch&aacute; (via Macau &#8212; the famous sea-nation exception)</li></ul><p><strong>Portuguese</strong> is an often-cited exception &mdash; a seafaring nation with <em>ch&aacute;</em> because its first tea contact was via Macau (a Cantonese-speaking port), not the Dutch-Hokkien network. In fact the Portuguese form reached English first: <em>cha</em> is recorded in the 1590s, decades before <em>tea</em>, and survives in the British slang \"a cup of char\".</p><div class='trivia-actions'><button type='button' class='trivia-action' data-action='panto' data-lat='24.5' data-lng='118.1' data-zoom='5'>🚢 Pan to Fujian coast (sea-route origin)</button><button type='button' class='trivia-action' data-action='focus' data-code='nan'>📍 See Min Nan on map</button></div><h3>Geographic Distribution</h3><p>The distribution broadly separates <em>tea / th&eacute; / Tee / t&eacute;</em> forms associated with maritime trade from <em>chai / chay / cha / &ccedil;ay</em> forms associated with overland routes and other Chinese contact zones. It reflects several early-modern routes by which tea and its name spread.</p><h3>What the Split Records</h3><p>The split records a dialect difference that travelled with trade. In most languages, the first consonant of the word for tea tells you, quite reliably, whether the word came down the trade chain that began with Hokkien speakers on the Fujian coast or by some other road out of China.</p>"},"sources":["Mair, V. H. & Hoh, E. (2009). The True History of Tea. Thames & Hudson.",{"text":"Tea etymology (Etymonline)","url":"https://www.etymonline.com/word/tea"},{"text":"Tea — Etymology section (Wikipedia)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tea#Etymology"},{"text":"Min Chinese phonology (Wikipedia)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Min_Chinese"},"Forrest, R.A.D. (1948). The Chinese Language. Faber & Faber.","Norman, J. (1988). Chinese. Cambridge University Press.",{"text":"Dahl, Östen (2013). Tea. WALS Online, Feature 138A.","url":"https://wals.info/feature/138A"}]},{"id":"oracle-bone-script","icon":"🐚","tags":["china","script","history","decipherment"],"title":{"en":"Oracle Bone Script: 甲骨文, China's Oldest Attested Writing","ja":"甲骨文：亀甲と獣骨に刻まれた最古の漢字","ko":"갑골문(甲骨文): 중국에서 가장 오래된 실증 문자","zh":"甲骨文：中国现存最早的可考文字","yue":"甲骨文：中國現存最早有實物可考嘅文字","vi":"Giáp cốt văn (甲骨文): hệ chữ viết được chứng thực sớm nhất của Trung Hoa","th":"อักษรกระดองเต่าและกระดูก (甲骨文): อักษรเก่าแก่ที่สุดของจีนที่มีหลักฐานยืนยัน","id":"Aksara tulang ramal: 甲骨文, tulisan terawal yang terbukti di Tiongkok","hi":"अस्थि-भविष्यवाणी लिपि: 甲骨文, चीन की सबसे पुरानी प्रमाणित लिपि","de":"Orakelknochenschrift: 甲骨文, Chinas älteste belegte Schrift","fr":"Écriture sur os oraculaires : 甲骨文, la plus ancienne écriture attestée de Chine","it":"Scrittura oracolare: 甲骨文, la più antica scrittura attestata della Cina","es":"Escritura oracular: 甲骨文, la escritura atestiguada más antigua de China","pt":"Escrita oracular: 甲骨文, a mais antiga escrita atestada da China","ru":"Письменность на гадательных костях: 甲骨文, древнейшая засвидетельствованная письменность Китая","uk":"Письмо на ворожбитних кістках: 甲骨文, найдавніша засвідчена писемність Китаю","ar":"نقوش عظام العرافة: 甲骨文، أقدم كتابة صينية موثَّقة","he":"כתב עצמות הניחוש: 甲骨文, הכתב המתועד הקדום ביותר של סין","sw":"Maandishi ya mifupa ya uaguzi: 甲骨文, maandishi ya kale zaidi yaliyothibitishwa nchini China"},"summary":{"en":"Oracle bone script (甲骨文), China's earliest attested writing, records the fire-divinations of the late Shang court around 1250–1050 BCE on ox scapulae and turtle plastrons. Sold in the 19th century as medicinal \"dragon bones,\" the inscriptions were recognised as ancient writing only in 1899; of their roughly 4,000 characters, between 1,500 and 2,000 can be read today.","ja":"甲骨文は、紀元前1250〜1050年頃、殷王朝末期の占いを牛の肩甲骨や亀の腹甲に刻んだ、現存最古の漢字である。19世紀には「龍骨」という生薬として売られ、古代の文字と見抜かれたのは1899年のこと。約4千字のうち、今読めるのは1500〜2000字にとどまる。","ko":"중국에서 가장 이른 시기의 실증 문자인 갑골문(甲骨文)은 기원전 1250~1050년경 상(商) 왕조 말기 궁정의 불점(火占)을 소 어깨뼈와 거북 배딱지에 새겨 기록한 것이다. 19세기에 약재인 \"용골(龍骨)\"로 거래되던 이 각문(刻文)이 고대 문자로 인정받은 것은 1899년에 이르러서였다. 약 4,000자 가운데 오늘날 읽어낼 수 있는 것은 1,500자에서 2,000자 사이다.","zh":"甲骨文是中国现存最早的可考文字，记录了约公元前1250至前1050年商代晚期王室在牛肩胛骨和龟腹甲上所作的火灼占卜。19世纪时，这些刻辞曾被当作药材“龙骨”买卖，直到1899年才被辨认出是一种上古文字；在其约4000个字中，今天能确读的介于1500至2000字之间。","yue":"甲骨文係中國現存最早有實物可考嘅文字，記低咗大約公元前1250至前1050年商朝末期王室用火占卜嘅事，刻喺牛膊頭骨同龜腹甲上面。呢啲刻辭喺19世紀曾經當做藥材「龍骨」嚟買賣，一直要到1899年先至畀人認出係上古文字；喺佢大約4000個字當中，今日讀得通嘅介乎1500到2000字之間。","vi":"Giáp cốt văn (甲骨文), hệ chữ viết được chứng thực sớm nhất của Trung Hoa, ghi lại những lần bói lửa của triều đình nhà Thương muộn vào khoảng năm 1250–1050 trước Công nguyên, khắc trên xương bả vai bò và yếm rùa. Vào thế kỷ 19, các dòng khắc này bị bán làm \"long cốt\" (xương rồng) để làm thuốc, mãi đến năm 1899 mới được nhận ra là chữ viết cổ; trong khoảng 4.000 chữ của nó, ngày nay đọc được từ 1.500 đến 2.000 chữ.","th":"อักษรกระดองเต่าและกระดูก (甲骨文) ซึ่งเป็นอักษรที่เก่าแก่ที่สุดของจีนที่มีหลักฐานยืนยัน บันทึกการเสี่ยงทายด้วยไฟของราชสำนักราชวงศ์ซางตอนปลายราว 1250–1050 ปีก่อนคริสตกาล ลงบนกระดูกสะบักวัวและกระดองท้องเต่า ในคริสต์ศตวรรษที่ 19 กระดูกที่มีจารึกเหล่านี้ถูกขายเป็นยาในชื่อ \"กระดูกมังกร\" (龍骨) และเพิ่งมีคนรู้ว่าเป็นอักษรโบราณในปี ค.ศ. 1899 จากอักษรราว 4,000 ตัว ปัจจุบันอ่านออกได้ระหว่าง 1,500 ถึง 2,000 ตัว","id":"Aksara tulang ramal (甲骨文), tulisan tertua yang terbukti di Tiongkok, mencatat ramalan api istana Shang akhir sekitar 1250–1050 SM pada tulang belikat sapi dan tempurung perut kura-kura. Pada abad ke-19 tulang-tulang itu dijual sebagai obat «tulang naga», dan tulisannya baru dikenali sebagai aksara kuno pada tahun 1899; dari sekitar 4.000 karakternya, antara 1.500 dan 2.000 dapat dibaca sekarang.","hi":"अस्थि-भविष्यवाणी लिपि (甲骨文), चीन की सबसे पुरानी प्रमाणित लिपि, लगभग १२५०–१०५० ईसा पूर्व के उत्तर-शांग दरबार की अग्नि-भविष्यवाणियों को बैलों की कंधे की हड्डियों और कछुओं के उदर-कवच पर अंकित करती है। १९वीं सदी में ये हड्डियाँ औषधीय «ड्रैगन-हड्डियों» के रूप में बिकती थीं, और इन पर के अभिलेखों को प्राचीन लिपि के रूप में केवल १८९९ में पहचाना गया; इनके लगभग ४,००० अक्षरों में से आज १,५०० से २,००० तक पढ़े जा सकते हैं।","de":"Die Orakelknochenschrift (甲骨文), Chinas früheste belegte Schrift, hält die Feuerdivinationen des späten Shang-Hofs um 1250–1050 v. Chr. auf Rinderschulterblättern und Schildkrötenplastren fest. Im 19. Jahrhundert als medizinische „Drachenknochen“ verkauft, wurden die Inschriften erst 1899 als alte Schrift erkannt; von ihren rund 4.000 Zeichen lassen sich heute zwischen 1.500 und 2.000 lesen.","fr":"L'écriture sur os oraculaires (甲骨文), la plus ancienne écriture attestée de Chine, consigne les divinations par le feu de la cour des Shang tardifs vers 1250–1050 av. J.-C. sur des scapulas de bœuf et des plastrons de tortue. Vendues au XIXe siècle comme des « os de dragon » médicinaux, les inscriptions ne furent reconnues comme une écriture ancienne qu'en 1899 ; de leurs quelque 4 000 caractères, on peut en lire aujourd'hui entre 1 500 et 2 000.","it":"La scrittura oracolare (甲骨文), la più antica scrittura attestata della Cina, registra le divinazioni col fuoco della tarda corte Shang intorno al 1250–1050 a.C. su scapole di bue e piastroni di tartaruga. Vendute nell'Ottocento come «ossa di drago» medicinali, le iscrizioni furono riconosciute come scrittura antica solo nel 1899; dei loro circa 4000 caratteri, oggi se ne possono leggere tra 1500 e 2000.","es":"La escritura oracular (甲骨文), la escritura atestiguada más antigua de China, registra las adivinaciones por fuego de la corte Shang tardía hacia 1250–1050 a. C. sobre escápulas de buey y plastrones de tortuga. Vendidas en el siglo XIX como «huesos de dragón» medicinales, las inscripciones no se reconocieron como escritura antigua hasta 1899; de sus aproximadamente 4000 caracteres, hoy pueden leerse entre 1500 y 2000.","pt":"A escrita oracular (甲骨文), a mais antiga escrita atestada da China, registra as adivinhações pelo fogo da corte Shang tardia por volta de 1250–1050 a.C. em escápulas de boi e plastrões de tartaruga. Vendidas no século XIX como «ossos de dragão» medicinais, as inscrições só foram reconhecidas como escrita antiga em 1899; de seus cerca de 4.000 caracteres, hoje é possível ler entre 1.500 e 2.000.","ru":"Письменность на гадательных костях (甲骨文) — древнейшая засвидетельствованная письменность Китая — фиксирует огненные гадания позднешанского двора примерно 1250–1050 годов до н. э. на лопатках быков и панцирях черепах. В XIX веке эти кости продавались как лечебные «драконьи кости», и лишь в 1899 году надписи на них были опознаны как древнее письмо; из примерно 4000 знаков сегодня поддаются прочтению от 1500 до 2000.","uk":"Письмо на ворожбитних кістках (甲骨文) — найдавніша засвідчена писемність Китаю — фіксує вогняні ворожіння пізньошанського двору приблизно 1250–1050 років до н. е. на лопатках волів і черепашачих панцирах. У XIX столітті ці кістки продавали як лікувальні «драконові кістки», і лише 1899 року написи на них розпізнали як давнє письмо; з-поміж їхніх приблизно 4000 знаків сьогодні можна прочитати від 1500 до 2000.","ar":"تُسجِّل كتابة عظام العرافة (甲骨文)، أقدم كتابة صينية موثَّقة، عرافات النار في بلاط شانغ المتأخّر نحو ١٢٥٠–١٠٥٠ قبل الميلاد على ألواح أكتاف الثيران وبطون دروع السلاحف. وقد بِيعت هذه العظام في القرن التاسع عشر بوصفها «عظام تنّين» دوائية، ولم يُتعرَّف على النقوش بوصفها كتابة قديمة إلا عام ١٨٩٩؛ ومن بين نحو ٤٠٠٠ حرف فيها يمكن اليوم قراءة ما بين ١٥٠٠ و٢٠٠٠.","he":"כתב עצמות הניחוש (甲骨文), הכתב המתועד הקדום ביותר של סין, מתעד את ניחושי האש של חצר שאנג המאוחרת סביב 1250–1050 לפנה״ס על עצמות השכם של שוורים ועל שריוני־הבטן של צבים. במאה ה־19 נמכרו העצמות כ״עצמות דרקון״ מרפא, והכתובות זוהו ככתב עתיק רק בשנת 1899; מבין כ־4,000 הסימנים שבהן אפשר כיום לקרוא בין 1,500 ל־2,000.","sw":"Maandishi ya mifupa ya uaguzi (甲骨文), maandishi ya kale zaidi yaliyothibitishwa nchini China, yanaandika uaguzi wa moto wa baraza la Shang la baadaye kuanzia takriban 1250–1050 KK kwenye mifupa ya begani ya ng'ombe na magamba ya chini ya kobe. Yakiwa yameuzwa katika karne ya 19 kama «mifupa ya joka» ya dawa, maandishi hayo yalitambuliwa kuwa maandishi ya kale mnamo 1899 tu; kati ya herufi zake zipatazo 4,000, kati ya 1,500 na 2,000 zinaweza kusomwa leo."},"body":{"en":"<p>Chinese writing has a continuous record reaching back to the <strong>甲骨文 (jiǎgǔwén)</strong>, the \"oracle bone script\" — inscriptions cut into ox shoulder blades and turtle plastrons by diviners of the late Shang court between roughly 1250 and 1050 BCE. About 150,000 inscribed pieces have been recovered, most from Yinxu (殷墟), the last Shang capital, near present-day Anyang in Henan province. The texts are court records: questions put to ancestors and gods about harvests, war, childbirth, the king's toothache, whether it would rain, whether the coming ten-day week would pass without disaster.</p><p>The writing was a by-product of divination by fire. A diviner pressed a heated point into a prepared hollow on the back of the bone or shell until it cracked with a sharp report, and the shape the crack ran in was read as the ancestors' answer. The character <em>卜</em> (bǔ, \"to divine\") is a picture of that crack. The charge — the question — and sometimes the king's reading of the crack and what actually came to pass were then carved beside it.</p><div class='trivia-actions'><button type='button' class='trivia-action' data-action='panto' data-lat='36.10' data-lng='114.35' data-zoom='6'>🌍 Pan to Anyang (Yinxu)</button><button type='button' class='trivia-action' data-action='setchar' data-char='牛'>Show 牛 (ox) on the map</button></div><p>The bones were lost for three thousand years and then found twice. For generations farmers around Anyang turned them up and sold them to apothecaries as <em>lónggǔ</em> (龍骨), \"dragon bones,\" to be ground into medicine. In 1899 Wang Yirong (王懿榮), chancellor of the Imperial Academy in Beijing, recognised the marks on bones in his own collection as an archaic form of Chinese writing. He died the following year; his friend Liu E (劉鶚) published the first printed collection, <em>Tieyun Canggui</em> (鐵雲藏龜), in 1903 — 1,058 rubbings, of which Liu correctly identified 34 characters. Luo Zhenyu traced the bones to Anyang and gathered more than 30,000; he, Wang Guowei, Dong Zuobin and Guo Moruo came to be called the \"four halls\" (四堂) of oracle-bone studies, after the hall-names in their pen names.</p><p>The script is large and only partly read. More than 30,000 distinct graphic forms have been catalogued across the corpus, probably representing about 4,000 separate characters; of these, scholars can read between 1,500 and 2,000 with confidence. The rest are names, place-names, and words whose line of descent into later Chinese has not been traced. In November 2016 the National Museum of Chinese Writing in Anyang offered 100,000 yuan — about US$15,000 — for a convincing first reading of any single undeciphered graph, and half that for a new reading of a disputed one. The inscriptions entered UNESCO's Memory of the World register in October 2017. Even so, half or more of the signs a Shang diviner cut beside a fire-crack three thousand years ago have not yet been matched to a word.</p>"},"sources":[{"text":"Oracle bone script — Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oracle_bone_script"},{"text":"Qiu Xigui, Chinese Writing (Wenzixue Gaiyao), trans. Gilbert L. Mattos & Jerry Norman, Society for the Study of Early China & Institute of East Asian Studies, Berkeley, 2000."},{"text":"\"Ancient oracle bones added as UNESCO memory,\" China Daily, 27 December 2017.","url":"https://global.chinadaily.com.cn/a/201712/27/WS5a42dfffa31008cf16da39cb.html"},{"text":"\"Decoding 3,000-year-old inscriptions on bones,\" China Daily, 9 January 2018.","url":"https://global.chinadaily.com.cn/a/201801/09/WS5a540d4ca31008cf16da5ddd.html"}]},{"id":"kana-genesis","icon":"🎎","tags":["japan","kana","writing-system","history"],"title":{"en":"Two Kana, One Character Pile: How 安 Became あ and 阿 Became ア","ja":"二つの仮名はこうして分かれた：安からあ、阿からア","ko":"가나 둘, 한자 무더기 하나: 어떻게 安이 あ가 되고 阿가 ア가 되었나","zh":"两套假名，同一堆汉字：安如何变成あ，阿如何变成ア","yue":"兩套假名，同一堆漢字：安點樣變成あ，阿點樣變成ア","vi":"Hai bộ kana, một đống chữ Hán: 安 thành あ và 阿 thành ア như thế nào","th":"คานะสองชุด จากกองอักษรเดียวกัน: 安 กลายเป็น あ และ 阿 กลายเป็น ア ได้อย่างไร","id":"Dua kana, satu sumber: bagaimana 安 menjadi あ dan 阿 menjadi ア","hi":"एक ही ढेर से दो कानाएँ: कैसे 安 बना あ और 阿 बना ア","de":"Zwei Kana, ein Zeichenhaufen: Wie aus 安 あ und aus 阿 ア wurde","fr":"Deux kana, un même tas de caractères : comment 安 est devenu あ et 阿 est devenu ア","it":"Due kana, un solo mucchio di caratteri: come 安 è diventato あ e 阿 è diventato ア","es":"Dos kana, un mismo montón de caracteres: cómo 安 se convirtió en あ y 阿 en ア","pt":"Dois kana, um só monte de caracteres: como 安 virou あ e 阿 virou ア","ru":"Две каны из одной кучи знаков: как 安 стало あ, а 阿 стало ア","uk":"Дві кани з однієї купи знаків: як 安 стало あ, а 阿 стало ア","ar":"نظامان مقطعيان من كومة حروف واحدة: كيف صار 安 حرف あ وصار 阿 حرف ア","he":"שתי קאנות מאותה ערימת סימנים: איך 安 הפך ל-あ ו-阿 ל-ア","sw":"Kana mbili, rundo moja la herufi: jinsi 安 lilivyokuwa あ na 阿 likawa ア"},"summary":{"en":"Japan built both kana syllabaries from the same 8th-century man'yōgana. Hiragana is the whole kanji run together in cursive 草書 (安→あ); katakana is a single fragment of a kanji (阿→ア). The two split by who used them: women's hand versus monks' reading-marks.","ja":"日本は同じ万葉仮名から二つの仮名を作った。平仮名は漢字一字を草書で崩した形（安→あ）、片仮名は一字の断片（阿→ア）。女手と僧侶の訓点という使い分けが、二つを分けた。","ko":"일본은 두 가나 음절 문자를 모두 같은 8세기 만요가나에서 만들어 냈다. 히라가나는 한자 전체를 초서(草書)로 흘려 쓴 것(安→あ)이고, 가타카나는 한자의 한 조각만 떼어 낸 것(阿→ア)이다. 둘은 누가 썼느냐에 따라 갈렸다. 여성의 글씨냐, 승려의 독법 표기냐.","zh":"日本的两套假名音节文字都出自同一批8世纪的万叶假名。平假名是把整个汉字以草书连笔写成（安→あ），片假名则是只取汉字的一个部件（阿→ア）。二者因使用者不同而分道：一边是女性的手笔，一边是僧人的训读标记。","yue":"日本兩套假名音節文字，都係出自同一批八世紀嘅萬葉假名。平假名係成個漢字用草書連筆寫落去（安→あ）；片假名就淨係取漢字其中一個部件（阿→ア）。兩者因為使用嘅人唔同而分家：一邊係女性嘅手筆，另一邊係僧人嘅訓讀標記。","vi":"Nhật Bản dựng cả hai bộ âm tiết kana từ cùng một nguồn man'yōgana thế kỷ 8. Hiragana là cả chữ Hán viết liền theo lối thảo thư 草書 (安→あ); katakana là một mảnh duy nhất của chữ Hán (阿→ア). Hai bộ tách ra theo người dùng: nét bút của phụ nữ so với dấu đọc của các nhà sư.","th":"ญี่ปุ่นสร้างคานะทั้งสองชุดจากมันโยงานะในศตวรรษที่ 8 ชุดเดียวกัน ฮิรางานะคือคันจิทั้งตัวที่เขียนหวัดต่อเนื่องแบบ 草書 (安→あ) ส่วนคาตากานะคือคันจิแค่ชิ้นเดียว (阿→ア) ที่แยกทางกันก็เพราะคนใช้ต่างกัน ฝ่ายหนึ่งคือลายมือผู้หญิง อีกฝ่ายคือเครื่องหมายช่วยอ่านของพระ","id":"Jepang membangun kedua silabari kana dari man'yōgana abad ke-8 yang sama. Hiragana adalah kanji utuh yang ditulis bersambung dalam gaya kursif 草書 (安→あ); katakana adalah sepotong bagian dari sebuah kanji (阿→ア). Keduanya berpisah jalan menurut pemakainya: tulisan tangan perempuan dan tanda baca para biksu.","hi":"जापान ने अपनी दोनों कानाओं को एक ही 8वीं सदी की मान्योगाना से गढ़ा। हिरागाना पूरे कांजी को घसीट शैली 草書 में एक ही प्रवाह में लिखकर बनी है (安→あ); काताकाना किसी कांजी का एक अकेला टुकड़ा है (阿→ア)। दोनों इस आधार पर अलग हुईं कि उन्हें किसने इस्तेमाल किया: स्त्रियों की लिखावट बनाम भिक्षुओं के पठन-चिह्न।","de":"Japan baute beide Kana-Silbenschriften aus demselben Man'yōgana des 8. Jahrhunderts. Hiragana ist das ganze Kanji, flüssig zusammengezogen in der Schreibschrift 草書 (安→あ); Katakana ist ein einzelnes Bruchstück eines Kanji (阿→ア). Die beiden trennten sich danach, wer sie benutzte: die Frauenhand gegen die Lesezeichen der Mönche.","fr":"Le Japon a bâti ses deux syllabaires kana à partir du même man'yōgana du VIIIᵉ siècle. Le hiragana, c'est le kanji entier tracé d'un seul jet en cursive 草書 (安→あ) ; le katakana, c'est un unique fragment d'un kanji (阿→ア). Les deux se sont séparés selon qui les employait : la main des femmes face aux marques de lecture des moines.","it":"Il Giappone ricavò entrambi i sillabari kana dallo stesso man’yōgana dell’VIII secolo. Lo hiragana è un kanji intero tracciato d’un fiato in corsivo 草書 (安→あ); il katakana è un frammento di kanji (阿→ア). Le due scritture si separarono secondo chi le usava: la mano delle donne da una parte, i segni di lettura dei monaci dall’altra.","es":"Japón construyó sus dos silabarios kana a partir del mismo man'yōgana del siglo VIII. El hiragana es el kanji entero trazado de corrido en la cursiva 草書 (安→あ); el katakana es un solo fragmento de un kanji (阿→ア). Los dos se separaron según quién los usaba: la mano de las mujeres frente a las marcas de lectura de los monjes.","pt":"O Japão construiu os dois silabários kana a partir do mesmo man'yōgana do século VIII. O hiragana é o kanji inteiro traçado de uma só vez na cursiva 草書 (安→あ); o katakana é um único fragmento de um kanji (阿→ア). Os dois se separaram conforme quem os usava: a mão das mulheres frente às marcas de leitura dos monges.","ru":"Обе японские слоговые азбуки выросли из одной и той же манъёганы VIII века. Хирагана — это целый иероглиф, слитно написанный скорописью 草書 (安→あ); катакана — единственный осколок иероглифа (阿→ア). Разошлись они по тому, кто ими пользовался: женская рука против читательских помет монахов.","uk":"Обидві японські складові абетки постали з однієї й тієї ж манйоґани VIII століття. Хіраґана — це цілий ієрогліф, написаний скорописом 草書 суцільно (安→あ); катакана — єдиний уламок ієрогліфа (阿→ア). Розійшлися вони через те, хто ними писав: жіноча рука — і читацькі позначки ченців.","ar":"بنت اليابان نظامَي الكانا المقطعيين كليهما من المان'يوغانا نفسها التي تعود إلى القرن الثامن. الهيراغانا هي الكانجي كاملًا مكتوبًا بخط متصل سريع 草書 (安→あ)، أما الكاتاكانا فهي شظية واحدة مقتطعة من كانجي (阿→ア). وقد افترق الخطان بحسب مَن استعملهما: خط النساء مقابل علامات القراءة عند الرهبان.","he":"יפן בנתה את שתי מערכות הקאנה ההברתיות מאותה מאניוגאנה בת המאה ה-8. ההיראגאנה היא קאנג'י שלם שנכתב ברצף בכתב הרהוט 草書 (安→あ); הקאטאקאנה היא רסיס אחד של קאנג'י (阿→ア). השתיים נפרדו לפי מי שהשתמש בהן: כתב ידן של נשים מול סימוני הקריאה של הנזירים.","sw":"Japani ilijenga silabari zote mbili za kana kutoka man'yōgana ileile ya karne ya 8. Hiragana ni kanji nzima iliyoandikwa kwa mtiririko katika hati ya mkato 草書 (安→あ); katakana ni kipande kimoja tu cha kanji (阿→ア). Zote mbili zilitengana kutokana na nani aliyezitumia: mkono wa wanawake dhidi ya alama za kusomea za watawa."},"body":{"en":"<h3>Two Syllabaries From One Pile of Borrowed Characters</h3><p>Japanese has two phonetic syllabaries, hiragana and katakana, with the same 46 basic sounds and a completely different shape for each. Both were cut from the same block: <em>man'yōgana</em>, the practice of writing Japanese syllables with Chinese characters chosen for sound alone. In the <em>Man'yōshū</em> anthology (compiled after 759) the syllable <em>a</em> could be written 安 or 阿, and <em>i</em> could be 伊 or 以. A reader had to know dozens of characters just to spell a short poem phonetically. The two kana are two different ways of making that shorter.</p><p>Hiragana took the whole character and ran it together. Scribes wrote man'yōgana in 草書 (<em>sōsho</em>), the fastest cursive hand of Chinese calligraphy, and let the brush dissolve the strokes until only a flowing outline remained. 安 (<em>an</em>), used for <em>a</em>, melted into あ; 以 for <em>i</em> into い; 宇 into う; 衣 into え; 於 into お. The forms are datable to around 800.</p><p>Katakana did the opposite: it kept one piece and discarded the rest. The source was the same pile of man'yōgana, but written in the square 楷書 (<em>kaisho</em>) hand, and the simplification was to take a single component. ア is the left side of 阿 (the 阝 radical); イ the left side of 伊; ウ the crown 宀 of 宇; エ from 江; オ from 於; カ is the left part of 加. What survives is a fragment, which is why katakana looks angular where hiragana looks round.</p><p>The two scripts split by who used them and for what. Katakana was built in the 9th century by Buddhist monks in and around Nara who needed to read Literary Chinese aloud in Japanese. They jotted tiny syllable-marks and grammatical particles in the gaps between the columns of a Chinese sutra — the <em>kunten</em> glosses of <em>kanbun kundoku</em> — and a glyph squeezed into a margin had to be small and fast, so a fragment beat a cursive whole. For centuries katakana stayed the script of male scholarship: Buddhist and Confucian commentary and official annotation.</p><p>Hiragana went the other way, into the hands of Heian court women, who were not formally taught Chinese and wrote the national language in the cursive forms. It was called 女手 (<em>onnade</em>), \"women's hand\". The major works of Heian literature are in it: Ki no Tsurayuki's <em>Tosa Nikki</em> (935) is written in kana throughout, with Tsurayuki — a man — opening in the voice of a woman so that he could use it; Murasaki Shikibu's <em>Tale of Genji</em> (c. 1008) is almost entirely hiragana and native vocabulary.</p><p>For a thousand years there was no single correct shape per sound. The same syllable could be written with several different cursive characters — <em>ha</em> from 波, 者, or 八 — and all were ordinary. These variant forms are called 変体仮名 (<em>hentaigana</em>). The one-sign-per-sound set now taught was fixed only by a government rule for elementary schools in 1900, which chose one hiragana for each syllable and retired the rest; the discarded shapes still turn up on old shop signs and soba-restaurant curtains.</p><p>The division of labour left over from the Heian split is still working. Hiragana writes grammar and native words; katakana writes loanwords, foreign names, onomatopoeia, and emphasis. A single sentence routinely runs kanji, hiragana and katakana together — three outputs of the same 8th-century decision to spell Japanese with Chinese characters, kept apart by which part of the character each one chose to keep.</p><div class='trivia-actions'><button type='button' class='trivia-action' data-action='panto' data-lat='35.01' data-lng='135.77' data-zoom='6'>🌍 Pan to Heian-kyō (Kyoto)</button></div>"},"sources":[{"text":"Hiragana, Wikipedia (accessed 2026): origin of あ from cursive 安, period c. 800, onnade women's hand","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hiragana"},{"text":"Katakana, Wikipedia (accessed 2026): 9th-century development by Buddhist monks near Nara from kanji fragments","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Katakana"},{"text":"Hentaigana, Wikipedia (accessed 2026): variant cursive kana standardized to one sign per sound by the 1900 elementary-school ordinance","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hentaigana"},{"text":"Christopher Seeley (1991), A History of Writing in Japan, Leiden: Brill (man'yōgana to kana, Heian development)"}]},{"id":"bopomofo-zhuyin","icon":"🔤","tags":["taiwan","phonetics","writing-system","history","reform"],"title":{"en":"ㄅㄆㄇㄈ: The 1918 Alphabet Carved from Pieces of Characters","ja":"ㄅㄆㄇㄈ：漢字の断片からつくった1918年の表音文字","ko":"ㄅㄆㄇㄈ: 한자 조각으로 만든 1918년의 자모","zh":"ㄅㄆㄇㄈ：1918年用汉字碎片刻成的字母","yue":"ㄅㄆㄇㄈ：1918年用漢字碎片砌出嚟嘅字母","vi":"ㄅㄆㄇㄈ: bộ chữ cái năm 1918 cắt ra từ những mảnh chữ Hán","th":"ㄅㄆㄇㄈ: ชุดอักษรปี 1918 ที่ตัดมาจากชิ้นส่วนของตัวอักษรจีน","id":"ㄅㄆㄇㄈ: alfabet 1918 yang dipahat dari potongan aksara","hi":"ㄅㄆㄇㄈ: वर्णों के टुकड़ों से गढ़ी गई १९१८ की वर्णमाला","de":"ㄅㄆㄇㄈ: Das Alphabet von 1918, geschnitten aus Teilen von Schriftzeichen","fr":"ㄅㄆㄇㄈ : l'alphabet de 1918 taillé dans des morceaux de caractères","it":"ㄅㄆㄇㄈ: l'alfabeto del 1918 ricavato da frammenti di caratteri","es":"ㄅㄆㄇㄈ: el alfabeto de 1918 tallado a partir de fragmentos de caracteres","pt":"ㄅㄆㄇㄈ: o alfabeto de 1918 esculpido a partir de pedaços de caracteres","ru":"ㄅㄆㄇㄈ: алфавит 1918 года, вырезанный из частей иероглифов","uk":"ㄅㄆㄇㄈ: абетка 1918 року, вирізана з частин ієрогліфів","ar":"ㄅㄆㄇㄈ: الأبجدية التي نُحتت عام ١٩١٨ من أجزاء الحروف الصينية","he":"ㄅㄆㄇㄈ: האלפבית משנת 1918 שנחצב מחלקי סימנים","sw":"ㄅㄆㄇㄈ: alfabeti ya 1918 iliyochongwa kutoka vipande vya herufi za Kichina"},"summary":{"en":"In 1918 the Republic of China promulgated a phonetic script whose 37 symbols are drawn from Chinese characters, not Latin letters. The mainland later switched to Pinyin; Taiwan still teaches and types with it.","ja":"1918年、中華民国はラテン文字ではなく漢字に由来する37の記号で表音文字を公布した。大陸はのちにピンインへ移ったが、台湾は今も学習と入力に使い続けている。","ko":"1918년 중화민국이 반포한 표음 문자로, 37개 기호가 라틴 문자가 아니라 한자에서 따온 것이다. 본토는 훗날 병음으로 바꿨지만, 대만은 지금도 이 문자로 가르치고 입력한다.","zh":"1918年，中华民国颁布了一套表音文字，它的37个符号不是拉丁字母，而是取自汉字。大陆后来改用拼音，台湾至今仍用它教学和打字。","yue":"1918年，中華民國頒布咗一套表音文字，佢嘅37個符號唔係拉丁字母，而係取自漢字。大陸後來改用拼音，台灣到今日都仲用佢嚟教書同打字。","vi":"Năm 1918, Trung Hoa Dân Quốc ban hành một hệ ký hiệu ghi âm mà 37 ký hiệu của nó được lấy từ chữ Hán chứ không phải chữ cái Latinh. Đại lục về sau chuyển sang Bính âm; Đài Loan đến nay vẫn dạy và gõ bằng nó.","th":"ในปี 1918 สาธารณรัฐจีนประกาศใช้ระบบสัญลักษณ์แทนเสียง 37 ตัวที่ดัดมาจากตัวอักษรจีน ไม่ใช่อักษรละติน ต่อมาจีนแผ่นดินใหญ่เปลี่ยนไปใช้พินอิน แต่ไต้หวันยังใช้ระบบนี้ทั้งในการสอนและการพิมพ์","id":"Pada 1918 Republik Tiongkok mengumumkan sistem tulisan fonetis yang ke-37 simbolnya diambil dari aksara Tionghoa, bukan huruf Latin. Tiongkok Daratan kemudian beralih ke Pinyin, sedangkan Taiwan masih mengajarkannya dan memakainya untuk mengetik.","hi":"१९१८ में चीनी गणराज्य ने एक ध्वन्यात्मक लिपि जारी की जिसके ३७ चिह्न लैटिन अक्षर नहीं, बल्कि चीनी वर्णों से लिए गए हैं। मुख्यभूमि बाद में पिनयिन पर चली गई; ताइवान आज भी इसी से पढ़ाता और टाइप करता है।","de":"1918 verkündete die Republik China eine Lautschrift, deren 37 Zeichen von chinesischen Schriftzeichen abgeleitet sind, nicht von lateinischen Buchstaben. Das Festland wechselte später zu Pinyin; Taiwan unterrichtet und tippt noch immer mit ihr.","fr":"En 1918, la République de Chine promulgua une écriture phonétique dont les 37 symboles sont tirés de caractères chinois, et non de lettres latines. Le continent est passé plus tard au pinyin ; Taïwan l'enseigne et l'utilise encore pour taper.","it":"Nel 1918 la Repubblica di Cina promulgò una scrittura fonetica i cui 37 simboli sono tratti da caratteri cinesi, non da lettere latine. La Cina continentale passò poi al pinyin; Taiwan lo insegna e lo usa ancora per scrivere.","es":"En 1918 la República de China promulgó una escritura fonética cuyos 37 símbolos proceden de caracteres chinos, no de letras latinas. Más tarde, el continente adoptó el pinyin; Taiwán todavía lo enseña y lo usa para escribir.","pt":"Em 1918, a República da China promulgou uma escrita fonética cujos 37 símbolos derivam de caracteres chineses, e não de letras latinas. O continente passou mais tarde ao pinyin; Taiwan ainda o ensina e o usa para digitar.","ru":"В 1918 году Китайская Республика ввела фонетическое письмо, 37 знаков которого взяты из китайских иероглифов, а не из латинского алфавита. Материковый Китай позднее перешёл на пиньинь; на Тайване по нему до сих пор учат и набирают текст.","uk":"1918 року Китайська Республіка оприлюднила фонетичне письмо, 37 знаків якого взято з китайських ієрогліфів, а не з латинської абетки. Материк згодом перейшов на піньїнь; Тайвань досі навчає й набирає текст саме ним.","ar":"في عام ١٩١٨ أصدرت جمهورية الصين نصًّا صوتيًّا رموزه الـ٣٧ مأخوذة من حروف صينية، لا من حروف لاتينية. وقد انتقل البرّ الصيني لاحقًا إلى البينيين، بينما لا تزال تايوان تُدرّسه وتكتب به.","he":"ב-1918 הכריזה הרפובליקה הסינית על כתב פונטי ש-37 סימניו נלקחו מסימנים סיניים ולא מאותיות לטיניות. סין היבשתית עברה לימים לפ'ין-יין; בטייוואן עדיין מלמדים ומקלידים בו.","sw":"Mnamo 1918 Jamhuri ya China ilitangaza mfumo wa maandishi wa kifonetiki ambao alama zake 37 zimetokana na herufi za Kichina, si herufi za Kilatini. Baadaye bara likahamia Pinyin; Taiwan bado inaufundisha na kuutumia kuchapa."},"body":{"en":"<p>The name <strong>bopomofo</strong> is simply its first four symbols read aloud: <strong>ㄅ ㄆ ㄇ ㄈ</strong> (<em>b p m f</em>). It is a phonetic script that annotates the sounds of characters rather than replacing them: its 37 symbols are 21 initial consonants, 3 medials and 13 rhymes such as ㄢ (<em>an</em>) and ㄤ (<em>ang</em>), and five tone marks give the tone. What makes it unusual is that none of those symbols is a Latin letter. Every one is taken from a Chinese character — usually a component in an ancient form, occasionally a whole simple character such as 一 (ㄧ) or 丫 (ㄚ).</p><h3>A commission, three camps, two months</h3><p>In December 1912 the new Republic of China set up the Commission on the Unification of Pronunciation (讀音統一會). Forty-four delegates met in Beijing on 15 February 1913 under the chairmanship of Wu Zhihui, and split into three camps: one wanted whole Chinese characters used as phonetic markers, one wanted Latin letters, and one wanted newly invented symbols. They argued for about two months before the commission closed on 22 May 1913. The design they settled on took 15 symbols from a set the scholar Zhang Binglin had drawn in <em>zhuanshu</em> (seal script) and called 記音字母, \"sound-recording letters\".</p><h3>Where the shapes come from</h3><p>Each symbol is a stripped-down form of a character whose own reading contains that sound — at the start for a consonant, in the rhyme for a final, so ㄢ (<em>an</em>) comes from the archaic 𢎘 (<em>hàn</em>). ㄅ (<em>b</em>) is 勹, the top of 包 (<em>bāo</em>). ㄆ (<em>p</em>) is 攵, a variant of 攴 (<em>pū</em>). ㄇ (<em>m</em>) is 冂, the archaic form of the \"cover\" radical 冖 (<em>mì</em>). ㄈ (<em>f</em>) is 匚 (<em>fāng</em>), the \"open box\" radical. The principle is acrophony — taking a symbol's value from the start of the thing it is drawn from — the same move that turned an ox's head into Greek <em>alpha</em>.</p><div class='trivia-actions'><button type='button' class='trivia-action' data-action='panto' data-lat='39.90' data-lng='116.40' data-zoom='5'>🌍 Pan to Beijing, 1913</button></div><h3>From \"letters\" to \"symbols\"</h3><p>The script was proclaimed on 23 November 1918 as 注音字母 (<em>Zhuyin Zimu</em>), \"sound-annotating letters\". In April 1930 it was renamed 注音符號 (<em>Zhuyin Fuhao</em>), \"sound-annotating symbols\". The change of one word mattered: 字母 (\"letters\") suggested an alphabet that might one day stand on its own and push characters aside, and \"symbols\" put it back in its place as an aid to reading characters, never a replacement. Along the way three symbols for dialect sounds — ㄪ (<em>v</em>), ㄫ (<em>ng</em>) and ㄬ (<em>gn</em>) — were dropped from standard use, though all three still sit in the Unicode block.</p><h3>A split down the strait</h3><p>On 11 February 1958 the First National People's Congress adopted Hanyu Pinyin, the Latin-based system developed by a team led by Zhou Youguang. Mainland schools moved to it, and a child learning to read in Beijing today meets <em>b p m f</em> in Roman letters. Taiwan kept the original. In Taiwanese primary schools ㄅㄆㄇㄈ is still the first thing a reader learns, printed as small annotations beside the characters, and it is the most common way people type Chinese on a phone or keyboard. The same symbols have been extended with extra shapes to write Taiwanese Hokkien and Hakka. A hundred-odd years after the shapes were lifted out of ancient characters, they remain the writing a Taiwanese child meets months before the characters they annotate.</p><div class='trivia-actions'><button type='button' class='trivia-action' data-action='panto' data-lat='25.03' data-lng='121.56' data-zoom='6'>🌍 Pan to Taipei</button></div>"},"sources":[{"text":"Bopomofo — Wikipedia (symbol origins, 37 symbols, 1918/1930 dates)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bopomofo"},{"text":"Commission on the Unification of Pronunciation — Wikipedia (1912–1913 commission, 44 delegates, Zhang Binglin's 15 symbols)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Commission_on_the_Unification_of_Pronunciation"},{"text":"Pinyin — Wikipedia (Hanyu Pinyin adopted 11 Feb 1958, Zhou Youguang)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pinyin"},{"text":"DeFrancis, John (1984). The Chinese Language: Fact and Fantasy. University of Hawaii Press.","url":""}]},{"id":"kangxi-214-radicals","icon":"📖","tags":["china","writing","history","dictionary"],"title":{"en":"214 Drawers: How the Kangxi Dictionary Filed 47,000 Characters","ja":"214の引き出し：康熙字典は4万7千字をどう並べたか","ko":"214개의 서랍: 강희자전은 어떻게 47,000자를 분류했는가","zh":"214个抽屉：《康熙字典》如何归档四万七千个汉字","yue":"214個抽屜：《康熙字典》點樣收納四萬七千個漢字","vi":"214 ngăn kéo: Tự điển Khang Hy đã sắp xếp 47.000 chữ Hán như thế nào","th":"ลิ้นชัก 214 ใบ: พจนานุกรมคังซีจัดเก็บอักษร 47,000 ตัวได้อย่างไร","id":"214 laci: cara Kamus Kangxi menata 47.000 aksara","hi":"214 दराज़ें: कांगशी शब्दकोश ने 47,000 अक्षरों को कैसे सहेजा","de":"214 Schubladen: Wie das Kangxi-Wörterbuch 47.000 Schriftzeichen ordnete","fr":"214 tiroirs : comment le dictionnaire de Kangxi a classé 47 000 caractères","it":"214 cassetti: come il Dizionario Kangxi archiviò 47.000 caratteri","es":"214 cajones: cómo el Diccionario Kangxi archivó 47.000 caracteres","pt":"214 gavetas: como o Dicionário Kangxi arquivou 47.000 caracteres","ru":"214 ящиков: как словарь Канси разложил 47 000 иероглифов","uk":"214 шухляд: як словник Кансі розклав 47 000 ієрогліфів","ar":"214 درجًا: كيف صنّف معجم كانغشي 47,000 حرف","he":"214 מגירות: איך מילון קאנגשי תייק 47,000 סימנים","sw":"Droo 214: jinsi kamusi ya Kangxi ilivyopanga herufi 47,000"},"summary":{"en":"The 1716 Kangxi Dictionary sorted about 47,035 characters under 214 radicals, placing each character first by a shared graphic component and then by residual stroke count — a scheme that had cut Xu Shen's 540 radicals to 214 and still orders dictionaries today.","ja":"1716年の『康熙字典』は約47,035字を214の部首に分け、各字をまず共有する字形部分で、次いで残り画数で並べた。許慎の540部首を214に削ったこの方式は、今も辞書の配列を支えている。","ko":"1716년 강희자전은 약 47,035자를 214개 부수 아래 분류했다. 각 글자를 먼저 공통된 자형 요소로, 그다음 남은 획수로 자리매김하는 방식으로, 허신의 540부수를 214개로 줄였으면서도 오늘날까지 자전의 배열을 지배하는 체계다.","zh":"1716年刊行的《康熙字典》将约47,035个汉字归入214个部首之下，每个字先按共有的字形部件、再按剩余笔画数定位——这套体系把许慎的540部首削减为214部，至今仍在为字典排序。","yue":"1716年刊行嘅《康熙字典》，將大約47,035個漢字歸入214個部首之下，每個字先按共有嘅字形部件、再按剩低嘅筆畫數嚟定位——呢套方法將許慎嘅540部首削到214部，到今日仲係字典排序嘅準則。","vi":"Tự điển Khang Hy năm 1716 sắp khoảng 47.035 chữ Hán vào 214 bộ thủ, xếp mỗi chữ trước hết theo một thành phần tự hình chung rồi theo số nét còn lại — một cách thức đã rút 540 bộ thủ của Hứa Thận xuống còn 214 và đến nay vẫn định thứ tự cho các từ điển.","th":"พจนานุกรมคังซีปี 1716 จัดเรียงอักษรราว 47,035 ตัวไว้ภายใต้หมวดนำ 214 หมวด โดยจัดอักษรตามส่วนประกอบที่มีร่วมกันก่อน แล้วจึงเรียงตามจำนวนขีดที่เหลือ ระบบนี้ลดหมวดนำ 540 หมวดของสวี่เชินลงเหลือ 214 และยังเป็นแม่แบบการเรียงพจนานุกรมมาจนทุกวันนี้","id":"Kamus Kangxi tahun 1716 menata sekitar 47.035 aksara di bawah 214 radikal: setiap aksara dikelompokkan menurut komponen grafis bersama, lalu menurut jumlah goresan sisanya. Skema yang memangkas 540 radikal Xu Shen menjadi 214 ini masih dipakai untuk menyusun kamus hingga kini.","hi":"1716 के कांगशी शब्दकोश ने लगभग 47,035 अक्षरों को 214 रैडिकलों में बाँटा: हर अक्षर पहले एक साझा घटक के हिसाब से रखा गया, फिर बचे स्ट्रोकों की गिनती के हिसाब से। इस पद्धति ने शू शेन के 540 रैडिकलों को घटाकर 214 कर दिया था, और शब्दकोश आज भी इसी क्रम पर चलते हैं।","de":"Das Kangxi-Wörterbuch von 1716 ordnete rund 47.035 Schriftzeichen 214 Radikalen zu: jedes Zeichen zuerst nach einem gemeinsamen grafischen Bestandteil, dann nach der Zahl der verbleibenden Striche – ein Schema, das die 540 Radikale Xu Shens auf 214 reduziert hatte und bis heute die Ordnung von Wörterbüchern bestimmt.","fr":"Le dictionnaire de Kangxi, paru en 1716, rangeait environ 47 035 caractères sous 214 clés : chaque caractère était placé d'abord selon un composant graphique commun, puis selon le nombre de traits restants. Ce système, qui avait ramené les 540 clés de Xu Shen à 214, ordonne encore les dictionnaires aujourd'hui.","it":"Il Dizionario Kangxi del 1716 ordinò circa 47.035 caratteri sotto 214 radicali, classificando ogni carattere prima in base a una componente grafica e poi in base ai tratti rimanenti: uno schema che aveva ridotto a 214 i 540 radicali di Xu Shen e che ordina ancora oggi i dizionari.","es":"El Diccionario Kangxi de 1716 clasificó unos 47.035 caracteres bajo 214 radicales: cada carácter se ubicaba primero por un componente gráfico compartido y después por el número de trazos restantes, un sistema que había reducido los 540 radicales de Xu Shen a 214 y que todavía hoy ordena los diccionarios.","pt":"O Dicionário Kangxi, de 1716, organizou cerca de 47.035 caracteres sob 214 radicais, situando cada caractere primeiro por um componente gráfico comum e depois pelo número de traços restantes — um esquema que havia reduzido os 540 radicais de Xu Shen a 214 e que ainda hoje ordena os dicionários.","ru":"Словарь Канси 1716 года распределил около 47 035 иероглифов по 214 ключам: каждый знак сначала относили к общему графическому элементу, а затем упорядочивали по числу оставшихся черт. Эта схема, сократившая 540 ключей Сюй Шэня до 214, по сей день задаёт порядок в словарях.","uk":"Словник Кансі 1716 року розподілив близько 47 035 ієрогліфів за 214 ключами: кожен знак спершу відносили до спільного графічного елемента, а тоді впорядковували за кількістю решти рисок. Ця схема, що скоротила 540 ключів Сюй Шеня до 214, і досі визначає порядок у словниках.","ar":"صنّف معجم كانغشي الصادر عام 1716 نحو 47,035 حرفًا تحت 214 جذرًا، فوضع كل حرف أولًا بحسب مكوِّن خطّي مشترك ثم بحسب عدد الضربات المتبقية، وهو نظام اختزل جذور شو شين الـ540 إلى 214، ولا تزال المعاجم تُرتَّب به حتى اليوم.","he":"מילון קאנגשי משנת 1716 מיין כ־47,035 סימנים תחת 214 רדיקלים: כל סימן סווג תחילה לפי רכיב גרפי משותף ואחר כך לפי מספר המשיכות הנותרות. השיטה צמצמה את 540 הרדיקלים של שו שן ל־214, ומילונים ממוינים לפיה עד היום.","sw":"Kamusi ya Kangxi ya mwaka 1716 ilipanga takriban herufi 47,035 chini ya radikali 214, ikiweka kila herufi kwanza kwa kijenzi cha kimaumbo kinachoshirikiwa na kisha kwa idadi ya mistari iliyobaki. Mfumo huo ulikuwa umepunguza radikali 540 za Xu Shen hadi 214, na bado unapanga kamusi hadi leo."},"body":{"en":"<h3>One Character, One Drawer</h3><p>The Kangxi Dictionary (康熙字典) was ordered by the Kangxi Emperor in 1710 and published in 1716. Its first two editors, Zhang Yushu (1642–1711) and Chen Tingjing (1639–1712), both died within two years of being appointed, and scholars of the Hanlin Academy finished the work. It filed roughly 47,035 characters into 214 drawers. Every character belonged to exactly one drawer, identified by a shared graphic component — its <strong>部首 (bùshǒu, section head, or radical)</strong> — and was then placed inside that drawer by a second number: the strokes left over once the radical is subtracted.</p><h3>Radical Plus Residue</h3><p>To look up 校, you find its radical 木 (tree, radical 75), count the remaining strokes — 交 adds six — and turn to the wood, +6 section. The index is mechanical, but the drawers are wildly uneven. Radical 140 艸 (grass) holds 1,902 characters, the most; radical 85 水 (water) holds 1,595 and radical 75 木 holds 1,369, while radical 138 艮 holds just 5. The radicals themselves run from one stroke (一, radical 1) to seventeen (龠, a flute, radical 214), with a median of five.</p><div class='trivia-actions'><button type='button' class='trivia-action' data-action='setchar' data-char='水'>🔡 Show 水 (radical 85)</button><button type='button' class='trivia-action' data-action='panto' data-lat='39.92' data-lng='116.39' data-zoom='4'>🌍 Pan to Beijing</button></div><h3>Which Part Counts</h3><p>The system only works if you already know which component is the radical, and that is not always the obvious one. 聞 to hear is filed under 耳 (ear, radical 128), not under the 門 (gate) that surrounds it. 問 to ask goes under 口 (mouth), while 間 interval goes under 門. A character with no obvious semantic part is assigned a radical on graphic grounds, so finding 甚 or 乃 in a paper dictionary is a small test of whether you have internalised the convention.</p><h3>From 540 to 214</h3><p>The radical was not a Qing invention. Xu Shen's <em>Shuowen Jiezi</em> (說文解字), presented to the Han court in 121 CE, already sorted 9,353 characters under 540 section heads — a figure later readers explained as 6 × 9 × 10, a cosmological number. 540 was many and fine-grained; some headed only a single character. Mei Yingzuo's <em>Zihui</em> (字彙) of 1615 cut the list to 214 and added the residual-stroke ordering, and the <em>Zhengzitong</em> (正字通) of 1627 kept the same 214. The Kangxi Dictionary adopted both innovations, and because the dictionary became the standard reference, these 214 are known today as the <strong>Kangxi radicals</strong>.</p><h3>Still in the Machinery</h3><p>The 214 outlived the empire. Japanese dictionaries index kanji by 部首 (bushu), Korean by 부수 (busu), and Vietnamese Hán-Nôm catalogues work the same way. Unicode encodes the set as a dedicated block, Kangxi Radicals, at U+2F00–U+2FD5 (added in version 3.0, 1999), kept separate from the ordinary characters that double as those radicals in running text.</p><h3>The Open Seam</h3><p>Simplification broke the fit. When mainland China reformed thousands of characters after 1956, components that had carried the radical sometimes vanished, so the Kangxi list no longer indexes the simplified set cleanly. The current mainland standard, GF 0011-2009, uses 201 principal indexing components plus 100 associated ones, and it is this table — not the Kangxi 214 — that sets the radical order in today's Xinhua Zidian. Even the headline count is unsettled: it is often given as 47,043 character entries, a count of the supplemented edition gives 47,035 distinct entries plus 1,995 graphical variants, and Wang Yinzhi's 1831 <em>Zidian kaozheng</em> catalogued 2,588 outright errors in the 1716 text.</p>"},"sources":[{"text":"Kangxi Dictionary — Wikipedia (editors, 1716 date, 47,035 entries, 1831 Zidian kaozheng corrections)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kangxi_Dictionary"},{"text":"Kangxi radicals — Wikipedia (214 radicals, 1–17 stroke range, per-radical character counts, Unicode U+2F00–2FD5)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kangxi_radicals"},{"text":"Chinese character radicals — Wikipedia (Mei Yingzuo's Zihui 1615 cut to 214; Zhengzitong 1627; GF 0011-2009 201 components)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chinese_character_radicals"},{"text":"Shuowen Jiezi — Wikipedia (Xu Shen, 121 CE, 540 section heads, 9,353 characters)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shuowen_Jiezi"}]},{"id":"clerical-change-lishu","icon":"🖌️","tags":["sinosphere","china","script-history","history","paleography"],"title":{"en":"The Clerical Change: How 隸變 Broke the Picture Inside Every Character","ja":"隸變：すべての漢字の中の絵を壊した変化","ko":"예변(隸變): 모든 한자 속 그림을 부순 서체의 대변혁","zh":"隶变：打碎每个汉字内部图画的那场变革","yue":"隸變：點樣打碎每個漢字入面嘅圖畫","vi":"Lệ biến: cuộc chuyển đổi đã phá vỡ bức tranh trong mỗi chữ Hán","th":"การเปลี่ยนแปลงสู่อักษรลี่: 隸變 ลบภาพออกจากตัวอักษรจีนทุกตัวได้อย่างไร","id":"Perubahan klerikal: bagaimana 隸變 memecah gambar di dalam setiap aksara","hi":"लिपिक परिवर्तन: 隸變 ने हर अक्षर के भीतर की तस्वीर कैसे तोड़ दी","de":"Der Kanzleischrift-Umbruch: Wie 隸變 das Bild in jedem Schriftzeichen zerbrach","fr":"La mutation cléricale : comment le 隸變 a brisé l'image cachée dans chaque caractère","it":"Il mutamento clericale: come il 隸變 spezzò l'immagine dentro ogni carattere","es":"El cambio clerical: cómo el 隸變 rompió la imagen que había dentro de cada carácter","pt":"A mudança clerical: como o 隸變 quebrou a imagem dentro de cada caractere","ru":"Канцелярский перелом: как 隸變 сломал картинку внутри каждого иероглифа","uk":"Канцелярський злам: як 隸變 зруйнував малюнок усередині кожного ієрогліфа","ar":"تحوّل الكتبة: كيف حطّم 隸變 الصورةَ الكامنة في كل حرف صيني","he":"מהפך כתב הפקידים: איך 隸變 שבר את התמונה שבתוך כל סימן סיני","sw":"Badiliko la hati ya makarani: jinsi 隸變 lilivyovunja picha iliyomo ndani ya kila herufi"},"summary":{"en":"The 隸變 (clerical change) of the Qin–Han transition turned the curved, pictographic lines of seal script into the straight strokes of clerical script. It fixed the modern shape of nearly every Han character and cut most of them off from their own etymology.","ja":"秦から漢への移行期に起きた隸變は、篆書の曲線的で象形的な線を隷書の直線に変えた。ほぼすべての漢字の現代の姿を定め、そして多くの字を字源から切り離した。","ko":"진·한 교체기의 예변(隸變)은 전서의 구불구불하고 그림 같은 선을 예서의 곧은 획으로 바꾸어 놓았다. 이 변화로 거의 모든 한자의 현대 자형이 정해졌고, 대부분의 글자는 자기 어원과의 연결 고리를 잃었다.","zh":"秦汉之际的隶变，把篆书弯曲、象形的线条变成了隶书平直的笔画。它奠定了几乎所有汉字的现代字形，也让大多数汉字从此与自身的字源断了联系。","yue":"秦漢之交嘅隸變，將篆書彎彎曲曲、象形嘅線條變成隸書平直嘅筆畫。佢定咗差唔多所有漢字嘅現代字形，亦令大部分字從此同自己嘅字源斷咗聯繫。","vi":"Lệ biến (隸變) thời chuyển giao Tần–Hán đã biến những đường cong mang tính tượng hình của chữ triện thành những nét thẳng của chữ lệ. Nó định hình dạng chữ hiện đại của hầu hết mọi chữ Hán, đồng thời cắt đứt phần lớn chúng khỏi chính từ nguyên của mình.","th":"隸變 (การเปลี่ยนแปลงสู่อักษรลี่) ในช่วงรอยต่อระหว่างราชวงศ์ฉินกับฮั่น ได้เปลี่ยนเส้นโค้งที่มีลักษณะเป็นภาพของอักษรจ้วนให้กลายเป็นขีดตรงของอักษรลี่ รูปร่างของอักษรจีนแทบทุกตัวในปัจจุบันมาจากการเปลี่ยนแปลงครั้งนี้ และอักษรส่วนใหญ่ก็ขาดจากที่มาของตัวเองไปด้วย","id":"隸變 (perubahan klerikal) pada masa peralihan Qin–Han mengubah garis-garis lengkung yang piktografis dalam aksara segel menjadi goresan lurus aksara klerikal. Peristiwa ini menetapkan bentuk modern hampir semua aksara Han dan memutus sebagian besar aksara itu dari etimologinya sendiri.","hi":"छिन से हान के संक्रमण-काल के 隸變 (लिपिक परिवर्तन) ने मुहर लिपि की घुमावदार, चित्रात्मक रेखाओं को लिपिक लिपि के सीधे स्ट्रोकों में बदल दिया। इसने लगभग हर हान अक्षर का आधुनिक आकार तय कर दिया और उनमें से अधिकांश को उनकी अपनी व्युत्पत्ति से काट दिया।","de":"Der 隸變 (Kanzleischrift-Umbruch) am Übergang von der Qin- zur Han-Zeit verwandelte die geschwungenen, bildhaften Linien der Siegelschrift in die geraden Striche der Kanzleischrift. Er legte die heutige Gestalt fast aller Han-Schriftzeichen fest – und schnitt die meisten von ihnen von ihrer eigenen Etymologie ab.","fr":"Le 隸變 (mutation cléricale) de la transition Qin–Han a transformé les lignes courbes et pictographiques de l'écriture sigillaire en traits droits de l'écriture des clercs. Il a fixé la forme moderne de presque tous les caractères han et coupé la plupart d'entre eux de leur propre étymologie.","it":"Il 隸變 (mutamento clericale), nel passaggio dai Qin agli Han, trasformò le linee curve e pittografiche della scrittura dei sigilli nei tratti diritti della scrittura clericale. Fissò la forma moderna di quasi tutti i caratteri han e ne tagliò quasi sempre il legame con l’etimologia.","es":"El 隸變 (cambio clerical) de la transición entre los Qin y los Han convirtió las líneas curvas y pictográficas de la escritura de sello en los trazos rectos de la escritura clerical. Fijó la forma moderna de casi todos los caracteres han y desconectó a la mayoría de ellos de su propia etimología.","pt":"O 隸變 (mudança clerical) da transição Qin–Han transformou as linhas curvas e pictográficas da escrita de selo nos traços retos da escrita clerical. Fixou a forma moderna de quase todos os caracteres han e separou a maioria deles da sua própria etimologia.","ru":"隸變 («канцелярский перелом») эпохи перехода от Цинь к Хань превратил изогнутые пиктографические линии печатного письма в прямые черты канцелярского письма. Он закрепил современный облик почти каждого китайского иероглифа и отрезал большинство из них от их собственной этимологии.","uk":"隸變 («канцелярський злам») доби переходу від Цінь до Хань перетворив вигнуті піктографічні лінії печатного письма на прямі риски канцелярського письма. Він закріпив сучасний вигляд майже кожного китайського ієрогліфа й відрізав більшість із них від їхньої власної етимології.","ar":"حوّل 隸變 («تحوّل الكتبة») في مرحلة الانتقال من أسرة تشين إلى أسرة هان الخطوطَ المنحنية التصويرية لخط الأختام إلى الضربات المستقيمة لخط الكتبة. فثبّت الشكل الحديث لكل حرف صيني تقريبًا، وقطع صلة معظم الحروف بأصولها الاشتقاقية.","he":"ה־隸變 (“מהפך כתב הפקידים”) של תקופת המעבר מצ'ין להאן הפך את הקווים המעוקלים והציוריים של כתב החותם לקווים הישרים של כתב הפקידים. הוא קיבע את צורתו המודרנית של כמעט כל סימן סיני, וניתק את רובם מהאטימולוגיה שלהם עצמם.","sw":"隸變 (badiliko la hati ya makarani) wakati wa mpito kutoka enzi ya Qin hadi Han liligeuza mistari iliyopinda, yenye sura ya picha, ya hati ya muhuri kuwa mistari iliyonyooka ya hati ya makarani. Liliweka umbo la kisasa la karibu kila herufi ya Han, na likazitenganisha nyingi kati yake na asili yake yenyewe."},"body":{"en":"<h3>A prison clerk, a legend, and the slips that broke it</h3><p>Tradition tells a tidy story: a Qin official named Cheng Miao (程邈), jailed by the First Emperor, spent ten years in prison straightening the curves of seal script into a plain hand fit for government paperwork, and was pardoned for it. The Han sources say less. The late-Han scholar Cai Yong credits Cheng Miao with clerical script. The postface to Xu Shen's <em>Shuowen Jiezi</em> (說文解字, presented to the throne in 121 CE) says clerical script began among Qin officials swamped by prison and office work, and its received text attaches Cheng Miao's name to small seal instead. The prison first appears with Yang Xin in the 5th century, and the ten years only in Zhang Huaiguan's <em>Shuduan</em> in the 8th. Then in December 1975, a tomb at Shuihudi (睡虎地) in Yunmeng County, Hubei, gave up more than 1,100 bamboo slips of Qin law, written around 217 BCE — and the hand on them is already clerical, or on its way there, only four years after unification. Wooden slips from Qingchuan in Sichuan, from around 309 BCE, already show the same trend nearly a century before the First Emperor unified China. Clerical script was not invented by one man. It grew out of the fast, rectilinear \"vulgar\" writing that clerks had been using alongside formal seal script all along.</p><h3>What broke</h3><p>The change has a name: <strong>隸變</strong> (lìbiàn, \"the clerical change\"), the single largest break in the history of the Chinese character. Seal script (篆書) kept the drawn, curving line of its bronze-age ancestors; a character was still, in principle, a stylised picture. Clerical script (隸書) replaced the curve with a straight stroke drawn by a brush pressed and lifted — the horizontal that opens with a blunt \"silkworm head\" and closes in a flared \"wild-goose tail\" (蠶頭雁尾). Rounds became corners, loops became boxes, and the pictographic logic that <em>Shuowen</em> used to explain each character stopped matching the character on the page. The modern shape of almost every Han character is a clerical shape; regular script, the standard for roughly 1,800 years since, changed proportion and stroke detail, not structure.</p><h3>The 月 that is three different things</h3><p>The clearest damage is in the components. Before libian, \"meat\" (肉), \"boat\" (舟) and \"moon\" (月) were three distinct seal elements. Flattened into straight strokes, all three collapsed into the same ⺝ shape on the side of a character. So the 月 in 期 (period) really is the moon; the 月 in 服 (clothing) and 朕 (the imperial \"we\") is a boat; and the 月 in 肝 (liver), 腸 (intestine), 肘 (elbow) and dozens of other body words is meat. Traditional typesetting in Taiwan still tries to keep meat as ⺼, but most fonts no longer distinguish them. The reverse happens too: the single \"fire\" element 火 split, at the foot of a character, into the four dots 灬 — yet the four dots under 魚 (fish) and 燕 (swallow) are tails, and those under 馬 (horse) are legs, not fire at all.</p><h3>Why it matters for reading</h3><p>Libian is the wall between a modern reader and the etymology of a character. You cannot, in general, decompose a present-day character back into its original parts, because libian both merged unrelated elements and split single ones. Scholars sort the cases into omission (省變), reduction (簡變), substitution of one component by an unrelated one (調變), and outright mutation into an unrecognisable shape (突變). This is why serious paleography still begins from the seal and bronze forms rather than the character you can type: <em>Shuowen</em>'s analyses were written on the small-seal side of the wall, and often describe a character that the clerical change had already dismantled by the time the book was finished.</p><div class=\"trivia-actions\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"setchar\" data-char=\"月\">🀄 Show 月 on the map</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"panto\" data-lat=\"30.8\" data-lng=\"113.75\" data-zoom=\"6\">🌍 Pan to Shuihudi (Yunmeng)</button></div>"},"sources":[{"text":"Libian — Wikipedia (the clerical change; mechanisms 省變/簡變/調變/突變)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Libian"},{"text":"Clerical script — Wikipedia (origins, Cheng Miao legend, bafen stroke features)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Clerical_script"},{"text":"Shuihudi Qin bamboo texts — Wikipedia (excavated Dec 1975, Yunmeng, Hubei; c. 217 BCE)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shuihudi_Qin_bamboo_texts"},{"text":"City University of Hong Kong Library, 'Origin and Development of Categories of Calligraphy: Clerical Script'","url":"https://www.cityu.edu.hk/lib/about/event/ch_calligraphy/clerical_eng.htm"}]},{"id":"rhyme-dictionaries-qieyun","icon":"📚","tags":["sinosphere","middle-chinese","rhyme-book","historical-phonology","reconstruction"],"title":{"en":"Qièyùn and Guǎngyùn: The Rhyme Books That Let Us Hear Middle Chinese","ja":"『切韻』と『広韻』：中古漢語の音をよみがえらせた韻書","ko":"절운과 광운: 중고한어를 들려주는 운서","zh":"《切韵》与《广韵》：让我们听见中古汉语的韵书","yue":"《切韻》同《廣韻》：令我哋聽得到中古漢語嘅韻書","vi":"Thiết vận và Quảng vận: những bộ vận thư cho ta nghe được tiếng Hán trung cổ","th":"Qièyùn และ Guǎngyùn: ตำราสัมผัสที่ทำให้เราได้ยินเสียงภาษาจีนยุคกลาง","id":"Qièyùn dan Guǎngyùn: kamus rima yang membuat kita bisa mendengar bahasa Tionghoa Pertengahan","hi":"चिएयुन और ग्वांगयुन: वे तुक-कोश जिनसे हम मध्यकालीन चीनी को सुन पाते हैं","de":"Qièyùn und Guǎngyùn: Die Reimwörterbücher, durch die wir das Mittelchinesische hören","fr":"Qièyùn et Guǎngyùn : les dictionnaires de rimes qui nous font entendre le chinois médiéval","it":"Qièyùn e Guǎngyùn: i dizionari di rime che ci fanno sentire il cinese medio","es":"Qièyùn y Guǎngyùn: los diccionarios de rimas que nos dejan oír el chino medio","pt":"Qièyùn e Guǎngyùn: os dicionários de rimas que nos deixam ouvir o chinês médio","ru":"Цеюнь и Гуанъюнь: словари рифм, позволяющие услышать среднекитайский язык","uk":"Цеюнь і Гуан'юнь: словники рим, що дають змогу почути середньокитайську","ar":"تشيه يون وغوانغ يون: كتابا القوافي اللذان يتيحان لنا سماع الصينية الوسطى","he":"צ'ייון וגואנגיון: ספרי החריזה שמאפשרים לנו לשמוע את הסינית התיכונה","sw":"Qièyùn na Guǎngyùn: kamusi za vina zinazotuwezesha kusikia Kichina cha Kati"},"summary":{"en":"Lu Fayan's Qieyun (601) sorted 12,158 characters into 193 rhymes and spelled each sound with the fanqie method. Through its expansion, the Guangyun (1008, 206 rhymes), it is still the backbone of every Middle Chinese reconstruction.","ja":"陸法言の『切韻』(601年)は12,158字を193の韻に分け、反切で各字の音を示した。その増補版『広韻』(1008年、206韻)を通じて、今も中古漢語再建の土台であり続けている。","ko":"육법언의 『절운』(601)은 12,158자를 193운으로 나누고 각 음을 반절로 표기했다. 이를 증보한 『광운』(1008, 206운)을 통해 지금도 모든 중고한어 재구의 뼈대가 되고 있다.","zh":"陆法言的《切韵》（601年）把12158个字分入193韵，并用反切为每个音注音。经由其增订本《广韵》（1008年，206韵），它至今仍是一切中古汉语构拟的骨架。","yue":"陸法言嘅《切韻》（601年）將12,158個字分入193韻，每個音都用反切標注。經由佢嘅增訂本《廣韻》（1008年，206韻），到今日仍然係所有中古漢語構擬嘅骨幹。","vi":"Thiết vận (601) của Lục Pháp Ngôn xếp 12.158 chữ vào 193 vần và ghi âm từng chữ bằng phương pháp phiên thiết. Qua bản mở rộng là Quảng vận (1008, 206 vần), bộ sách này đến nay vẫn là xương sống của mọi công trình phục nguyên tiếng Hán trung cổ.","th":"Qieyun (ค.ศ. 601) ของหลู่ฝ่าเหยียนจัดอักษร 12,158 ตัวลงใน 193 หมวดสัมผัส และระบุเสียงของแต่ละตัวด้วยวิธีฝ่านเชี่ย ผ่านฉบับขยายอย่าง Guangyun (ค.ศ. 1008, 206 หมวดสัมผัส) ตำรานี้ยังเป็นแกนของการสร้างภาษาจีนยุคกลางขึ้นใหม่ทุกสำนักมาจนทุกวันนี้","id":"Qieyun karya Lu Fayan (601) mengelompokkan 12.158 aksara ke dalam 193 rima dan mengeja setiap bunyi dengan metode fanqie. Melalui edisi perluasannya, Guangyun (1008, 206 rima), karya ini tetap menjadi tulang punggung setiap rekonstruksi bahasa Tionghoa Pertengahan.","hi":"लू फ़ायान के चिएयुन (601) ने 12,158 अक्षरों को 193 तुकों में बाँटा और हर ध्वनि को फ़ानचिए पद्धति से लिखा। अपने विस्तारित रूप ग्वांगयुन (1008, 206 तुकें) के ज़रिए यह आज भी मध्यकालीन चीनी के हर पुनर्निर्माण की रीढ़ है।","de":"Lu Fayans Qieyun (601) ordnete 12.158 Schriftzeichen in 193 Reime und gab jeden Laut mit der Fanqie-Methode an. In seiner erweiterten Fassung, dem Guangyun (1008, 206 Reime), bildet es bis heute das Rückgrat jeder Rekonstruktion des Mittelchinesischen.","fr":"Le Qieyun de Lu Fayan (601) classait 12 158 caractères en 193 rimes et notait chaque son par la méthode du fanqie. À travers sa version augmentée, le Guangyun (1008, 206 rimes), il reste la colonne vertébrale de toute reconstruction du chinois médiéval.","it":"Il Qieyun di Lu Fayan (601) ordinò 12.158 caratteri in 193 rime e trascrisse ogni suono con il metodo fanqie. Attraverso la sua versione ampliata, il Guangyun (1008, 206 rime), resta ancora oggi la spina dorsale di ogni ricostruzione del cinese medio.","es":"El Qieyun (601) de Lu Fayan ordenó 12 158 caracteres en 193 rimas y transcribió cada sonido con el método fanqie. A través de su ampliación, el Guangyun (1008, 206 rimas), sigue siendo la columna vertebral de toda reconstrucción del chino medio.","pt":"O Qieyun de Lu Fayan (601) organizou 12.158 caracteres em 193 rimas e grafou cada som pelo método fanqie. Por meio de sua versão ampliada, o Guangyun (1008, 206 rimas), continua sendo a espinha dorsal de toda reconstrução do chinês médio.","ru":"Словарь Цеюнь, составленный Лу Фаянем в 601 году, распределил 12 158 иероглифов по 193 рифмам и записал звучание каждого методом фаньце. Через своё расширенное издание — Гуанъюнь (1008, 206 рифм) — он по сей день остаётся основой любой реконструкции среднекитайского.","uk":"Словник Цеюнь, укладений Лу Фаянем 601 року, розподілив 12 158 ієрогліфів за 193 римами й записав звучання кожного методом фаньце. Через своє розширене видання — Гуан'юнь (1008, 206 рим) — він і досі є кістяком будь-якої реконструкції середньокитайської.","ar":"رتّب لو فا يان في معجمه تشيه يون (601) ما مجموعه 12,158 حرفًا في 193 قافية، وتهجّى صوت كلٍّ منها بطريقة فان تشيه. وعبر توسيعه اللاحق، معجم غوانغ يون (1008، 206 قوافٍ)، لا يزال العمود الفقري لكل إعادة بناء للصينية الوسطى.","he":"הצ'ייון של לו פאיאן (601) מיין 12,158 סימנים ל-193 חרוזים ואיית כל צליל בשיטת הפאנצ'יה. בזכות הרחבתו, הגואנגיון (1008, 206 חרוזים), הוא עדיין עמוד השדרה של כל שחזור של הסינית התיכונה.","sw":"Qieyun ya Lu Fayan (601) ilipanga herufi 12,158 katika vina 193 na kuandika kila sauti kwa mbinu ya fanqie. Kupitia toleo lake lililopanuliwa, Guangyun (1008, vina 206), bado ndiyo uti wa mgongo wa kila uundaji upya wa Kichina cha Kati."},"body":{"en":"<h3>A dictionary built to settle arguments</h3><p>In 601, under the Sui dynasty, Lu Fayan (陸法言) finished the <em>Qièyùn</em> (切韻), sorting 12,158 character entries into 193 rhyme groups across five volumes — two for the level tone, one each for the rising, departing and entering tones. Its purpose was not to describe one city's speech. In his preface Lu recalls an evening some twenty years earlier at his home in Chang'an, where eight scholars — five of northern and three of southern background, Yan Zhitui (顏之推) and Xiao Gai (蕭該) the most influential — argued over which pronunciations were correct. The book is the referee: it keeps a distinction wherever any respected tradition, northern or southern, ancient or modern, still observed it.</p><p>Each syllable is spelled by <strong>fǎnqiè</strong> (反切): two characters, the first supplying the initial consonant, the second the rest of the syllable and the tone. 東 <em>dōng</em> is given as 德紅切 — 德 (<em>dé</em>) for the <em>d-</em>, 紅 (<em>hóng</em>) for the <em>-ōng</em>. No sound is written out directly, yet the system is internally exact: trace the chains of fanqie spellers and the full inventory of initials and finals falls out, even for sounds that no living variety still keeps apart.</p><div class=\"trivia-actions\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"setchar\" data-char=\"東\">🀄 Show 東 (dōng)</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"panto\" data-lat=\"34.27\" data-lng=\"108.95\" data-zoom=\"5\">🌍 Fly to Chang'an (today's Xi'an)</button></div><p>The <em>Qièyùn</em> itself was lost. It survived by being copied and expanded: Wang Renxu's (王仁昫) revision of 706, then the Song-dynasty <em>Guǎngyùn</em> (廣韻) of 1007–1008, compiled by Chen Pengnian (陳彭年) and Qiu Yong, which grew the count to 206 rhymes and 26,194 entries while preserving the older rhyme categories almost intact. For centuries the <em>Guǎngyùn</em> was the closest thing scholars had to the original. Then in 1947, at a book stall in Beijing's Liulichang, two scholars found a nearly complete Tang manuscript of Wang Renxu's edition, copied in the early 9th century — confirming that the Song book had faithfully carried the 601 system forward.</p><p>Then came the <strong>rhyme tables</strong> (韻圖). The <em>Yùnjìng</em> (韻鏡), whose author and date are unknown and which survives in Zhang Linzhi's editions of 1161 and 1203, lays the dictionary's syllables onto 43 grids: 36 initials packed into 23 columns, and down each column the four tones, each split into four \"divisions\" (等) by medial and vowel. The tables turned the fanqie web into a map — one could see at a glance which finals shared a column and which initials contrasted.</p><p>This is the raw material of Middle Chinese. The Swedish sinologist Bernhard Karlgren (高本漢) took the <em>Guǎngyùn</em> categories and the rhyme tables as his grid and filled in phonetic values using modern Chinese dialects, which he surveyed himself, together with the Chinese loan-readings preserved in Japanese, Korean and Vietnamese. William Baxter's 1992 transcription made the logic explicit: his Middle Chinese is a notation for the distinctions the <em>Qièyùn</em> records, not a claim about how any one person spoke. It is that notation — 東 <em>tuwng</em>, 一 <em>'jit</em> — that Baxter and Sagart use as the upper anchor for their 2014 reconstruction of Old Chinese.</p><p>Which leaves the question the rhyme books cannot answer themselves. Lu Fayan assembled distinctions from several regions and periods on purpose; some of the contrasts the <em>Qièyùn</em> keeps may never have coexisted in a single mouth. Most linguists now take Middle Chinese to be a diasystem: each distinction was spoken somewhere, but no one pronounced them all.</p>"},"sources":[{"text":"Wikipedia: Qieyun (切韻, 601 CE) — 193 rhymes, 12,158 entries, fanqie method, 1947 Liulichang manuscript","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Qieyun"},{"text":"Wikipedia: Guangyun (廣韻, 1007–1008) — 206 rhymes, 26,194 entries, compiled by Chen Pengnian & Qiu Yong","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Guangyun"},{"text":"Wikipedia: Yunjing (韻鏡) rhyme table — 43 charts, 36 initials, four divisions (printed 1161, 1203)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yunjing"},{"text":"Baxter, W. H. & Sagart, L. (2014). Old Chinese: A New Reconstruction (Middle Chinese notation; online database)","url":"https://sites.lsa.umich.edu/ocbaxtersagart/"}]},{"id":"cjkv-false-friends","icon":"🔁","tags":["false-friends","vocabulary","semantics","sinosphere","japan"],"title":{"en":"手紙 Is a Letter in Tokyo and Toilet Paper in Beijing: CJKV False Friends","ja":"東京の「手紙」は letter、北京の「手紙」はトイレットペーパー：漢字圏の空似言葉","ko":"手紙는 도쿄에선 편지, 베이징에선 화장지: 한중일·베트남 한자어의 '가짜 친구'","zh":"手紙在东京是信，在北京是厕纸：中日韩越的“假朋友”","yue":"手紙喺東京係信，喺北京係廁紙：中日韓越嘅「假朋友」","vi":"手紙 là lá thư ở Tokyo nhưng là giấy vệ sinh ở Bắc Kinh: những \"người bạn giả\" trong vùng chữ Hán","th":"手紙 คือจดหมายในโตเกียว แต่เป็นกระดาษชำระในปักกิ่ง: \"เพื่อนหลอก\" ในภาษาจีน ญี่ปุ่น เกาหลี และเวียดนาม","id":"手紙: surat di Tokyo, tisu toilet di Beijing; teman palsu dalam bahasa-bahasa CJKV","hi":"手紙 टोक्यो में चिट्ठी है और बीजिंग में टॉयलेट पेपर: CJKV के झूठे दोस्त","de":"手紙 ist in Tokio ein Brief und in Peking Toilettenpapier: falsche Freunde im CJKV-Raum","fr":"手紙, une lettre à Tokyo et du papier toilette à Pékin : les faux amis CJKV","it":"手紙 è una lettera a Tokyo e carta igienica a Pechino: i falsi amici dell'area CJKV","es":"手紙 es una carta en Tokio y papel higiénico en Pekín: los falsos amigos del mundo CJKV","pt":"手紙 é carta em Tóquio e papel higiênico em Pequim: os falsos amigos do mundo CJKV","ru":"手紙 — письмо в Токио и туалетная бумага в Пекине: ложные друзья в CJKV","uk":"手紙 — лист у Токіо й туалетний папір у Пекіні: фальшиві друзі в CJKV","ar":"手紙 رسالةٌ في طوكيو وورقُ مرحاض في بكين: الأصدقاء الزائفون في لغات CJKV","he":"手紙 הוא מכתב בטוקיו ונייר טואלט בבייג'ינג: חברים כוזבים ב־CJKV","sw":"手紙 ni barua Tokyo na karatasi ya chooni Beijing: marafiki bandia wa lugha za CJKV"},"summary":{"en":"Chinese characters were borrowed into Japanese, Korean and Vietnamese and then drifted apart, leaving the same compound meaning a letter in one language and toilet paper in another, a spouse here and a mistress there.","ja":"同じ漢字を日本・韓国・ベトナムが別々の時代に借り、意味がそれぞれ独立に変化した。その結果、同一の熟語が片や「手紙」、片やトイレットペーパー、片や配偶者、片や愛人を指すようになった。","ko":"한자는 일본어·한국어·베트남어로 차용된 뒤 저마다 다른 길을 걸었다. 그 결과 같은 한자어가 한 언어에서는 편지, 다른 언어에서는 화장지를 뜻하고, 여기서는 배우자인 말이 저기서는 내연녀가 되기도 한다.","zh":"汉字先后传入日语、韩语和越南语，此后各自演变，结果同一个词在一种语言里是信件，在另一种语言里却是厕纸；在这里指配偶，到了那里却成了情妇。","yue":"漢字先後傳入日文、韓文同越南文，之後各自演變，結果同一個詞喺一種語言係信，喺另一種語言就變咗廁紙；喺呢度係配偶，去到嗰度就變咗情婦。","vi":"Chữ Hán được vay mượn vào tiếng Nhật, tiếng Hàn và tiếng Việt rồi mỗi nơi trôi theo một ngả, khiến cùng một từ ghép ở ngôn ngữ này là lá thư, ở ngôn ngữ kia lại là giấy vệ sinh; nơi này là vợ hoặc chồng, nơi khác lại là nhân tình.","th":"อักษรจีนถูกยืมไปใช้ในภาษาญี่ปุ่น เกาหลี และเวียดนาม แล้วความหมายก็ค่อย ๆ แยกไปคนละทาง คำประสมคำเดียวกันจึงเป็นจดหมายในภาษาหนึ่ง แต่เป็นกระดาษชำระในอีกภาษา เป็นคู่สมรสที่นี่ แต่เป็นเมียน้อยที่โน่น","id":"Aksara Tionghoa dipinjam ke dalam bahasa Jepang, Korea, dan Vietnam, lalu maknanya berjalan sendiri-sendiri, sehingga satu kata majemuk yang sama bisa berarti surat dalam satu bahasa dan tisu toilet dalam bahasa lain, suami atau istri di sini dan wanita simpanan di sana.","hi":"चीनी अक्षर जापानी, कोरियाई और वियतनामी में उधार लिए गए और फिर उनके अर्थ अलग-अलग दिशाओं में बह गए; नतीजा यह कि एक ही समास एक भाषा में चिट्ठी है तो दूसरी में टॉयलेट पेपर, कहीं जीवनसाथी तो कहीं विवाहेतर प्रेमिका।","de":"Chinesische Schriftzeichen wurden ins Japanische, Koreanische und Vietnamesische entlehnt und haben sich dort auseinanderentwickelt – mit dem Ergebnis, dass dasselbe Kompositum in der einen Sprache einen Brief und in der anderen Toilettenpapier bezeichnet, hier den Ehepartner und dort die Geliebte.","fr":"Empruntés par le japonais, le coréen et le vietnamien, les caractères chinois ont ensuite dérivé chacun de leur côté : un même composé désigne une lettre dans une langue et du papier toilette dans une autre, un conjoint ici et une maîtresse là-bas.","it":"Giapponese, coreano e vietnamita hanno preso in prestito i caratteri cinesi, che poi hanno fatto strade diverse: lo stesso composto significa lettera in una lingua e carta igienica in un’altra, coniuge da una parte e amante dall’altra.","es":"Los caracteres chinos pasaron al japonés, al coreano y al vietnamita y después cada lengua siguió su propio camino, de modo que un mismo compuesto significa carta en una y papel higiénico en otra, cónyuge aquí y amante allá.","pt":"Os caracteres chineses foram emprestados ao japonês, ao coreano e ao vietnamita e depois seguiram caminhos próprios, fazendo com que o mesmo composto signifique carta em uma língua e papel higiênico em outra, cônjuge aqui e amante ali.","ru":"Китайские иероглифы были заимствованы японским, корейским и вьетнамским языками, а затем их значения разошлись: одно и то же сочетание означает письмо в одном языке и туалетную бумагу в другом, супруга здесь и любовницу там.","uk":"Китайські ієрогліфи запозичили японська, корейська та в'єтнамська, а згодом значення слів розійшлися: та сама сполука означає лист в одній мові й туалетний папір в іншій, подружжя тут і коханку там.","ar":"استُعيرت الرموز الصينية إلى اليابانية والكورية والفيتنامية ثم تباعدت معانيها، فصار المركّب نفسه يعني رسالةً في لغة وورقَ مرحاض في أخرى، وزوجًا أو زوجةً هنا وعشيقةً هناك.","he":"הסימנים הסיניים נשאלו ליפנית, לקוריאנית ולווייטנאמית ומאז התרחקו זה מזה, כך שאותו צירוף פירושו מכתב בשפה אחת ונייר טואלט בשפה אחרת, בן או בת זוג כאן ומאהבת שם.","sw":"Herufi za Kichina zilikopwa na Kijapani, Kikorea na Kivietinamu, kisha maana zake zikaachana, hivi kwamba neno ambatani lilelile linamaanisha barua katika lugha moja na karatasi ya chooni katika nyingine, mume au mke huku na hawara kule."},"body":{"en":"<p>In Tokyo, <strong>手紙</strong> (<em>tegami</em>) is a letter — an envelope, a stamp, a written message. In Beijing the same two characters, written 手纸 (<em>shǒuzhǐ</em>), are toilet paper. A Japanese note that mentions 手紙 reads, to a Chinese eye, as a reference to the bathroom roll. The graphs are identical; the meanings do not overlap at all.</p>\n\n<div class=\"trivia-actions\">\n  <button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"panto\" data-lat=\"35.68\" data-lng=\"139.69\" data-zoom=\"4\">🗼 Pan to Tokyo</button>\n  <button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"panto\" data-lat=\"39.90\" data-lng=\"116.40\" data-zoom=\"4\">🏯 Pan to Beijing</button>\n</div>\n\n<p>Pairs like this run through the whole Sinosphere. Chinese characters were borrowed into Japanese, Korean and Vietnamese across different centuries, and once a compound landed, its meaning changed inside each language on its own schedule. The characters stayed frozen on the page while the words underneath them drifted. Lexicographers have a name for the result: false friends.</p>\n\n<p><strong>汽車</strong> literally reads \"steam vehicle,\" and the two neighbours pinned it to different machines. Japan fixed it on the railway: <em>kisha</em> is a steam train, so a Japanese sentence about taking the 汽車 is about boarding a train. Chinese attached the compound to the road: 汽车 (<em>qìchē</em>) is an automobile. Say you arrived by 汽車 and a Japanese listener pictures a platform while a Chinese listener pictures a car.</p>\n\n<p>The widest split is <strong>愛人</strong>, \"loved person.\" In mainland China 爱人 (<em>àirén</em>) is your spouse — the husband or wife you introduce in public — and for a romantic partner, Wiktionary notes, older speakers also say 爱人 while younger ones reach for 恋人 (<em>liànrén</em>). Japanese 愛人 (<em>aijin</em>) once meant a sweetheart or loved one, then after the Second World War slid to \"mistress,\" the partner in an affair. Korean 애인 (<em>aein</em>) sits in between as an ordinary boyfriend or girlfriend. One compound means the person you married, the person you are cheating with, and the person you are dating, depending on which capital you are in.</p>\n\n<p><strong>工夫</strong> splits four ways. Korean 공부 (<em>gongbu</em>) is study, the word a student uses for homework. Japanese 工夫 (<em>kufū</em>) is devising a method, ingenuity — and in Zen, disciplined mental effort. Chinese 工夫 (<em>gōngfu</em>) is time or effort spent on something, and its twin graph 功夫 is skill earned that way: the <em>kung fu</em> of martial arts. <strong>先生</strong>, \"born before,\" is a teacher in Japanese (<em>sensei</em>) and Korean (선생, <em>seonsaeng</em>), a plain \"Mr.\" for any grown man in Mandarin (<em>xiānsheng</em>), and in Vietnamese the literary <em>tiên sinh</em> you mostly hear in period and martial-arts dramas — everyday \"Mr.\" in Vietnam is <em>ông</em>, itself an older Sino-Vietnamese borrowing of 翁 \"old man.\"</p>\n\n<h3>Why the same character drifts</h3>\n<p>Two forces do most of the work. The first is ordinary semantic change running separately in each country: 丈夫 is a sturdy, healthy thing in Japanese (<em>jōbu</em>) but a husband in Chinese (<em>zhàngfu</em>); 娘 is a daughter in Japanese (<em>musume</em>) and a mother or woman in Chinese (<em>niáng</em>). The second is re-export. Many two-character compounds were coined in Meiji-era Japan (1868–1912) to translate Western concepts — <em>wasei-kango</em>, \"Japanese-made Chinese words\" — and shipped back into Chinese, Korean and Vietnamese, where they then drifted again. 新聞, by contrast, is an old Chinese word for news that changed on the Japanese side alone: clipped from 新聞紙 \"news-paper-sheet,\" <em>shinbun</em> became the newspaper itself, while Chinese 新闻 (<em>xīnwén</em>) is still plain news.</p>\n\n<p>Each pair is a record of a shared character borrowed once and then left to mean whatever its host language needed. Whether constant cross-border contact online pulls any of these back together, or simply adds new divergences, is not yet settled.</p>"},"sources":[{"text":"Wikipedia, \"List of Chinese–Japanese false friends\" (semantic change; 手紙, 汽車, 愛人, 工夫, 先生, 丈夫, 娘, 新聞)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_Chinese%E2%80%93Japanese_false_friends"},{"text":"Wiktionary, 愛人 (Chinese àirén 'spouse'; Japanese aijin shift from 'sweetheart' to 'mistress'; usage note on 恋人)","url":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/%E6%84%9B%E4%BA%BA"},{"text":"Wiktionary, 工夫 (Chinese gōngfu 'time/effort'; Japanese kufū 'contriving'; Korean 공부 'study')","url":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/%E5%B7%A5%E5%A4%AB"},{"text":"Bauer & cjvlang.com, \"汽車 kisha / 汽车 qìchē\" (steam-vehicle compound: train in Japanese, car in Chinese)","url":"https://cjvlang.com/Spicks/kashahuoche.html"}]},{"id":"chinese-telegraph-code","icon":"📡","tags":["china","telegraph","history","writing-system","hong-kong"],"title":{"en":"0022 Means 中: The Four-Digit Code That Put Chinese on the Telegraph","ja":"0022が「中」になる：漢字を電報に乗せた四桁コード","ko":"0022는 中: 한자를 전신에 실어 보낸 네 자리 부호","zh":"0022 代表中字：让汉字登上电报的四位数码","yue":"0022 即係中字：令中文登上電報嘅四位數碼","vi":"0022 nghĩa là 中: mã bốn chữ số đưa chữ Hán lên điện báo","th":"0022 คือ 中: รหัสสี่หลักที่พาภาษาจีนขึ้นสายโทรเลข","id":"0022 berarti 中: kode empat angka yang membawa bahasa Tionghoa ke telegraf","hi":"0022 का अर्थ है 中: वह चार अंकों वाला कोड जिसने चीनी को टेलीग्राफ़ तक पहुँचाया","de":"0022 bedeutet 中: Der vierstellige Code, der das Chinesische auf den Telegrafen brachte","fr":"0022 veut dire 中 : le code à quatre chiffres qui a fait entrer le chinois dans le télégraphe","it":"0022 significa 中: il codice a quattro cifre che portò il cinese sul telegrafo","es":"0022 significa 中: el código de cuatro cifras que llevó el chino al telégrafo","pt":"0022 quer dizer 中: o código de quatro dígitos que levou o chinês ao telégrafo","ru":"0022 значит 中: четырёхзначный код, который научил телеграф китайскому","uk":"0022 означає 中: чотиризначний код, що навчив телеграф китайської","ar":"0022 تعني 中: الشيفرة ذات الأرقام الأربعة التي أوصلت الصينية إلى التلغراف","he":"0022 פירושו 中: הקוד בן ארבע הספרות שהעלה את הסינית על הטלגרף","sw":"0022 maana yake ni 中: msimbo wa tarakimu nne uliokiingiza Kichina kwenye telegrafu"},"summary":{"en":"Morse code was built for an alphabet, so in 1872 a customs official in Shanghai turned every Chinese character into a four-digit number. The 電報碼 outlived the telegraph and still prints on Hong Kong ID cards.","ja":"モールス信号はアルファベット用に作られていた。そこで1872年、上海の税関職員は漢字を四桁の数字に置き換えた。電報碼は電報そのものより長生きし、今も香港の身分証に印字されている。","ko":"모스 부호는 알파벳을 위해 만들어졌다. 그래서 1872년 상하이의 한 세관 관리가 한자 하나하나를 네 자리 숫자로 바꾸었다. 이 電報碼는 전신보다 오래 살아남아 지금도 홍콩 신분증에 찍혀 나온다.","zh":"摩尔斯电码是为字母文字设计的，于是在1872年，上海的一位海关官员把每个汉字都变成了一个四位数字。这套电报码（電報碼）比电报本身活得更久，至今仍印在香港身份证上。","yue":"摩斯電碼係為字母文字而設嘅，所以喺1872年，上海一位海關官員將每個漢字都變成一個四位數字。呢套電報碼比電報本身命仲長，到今日仲印喺香港身份證上面。","vi":"Mã Morse được tạo ra cho chữ cái, nên năm 1872, một viên chức hải quan ở Thượng Hải đã biến mỗi chữ Hán thành một con số bốn chữ số. 電報碼 sống lâu hơn cả điện báo và đến nay vẫn được in trên thẻ căn cước Hồng Kông.","th":"รหัสมอร์สทำมาเพื่ออักษรแบบตัวอักษร ในปี 1872 เจ้าหน้าที่ศุลกากรคนหนึ่งในเซี่ยงไฮ้จึงแปลงอักษรจีนแต่ละตัวเป็นตัวเลขสี่หลัก 電報碼 นี้อยู่มานานกว่าโทรเลขเสียอีก และทุกวันนี้ยังพิมพ์อยู่บนบัตรประจำตัวของฮ่องกง","id":"Kode Morse dibuat untuk alfabet, sehingga pada 1872 seorang pejabat bea cukai di Shanghai mengubah setiap aksara Tionghoa menjadi angka empat digit. 電報碼 bertahan lebih lama daripada telegraf dan masih tercetak di kartu identitas Hong Kong.","hi":"मोर्स कोड एक वर्णमाला के लिए बना था, इसलिए 1872 में शंघाई के एक सीमा-शुल्क अधिकारी ने हर चीनी अक्षर को चार अंकों की संख्या में बदल दिया। 電報碼 टेलीग्राफ़ से भी ज़्यादा टिका और आज भी हांगकांग के पहचान-पत्रों पर छपता है।","de":"Der Morsecode war für ein Alphabet gemacht. Deshalb verwandelte ein Zollbeamter in Shanghai 1872 jedes chinesische Schriftzeichen in eine vierstellige Zahl. Der 電報碼 hat den Telegrafen überlebt und steht bis heute auf Hongkonger Personalausweisen.","fr":"Le morse était conçu pour un alphabet : en 1872, un fonctionnaire des douanes de Shanghai transforma donc chaque caractère chinois en un nombre à quatre chiffres. Le 電報碼 a survécu au télégraphe et figure encore sur les cartes d'identité de Hong Kong.","it":"Il codice Morse era pensato per un alfabeto, e così nel 1872 un funzionario doganale di Shanghai trasformò ogni carattere cinese in un numero di quattro cifre. Il 電報碼 è sopravvissuto al telegrafo ed è ancora stampato sulle carte d'identità di Hong Kong.","es":"El código Morse se diseñó para un alfabeto, así que en 1872 un funcionario de aduanas de Shanghái convirtió cada carácter chino en un número de cuatro cifras. El 電報碼 sobrevivió al telégrafo y todavía aparece impreso en los documentos de identidad de Hong Kong.","pt":"O código Morse foi feito para um alfabeto, por isso, em 1872, um funcionário da alfândega de Xangai transformou cada caractere chinês em um número de quatro dígitos. O 電報碼 sobreviveu ao telégrafo e ainda aparece impresso nas carteiras de identidade de Hong Kong.","ru":"Азбука Морзе создавалась для алфавита, поэтому в 1872 году таможенный чиновник в Шанхае превратил каждый китайский иероглиф в четырёхзначное число. 電報碼 пережил телеграф и до сих пор печатается на гонконгских удостоверениях личности.","uk":"Азбуку Морзе створили для алфавіту, тож 1872 року митний службовець у Шанхаї перетворив кожен китайський ієрогліф на чотиризначне число. 電報碼 пережив телеграф і досі друкується на гонконзьких посвідченнях особи.","ar":"صُممت شيفرة مورس لأبجدية، فحوّل موظف جمارك في شنغهاي عام 1872 كل رمز صيني إلى عدد من أربعة أرقام. وقد عاش 電報碼 أطول من التلغراف نفسه، ولا يزال يُطبع على بطاقات الهوية في هونغ كونغ.","he":"קוד מורס נבנה עבור אלפבית, ולכן ב־1872 הפך פקיד מכס בשנגחאי כל סימן סיני למספר בן ארבע ספרות. ה־電報碼 האריך ימים יותר מהטלגרף, והוא מודפס עד היום על תעודות זהות בהונג קונג.","sw":"Msimbo wa Morse ulibuniwa kwa ajili ya alfabeti, kwa hiyo mwaka 1872 afisa wa forodha huko Shanghai aligeuza kila herufi ya Kichina kuwa namba ya tarakimu nne. 電報碼 imeishi kuliko telegrafu yenyewe na bado huchapishwa kwenye vitambulisho vya Hong Kong."},"body":{"en":"<p>Morse code was built for an alphabet: short and long pulses stand for the twenty-six Latin letters and the ten digits. Chinese writing has no alphabet and tens of thousands of characters, so there was no way to key a character directly onto the wire. When the Danish-owned Great Northern Telegraph Company opened a cable between Shanghai and Hong Kong in 1871, that gap had to be closed before Chinese could be sent at all.</p><p>The answer was to stop sending characters and send numbers instead. <strong>Septime Auguste Viguier</strong> (威基謁), a French customs official in Shanghai, published <em>電報新書</em> (\"New Book for the Telegraph\") in 1872, assigning a four-digit code between 0001 and 9999 to each character. He built on an earlier list by the Danish astronomer Hans Schjellerup, and the two selected only a subset of commonly used characters; most of the writing system was left out.</p><p>The lookup is a grid. The codebook runs to 100 pages, each a 10×10 table of 100 characters. The first two digits of a code give the page, the third the row, and the fourth the column, with column one on the far right. 中 is <strong>0022</strong> — page 00, row 2, column 2; 文 is <strong>2429</strong>. A clerk read the message, found each character in the book, and keyed four Morse digits; a clerk at the far end ran the lookup in reverse. Every character cost four digits, and the overhead was real enough that a later three-letter version of the code was devised to cut international fees by 25% compared with the four-digit code.</p><div class='trivia-actions'><button type='button' class='trivia-action' data-action='panto' data-lat='31.23' data-lng='121.47' data-zoom='5'>🌍 Pan to Shanghai</button></div><p>The first books were criticized for thin coverage and disordered arrangement. The reformer <strong>Zheng Guanying</strong> compiled a new codebook in 1881, and it stayed in use until the Ministry of Transportation and Communications issued a replacement in 1929, with a supplement in 1933. After 1949 the code split into separate mainland and Taiwan editions; the People's Republic's <em>Standard Telegraph Codebook</em> provides codes for about 7,000 characters and switched to simplified forms in 1983.</p><p>The telegraph is gone, but the numbers are not. Hong Kong and Macau Resident Identity Cards print the Chinese telegraph code of the holder's name, and Hong Kong government and business forms routinely ask for it. The reason is no longer transmission: a four-digit code pins down exactly which character a name uses, which a romanized spelling cannot do when many characters share one pronunciation.</p><p>What began as a workaround for Morse has become a character index. The same 0001–9999 table that once let 中 cross a cable as 0022 now survives as a disambiguator printed on plastic, under the holder's name.</p>"},"sources":[{"text":"\"Chinese telegraph code\", Wikipedia (accessed 2026)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chinese_telegraph_code"},{"text":"National Security Agency, \"The Chinese Telegraph Code\" (declassified, 2018)","url":"https://www.nsa.gov/portals/75/documents/news-features/declassified-documents/history-today-articles/02%202018/15FEB2018%20The%20Chinese%20Telegraph%20Code.pdf"},{"text":"Jim Reeds, \"Chinese Telegraph Code (CTC)\" (NJStar notes)","url":"https://www.njstar.com/tools/telecode/jim-reeds-ctc.htm"}]},{"id":"chinese-input-methods","icon":"⌨️","tags":["sinosphere","input-method","technology","writing"],"title":{"en":"Fifty Thousand Characters, Twenty-Six Keys: How Chinese Is Typed","ja":"5万字を26キーで打つ：中国語入力法のしくみ","ko":"5만 자의 한자, 26개의 키: 중국어는 어떻게 입력하는가","zh":"五万汉字，二十六键：中文是怎么打出来的","yue":"五萬漢字，廿六個鍵：中文係點樣打出嚟","vi":"Năm vạn chữ Hán, hai mươi sáu phím: tiếng Trung được gõ như thế nào","th":"ห้าหมื่นตัวอักษร ยี่สิบหกแป้น: พิมพ์ภาษาจีนกันอย่างไร","id":"Lima puluh ribu aksara, dua puluh enam tombol: cara mengetik bahasa Tionghoa","hi":"पचास हज़ार अक्षर, छब्बीस कुंजियाँ: चीनी भाषा टाइप कैसे होती है","de":"Fünfzigtausend Schriftzeichen, sechsundzwanzig Tasten: Wie man Chinesisch tippt","fr":"Cinquante mille caractères, vingt-six touches : comment on tape le chinois","it":"Cinquantamila caratteri, ventisei tasti: come si digita il cinese","es":"Cincuenta mil caracteres, veintiséis teclas: cómo se teclea el chino","pt":"Cinquenta mil caracteres, vinte e seis teclas: como se digita o chinês","ru":"Пятьдесят тысяч иероглифов и двадцать шесть клавиш: как набирают текст по-китайски","uk":"П'ятдесят тисяч ієрогліфів і двадцять шість клавіш: як набирають китайською","ar":"خمسون ألف رمز وستة وعشرون مفتاحًا: كيف تُكتب الصينية على لوحة المفاتيح","he":"חמישים אלף סימנים, עשרים ושישה מקשים: איך מקלידים סינית","sw":"Herufi elfu hamsini, vitufe ishirini na sita: jinsi Kichina kinavyochapwa"},"summary":{"en":"How do tens of thousands of Chinese characters meet a 26-key keyboard? Input methods split into shape-based (Cangjie, 1976; Wubi, 1983) and sound-based (Pinyin, Zhuyin) schemes, resolved through predictive candidate lists — with a side effect named 提筆忘字, the inability to handwrite characters one can still read.","ja":"数万もの漢字は、どうやって26個のキーしかないキーボードに収まるのか。入力法は字形式（倉頡1976年・五筆1983年）と字音式（ピンイン・注音）に分かれ、予測候補列で打鍵を減らす。その副作用が「提筆忘字」——読めるのに手で書けなくなる現象である。","ko":"수만 자에 이르는 한자를 어떻게 26개 키의 자판에 담을까? 입력법은 글자의 모양을 치는 방식(창힐, 1976년; 우비, 1983년)과 소리를 치는 방식(병음, 주음)으로 나뉘며, 예측 후보 목록에서 최종 글자를 고른다. 그 부작용에는 이름까지 붙었다. 읽을 수는 있지만 손으로는 쓰지 못하는 현상, 제필망자(提筆忘字)다.","zh":"数以万计的汉字，如何对上只有26个字母键的键盘？输入法分为字形派（仓颉，1976年；五笔，1983年）和字音派（拼音、注音），再靠预测候选列表选定汉字——由此带来的副作用也有了名字：提笔忘字，即字还认得，却写不出来。","yue":"幾萬個漢字，點樣塞落一個得26個字母鍵嘅鍵盤？輸入法分做字形派（倉頡，1976年；五筆，1983年）同字音派（拼音、注音），再靠預測候選字表揀字——副作用仲有埋個名：提筆忘字，即係識讀但寫唔出。","vi":"Làm sao để hàng vạn chữ Hán gặp được bàn phím chỉ có 26 phím chữ cái? Các phương pháp nhập liệu chia thành hai nhánh: dựa trên hình dạng (Thương Hiệt, 1976; Ngũ Bút, 1983) và dựa trên âm đọc (Bính âm, Chú âm), rồi chốt chữ qua danh sách gợi ý dự đoán — kèm một tác dụng phụ đã có tên gọi: 提筆忘字, tình trạng vẫn đọc được chữ nhưng không viết tay ra được.","th":"ตัวอักษรจีนนับหมื่นตัวจะพิมพ์บนแป้นพิมพ์ที่มีแป้นตัวอักษรแค่ 26 แป้นได้อย่างไร วิธีป้อนข้อมูลแบ่งเป็นแบบอิงรูปตัวอักษร (ชางเจี๋ย ค.ศ. 1976; อู่ปี่ ค.ศ. 1983) และแบบอิงเสียง (พินอิน, จู้อิน) แล้วเลือกตัวที่ต้องการจากรายการที่ระบบคาดเดาให้ และยังมีผลข้างเคียงที่มีชื่อเรียกว่า 提筆忘字 คืออาการที่ยังอ่านตัวอักษรออก แต่เขียนด้วยมือไม่ได้","id":"Bagaimana puluhan ribu aksara Tionghoa bisa diketik dengan papan ketik 26 tombol? Metode input terbagi menjadi sistem berbasis bentuk (Cangjie, 1976; Wubi, 1983) dan berbasis bunyi (Pinyin, Zhuyin) yang dibantu daftar kandidat prediktif, dengan efek samping bernama 提筆忘字: tak bisa lagi menulis dengan tangan aksara yang masih bisa dibaca.","hi":"चीनी भाषा के दसियों हज़ार अक्षर 26 कुंजियों वाले कीबोर्ड से कैसे मिलते हैं? इनपुट पद्धतियाँ आकार-आधारित (त्सांगजिए, 1976; वूबी, 1983) और ध्वनि-आधारित (पिनयिन, झूयिन) योजनाओं में बँटती हैं, जिनमें अंतिम चुनाव पूर्वानुमान लगाने वाली उम्मीदवार-सूचियों से होता है। इसका एक दुष्प्रभाव भी है, जिसका नाम है 提筆忘字: उन अक्षरों को हाथ से न लिख पाना जिन्हें आप अब भी पढ़ सकते हैं।","de":"Zehntausende chinesische Schriftzeichen müssen auf eine Tastatur mit 26 Buchstabentasten passen. Eingabemethoden teilen sich in formbasierte (Cangjie, 1976; Wubi, 1983) und lautbasierte Verfahren (Pinyin, Zhuyin), die über vorausschauende Kandidatenlisten aufgelöst werden – mit einer Nebenwirkung namens 提筆忘字: der Unfähigkeit, Zeichen von Hand zu schreiben, die man noch lesen kann.","fr":"Comment des dizaines de milliers de caractères chinois tiennent-ils sur un clavier de 26 touches ? Les méthodes de saisie se partagent entre systèmes fondés sur la forme (Cangjie, 1976 ; Wubi, 1983) et systèmes fondés sur le son (Pinyin, Zhuyin), départagés par des listes de candidats prédictives, avec un effet secondaire baptisé 提筆忘字 : l’incapacité d’écrire à la main des caractères que l’on sait encore lire.","it":"Per far stare decine di migliaia di caratteri cinesi su una tastiera da 26 tasti, i metodi di input si basano sulla forma (Cangjie, 1976; Wubi, 1983) o sul suono (Pinyin, Zhuyin), con liste predittive di candidati, e con un effetto collaterale che ha un nome, 提筆忘字: l’incapacità di scrivere a mano caratteri che si sanno ancora leggere.","es":"¿Cómo caben decenas de miles de caracteres chinos en un teclado de 26 teclas? Los métodos de entrada se dividen en sistemas basados en la forma (Cangjie, 1976; Wubi, 1983) y en el sonido (Pinyin, Zhuyin), que se resuelven mediante listas predictivas de candidatos, con un efecto secundario llamado 提筆忘字: la incapacidad de escribir a mano caracteres que todavía se saben leer.","pt":"Para pôr dezenas de milhares de caracteres chineses num teclado de 26 teclas, os métodos de entrada dividem-se em sistemas baseados na forma (Cangjie, 1976; Wubi, 1983) e no som (Pinyin, Zhuyin), resolvidos por listas preditivas de candidatos — com um efeito colateral chamado 提筆忘字: a incapacidade de escrever à mão caracteres que ainda se consegue ler.","ru":"Как десятки тысяч китайских иероглифов уживаются с клавиатурой из 26 клавиш? Методы ввода делятся на графические (цанцзе, 1976; уби, 1983) и фонетические (пиньинь, чжуинь), а нужный знак выбирается из предиктивных списков кандидатов — с побочным эффектом под названием 提筆忘字: неспособностью написать от руки иероглифы, которые человек по-прежнему умеет читать.","uk":"Як десятки тисяч китайських ієрогліфів уживаються з клавіатурою з 26 клавіш? Методи введення поділяються на графічні (цанцзє, 1976; убі, 1983) і фонетичні (піньїнь, чжуїнь), а потрібний знак обирають із предиктивних списків кандидатів — із побічним ефектом під назвою 提筆忘字: нездатністю написати від руки ієрогліфи, які людина досі вміє читати.","ar":"عشرات الآلاف من الرموز الصينية ولوحة مفاتيح من 26 مفتاحًا: تنقسم طرق الإدخال إلى أنظمة قائمة على الشكل (تسانغجيه، 1976؛ ووبي، 1983) وأخرى قائمة على الصوت (بينيين، جويين)، ويُحسم الاختيار عبر قوائم مرشّحات تنبّؤية، ولذلك أثر جانبي يُسمّى 提筆忘字: العجز عن كتابة رموز باليد رغم القدرة على قراءتها.","he":"שיטות הקלט שמכניסות עשרות אלפי סימנים סיניים למקלדת של 26 מקשים מתחלקות לשיטות לפי צורה (צאנגג'יה, 1976; וובי, 1983) ולשיטות לפי צליל (פיניין, ג'ויין), והבחירה נעשית מתוך רשימות מועמדים חזויות. לזה יש תופעת לוואי ששמה 提筆忘字: אנשים לא מצליחים לכתוב ביד סימנים שהם עדיין יודעים לקרוא.","sw":"Makumi ya maelfu ya herufi za Kichina hulazimika kutoshea kwenye kibodi yenye vitufe 26. Mbinu za kuingiza maandishi zimegawanyika kati ya mifumo inayotegemea umbo (Cangjie, 1976; Wubi, 1983) na ile inayotegemea sauti (Pinyin, Zhuyin), na utata wake hutatuliwa kwa orodha za machaguo zinazotabiri. Zina pia athari ya pembeni iitwayo 提筆忘字: kushindwa kuandika kwa mkono herufi ambazo bado mtu anaweza kuzisoma."},"body":{"en":"<h3>Fifty Thousand Characters, Twenty-Six Keys</h3><p>The Kangxi Dictionary of 1716 lists 47,035 characters; the 1994 <em>Zhonghua Zihai</em> pushes the count past 85,000. A QWERTY keyboard has 26 letter keys. Every Chinese input method (輸入法, <em>shūrùfǎ</em>) is a scheme for closing that gap — mapping tens of thousands of logographs onto a few dozen keys, then letting the typist pick the intended character from the handful that match.</p><p>The schemes split along one line: do you type what a character looks like, or what it sounds like? Shape-based methods came first, because they sidestep the homophone problem. Chu Bong-Foo (朱邦復), working in Taiwan, built Cangjie (倉頡輸入法) around 1976 and named it after the mythical inventor of writing. Cangjie decomposes each character into up to five basic shape units drawn from 24 letter keys — 日 on the A key, 月 on B — so 明 \"bright\" is typed <strong>AB</strong>. It was the first Chinese input method to run on an ordinary QWERTY keyboard, and Chu released the patent in 1982, calling the method cultural heritage.</p><p>In August 1983 Wang Yongmin (王永民) released Wubizixing (五筆字型, \"five-stroke shape\") on the mainland. Wubi sorts every character into five stroke categories and packs several components onto each key, so a common character needs at most four keystrokes and — unlike sound-based input — produces almost no ambiguity. A trained Wubi typist can outrun a Pinyin one, which is why it held on among professional typists and stenographers long after casual users drifted away. Its price is memorisation: there is no way to guess a character's Wubi code from its pronunciation.</p><p>Sound-based input removed that barrier. Hanyu Pinyin, the romanisation standardised in 1958, lets anyone who speaks Mandarin type <em>luó</em> for 羅, 蘿, 鑼 and dozens more — and there is the catch. Mandarin has only about 1,300 tone-marked syllables carrying thousands of characters, so every Pinyin string returns a long candidate list, and the typist chooses by number. Taiwan applies the same principle through Zhuyin (注音, Bopomofo), the phonetic alphabet adopted in 1918, whose 37 symbols sit on their own keyboard keys.</p><p>The homophone list is where the modern IME does its real work. Sogou Pinyin, launched in 2006, built its dictionary not from a lexicographer but from the pages its parent search engine had already indexed, so new slang and names reached the candidate list within days. It ranked candidates by frequency, learned from what each user picked, and predicted the next word before it was typed: type a surname and it offers the full name; type two syllables and it offers a four-character idiom.</p><p>The side effect has a name: 提筆忘字 (<em>tí bǐ wàng zì</em>), \"pick up the pen and forget the character.\" Someone who has selected 饕餮 from a list for years may no longer be able to draw its 40 strokes by hand. A China Youth Daily survey of 2,072 people in April 2010 found 83% reported trouble writing characters they could still read. The polls are informal, and a 2018 reassessment in <em>The China Quarterly</em> questioned how much they actually measure; a 2024 study in <em>Reading and Writing</em> noted that no diagnostic test existed and built a 30-character one to supply the missing figure. Recognition stays intact while production decays — you know the character on sight and cannot summon it onto a blank page.</p><div class=\"trivia-actions\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"panto\" data-lat=\"25.03\" data-lng=\"121.57\" data-zoom=\"4\">🌍 Taipei — where Cangjie was built</button></div>"},"sources":[{"text":"Thomas S. Mullaney (2023), \"The fascinating evolution of typing Chinese characters,\" MIT Technology Review","url":"https://www.technologyreview.com/2023/08/23/1078274/fascinating-evolution-typing-chinese-characters/"},{"text":"Wikipedia: Cangjie input method (Chu Bong-Foo, c. 1976; patent released 1982)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cangjie_input_method"},{"text":"Wikipedia: Wubi method (Wang Yongmin, August 1983)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wubi_method"},{"text":"Wikipedia: Character amnesia (提筆忘字; China Youth Daily 2010 survey, 2,072 respondents)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Character_amnesia"}]},{"id":"capital-numerals-daxie","icon":"💰","tags":["sinosphere","numbers","history","writing-system"],"title":{"en":"Why Chinese Keeps a Second Set of Numerals for Money","ja":"お金のためだけにある漢数字：大写（壹貳參）の話","ko":"중국어는 왜 돈에 쓰는 숫자를 따로 한 벌 더 갖고 있을까","zh":"为什么中文要为钱另备一套数字","yue":"點解中文要為錢另外預備一套數字","vi":"Vì sao tiếng Trung giữ riêng một bộ chữ số cho tiền bạc","th":"ทำไมภาษาจีนจึงมีตัวเลขอีกชุดหนึ่งไว้ใช้กับเรื่องเงิน","id":"Mengapa bahasa Tionghoa punya angka khusus untuk uang","hi":"चीनी में पैसों के लिए अंकों का दूसरा सेट क्यों है","de":"Warum das Chinesische für Geldbeträge eigene Zahlzeichen hat","fr":"Pourquoi le chinois a une seconde série de chiffres pour l'argent","it":"Perché il cinese ha una seconda serie di numeri per il denaro","es":"Por qué el chino guarda un segundo juego de numerales para el dinero","pt":"Por que o chinês mantém um segundo conjunto de numerais para dinheiro","ru":"Зачем китайскому второй набор цифр для денег","uk":"Навіщо китайській другий набір цифр для грошей","ar":"لماذا تحتفظ الصينية بمجموعة ثانية من الأرقام للمال","he":"למה הסינית שומרת מערכת ספרות שנייה לכסף","sw":"Kwa nini Kichina kina seti ya pili ya tarakimu kwa ajili ya fedha"},"summary":{"en":"The everyday numerals 一二三 are one, two and three strokes — too easy to alter on a contract. So Chinese keeps a second set, 壹貳參肆伍陸柒捌玖拾, standard on ledgers since the Ming and still printed on every renminbi note.","ja":"一・二・三は横棒を一〜三本引いただけの字で、契約書の上では書き換えが容易すぎる。そこで中国語は壹貳參肆伍陸柒捌玖拾というもう一組の数字を保っている。明代から帳簿の標準で、今も人民元の紙幣すべてに刷られている。","ko":"일상에서 쓰는 숫자 一二三은 각각 획이 한 개, 두 개, 세 개뿐이라 계약서에서 고쳐 쓰기가 너무 쉽다. 그래서 중국어에는 壹貳參肆伍陸柒捌玖拾이라는 두 번째 숫자 체계가 있다. 명나라 이후 장부의 표준이 되었고, 지금도 모든 인민폐 지폐에 인쇄되어 있다.","zh":"日常用的数字一二三分别只有一笔、两笔、三笔，写在合同上太容易被篡改。所以中文另有一套数字：壹貳參肆伍陸柒捌玖拾，自明代起成为账簿上的标准写法，至今仍印在每一张人民币上。","yue":"日常用嘅數字一二三，分別只係一劃、兩劃、三劃，寫喺合約上面太容易俾人改。所以中文另外有一套數字：壹貳參肆伍陸柒捌玖拾，由明朝起成為賬簿標準，到今日仍然印喺每一張人民幣上面。","vi":"Các chữ số thường ngày 一二三 chỉ gồm một, hai và ba nét — quá dễ sửa trên hợp đồng. Vì vậy tiếng Trung giữ một bộ chữ số thứ hai, 壹貳參肆伍陸柒捌玖拾, vốn là chuẩn mực trên sổ sách từ thời Minh và đến nay vẫn được in trên mọi tờ nhân dân tệ.","th":"ตัวเลขที่ใช้ทั่วไปอย่าง 一二三 มีแค่หนึ่ง สอง และสามขีด แก้ในสัญญาได้ง่ายเกินไป ภาษาจีนจึงมีตัวเลขชุดที่สอง คือ 壹貳參肆伍陸柒捌玖拾 ซึ่งเป็นมาตรฐานในสมุดบัญชีมาตั้งแต่สมัยราชวงศ์หมิง และยังพิมพ์อยู่บนธนบัตรเหรินหมินปี้ทุกฉบับ","id":"Angka sehari-hari 一二三 hanya terdiri atas satu, dua, dan tiga goresan, terlalu mudah diubah di atas kontrak. Karena itu bahasa Tionghoa menyimpan perangkat angka kedua, 壹貳參肆伍陸柒捌玖拾, yang menjadi standar di buku besar sejak Dinasti Ming dan masih tercetak di setiap uang kertas renminbi.","hi":"रोज़मर्रा के अंक 一二三 एक, दो और तीन रेखाओं से बने हैं, इसलिए अनुबंध में इनसे छेड़छाड़ करना बहुत आसान है। इसीलिए चीनी भाषा अंकों का एक दूसरा सेट 壹貳參肆伍陸柒捌玖拾 रखती है। मिंग काल से यह बहीखातों का मानक है और आज भी रेनमिनबी के हर नोट पर छपता है।","de":"Die Alltagsziffern 一二三 bestehen aus einem, zwei und drei Strichen – auf einem Vertrag viel zu leicht zu fälschen. Deshalb kennt das Chinesische einen zweiten Satz, 壹貳參肆伍陸柒捌玖拾, seit der Ming-Zeit Standard in Geschäftsbüchern und bis heute auf jedem Renminbi-Schein gedruckt.","fr":"Les chiffres courants 一二三 s'écrivent en un, deux et trois traits : bien trop faciles à falsifier sur un contrat. Le chinois conserve donc une seconde série, 壹貳參肆伍陸柒捌玖拾, de rigueur dans les registres comptables depuis les Ming et toujours imprimée sur chaque billet en renminbi.","it":"Nei numeri di tutti i giorni 一二三 sono uno, due e tre tratti: troppo facili da alterare su un contratto. Per questo il cinese conserva una seconda serie, 壹貳參肆伍陸柒捌玖拾, standard nei libri contabili fin dai Ming e stampata ancora oggi su ogni banconota in renminbi.","es":"Los numerales de uso diario 一二三 son uno, dos y tres trazos: demasiado fáciles de alterar en un contrato. Por eso el chino conserva un segundo juego, 壹貳參肆伍陸柒捌玖拾, habitual en los libros de cuentas desde la dinastía Ming y que todavía se imprime en todos los billetes de renminbi.","pt":"Os numerais do dia a dia 一二三 têm um, dois e três traços — fáceis demais de adulterar em um contrato. Por isso o chinês mantém um segundo conjunto, 壹貳參肆伍陸柒捌玖拾, padrão nos livros contábeis desde a dinastia Ming e ainda impresso em todas as cédulas de renminbi.","ru":"Обычные цифры 一二三 — это одна, две и три черты: в договоре их слишком легко подправить. Поэтому в китайском есть второй набор, 壹貳參肆伍陸柒捌玖拾. Со времён Мин он стал стандартом счётных книг и до сих пор печатается на каждой банкноте жэньминьби.","uk":"Звичайні цифри 一二三 — це одна, дві й три риски, і в договорі їх надто легко підправити. Тому китайська має другий набір, 壹貳參肆伍陸柒捌玖拾. Від часів Мін він став стандартом рахункових книг і досі друкується на кожній банкноті женьміньбі.","ar":"الأرقام اليومية 一二三 هي خط واحد وخطان وثلاثة خطوط، ومن السهل جدًا التلاعب بها في عقد. لذلك تحتفظ الصينية بمجموعة ثانية هي 壹貳參肆伍陸柒捌玖拾، صارت المعيار في دفاتر الحسابات منذ عهد أسرة مينغ، وما زالت تُطبع على كل ورقة نقدية من الرنمينبي.","he":"הספרות היומיומיות 一二三 הן קו אחד, שניים ושלושה, וקל מדי לזייף אותן בחוזה. לכן הסינית שומרת מערכת שנייה, 壹貳參肆伍陸柒捌玖拾, שהייתה לתקן בספרי החשבונות מאז שושלת מינג ועדיין מודפסת על כל שטר רנמינבי.","sw":"Tarakimu za kila siku 一二三 ni mstari mmoja, miwili na mitatu, rahisi mno kubadilishwa kwenye mkataba. Kwa hiyo Kichina kinatunza seti ya pili, 壹貳參肆伍陸柒捌玖拾, iliyo kiwango katika daftari za hesabu tangu enzi ya Ming na ambayo bado huchapishwa kwenye kila noti ya renminbi."},"body":{"en":"<h3>Why 一二三 Cannot Be Trusted on a Ledger</h3><p>The everyday Chinese numerals for one, two and three are one, two and three horizontal strokes: <strong>一 二 三</strong> (yī èr sān). On a receipt that is a weakness. One added stroke turns 一 into 二, and another makes it 三; 十 (ten) becomes 千 (thousand) with a single short stroke on top, and 三十 (thirty) can be reworked into 五千 (five thousand) by anyone with a brush and a motive. So for contracts, tax records and bank drafts, Chinese keeps a second set of numerals built so that no such edit works: 壹 貳 參 肆 伍 陸 柒 捌 玖 拾 佰 仟 萬, called 大寫 (<em>dàxiě</em>, \"capital writing\") or banker's numerals.</p><p>Each capital form is a pre-existing character borrowed for its sound, not its meaning. 壹 (one) also meant \"whole, to concentrate\"; 貳 (two) meant \"to double, to betray\"; 參 (three) is the word for ginseng and \"to take part\"; 肆 (four) meant \"to indulge\" and still names a shop; 陸 (six) is dry land; 玖 (nine) is a dark jade-like stone; 萬 (ten thousand) was originally a drawing of a scorpion. Because the thirteen characters are unrelated in shape, there is no stroke you can add to 壹 to make 貳, and no way to inflate 參 (three) into anything larger. The forger's edit has nothing to grab.</p><h3>Wu Zetian, then Hongwu</h3><p>The homophone-substitution trick is old. It is traditionally credited to Wu Zetian (r. 690–705), China's only female emperor, though the Tang sources do not explain why she used the forms, and the Song-dynasty scholar Cheng Dachang later described officials swapping in complex homophones for bookkeeping without naming her. The decisive standardization is attributed to the founding Ming emperor Zhu Yuanzhang (the Hongwu Emperor, r. 1368–1398), who is said to have ordered accounts kept in the capital numerals after a large embezzlement scandal — popularly the Guo Huan (郭桓) case of 1385, one of the Ming dynasty's \"four major cases.\" No surviving passage of the Ming histories ties the decree to that specific case, so the Hongwu link is firm on the practice and soft on the date.</p><div class='trivia-actions'><button type='button' class='trivia-action' data-action='panto' data-lat='32.06' data-lng='118.80' data-zoom='5'>🌍 Pan to Nanjing, first Ming capital</button></div><h3>Still on Every Banknote</h3><p>The system never retired. A 100-yuan renminbi note prints <strong>壹佰圆</strong> (yībǎi yuán) beside the Arabic \"100\". The unit the People's Bank of China Law prescribes is 元; the note keeps the older 圆, and when the 2015 edition appeared, Hao Mingjian, editor of the usage magazine <em>Yaowen Jiaozi</em>, called it an error. Mainland cheques, invoices and formal contracts still require the amount written out in 大写, where ¥1,250 is 人民币壹仟贰佰伍拾元整 — and the trailing 整 (\"whole\") seals the end of the figure so nothing can be appended. Zero has its own capital form, 零, used to mark a skipped place: 1,005 is 壹仟零伍.</p><h3>The Habit Travelled</h3><p>Japan and Korea took the convention along with the characters. Japanese law still stipulates the <em>daiji</em> (大字) forms 壱 (1), 弐 (2), 参 (3) and 拾 (10) for amounts and for figures in official registers; the Commercial Registration Regulations, for instance, require them for sums of money, quantities, dates and serial numbers, though Arabic numerals are allowed when the text runs horizontally. The forms drifted from the Chinese originals (Japanese 壱弐参 against Chinese 壹貳參), but the logic is identical: in the one place where a changed digit is worth money, use a character too complicated to change.</p>"},"sources":[{"text":"Chinese numerals — Financial (capital) numerals (Wikipedia)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chinese_numerals"},{"text":"Daiji (大字) — kanji numerals for legal and financial documents (Wikipedia)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Daiji"},{"text":"Four Major Cases of the early Ming dynasty — Guo Huan case, 1385 (Wikipedia)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Four_Major_Cases_of_the_early_Ming_dynasty"},{"text":"郝铭鉴 (Hao Mingjian), editor of 《咬文嚼字》, on the 圆 of the 2015 100-yuan note — ifeng.com, 17 December 2015","url":"https://news.ifeng.com/a/20151217/46705689_0.shtml"},{"text":"Ministry of Finance of the PRC, 会计基础工作规范 (Basic Norms for Accounting Work), 1996 — capital numerals 壹贰叁肆伍陆柒捌玖拾佰仟万亿, unit 元, closing 整"}]},{"id":"ateji-sound-kanji","icon":"🈁","tags":["japan","characters","writing"],"title":{"en":"Ateji: When Kanji Are Chosen for Their Sound","ja":"当て字：音のために選ばれた漢字","ko":"아테지: 소리 때문에 고른 한자","zh":"当て字：为了读音选汉字","yue":"当て字：為咗讀音揀漢字","vi":"Ateji: khi kanji được chọn vì âm","th":"อาเทะจิ: เมื่อเลือกคันจิเพราะเสียง","id":"Ateji: ketika kanji dipilih demi bunyinya","hi":"आतेजी: जब कांजी ध्वनि के लिए चुने जाएँ","de":"Ateji: Wenn Kanji nach dem Klang gewählt werden","fr":"Ateji : quand les kanji sont choisis pour leur son","it":"Ateji: quando i kanji si scelgono per il suono","es":"Ateji: cuando los kanji se eligen por su sonido","pt":"Ateji: quando os kanji são escolhidos pelo som","ru":"Атэдзи: когда кандзи выбирают ради звука","uk":"Атедзі: коли кандзі обирають заради звуку","ar":"أتيجي: حين تُختار رموز الكانجي لصوتها","he":"אטג'י: כשקאנג'י נבחרים בגלל הצליל","sw":"Ateji: kanji zinapochaguliwa kwa sauti yake"},"summary":{"en":"Sushi is written “long life, administer” and nonsense is written “no tea”. Japanese has a long habit of choosing kanji for their sound and ignoring what they mean.","ja":"寿司は「寿」を「司る」と書き、無茶は「茶が無い」と書く。日本語には、意味を気にせず音で漢字を選ぶ長い習慣がある。","ko":"스시는 ‘목숨’과 ‘맡다’로 쓰고, 터무니없다는 말은 ‘차가 없다’로 쓴다. 일본어에는 뜻을 무시하고 소리로 한자를 고르는 오랜 습관이 있다.","zh":"寿司写成“寿”和“司”，胡闹写成“没有茶”。日语有一个悠久的习惯：只看读音选汉字，不管字义。","yue":"壽司寫成「寿」同「司」，亂嚟寫成「冇茶」。日語有個悠久習慣：淨係睇讀音揀漢字，唔理字義。","vi":"Sushi viết là “thọ” và “ti”, còn vô lý viết là “không có trà”. Tiếng Nhật có thói quen lâu đời chọn kanji theo âm và mặc kệ nghĩa.","th":"ซูชิเขียนด้วยคำว่า “อายุยืน” กับ “ดูแล” ส่วนคำว่าไร้สาระเขียนว่า “ไม่มีชา” ภาษาญี่ปุ่นเลือกคันจิตามเสียงโดยไม่สนความหมายมานานแล้ว","id":"Sushi ditulis “umur panjang, mengurus” dan omong kosong ditulis “tanpa teh”. Bahasa Jepang punya kebiasaan lama memilih kanji karena bunyinya dan mengabaikan artinya.","hi":"सुशी लिखा जाता है “दीर्घायु, प्रबंध करना” और बकवास लिखी जाती है “चाय नहीं”। जापानी में कांजी को ध्वनि के लिए चुनने और अर्थ को अनदेखा करने की पुरानी आदत है।","de":"Sushi schreibt man mit den Zeichen für langes Leben und verwalten, Unsinn mit denen für kein Tee. Das Japanische wählt seit Langem Kanji nach dem Klang und schert sich nicht um ihre Bedeutung.","fr":"Le mot sushi s’écrit avec les caractères « longue vie » et « administrer », et le mot absurde avec « sans thé ». Le japonais a la vieille habitude de choisir des kanji pour leur son sans se soucier de leur sens.","it":"La parola sushi si scrive con i caratteri «lunga vita» e «amministrare», e la parola assurdo con «senza tè». Il giapponese ha la lunga abitudine di scegliere i kanji per il suono, ignorando il significato.","es":"La palabra sushi se escribe con los caracteres «larga vida» y «administrar», y la palabra absurdo con «sin té». El japonés tiene la vieja costumbre de elegir kanji por su sonido sin atender a lo que significan.","pt":"A palavra sushi se escreve com os caracteres “vida longa” e “administrar”, e a palavra absurdo com “sem chá”. O japonês tem o velho hábito de escolher kanji pelo som e ignorar o que significam.","ru":"Суши пишут «долгая жизнь, управлять», а чепуху пишут «без чая». В японском давно принято выбирать кандзи за звучание и не обращать внимания на смысл.","uk":"Суші пишуть як «довге життя, керувати», а дурницю — як «без чаю». У японській давно заведено обирати кандзі за звучанням і не зважати на зміст.","ar":"تُكتب «سوشي» برمزَي «طول العمر» و«الإدارة»، و«العبث» برمزَي «بلا شاي». ولليابانية عادة قديمة في اختيار الكانجي لصوتها وإهمال معناها.","he":"סושי נכתב ״אריכות ימים, לנהל״ ושטויות נכתבות ״בלי תה״. ביפנית יש הרגל ותיק לבחור קאנג'י לפי הצליל ולהתעלם מהמשמעות.","sw":"Sushi huandikwa “maisha marefu, kusimamia” na upuuzi huandikwa “bila chai”. Kijapani kina tabia ya muda mrefu ya kuchagua kanji kwa sauti yake bila kujali maana."},"body":{"en":"<p>Written out in kanji, the Japanese word <em>mucha</em>, “absurd” or “reckless”, says 無茶, literally “tealess”. It has nothing to do with tea. The first character is the Go-on reading <strong>mu</strong>, and the pair were picked for how they sound. Such spellings are called <em>ateji</em>, 当て字, “assigned characters”: kanji used to represent a word with little regard for what they mean.</p><div class=\"trivia-actions\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"setchar\" data-char=\"無\">🗺️ Show 無</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"setchar\" data-char=\"茶\">🗺️ Show 茶</button></div><p>Sushi is the everyday example. Its usual ateji is 寿司, and neither part relates to food. 寿 means a natural life span and 司 means to administer. Food is in fact a good place to look, since ateji are particularly common on traditional shop signs and menus. Coffee is generally コーヒー, but a coffee shop sign may read 珈琲.</p><h3>Layers of history</h3><p>History left layers. The fried dish tempura may be written 天麩羅, with 天 read <strong>ten</strong>, and it goes back to Latin <em>tempora</em> through the Portuguese. The insult <em>baka</em>, “fool”, is 馬鹿, “horse deer”, with the Kan-on reading <strong>ba</strong>, though the characters look like a disguise. The word was probably a transcription of Sanskrit <em>moha</em>, “folly”, used as a slang term among monks. Ateji arrived in rough historical order: Sanskrit terms with Buddhism, Portuguese words in the 16th and 17th centuries, and Dutch words up to the 19th.</p><div class=\"trivia-actions\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"setchar\" data-char=\"馬\">🗺️ Show 馬</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"setchar\" data-char=\"天\">🗺️ Show 天</button></div><p>Some spellings had a further effect on the characters themselves. Foreign place names were once widely written this way, Asia as 亜細亜 and America as 亜米利加. The long forms are archaic now, but they left traces. 亜 picked up the meaning “Asia” in compounds such as 東亜, <strong>tō</strong>-a, “East Asia”, though it originally meant “second-rate” and still does. In the same way, the middle of the word for America gave 米国, literally “rice country”.</p><div class=\"trivia-actions\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"setchar\" data-char=\"東\">🗺️ Show 東</button></div><h3>Sound and sense together</h3><p>The best ones have it both ways, with kanji that fit the sound and also hint at the sense. Club is 倶楽部, <em>kurabu</em>, which can be read loosely as “together”, “fun” and “place”. <em>Kappa</em>, the Portuguese raincoat called <em>capa</em>, is 合羽, “wings coming together”, because the pointed cloak looks like a bird with folded wings. Others are pure sound with a straight face: the congratulatory <em>medetai</em> appears as 目出度い, with 目 read by its native word <strong>me</strong>, “eye”.</p><div class=\"trivia-actions\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"setchar\" data-char=\"目\">🗺️ Show 目</button></div><h3>Older than kana</h3><p>The habit goes back nearly to the arrival of Chinese characters in Japan. Poets used kanji purely for their sounds in the system called <em>man'yōgana</em>, and the kana in use today developed from simplified forms of those same characters. Ateji are therefore the older sibling of hiragana and katakana. Katakana has since taken over nearly all of their work for loanwords, and the gas companies show the split neatly: 瓦斯 in the company name, ガス in the trade name.</p><p>Spellings like this still turn up. One Japanese trash bin is labelled 護美入れ. The word it spells is the ordinary <em>gomi</em>, “trash”, but the characters say “protect beauty”.</p><div class=\"trivia-actions\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"panto\" data-lat=\"35.68\" data-lng=\"139.76\" data-zoom=\"6\">📍 Move the map to Tokyo</button></div>"},"sources":[{"text":"\"Ateji\". Wikipedia, accessed 2026.","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ateji"},{"text":"\"Tempura\". Wikipedia, accessed 2026.","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tempura"},{"text":"\"無茶\". Wiktionary, accessed 2026.","url":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/無茶"},{"text":"\"馬鹿\". Wiktionary, accessed 2026.","url":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/馬鹿"},{"text":"\"米国\". Wiktionary, accessed 2026.","url":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/米国"}]},{"id":"bi-sheng-movable-type","icon":"🖨️","tags":["history","printing","china"],"title":{"en":"Bi Sheng and the Clay Type That Came Before Gutenberg","ja":"畢昇：グーテンベルクより前の粘土の活字","ko":"필승: 구텐베르크보다 먼저 나온 진흙 활자","zh":"毕昇和早于古腾堡的泥活字","yue":"畢昇同早過古騰堡嘅泥活字","vi":"Tất Thăng và chữ đất sét trước Gutenberg","th":"ปี้เซิงกับตัวพิมพ์ดินเหนียวก่อนกูเทนแบร์ก","id":"Bi Sheng dan huruf tanah liat sebelum Gutenberg","hi":"बी शंग और गुटेनबर्ग से पहले के मिट्टी के टाइप","de":"Bi Sheng und die Tonlettern vor Gutenberg","fr":"Bi Sheng et les caractères d’argile d’avant Gutenberg","it":"Bi Sheng e i caratteri di argilla prima di Gutenberg","es":"Bi Sheng y los tipos de arcilla anteriores a Gutenberg","pt":"Bi Sheng e os tipos de argila anteriores a Gutenberg","ru":"Би Шэн и глиняные литеры до Гутенберга","uk":"Бі Шен і глиняні літери до Ґутенберга","ar":"بي شنغ وحروف الطين التي سبقت غوتنبرغ","he":"בי שנג והאותיות מחימר שקדמו לגוטנברג","sw":"Bi Sheng na chapa za udongo kabla ya Gutenberg"},"summary":{"en":"In the 1040s a commoner baked Chinese characters in clay and printed with them. We know his name because one scholar wrote it down.","ja":"1040年代、一人の庶民が漢字を粘土で焼き、それで印刷した。名が残ったのは、一人の学者が書き留めたからである。","ko":"1040년대에 한 평민이 한자를 진흙으로 구워 인쇄했다. 그의 이름이 남은 것은 한 학자가 적어 두었기 때문이다.","zh":"1040 年代，一位平民把汉字烧在泥上，用来印刷。我们知道他的名字，是因为有一位学者把它写了下来。","yue":"1040 年代，一個平民將漢字燒喺泥上面，用嚟印刷。我哋知道佢個名，係因為有一位學者寫低咗。","vi":"Thập niên 1040, một thường dân nung chữ Hán bằng đất sét rồi in. Tên ông còn lại là nhờ một học giả ghi chép.","th":"ในทศวรรษ 1040 สามัญชนคนหนึ่งปั้นอักษรจีนจากดินเหนียว เผาให้แข็ง แล้วใช้พิมพ์หนังสือ ที่เรารู้ชื่อเขาก็เพราะนักปราชญ์คนหนึ่งจดไว้","id":"Pada 1040-an seorang rakyat biasa membakar aksara Tionghoa dari tanah liat dan mencetak dengannya. Kita tahu namanya karena seorang sarjana menuliskannya.","hi":"1040 के दशक में एक आम आदमी ने चीनी अक्षरों को मिट्टी में पकाया और उनसे छपाई की। उसका नाम हम इसलिए जानते हैं कि एक विद्वान ने उसे लिख लिया।","de":"In den 1040er-Jahren brannte ein einfacher Mann chinesische Zeichen in Ton und druckte damit. Seinen Namen kennen wir, weil ein Gelehrter ihn aufschrieb.","fr":"Dans les années 1040, un homme du peuple a cuit des caractères chinois dans l’argile et imprimé avec. Nous connaissons son nom parce qu’un lettré l’a noté.","it":"Negli anni Quaranta dell’XI secolo un uomo comune cosse nell’argilla dei caratteri cinesi e ci stampò. Conosciamo il suo nome perché uno studioso lo annotò.","es":"En la década de 1040 un hombre del pueblo coció caracteres chinos en arcilla e imprimió con ellos. Conocemos su nombre porque un erudito lo dejó escrito.","pt":"Na década de 1040 um homem do povo fez caracteres chineses de argila cozida e imprimiu com eles. Sabemos seu nome porque um erudito o anotou.","ru":"В 1040-х годах простолюдин обжёг в глине китайские иероглифы и стал ими печатать. Мы знаем его имя, потому что его записал один учёный.","uk":"У 1040-х роках простолюдин випалив у глині китайські ієрогліфи й почав ними друкувати. Ми знаємо його ім’я, бо його записав один учений.","ar":"في أربعينيات القرن 11 شوى رجل من عامة الناس رموزًا صينية في الطين وطبع بها. ونعرف اسمه لأن عالمًا دوّنه.","he":"בשנות הארבעים של המאה ה-11 הכין אדם פשוט תווים סיניים מחימר שרוף והדפיס בהם. את שמו אנחנו יודעים כי מלומד אחד רשם אותו.","sw":"Katika miaka ya 1040 mtu wa kawaida alichoma herufi za Kichina za udongo na kuchapa nazo. Tunajua jina lake kwa sababu mwanazuoni mmoja aliliandika."},"body":{"en":"<p>Almost everything known about the inventor of movable type comes from one passage. Bi Sheng was a commoner, “a man of unofficial position”, and little is known about his life beyond the invention. Around 1041 to 1048 he made printing type from clay, each piece carrying a single character. The scholar Shen Kuo, who was a child at the time, described the method in detail in his <em>Dream Pool Essays</em>.</p><h3>Clay, fire and iron</h3><p>Three materials do most of the work in Shen Kuo’s description. The characters were cut into sticky clay, 土, <strong>tǔ</strong>, “as thin as the edge of a coin”, and then baked hard in the fire, 火, <strong>huǒ</strong>. The type was set in a frame on an iron plate, 鐵, <strong>tiě</strong>, coated with a mixture of pine resin, wax and paper ashes. Warming the plate softened the coating, and pressing it with a smooth board made the block “as even as a whetstone”.</p><div class=\"trivia-actions\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"setchar\" data-char=\"土\">🗺️ Show 土</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"setchar\" data-char=\"火\">🗺️ Show 火</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"setchar\" data-char=\"鐵\">🗺️ Show 鐵</button></div><p>The account is practical. For two or three copies the method was “neither simple nor easy”, Shen Kuo says, but for hundreds or thousands it was “marvelously quick”. Bi Sheng kept two formes going, setting one while the other printed. For common characters there were twenty or more types each, because the same word might recur on a page. When not in use the types were sorted by rhyme group, with paper labels, in wooden cases. With so many pieces to manage, sound was the index.</p><h3>Wood and bronze</h3><p>Wood, 木, <strong>mù</strong>, was the obvious alternative. Bi Sheng tried it and dropped it, because of the grain and because the type became uneven after being soaked in ink. Later printers came back to it. Wang Zhen, a county magistrate at Jingde in the 1290s, was a pioneer of wooden type, and a book of his published in 1313 shows a revolving typecase. Bronze type came in by 1490, with the wealthy printer Hua Sui. Clay may have had followers too: a Buddhist text found at Wuwei in Gansu, from the Tangut state, shows the hollow and broken strokes associated with it.</p><div class=\"trivia-actions\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"setchar\" data-char=\"木\">🗺️ Show 木</button></div><p>Even so, movable type never displaced woodblock printing in China. Wooden type became widespread only in the Qing dynasty, and even then the block stayed ahead, because it proved cheaper for printing so many different characters. For a script with thousands of characters the arithmetic is different from the one that worked in Europe, where Gutenberg’s press around 1450 needed only a small set of letters.</p><h3>The oldest surviving book</h3><p>The oldest book that survives printed from movable metal type is Korean. <em>Jikji</em>, a collection of Buddhist teachings, was printed at Heungdeok Temple outside Cheongju in 1377, 78 years before the Gutenberg Bible, and its surviving volume is in the National Library of France. Bi Sheng’s own memorial arrived late. His gravestone was found in Hubei in 1990, and a park and museum opened at the site in April 2023. A crater on the far side of the Moon carries his name.</p><div class=\"trivia-actions\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"panto\" data-lat=\"36.64\" data-lng=\"127.49\" data-zoom=\"7\">📍 Move the map to Cheongju</button></div>"},"sources":[{"text":"\"Bi Sheng\". Wikipedia, accessed 2026.","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bi_Sheng"},{"text":"\"Movable type\". Wikipedia, accessed 2026.","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Movable_type"},{"text":"\"Wang Zhen (official)\". Wikipedia, accessed 2026.","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wang_Zhen_(official)"},{"text":"\"王禎 (元朝)\". Chinese Wikipedia, accessed 2026.","url":"https://zh.wikipedia.org/wiki/王禎_(元朝)"},{"text":"\"Jikji\". Wikipedia, accessed 2026.","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jikji"},{"text":"\"Printing\". Wikipedia, accessed 2026.","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Printing"}]},{"id":"biang-noodles","icon":"🍜","tags":["characters","food","unicode"],"title":{"en":"Biáng: 58 Strokes for a Bowl of Noodles","ja":"ビャン：一杯の麺のための58画","ko":"뱡: 국수 한 그릇을 위한 58획","zh":"Biáng：一碗面的 58 笔","yue":"Biáng：一碗麵嘅 58 筆","vi":"Biáng: 58 nét cho một bát mì","th":"เปียง: 58 ขีดเพื่อบะหมี่ชามเดียว","id":"Biáng: 58 guratan untuk semangkuk mi","hi":"Biáng: एक कटोरी नूडल्स के लिए 58 रेखाएँ","de":"Biáng: 58 Striche für eine Schüssel Nudeln","fr":"Biáng : 58 traits pour un bol de nouilles","it":"Biáng: 58 tratti per una ciotola di tagliatelle","es":"Biáng: 58 trazos para un cuenco de fideos","pt":"Biáng: 58 traços por uma tigela de macarrão","ru":"Biáng: 58 черт ради миски лапши","uk":"Biáng: 58 рисок заради миски локшини","ar":"بيانغ: 58 خطًا من أجل وعاء نودلز","he":"Biáng: 58 קווים בשביל קערת אטריות","sw":"Biáng: mistari 58 kwa bakuli la tambi"},"summary":{"en":"A belt-wide noodle from Xi'an has a name written with an unusually complex character, and for years no computer could type it.","ja":"西安の帯のように幅広い麺は、並外れて複雑な字で書かれる。長いあいだ、どのコンピュータでも打てなかった。","ko":"시안의 허리띠처럼 넓은 국수는 유난히 복잡한 글자로 쓰며, 오랫동안 어떤 컴퓨터로도 입력할 수 없었다.","zh":"西安一种宽如腰带的面，名字用一个格外复杂的字来写，多年来没有一台电脑打得出来。","yue":"西安一種闊過皮帶嘅麵，個名用一個格外複雜嘅字嚟寫，好多年都冇一部電腦打得出。","vi":"Một loại mì to bản như thắt lưng ở Tây An có tên viết bằng một chữ phức tạp khác thường, và suốt nhiều năm không máy tính nào gõ được.","th":"บะหมี่เส้นกว้างเท่าเข็มขัดจากซีอานมีชื่อที่เขียนด้วยอักษรซับซ้อนสุด ๆ และหลายปีไม่มีคอมพิวเตอร์เครื่องไหนพิมพ์ได้","id":"Mi selebar ikat pinggang dari Xi'an ini namanya ditulis dengan aksara yang luar biasa rumit, dan selama bertahun-tahun tak ada komputer yang bisa mengetiknya.","hi":"शीआन के बेल्ट जितने चौड़े नूडल का नाम एक असाधारण जटिल अक्षर से लिखा जाता है, और बरसों तक कोई कंप्यूटर उसे टाइप नहीं कर पाता था।","de":"Eine gürtelbreite Nudel aus Xi'an trägt einen Namen mit einem ungewöhnlich komplizierten Zeichen, das lange kein Computer schreiben konnte.","fr":"Une nouille de Xi'an large comme une ceinture porte un nom écrit avec un caractère d’une complexité rare, et pendant des années aucun ordinateur n’a su le taper.","it":"Una tagliatella di Xi'an larga come una cintura ha un nome scritto con un carattere insolitamente complesso, e per anni nessun computer ha potuto digitarlo.","es":"Un fideo de Xi'an ancho como un cinturón tiene un nombre escrito con un carácter inusualmente complejo, y durante años ninguna computadora pudo escribirlo.","pt":"Um macarrão de Xi'an largo como um cinto tem um nome escrito com um caractere incomumente complexo, e por anos nenhum computador conseguiu digitá-lo.","ru":"У широкой, как ремень, лапши из Сианя есть название, которое пишется необычайно сложным иероглифом, и долгие годы ни один компьютер не мог его набрать.","uk":"Назву широкої, як пасок, локшини із Сіаня пишуть надзвичайно складним ієрогліфом, і довгі роки жоден комп’ютер не міг його набрати.","ar":"نودلز من شيان بعرض الحزام، يُكتب اسمها برمز بالغ التعقيد عجزت الحواسيب عن طباعته سنين.","he":"לאטרייה רחבה כחגורה משיאן יש שם שנכתב בתו מורכב במיוחד, ובמשך שנים אף מחשב לא יכול היה להקליד אותו.","sw":"Tambi pana kama mkanda kutoka Xi'an ina jina linaloandikwa kwa herufi ngumu isivyo kawaida, na kwa miaka mingi hakuna kompyuta iliyoweza kuiandika."},"body":{"en":"<p>In Xi'an you can order a noodle that is hard to write. Biangbiang noodles are thick and belt-like, usually made by hand, and for most of their existence they were an obscure local dish, eaten by workers who lacked the time to make thinner noodles. They are counted among the Eight Curiosities of Shaanxi. What made them famous across China was less the food than the character on the sign: 𰻞. Its standard traditional form has 58 strokes.</p><div class=\"trivia-actions\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"panto\" data-lat=\"34.26\" data-lng=\"108.94\" data-zoom=\"7\">📍 Move the map to Xi'an</button></div><h3>Taking it apart</h3><p>Nobody is sure where the word comes from. According to an article in <em>China Daily</em>, <em>biang</em> imitates a sound, the noise of the dough as the cook pulls it and slaps it on the table. The character has a legend too, which says the Qin premier Li Si invented it. That seems unlikely, because it is missing from the Kangxi Dictionary, and even from modern dictionaries, so it may be much younger than that.</p><p>It helps to take it apart, because almost everything inside is a common character. At the centre, 言, “speech”, is flanked by two 幺, “tiny”. Below that, 馬, “horse”, stands between two 長, “long”. Those two are on the Han Map, and in Xi'an they are <strong>ma⁵³</strong> and <strong>chang²⁴</strong> where Mandarin has <strong>mǎ</strong> and <strong>cháng</strong>. Around this block sit 月, “moon”, on the left, 刂, “knife”, on the right, and 心, “heart”, below. On top is 穴, “cave”, and 辶, “walk”, curves around the left and bottom.</p><div class=\"trivia-actions\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"setchar\" data-char=\"馬\">🗺️ Show 馬</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"setchar\" data-char=\"長:1\">🗺️ Show 長</button></div><h3>The rhyme</h3><p>People in Shaanxi do not learn this by counting strokes. They use a rhyme. One version starts: “A dot (丶) rises to the sky, Over two bends (冖) by Huanghe’s side.” It goes on through an eight, speech, “You twist, I twist too”, and “Inside, a horse (馬) king will rule”, then “A heart (心) below, A moon (月) by the side”. It ends on the road: “On our carriage, to Xianyang we’ll ride.” That last line stands for 辶, the walking radical, which wraps around the whole character.</p><div class=\"trivia-actions\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"setchar\" data-char=\"心\">🗺️ Show 心</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"setchar\" data-char=\"月\">🗺️ Show 月</button></div><p>There is no single correct form either. More than twenty variants of the traditional character are known, with between 56 and 71 strokes, and there is a simplified form, 𰻝, in which 馬 and 長 become 马 and 长. Heart and moon stay the same in both, and in the map’s Xi'an row they are <strong>xin²¹</strong> and <strong>yue²¹</strong>.</p><h3>Getting it onto a computer</h3><p>For years the problem was practical. No standard way existed to enter the character on a computer. In 2006 John Jenkins submitted one form to Unicode after an email discussion with Lee Collins, but its description was too long for the rules and it left out the heart. Ming Fan then submitted an application of his own, and in March 2020 both forms were encoded in Unicode 13.0, the traditional at U+30EDE and the simplified at U+30EDD. Until then people used images, the pinyin or substitutes such as 彪彪面 and 冰冰面. Many fonts still lack it, so on some screens this page may show an empty box where the noodle should be.</p>"},"sources":[{"text":"\"Biangbiang noodles\". Wikipedia, accessed 2026.","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Biangbiang_noodles"}]},{"id":"country-names-mei-bei","icon":"🌏","tags":["names","countries","characters"],"title":{"en":"America Is Beautiful in China and Rice in Japan","ja":"中国では美しく、日本では米：アメリカの名前","ko":"중국에서는 아름답고 일본에서는 쌀인 나라","zh":"美国的“美”，日本的“米”","yue":"美國嘅「美」，日本嘅「米」","vi":"Nước Mỹ đẹp ở Trung Quốc và là gạo ở Nhật Bản","th":"อเมริกา: สวยในจีน เป็นข้าวในญี่ปุ่น","id":"Amerika: indah di Tiongkok, beras di Jepang","hi":"चीन में अमेरिका सुंदर है, जापान में चावल","de":"Amerika ist in China schön und in Japan Reis","fr":"L’Amérique est belle en Chine et riz au Japon","it":"L’America è bella in Cina e riso in Giappone","es":"América es bella en China y arroz en Japón","pt":"A América é bela na China e arroz no Japão","ru":"Америка: красивая в Китае, рисовая в Японии","uk":"Америка: гарна в Китаї, рисова в Японії","ar":"أمريكا جميلة في الصين وأرز في اليابان","he":"אמריקה יפה בסין ואורז ביפן","sw":"Kwa Kichina Amerika ni nzuri, kwa Kijapani ni mchele"},"summary":{"en":"China writes the United States as 美国, “beautiful country”, and Japan as 米国, “rice country”. Neither is a compliment or a comment on farming. Both are leftovers of a sound.","ja":"中国はアメリカを「美国」、日本は「米国」と書く。褒め言葉でも農業の話でもなく、どちらも音の切れ端である。","ko":"중국은 미국을 ‘아름다운 나라’ 美国으로, 일본은 ‘쌀의 나라’ 米国으로 쓴다. 칭찬도 농업 이야기도 아니다. 둘 다 소리의 부스러기다.","zh":"中国把美国写成“美国”，日本写成“米国”。这既不是夸奖，也不是谈农业，两者都是音译留下的碎片。","yue":"中國將美國寫成「美国」，日本寫成「米国」。呢個唔係讚人，亦都唔係講農業，兩個都係音譯留低嘅碎片。","vi":"Trung Quốc viết Hoa Kỳ là 美国, “nước đẹp”, Nhật viết 米国, “nước gạo”. Chẳng phải khen, cũng chẳng bàn nông nghiệp. Cả hai đều là mảnh vụn của một âm thanh.","th":"จีนเขียนสหรัฐอเมริกาว่า 美国 “ประเทศงาม” ญี่ปุ่นเขียนว่า 米国 “ประเทศข้าว” ทั้งสองชื่อไม่ได้ชมหรือพูดถึงการเกษตร เป็นแค่เศษที่เหลือจากการถอดเสียง","id":"Bahasa Tionghoa menulis Amerika Serikat 美国, “negeri indah”, dan bahasa Jepang 米国, “negeri beras”. Keduanya bukan pujian atau komentar soal pertanian, melainkan sisa-sisa sebuah bunyi.","hi":"चीन संयुक्त राज्य को 美国 लिखता है, “सुंदर देश”, और जापान 米国, “चावल का देश”। न यह तारीफ़ है, न खेती पर टिप्पणी। दोनों एक ध्वनि के बचे हुए टुकड़े हैं।","de":"China schreibt die USA 美国, „schönes Land“, Japan 米国, „Reisland“. Das ist weder ein Kompliment noch eine Aussage über Landwirtschaft. Beides sind Reste eines Klangs.","fr":"La Chine écrit les États-Unis 美国, « beau pays », et le Japon 米国, « pays du riz ». Ce n’est ni un compliment ni une remarque agricole, seulement les restes d’un son.","it":"La Cina scrive gli Stati Uniti 美国, «paese bello», e il Giappone 米国, «paese del riso». Non è un complimento né un commento sull’agricoltura: sono solo i resti di un suono.","es":"China escribe Estados Unidos como 美国, «país bello», y Japón como 米国, «país del arroz». Ninguno es un cumplido ni un comentario agrícola. Ambos son restos de un sonido.","pt":"A China escreve Estados Unidos como 美国, “país belo”, e o Japão como 米国, “país do arroz”. Nenhum é elogio nem comentário agrícola. Ambos são sobras de um som.","ru":"Китай пишет США как 美国, «красивая страна», а Япония — как 米国, «рисовая страна». Это не комплимент и не замечание о сельском хозяйстве. Оба названия остались от одного звука.","uk":"Китай пише США як 美国, «гарна країна», а Японія — як 米国, «рисова країна». Це не комплімент і не зауваження про сільське господарство: обидві назви лишилися від одного звуку.","ar":"تكتب الصين الولايات المتحدة 美国، «البلد الجميل»، وتكتبها اليابان 米国، «بلد الأرز». وليس في ذلك مديح ولا تعليق على الزراعة، فالاسمان بقايا صوت.","he":"סין כותבת את ארצות הברית 美国, ״ארץ יפה״, ויפן 米国, ״ארץ האורז״. אף אחד מהם אינו מחמאה או הערה על חקלאות. שניהם שאריות של צליל.","sw":"China inaandika Marekani 美国, “nchi nzuri”, na Japani 米国, “nchi ya mchele”. Hakuna kati ya hizi iliyo sifa au maoni kuhusu kilimo. Zote ni mabaki ya sauti."},"body":{"en":"<p>China calls the United States 美国, and the first character means “beautiful”. Japan calls it 米国, and the first character means “rice”. Each looks like a remark about America, and neither one is. Both are fragments of an attempt to spell a foreign sound with Chinese characters, and the meaning that shows through is an accident. Wiktionary describes the “beautiful” reading of the Chinese name as the source of a false etymology.</p><div class=\"trivia-actions\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"panto\" data-lat=\"39.9\" data-lng=\"116.4\" data-zoom=\"5\">📍 Move the map to Beijing</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"panto\" data-lat=\"35.68\" data-lng=\"139.76\" data-zoom=\"5\">📍 Move the map to Tokyo</button></div><p>The Chinese name is attested from 1854. It may be short for 亞美理駕洲大合眾國, a long spelling of “United States of America” found in the Treaty of Wanghia of 1844. Other spellings were in the running: 美利堅, a rendering of the word <em>American</em>, had earlier rivals in 米利堅 and 彌利堅. So 米, rice, had a Chinese history in this job as well. China kept 美, and the full official form is still built on 美利堅.</p><h3>Rice country</h3><p>Japan went another way. It spelled the continent 亜米利加, took the second character, and added “country”. The result, <em>Beikoku</em>, was an existing word with a different job. It appears in the Japanese-Portuguese dictionary of 1603, where it meant any country rich in rice, and by 1819 it covered major rice exporters. The sense “America” is cited first in 1869. Everyday Japanese still prefers アメリカ, and 米国 remains semi-formal.</p><h3>The tooth of Spain</h3><p>Spain shows how little the characters may mean. Chinese 西班牙 starts with 西, “west”, read <strong>xī</strong>, and ends with 牙, “tooth”, read <strong>yá</strong>. It is a sound spelling, attested from 1835, not a description of anything. An earlier spelling from 1730, 是班呀, shows that the same sounds could be written in quite different ways. The choice of 牙 may have been helped along by 葡萄牙, Portugal, next door, so the two countries of Iberia carry the same tooth.</p><div class=\"trivia-actions\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"setchar\" data-char=\"西\">🗺️ Show 西</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"setchar\" data-char=\"牙\">🗺️ Show 牙</button></div><p>Turkey is 土耳其, with 土, “earth”, read <strong>tǔ</strong>, first attested in 1820. France is 法国, where 法 is an abbreviation of 法蘭西, and the character’s meaning of “law” plays no part. Germany shows the two traditions pulling apart. Mandarin 德国 approximates the German word <em>deutsch</em>. Japanese once used 獨逸 for the Dutch form of it, and Korean 독일 is the Korean reading of those same Japanese characters, which is why Korean and Chinese do not match here. Greece has the same story: 希臘 was the Chinese spelling of <em>Hellas</em>, and Japanese wrote those characters while saying <em>Girisha</em>.</p><div class=\"trivia-actions\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"setchar\" data-char=\"土\">🗺️ Show 土</button></div><h3>One character</h3><p>The habit of cutting a name down to one character is alive. Korean newspaper headlines still use 中 for China, 韓 for Korea, 美 for the United States and 日 for Japan, so Seoul writes America in the Chinese way and not the Japanese way. The region’s own names work differently. 中, “middle”, read <strong>zhōng</strong>, and 日, “sun”, read <strong>rì</strong> in Mandarin and <strong>il</strong> in Korean, are there for what they mean.</p><div class=\"trivia-actions\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"setchar\" data-char=\"日\">🗺️ Show 日</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"setchar\" data-char=\"中:1\">🗺️ Show 中</button></div>"},"sources":[{"text":"\"美國\". Wiktionary, accessed 2026.","url":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/美國"},{"text":"\"美利堅\". Wiktionary, accessed 2026.","url":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/美利堅"},{"text":"\"米国\". Wiktionary, accessed 2026.","url":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/米国"},{"text":"\"西班牙\". Wiktionary, accessed 2026.","url":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/西班牙"},{"text":"\"土耳其\". Wiktionary, accessed 2026.","url":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/土耳其"},{"text":"\"法國\". Wiktionary, accessed 2026.","url":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/法國"},{"text":"\"希臘\". Wiktionary, accessed 2026.","url":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/希臘"},{"text":"\"Names of the United States\". Wikipedia, accessed 2026.","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Names_of_the_United_States"},{"text":"\"Names of Germany\". Wikipedia, accessed 2026.","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Names_of_Germany"},{"text":"\"Ateji\". Wikipedia, accessed 2026.","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ateji"},{"text":"\"Hanja\". Wikipedia, accessed 2026.","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hanja"}]},{"id":"dragon-simplified","icon":"🐉","tags":["characters","simplification","variants"],"title":{"en":"龍, 竜 and 龙: Three Ways to Draw a Dragon","ja":"龍、竜、龙：竜の三つの描き方","ko":"龍, 竜, 龙: 용을 그리는 세 가지 방법","zh":"龍、竜、龙：龙的三种画法","yue":"龍、竜、龙：龍嘅三種畫法","vi":"龍, 竜 và 龙: ba cách vẽ con rồng","th":"龍 竜 และ 龙: วาดมังกรสามแบบ","id":"龍, 竜, dan 龙: tiga cara menggambar naga","hi":"龍, 竜 और 龙: ड्रैगन बनाने के तीन तरीक़े","de":"龍, 竜 und 龙: Drei Arten, einen Drachen zu zeichnen","fr":"龍, 竜 et 龙 : trois façons de dessiner un dragon","it":"龍, 竜 e 龙: tre modi di disegnare un drago","es":"龍, 竜 y 龙: tres maneras de dibujar un dragón","pt":"龍, 竜 e 龙: três maneiras de desenhar um dragão","ru":"龍, 竜 и 龙: три способа нарисовать дракона","uk":"龍, 竜 і 龙: три способи намалювати дракона","ar":"龍 و竜 و龙: ثلاث طرق لرسم تنين","he":"龍, 竜 ו-龙: שלוש דרכים לצייר דרקון","sw":"龍, 竜 na 龙: Njia Tatu za Kuchora Joka"},"summary":{"en":"One animal, three standard characters. China cut it to five strokes, Japan adopted an old variant, and Taiwan and Korea kept all sixteen.","ja":"一匹の動物に、三つの標準字。中国は5画に削り、日本は古い異体を採り、台湾と韓国は16画を保った。","ko":"한 동물에 표준 글자가 셋이다. 중국은 다섯 획으로 줄였고, 일본은 옛 이체자를 택했으며, 대만과 한국은 열여섯 획을 지켰다.","zh":"一种动物，三个标准字。中国把它减到五笔，日本采用了一个古异体，台湾和韩国保住了全部十六笔。","yue":"一種動物，三個標準字。中國將佢減到五筆，日本採用一個古異體，台灣同韓國保住全部十六筆。","vi":"Một con vật, ba chữ chuẩn. Trung Quốc rút còn năm nét, Nhật Bản chọn một dị thể cũ, Đài Loan và Hàn Quốc giữ đủ mười sáu nét.","th":"สัตว์ตัวเดียว อักษรมาตรฐานสามแบบ จีนตัดเหลือห้าขีด ญี่ปุ่นหยิบรูปแปรเก่ามาใช้ ส่วนไต้หวันและเกาหลีเก็บไว้ครบสิบหกขีด","id":"Satu hewan, tiga aksara baku. Tiongkok memangkasnya menjadi lima guratan, Jepang mengangkat sebuah varian lama, dan Taiwan serta Korea mempertahankan keenam belas guratannya.","hi":"एक जानवर, तीन मानक अक्षर। चीन ने इसे पाँच रेखाओं तक काटा, जापान ने एक पुराना रूप अपनाया, और ताइवान और कोरिया ने सोलहों रखीं।","de":"Ein Tier, drei Standardzeichen. China kürzte es auf fünf Striche, Japan übernahm eine alte Variante, Taiwan und Korea behielten alle sechzehn.","fr":"Un animal, trois caractères standard. La Chine l’a réduit à cinq traits, le Japon a adopté une vieille variante, Taïwan et la Corée ont gardé les seize.","it":"Un animale, tre caratteri ufficiali. La Cina lo ha ridotto a cinque tratti, il Giappone ha adottato una vecchia variante, Taiwan e la Corea hanno tenuto tutti e sedici.","es":"Un animal, tres caracteres estándar. China lo redujo a cinco trazos, Japón adoptó una vieja variante, y Taiwán y Corea conservaron los dieciséis.","pt":"Um animal, três caracteres padrão. A China o reduziu a cinco traços, o Japão adotou uma variante antiga, e Taiwan e a Coreia mantiveram os dezesseis.","ru":"Одно животное, три стандартных иероглифа. Китай урезал его до пяти черт, Япония взяла старый вариант, а Тайвань и Корея сохранили все шестнадцать.","uk":"Одна тварина — три стандартні ієрогліфи. Китай урізав його до п’яти рисок, Японія взяла старий варіант, а Тайвань і Корея зберегли всі шістнадцять.","ar":"حيوان واحد وثلاثة رموز قياسية. اختصرته الصين إلى خمسة خطوط، واعتمدت اليابان صيغة قديمة، واحتفظت تايوان وكوريا بالستة عشر كلها.","he":"לאותה חיה שלושה תווים תקניים. סין קיצצה אותו לחמישה קווים, יפן אימצה גרסה ישנה, וטייוואן וקוריאה שמרו על כל השישה עשר.","sw":"Mnyama mmoja, herufi tatu sanifu. China ilimpunguza hadi mistari mitano, Japani ilichukua umbo la zamani, na Taiwan na Korea zilibaki na yote kumi na sita."},"body":{"en":"<p>The dragon has sixteen strokes in Taipei, ten in Tokyo and five in Beijing. Those are 龍, 竜 and 龙, and they are all the same character, though each form got where it is by a different route. 龍 itself goes back to Late Shang bronze inscriptions, and it is radical 212 of the 214 in the Kangxi Dictionary.</p><div class=\"trivia-actions\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"setchar\" data-char=\"龍\">🗺️ Show 龍</button></div><p>Mainland 龙 is the end of a series of cuts. Wiktionary traces it to a variant of 龍, 𢅛. Removing 帝 from that left 尨, and two of the three strokes of 彡 were then dropped. Once the piece was settled it spread through the system. 咙, 拢, 珑, 胧 and 陇 all carry it, so the simplification of one character changed many others. Taiwan kept the full form. The reading did not change with the drawing: the map has <strong>lóng</strong> in both Mandarin rows.</p><h3>Japan’s old new form</h3><p>Japan did something different again. 竜 looks like a modern shortcut, but it is not one. The Kangxi Dictionary of 1716 records it as an ancient form of 龍, and the dictionaries <em>Longkan Shoujian</em> of 997 and <em>Jiyun</em> of 1037 have versions of the glyph. It became the official form in 1946, in the Tōyō kanji list, as part of the postwar reform. 龍 remains on record as its older form. The dragon also turns up in places you might not look for one. 土竜, “earth dragon”, is a mole, and 石竜子, “stone dragon child”, is a lizard.</p><h3>The readings</h3><p>The readings have travelled further than the shapes. Japanese has <strong>ryū</strong> and <strong>ryō</strong> from two waves of borrowing, and the native word <strong>tatsu</strong>. Cantonese is <strong>lung4</strong>. Vietnamese is <strong>long</strong>, which is why Hanoi’s imperial citadel is Thăng Long, and why the city is still nicknamed the City of Soaring Dragon. South Korean is <strong>yong</strong>, North Korean <strong>ryong</strong>. Korean also keeps a native word that the map records, <strong>mireu</strong>, 미르. Further back, the map’s Old Chinese is <strong>*[mə]-roŋ</strong>, and Proto-Tibeto-Burman <strong>*m-bru(ŋ/k)</strong>.</p><div class=\"trivia-actions\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"panto\" data-lat=\"21.03\" data-lng=\"105.85\" data-zoom=\"6\">📍 Move the map to Hanoi</button></div><h3>More dragons</h3><p>The dragon has also been a favourite for piling up. Two make 龖, three make 龘, and four make 𪚥, which has 64 strokes, the sum of four copies of 龍’s sixteen. Even that is not the record. <em>Taito</em>, a character invented in Japan, is written with 84 strokes, and it is called the most graphically complex of all CJK characters. It is a rare character: three clouds above three dragons.</p><p>So a writer in Beijing uses five strokes and a writer in Taipei sixteen. The two forms sit in Unicode as separate characters, and a font does not turn one into the other. The dragon on a Hong Kong sign and the dragon on a Shanghai sign are the same word, and it takes eleven strokes to tell them apart.</p>"},"sources":[{"text":"\"龙\". Wiktionary, accessed 2026.","url":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/龙"},{"text":"\"竜\". Wiktionary, accessed 2026.","url":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/竜"},{"text":"\"龍\". Wiktionary, accessed 2026.","url":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/龍"},{"text":"\"𪚥\". Wiktionary, accessed 2026.","url":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/𪚥"},{"text":"\"Taito (kanji)\". Wikipedia, accessed 2026.","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Taito_(kanji)"},{"text":"\"Hanoi\". Wikipedia, accessed 2026.","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hanoi"}]},{"id":"fanqie-spelling","icon":"🔤","tags":["history","phonology","dictionaries"],"title":{"en":"Fanqie: Spelling a Sound with Two Characters","ja":"反切：二つの字で音を綴る","ko":"반절: 두 글자로 소리를 적다","zh":"反切：用两个字拼一个音","yue":"反切：用兩個字拼一個音","vi":"Phiên thiết: đánh vần một âm bằng hai chữ","th":"ฟ่านเชี่ย: สะกดเสียงด้วยอักษรสองตัว","id":"Fanqie: mengeja bunyi dengan dua aksara","hi":"फ़ानछिए: दो अक्षरों से एक ध्वनि की वर्तनी","de":"Fanqie: Einen Laut mit zwei Zeichen buchstabieren","fr":"Fanqie : épeler un son avec deux caractères","it":"Fanqie: compitare un suono con due caratteri","es":"Fanqie: deletrear un sonido con dos caracteres","pt":"Fanqie: soletrar um som com dois caracteres","ru":"Фаньце: как записать звук двумя иероглифами","uk":"Фаньце: як записати звук двома ієрогліфами","ar":"فانتشيه: تهجئة صوت برمزين","he":"פאנצ'יה: לאיית צליל בשני תווים","sw":"Fanqie: kuandika sauti kwa herufi mbili"},"summary":{"en":"With no alphabet, Chinese scholars spelled one syllable by cutting two others in half. The trick worked for a thousand years, and it also recorded sounds that have since disappeared.","ja":"字母を持たない漢字で、中国の学者は二つの字を半分ずつ使って一音節を綴った。この工夫は千年以上続き、今は失われた音の記録にもなった。","ko":"알파벳이 없던 중국 학자들은 두 글자를 반씩 잘라 한 음절을 적었다. 이 방법은 천 년 넘게 쓰였고, 지금은 사라진 소리의 기록이 되었다.","zh":"没有字母的中国学者，把两个字各取一半，拼出一个音节。这个办法用了一千多年，也记录下了后来消失的声音。","yue":"冇字母嘅中國學者，將兩個字各取一半，拼出一個音節。呢個方法用咗一千幾年，亦記錄低後來消失嘅聲音。","vi":"Không có bảng chữ cái, học giả Trung Hoa đánh vần một âm tiết bằng cách cắt đôi hai chữ. Cách này dùng hơn nghìn năm và còn ghi lại những âm nay đã mất.","th":"เมื่อไม่มีตัวอักษรแทนเสียง นักปราชญ์จีนก็สะกดพยางค์ด้วยการตัดอักษรสองตัวมาต่อกันคนละครึ่ง วิธีนี้ใช้กันมากว่าพันปี และเก็บเสียงที่หายไปแล้วไว้ให้เรา","id":"Tanpa alfabet, para sarjana Tionghoa mengeja satu suku kata dengan memotong dua suku kata lain. Cara ini bertahan seribu tahun dan sekaligus merekam bunyi yang kini sudah hilang.","hi":"वर्णमाला के बिना चीनी विद्वानों ने दो अक्षरों को आधा काटकर एक शब्दांश की वर्तनी लिखी। यह तरकीब हज़ार साल चली, और इसने ऐसी ध्वनियाँ भी दर्ज कीं जो अब मिट चुकी हैं।","de":"Ohne Alphabet buchstabierten chinesische Gelehrte eine Silbe, indem sie zwei andere halbierten. Das funktionierte tausend Jahre lang und hielt nebenbei Laute fest, die inzwischen verschwunden sind.","fr":"Sans alphabet, les lettrés chinois épelaient une syllabe en coupant deux autres en deux. L’astuce a marché mille ans, et elle a aussi enregistré des sons aujourd’hui disparus.","it":"Senza alfabeto, gli studiosi cinesi scrivevano il suono di una sillaba tagliandone a metà altre due. Il trucco ha funzionato per mille anni, e ha conservato suoni oggi scomparsi.","es":"Sin alfabeto, los eruditos chinos deletreaban una sílaba partiendo otras dos por la mitad. El truco funcionó mil años, y además dejó constancia de sonidos hoy desaparecidos.","pt":"Sem alfabeto, os eruditos chineses soletravam uma sílaba cortando duas outras ao meio. O truque funcionou por mil anos e ainda registrou sons hoje desaparecidos.","ru":"Без алфавита китайские учёные записывали слог, разрезая пополам два других. Приём прослужил тысячу лет и заодно сохранил звуки, которых больше нет.","uk":"Без алфавіту китайські вчені записували склад, розрізаючи навпіл два інші. Прийом прослужив тисячу років і заодно зберіг звуки, яких більше немає.","ar":"بلا أبجدية، كان العلماء الصينيون يتهجون المقطع بقطع مقطعين آخرين إلى نصفين. نجحت الحيلة ألف سنة، وسجلت أيضًا أصواتًا اختفت.","he":"בלי אלפבית, מלומדים סינים איותו הברה אחת בעזרת שתי הברות אחרות שחתכו לחצי. השיטה עבדה אלף שנה, ובדרך תיעדה גם צלילים שכבר נעלמו.","sw":"Bila alfabeti, wanazuoni wa China waliandika silabi moja kwa kukata nyingine mbili nusu. Ujanja huu ulidumu miaka elfu, na pia ulirekodi sauti ambazo zimetoweka."},"body":{"en":"<p>How do you write down a pronunciation when your script has no letters? For centuries the answer was a homophone: a dictionary such as the <em>Shuowen Jiezi</em> simply named another character that sounded the same. That fails if the reader does not know the other character, or if no good match exists. By the 3rd century a better idea had appeared. Take two characters, keep the start of the first and the rest of the second, and join them. That is <em>fanqie</em>.</p><h3>East is 德 plus 紅</h3><p>The first entry of the <em>Qieyun</em>, the rhyme dictionary of 601, shows how it works. The character 東, “east”, is spelled 德紅反. The last character only marks the formula. From 德 you keep the opening <em>t</em>, and from 紅, “red”, you keep the final <em>-uŋ</em> and the tone. The Han Map’s Middle Chinese row shows the match directly: 東 is <strong>tuɪŋ¹²³</strong> and 紅 is <strong>ɦuɪŋ¹²³</strong>, with the same rhyme and the same tone mark.</p><div class=\"trivia-actions\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"setchar\" data-char=\"東\">🗺️ Show 東</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"setchar\" data-char=\"紅\">🗺️ Show 紅</button></div><p>Where it came from is partly a guess. The 6th-century scholar Yan Zhitui credited Sun Yan, of the Wei kingdom, although earlier examples turn up in the late 2nd century. Buddhism had reached China around the 1st century, bringing Indian phonetic knowledge, and that may have inspired the idea. Even the label has a story. The marker 反, “turn back”, gave way to 切 in later dictionaries such as the <em>Guangyun</em>. The Qing scholar Gu Yanwu suggested that 反, which also meant “overthrow”, was avoided after the rebellions of the mid-Tang.</p><h3>A thousand years of use</h3><p>The method lasted. It ran through the Kangxi Dictionary of 1716 and on into the <em>Ciyuan</em> and <em>Cihai</em> of the 1930s. A Sino-Manchu dictionary of the mid-18th century even used it in reverse, spelling Manchu words with fanqie and Chinese words in Manchu script.</p><h3>When the formula breaks</h3><p>Try the formula on modern Mandarin and it breaks. 德 is <em>dé</em>, and 紅 is <strong>hóng</strong>, so the sum should be something like <em>dóng</em>. But 東 is <strong>dōng</strong>. The reason is that 紅 once began with a voiced sound. Syllables with voiced initials tended towards lower pitch, and by the late Tang each tone had split in two according to the initial. The tone that was shared in 601 became two different tones, and then voicing itself disappeared in most of China. It survives in Wu and Xiang. That is why Shanghainese still shows the old contrast on the map: 東 is <strong>ton1</strong> and 紅 is <strong>ghon6</strong>, with a voiced start.</p><div class=\"trivia-actions\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"panto\" data-lat=\"31.23\" data-lng=\"121.47\" data-zoom=\"6\">📍 Move the map to Shanghai</button></div><p>So fanqie turned out to be a recording. No one who wrote those formulas meant to leave evidence, yet in 1842 the Cantonese scholar Chen Li saw how to use it. If 東 is spelled with 德, 德 with 多, and 多 with 德, then all three must have had the same initial. Following such chains gives the categories of initials and finals. Bernard Karlgren, unaware of Chen’s work, repeated the analysis in the 1910s, and compared the results with modern varieties and Sino-Xenic readings to reconstruct the sounds of Middle Chinese.</p>"},"sources":[{"text":"\"Fanqie\". Wikipedia, accessed 2026.","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fanqie"}]},{"id":"hanja-korea-today","icon":"🇰🇷","tags":["korea","characters","policy"],"title":{"en":"Hanja Today: Banned, Unbanned and Still on the Noodle Packet","ja":"韓国の漢字の今：禁止、解禁、そしてラーメンの袋","ko":"오늘의 한자: 금지, 해제, 그리고 라면 봉지","zh":"今天的韩国汉字：禁了又解，还印在方便面袋上","yue":"今日韓國嘅漢字：禁完又解，仲印喺公仔麵袋上","vi":"Hanja hôm nay: cấm, bỏ cấm, và vẫn trên gói mì","th":"ฮันจาวันนี้: ถูกห้าม ถูกถอด แต่ยังอยู่บนซองบะหมี่","id":"Hanja kini: dilarang, dibolehkan, dan tetap di bungkus mi","hi":"आज का हांजा: प्रतिबंध, वापसी और नूडल का पैकेट","de":"Hanja heute: verboten, wieder erlaubt und noch immer auf der Nudelpackung","fr":"Les hanja aujourd’hui : interdits, réhabilités, toujours sur le paquet de nouilles","it":"Gli hanja oggi: vietati, riammessi e ancora sul pacchetto di noodles","es":"Los hanja hoy: prohibidos, readmitidos y aún en el paquete de fideos","pt":"Hanja hoje: proibidos, liberados e ainda no pacote de macarrão","ru":"Ханча сегодня: запретили, разрешили, а на лапше они остались","uk":"Ханча сьогодні: заборонили, дозволили, а на локшині лишили","ar":"الهانجا اليوم: ممنوعة ثم مسموحة وما زالت على كيس النودلز","he":"הנג'ה היום: נאסרו, הותרו ועדיין על שקית האטריות","sw":"Hanja leo: zilipigwa marufuku, zikarudi, na bado ziko kwenye pakiti ya tambi"},"summary":{"en":"South Korea tried to abolish Chinese characters, changed its mind several times, and now teaches 1,800 of them to students who hardly ever use them. North Korea, which dropped them first, teaches more.","ja":"韓国は漢字を廃止しようとし、何度も方針を変え、今はほとんど使わない生徒に1,800字を教えている。先に手放した北朝鮮は、もっと多くの字を教えている。","ko":"한국은 한자를 없애려 했고 몇 번이나 생각을 바꾸었으며, 이제는 거의 쓰지 않는 학생들에게 1,800자를 가르친다. 먼저 버린 북한은 더 많이 가르친다.","zh":"韩国曾想废除汉字，几次改变主意，如今把 1,800 个字教给几乎用不上的学生。先放弃汉字的朝鲜，教得反而更多。","yue":"韓國曾經想廢除漢字，幾次改變主意，而家將 1,800 個字教畀差唔多用唔著嘅學生。先放棄漢字嘅北韓，教得仲多。","vi":"Hàn Quốc từng định bỏ chữ Hán, đổi ý nhiều lần, và nay dạy 1.800 chữ cho học sinh hầu như không dùng. Triều Tiên, nơi bỏ trước, lại dạy nhiều hơn.","th":"เกาหลีใต้พยายามเลิกใช้อักษรจีน กลับลำไปมาหลายครั้ง และตอนนี้สอน 1,800 ตัวให้นักเรียนที่แทบไม่ได้ใช้ ส่วนเกาหลีเหนือที่เลิกไปก่อนกลับสอนมากกว่า","id":"Korea Selatan mencoba menghapus aksara Tionghoa, berubah pikiran beberapa kali, dan kini mengajarkan 1.800 aksara kepada siswa yang hampir tak pernah memakainya. Korea Utara, yang lebih dulu membuangnya, mengajarkan lebih banyak.","hi":"दक्षिण कोरिया ने चीनी अक्षर मिटाने की कोशिश की, कई बार इरादा बदला, और अब उन छात्रों को 1,800 अक्षर सिखाता है जो उन्हें लगभग कभी इस्तेमाल नहीं करते। उत्तर कोरिया, जिसने उन्हें पहले छोड़ा, ज़्यादा सिखाता है।","de":"Südkorea wollte die chinesischen Zeichen abschaffen, änderte mehrmals die Meinung und lehrt heute 1.800 davon, die kaum jemand benutzt. Nordkorea, das sie als Erstes aufgab, lehrt mehr.","fr":"La Corée du Sud a tenté d’abolir les caractères chinois, a changé d’avis plusieurs fois et en enseigne maintenant 1 800 à des élèves qui ne s’en servent presque jamais. La Corée du Nord, qui les a abandonnés la première, en enseigne davantage.","it":"La Corea del Sud ha cercato di abolire i caratteri cinesi, ha cambiato idea più volte e oggi ne insegna 1.800 a studenti che non li usano quasi mai. La Corea del Nord, che li abbandonò per prima, ne insegna di più.","es":"Corea del Sur intentó abolir los caracteres chinos, cambió de idea varias veces y hoy enseña 1800 a estudiantes que casi nunca los usan. Corea del Norte, que los dejó primero, enseña más.","pt":"A Coreia do Sul tentou abolir os caracteres chineses, mudou de ideia várias vezes e hoje ensina 1.800 a estudantes que quase nunca os usam. A Coreia do Norte, que os largou primeiro, ensina mais.","ru":"Южная Корея пыталась отменить китайские иероглифы, несколько раз передумывала и теперь учит 1800 из них школьников, которые ими почти не пользуются. Северная Корея, отказавшаяся от них первой, учит больше.","uk":"Південна Корея намагалася скасувати китайські ієрогліфи, кілька разів передумувала й тепер навчає 1800 із них школярів, які ними майже не користуються. Північна Корея, що відмовилася від них першою, навчає більше.","ar":"حاولت كوريا الجنوبية إلغاء الرموز الصينية، وغيرت رأيها مرارًا، وهي تعلّم الآن 1,800 منها لطلاب لا يكادون يستعملونها. وكوريا الشمالية التي تخلت عنها أولًا تعلّم أكثر.","he":"דרום קוריאה ניסתה לבטל את התווים הסיניים, שינתה את דעתה כמה פעמים, וכיום מלמדת 1,800 מהם תלמידים שכמעט לא משתמשים בהם. צפון קוריאה, שוויתרה עליהם ראשונה, מלמדת יותר.","sw":"Korea Kusini ilijaribu kufuta herufi za Kichina, ikabadili nia mara kadhaa, na sasa inafundisha 1,800 kwa wanafunzi wasiozitumia karibu kabisa. Korea Kaskazini, iliyoziacha kwanza, inafundisha zaidi."},"body":{"en":"<p>In Korean, 一, “one”, and 日, “sun, day”, sound exactly alike: <strong>il</strong>. In Chinese they are different, and in hanja, the Chinese characters used for Korean, you can see the difference at once. In Hangul you cannot. That is the whole argument over hanja in a single syllable. A very large part of the Korean vocabulary comes from Chinese, and written in the alphabet many of those words collapse into one another. Yet most Koreans today read and write almost none of the characters.</p><div class=\"trivia-actions\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"setchar\" data-char=\"一\">🗺️ Show 一</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"setchar\" data-char=\"日\">🗺️ Show 日</button></div><h3>From Literary Chinese to the ban</h3><p>For a long time things were the other way round. Korean documents, history and literature were written primarily in Literary Chinese. King Sejong promulgated Hangul in 1446, but it did not come into widespread official use until the late 19th and early 20th century. Mixed text, with hanja for Sino-Korean words and Hangul around them, was normal well into the 20th century.</p><p>The turning point in the South came in 1968, when hanja were removed from public schools and the military, with the aim of eliminating them from writing by 1972. There was a public backlash, and in 1972 Park Chung Hee’s government allowed hanja to be taught as a subject, but kept them out of textbooks and other class materials. Typewriters and later keyboards worked against the characters as well, since hanja slowed writing and printing. By the 1970s government documents had largely given up mixed script.</p><div class=\"trivia-actions\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"panto\" data-lat=\"37.57\" data-lng=\"126.98\" data-zoom=\"6\">📍 Move the map to Seoul</button></div><h3>Reversal</h3><p>Policy then went into reverse, without much effect. The ban was not formally lifted until 1992. In 1999 the government put hanja on road signs, at bus stops and in subways, and the same year classical Chinese came back as a school elective. A Hanja Proficiency Test followed in 2001. In 2005 the old Law Concerning Hangul Exclusivity was repealed, and in 2013 all elementary schools in Seoul started teaching hanja. Today a total of 1,800 are taught, 900 in junior high and 900 in senior high, from a list set in 1972 that had 44 characters swapped at the end of 2000. A generation had already grown up without them, however, and use kept falling.</p><p>North Korea is the surprise. It abandoned the general use of hanja soon after independence, and it replaced many Chinese-derived words with native ones. Kim Il Sung had called for gradual elimination, then reversed himself by the 1960s. In 1966 he was quoted as saying that “students must be exposed to the necessary Chinese characters and taught how to write them.” A textbook was designed that taught 1,500 characters in grades 5 to 9 and another 500 in high school. So the country that banned hanja from print teaches more of them than the South does.</p><div class=\"trivia-actions\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"panto\" data-lat=\"39.04\" data-lng=\"125.76\" data-zoom=\"6\">📍 Move the map to Pyongyang</button></div><h3>What is left</h3><p>What remains in the South is small and precise. Newspaper headlines use single characters as abbreviations: 中 for China, 美 for the United States, 日 for Japan. Formal publications often gloss people’s names in hanja. Characters clear up homophones, as between 이 사장, 李 社長, “President Lee”, and 이사장, 理事長, “chairman of the board”. They also sell things: 辛, <em>sin</em>, “spicy”, is printed large on packets of Shin Ramyun. And the old layers still show in the readings. The map gives 茶 two Korean readings, <strong>cha</strong> in 綠茶, green tea, and <strong>da</strong> in 茶道, the tea ceremony, and it pairs 水, <strong>su</strong>, with the native word <strong>mul</strong>, the way the character is named in class.</p><div class=\"trivia-actions\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"setchar\" data-char=\"茶\">🗺️ Show 茶</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"setchar\" data-char=\"水\">🗺️ Show 水</button></div>"},"sources":[{"text":"\"Hanja\". Wikipedia, accessed 2026.","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hanja"}]},{"id":"naming-taboo-bihui","icon":"🚫","tags":["history","characters","china"],"title":{"en":"The Forbidden Characters: How Emperors’ Names Rewrote Chinese","ja":"書いてはならない字：皇帝の名が書き換えた漢語","ko":"쓸 수 없는 글자: 황제의 이름이 고쳐 쓴 한문","zh":"不能写的字：皇帝的名字如何改写了汉语","yue":"唔寫得嘅字：皇帝個名點樣改寫中文","vi":"Những chữ cấm: tên hoàng đế đã viết lại chữ Hán thế nào","th":"อักษรต้องห้าม: พระนามจักรพรรดิเขียนภาษาจีนใหม่อย่างไร","id":"Aksara terlarang: nama kaisar menulis ulang bahasa Tionghoa","hi":"वर्जित अक्षर: जब सम्राटों के नामों ने चीनी को बदल दिया","de":"Die verbotenen Zeichen: Wie Kaisernamen das Chinesische umschrieben","fr":"Les caractères interdits : quand les noms d’empereurs ont réécrit le chinois","it":"I caratteri proibiti: come i nomi degli imperatori hanno riscritto il cinese","es":"Los caracteres prohibidos: cómo los nombres de emperadores reescribieron el chino","pt":"Os caracteres proibidos: como os nomes de imperadores reescreveram o chinês","ru":"Запретные иероглифы: как имена императоров переписали китайский","uk":"Заборонені ієрогліфи: як імена імператорів переписали китайську","ar":"الرموز الممنوعة: كيف أعادت أسماء الأباطرة كتابة الصينية","he":"התווים האסורים: איך שמות הקיסרים שינו את הסינית","sw":"Herufi zilizokatazwa: majina ya wafalme yalivyoandika upya Kichina"},"summary":{"en":"A character in an emperor’s name could not be written. So chamber pots changed species, mountains changed names and a dictionary-maker was executed.","ja":"皇帝の名にある字は書けなかった。おまるは動物を変え、山は名を変え、字書の編者は死刑になった。","ko":"황제의 이름에 든 글자는 쓸 수 없었다. 그래서 요강은 동물이 바뀌고, 산은 이름이 바뀌고, 자전을 만든 사람은 처형되었다.","zh":"皇帝名字里的字不能写。于是便器换了动物，山换了名字，一位编字典的人被处死。","yue":"皇帝名入面嘅字唔寫得。於是便器換咗動物，山換咗名，一個編字典嘅人畀人處死。","vi":"Chữ trong tên hoàng đế không được viết. Vì thế cái bô phải đổi tên con vật, núi phải đổi tên, và một người làm từ điển bị xử tử.","th":"ห้ามเขียนอักษรในพระนามจักรพรรดิ กระโถนจึงต้องเปลี่ยนสัตว์ ภูเขาต้องเปลี่ยนชื่อ และคนทำพจนานุกรมถึงกับถูกประหาร","id":"Aksara dalam nama kaisar tak boleh ditulis. Akibatnya pispot berganti spesies, gunung berganti nama, dan seorang penyusun kamus dihukum mati.","hi":"सम्राट के नाम का अक्षर लिखा नहीं जा सकता था। तो चैंबर पॉट की प्रजाति बदली, पहाड़ों के नाम बदले और एक शब्दकोशकार को मृत्युदंड मिला।","de":"Ein Zeichen aus dem Namen des Kaisers durfte nicht geschrieben werden. So wechselten Nachttöpfe die Tierart, Berge ihre Namen, und ein Wörterbuchmacher wurde hingerichtet.","fr":"Un caractère du nom de l’empereur ne pouvait pas s’écrire. Des pots de chambre ont donc changé d’espèce, des montagnes de nom, et un auteur de dictionnaire a été exécuté.","it":"I caratteri del nome dell’imperatore non si potevano scrivere. Così i vasi da notte cambiarono specie, le montagne cambiarono nome e un autore di dizionari fu giustiziato.","es":"Un carácter del nombre del emperador no podía escribirse. Así que los orinales cambiaron de especie, las montañas de nombre y un autor de diccionarios fue ejecutado.","pt":"Um caractere do nome do imperador não podia ser escrito. Assim, penicos mudaram de espécie, montanhas mudaram de nome e um autor de dicionário foi executado.","ru":"Иероглиф из имени императора нельзя было писать. Ночные горшки сменили породу, горы сменили названия, а составителя словаря казнили.","uk":"Ієрогліф з імені імператора не можна було писати. Через це нічні горщики змінили породу, гори — назви, а укладача словника стратили.","ar":"لم يكن يجوز كتابة رمز من اسم الإمبراطور. فتحولت المباول من نمر إلى حصان، وغيرت الجبال أسماءها، وأُعدم مؤلف قاموس.","he":"תו בשם של קיסר אסור היה לכתוב. אז סירי לילה הפכו לחיה אחרת, הרים החליפו שמות, ומחבר מילון הוצא להורג.","sw":"Herufi katika jina la mfalme haikuweza kuandikwa. Kwa hiyo vyungu vya choo vilibadili spishi, milima ilibadili majina na mtunzi wa kamusi aliuawa."},"body":{"en":"<p>In the Tang dynasty a chamber pot could not be called a tiger. The founder’s grandfather was named Li Hu, and 虎, “tiger”, read <strong>hǔ</strong> in Mandarin, had become unusable. Since the Han dynasty the pot had been called 虎子, “tiger child”. It became 馬子, “horse child”, with <strong>mǎ</strong> in place of <strong>hǔ</strong>. This was the naming taboo, 避諱, a rule against speaking or writing the given names of exalted persons, enforced by law in imperial China.</p><div class=\"trivia-actions\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"setchar\" data-char=\"虎\">🗺️ Show 虎</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"setchar\" data-char=\"馬\">🗺️ Show 馬</button></div><h3>Three ways around</h3><p>There were three ways around a forbidden character. You could change it for a synonym or something that sounded similar, leave a blank, or leave out a stroke, usually the last. The Kangxi Emperor’s name was Xuanye, 玄燁, so the Black Warrior Gate of the Forbidden City, 玄武門, became the Gate of Divine Might, 神武門. It keeps that name.</p><div class=\"trivia-actions\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"panto\" data-lat=\"39.92\" data-lng=\"116.39\" data-zoom=\"7\">📍 Move the map to Beijing</button></div><p>The rule reached the classics themselves. The first emperor’s given name was Zheng, 政, so the first month of the year, 正月, had its reading changed and was then renamed 端月. Under the Han, Liu Bang’s name 邦 turned the phrase 邦家 in the <em>Daodejing</em> into 國家. The name of Emperor Wen, 恆, changed 恆 to 常 in the same book, including in its opening line, and renamed Mount Heng. The moon goddess 姮娥 became 嫦娥, the name she carries now, for the sake of a similar sound.</p><h3>Emperors who tried to help</h3><p>Emperor Taizong of Tang was called Shimin, 世民, two very common characters, and he tried to soften the rule by requiring avoidance only when the pair appeared together. His son undid that. 世 was usually replaced by 代 and 民 by 人, read <strong>rén</strong>, and the general Li Shiji became Li Ji. A line by Liu Zongyuan that mentions 人風, “the people’s ways”, was written that way for this reason: the word he meant was 民風.</p><p>Some emperors tried to be considerate. Emperor Xuan of Han changed his own name from Bingyi, 病已, to the rarer Xun, 詢, to make life easier for his subjects, and later dynasties gave princes unusual names for the same reason. A basic problem remained: to avoid a name, you had to know it. In 435 ambassadors from Goguryeo formally asked the Northern Wei court for a document listing the emperors’ names so that they would not give offence, and the emperor agreed.</p><div class=\"trivia-actions\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"setchar\" data-char=\"人\">🗺️ Show 人</button></div><h3>The price</h3><p>Whole cities were caught up in it. Jianye, today’s Nanjing, was renamed Jiankang because of the sound of an emperor’s name. Surnames changed too: the surname Shuai, 帥, came from Shi, 師, by leaving out a stroke, for the sake of Sima Shi. The penalties were real. In 1777 Wang Xihou criticised the Kangxi Dictionary in a dictionary of his own. In it he also wrote the Qianlong Emperor’s name out in full, breaking the taboo, and for that he was executed. For historians all this avoidance has a use: a changed character can show whose reign a text was copied in.</p><div class=\"trivia-actions\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"panto\" data-lat=\"32.06\" data-lng=\"118.8\" data-zoom=\"7\">📍 Move the map to Nanjing</button></div>"},"sources":[{"text":"\"Naming taboo\". Wikipedia, accessed 2026.","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Naming_taboo"},{"text":"\"Wang Xihou\". Wikipedia, accessed 2026.","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wang_Xihou"},{"text":"\"避讳\". Chinese Wikipedia, accessed 2026.","url":"https://zh.wikipedia.org/wiki/避讳"},{"text":"\"字貫\". Chinese Wikipedia, accessed 2026.","url":"https://zh.wikipedia.org/wiki/字貫"}]},{"id":"shuowen-540","icon":"📖","tags":["history","dictionaries","characters"],"title":{"en":"Shuowen Jiezi: 9,353 Characters in 540 Drawers","ja":"説文解字：540の引き出しに収めた9,353字","ko":"설문해자: 540개 서랍에 넣은 9,353자","zh":"《说文解字》：540 个抽屉里的 9,353 个字","yue":"《說文解字》：540 個櫃桶入面嘅 9,353 個字","vi":"Thuyết văn giải tự: 9.353 chữ trong 540 ngăn kéo","th":"ซัวเหวินเจี่ยจื้อ: 9,353 ตัวในลิ้นชัก 540 ช่อง","id":"Shuowen Jiezi: 9.353 aksara dalam 540 laci","hi":"Shuowen Jiezi: 540 दराज़ों में 9,353 अक्षर","de":"Shuowen Jiezi: 9.353 Zeichen in 540 Schubladen","fr":"Shuowen Jiezi : 9 353 caractères dans 540 tiroirs","it":"Shuowen Jiezi: 9.353 caratteri in 540 cassetti","es":"Shuowen Jiezi: 9353 caracteres en 540 cajones","pt":"Shuowen Jiezi: 9.353 caracteres em 540 gavetas","ru":"«Шовэнь цзецзы»: 9353 иероглифа в 540 ящиках","uk":"«Шовень цзецзи»: 9353 ієрогліфи у 540 шухлядах","ar":"Shuowen Jiezi: 9,353 رمزًا في 540 درجًا","he":"״שואוון ג'יאזי״: 9,353 תווים ב-540 מגירות","sw":"Shuowen Jiezi: herufi 9,353 katika droo 540"},"summary":{"en":"Around the year 100 Xu Shen sorted the Chinese script by its parts, a new idea at the time. His system starts at one and ends at the last Earthly Branch.","ja":"西暦100年ごろ、許慎は漢字を部品で分類した。当時としては新しい発想である。その体系は「一」に始まり、十二支の最後の字で終わる。","ko":"서기 100년쯤 허신은 한자를 부품으로 분류했다. 당시로서는 새로운 생각이었다. 그의 체계는 一에서 시작해 십이지의 마지막 글자로 끝난다.","zh":"公元 100 年前后，许慎按部件给汉字分类，这在当时是新想法。他的体系始于“一”，终于地支的最后一字。","yue":"公元 100 年前後，許慎按部件幫漢字分類，呢個喺當時係新諗法。佢嘅體系始於「一」，終於地支最後一個字。","vi":"Khoảng năm 100, Hứa Thận xếp chữ Hán theo bộ phận, một ý tưởng mới lúc bấy giờ. Hệ thống của ông bắt đầu từ 一 và kết thúc ở chữ cuối của Địa chi.","th":"ราวปี 100 สวี่ เซิ่นจัดหมวดอักษรจีนตามส่วนประกอบ ซึ่งเป็นความคิดใหม่ในตอนนั้น ระบบของเขาเริ่มที่ 一 และจบที่กิ่งดินตัวสุดท้าย","id":"Sekitar tahun 100, Xu Shen memilah aksara Tionghoa menurut bagian-bagiannya, gagasan baru pada masa itu. Sistemnya dimulai dari \"satu\" dan berakhir di Cabang Bumi terakhir.","hi":"वर्ष 100 के आसपास शू शन ने चीनी लिपि को उसके हिस्सों के हिसाब से छाँटा, जो उस समय नया विचार था। उनकी व्यवस्था एक से शुरू होकर आख़िरी पार्थिव शाखा पर ख़त्म होती है।","de":"Um das Jahr 100 ordnete Xu Shen die chinesische Schrift nach ihren Bestandteilen, damals eine neue Idee. Sein System beginnt bei der Eins und endet beim letzten Erdzweig.","fr":"Vers l’an 100, Xu Shen a classé l’écriture chinoise selon ses composants, idée alors nouvelle. Son système part du chiffre un et finit sur le dernier rameau terrestre.","it":"Intorno all’anno 100 Xu Shen ebbe un’idea nuova: ordinare la scrittura cinese in base alle parti dei caratteri. Il suo sistema comincia dall’uno e finisce con l’ultimo Ramo terrestre.","es":"Hacia el año 100, Xu Shen ordenó la escritura china por sus partes, una idea nueva entonces. Su sistema empieza en el uno y acaba en la última Rama Terrestre.","pt":"Por volta do ano 100, Xu Shen ordenou a escrita chinesa por suas partes, ideia nova na época. Seu sistema começa no um e termina no último Ramo Terrestre.","ru":"Около 100 года Сюй Шэнь разложил китайское письмо по составным частям, и это было ново. Его система начинается с единицы и кончается последней Земной ветвью.","uk":"Близько 100 року Сюй Шень розклав китайське письмо за складовими частинами, і цього до нього ніхто не робив. Його система починається з одиниці й закінчується останньою Земною гілкою.","ar":"نحو عام 100 رتب شو شن الكتابة الصينية بحسب أجزائها، وكانت فكرة جديدة آنذاك. ويبدأ نظامه بالواحد وينتهي بآخر الفروع الأرضية.","he":"בסביבות שנת 100 מיין שו שן את הכתב הסיני לפי חלקיו, רעיון חדש באותה עת. השיטה שלו מתחילה באחד ומסתיימת בענף הארץ האחרון.","sw":"Karibu mwaka 100 Xu Shen alipanga maandishi ya Kichina kwa sehemu zake, wazo jipya wakati huo. Mfumo wake unaanza na moja na kuishia kwenye Tawi la Ardhi la mwisho."},"body":{"en":"<p>The first entry in the first Chinese dictionary organised by radicals is 一, “one”, read <strong>yī</strong> today. The last section ends at 亥, the final Earthly Branch. Between them, Xu Shen sorted 9,353 characters into 540 sections, and he reports the numbers himself in his postface, along with 1,163 graphic variants and a total length of 133,441 characters. Modern editions, after centuries of copying and correction, have 9,831 characters and 1,279 variants.</p><div class=\"trivia-actions\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"setchar\" data-char=\"一\">🗺️ Show 一</button></div><h3>A new way to file words</h3><p>Xu finished the <em>Shuowen Jiezi</em> in 100, under the Eastern Han. An unfavourable attitude at court towards scholarship made him wait until 121, when his son Xu Chong presented it to Emperor An. It was not a neutral project. Xu worked in a world that believed correct names underpinned good government, and his postface calls writing “the root of the classics, the origin of kingly government”.</p><p>Earlier reference books such as the <em>Erya</em> grouped words loosely by meaning and listed synonyms. Xu’s new idea was to file characters by a shared graphic part, the section header we now call a radical. Boltz, a modern scholar of the book, calls this “a major conceptual innovation”. Xu’s headers, however, look nothing like a modern list. He wanted one unbroken sequence in which each header led naturally to the next, and he wanted it to reflect the cosmos. So 炎, “flame”, and 熊, “bear”, get their own headers, where a modern dictionary files both under 火, <strong>huǒ</strong>.</p><div class=\"trivia-actions\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"setchar\" data-char=\"火\">🗺️ Show 火</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"setchar\" data-char=\"心\">🗺️ Show 心</button></div><h3>Empty drawers</h3><p>That design has some strange results. All ten Heavenly Stems and twelve Earthly Branches are section headers. Thirty-four headers have no characters at all, and 159 have only one. The rare 惢 is a header that indexes only 繠, “stamen”, though a modern compiler would simply put it under 心, <strong>xīn</strong>, “heart”.</p><p>A typical entry gives the seal-script form, a short definition, often a single synonym, then the sound and an analysis of the parts. The sound was given by a homophone, in the formula 讀若, “read as”, because there was as yet no better way. That gap was filled later by fanqie. Xu also gave the first definitions and examples for the “six principles” of classifying characters, and his entries often add older “ancient script” and Zhou-script forms.</p><h3>After the oracle bones</h3><p>His sound notes come from the period of Old Chinese, which is why the map’s <strong>*ʔi[t]</strong> for 一 is a long way from <em>yī</em>. His analysis has aged as well. Xu worked from seal script, and he never saw the much older oracle bones, which came to light only in the late 19th century. Today the book is no longer treated as an authority on definitions or on how characters were built. But it was the first to file characters by their parts, and dictionaries still do that today.</p>"},"sources":[{"text":"\"Shuowen Jiezi\". Wikipedia, accessed 2026.","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shuowen_Jiezi"},{"text":"\"Fanqie\". Wikipedia, accessed 2026.","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fanqie"}]},{"id":"wu-zetian-characters","icon":"👑","tags":["history","characters","china"],"title":{"en":"Empress Wu’s New Characters: A Script by Decree","ja":"則天文字：勅命で作られた漢字","ko":"측천문자: 칙령으로 만든 글자","zh":"则天文字：奉诏造出的汉字","yue":"則天文字：奉旨造出嚟嘅漢字","vi":"Chữ Tắc Thiên: văn tự theo sắc lệnh","th":"อักษรพระนางบูเช็กเทียน: ตัวเขียนตามพระราชโองการ","id":"Aksara baru Maharani Wu: tulisan lewat titah","hi":"साम्राज्ञी वू के नए अक्षर: फ़रमान से बनी लिपि","de":"Die neuen Zeichen der Kaiserin Wu: Schrift per Dekret","fr":"Les nouveaux caractères de l’impératrice Wu : une écriture par décret","it":"I nuovi caratteri dell’imperatrice Wu: una scrittura per decreto","es":"Los nuevos caracteres de la emperatriz Wu: una escritura por decreto","pt":"Os novos caracteres da imperatriz Wu: uma escrita por decreto","ru":"Новые иероглифы императрицы У: письмо по указу","uk":"Нові ієрогліфи імператриці У: письмо за указом","ar":"رموز الإمبراطورة وو الجديدة: كتابة بمرسوم","he":"התווים החדשים של הקיסרית וו: כתב בצו","sw":"Herufi mpya za Malkia Wu: maandishi kwa amri"},"summary":{"en":"China’s only empress regnant ordered her subjects to write new characters. They lasted as long as she did, except for a few that slipped away, one of them to a Japanese lord.","ja":"中国史上唯一の女帝は、新しい字の使用を臣民に命じた。字は彼女とともに消えたが、いくつかは逃げのび、一つは日本の大名の名に残った。","ko":"중국 유일의 여황제는 신하들에게 새 글자를 쓰라고 명했다. 글자는 그녀와 함께 사라졌지만 몇 개는 빠져나갔고, 그중 하나는 일본 영주의 이름에 남았다.","zh":"中国唯一的女皇帝命令臣民改用新字。这些字随她而去，只有几个溜走了，其中一个落到了一位日本大名的名字里。","yue":"中國唯一嘅女皇帝命令臣民改用新字。呢啲字跟佢一齊消失，只有幾個走甩咗，其中一個去咗一位日本大名嘅名度。","vi":"Nữ hoàng đế duy nhất của Trung Quốc buộc thần dân viết chữ mới. Những chữ ấy mất cùng bà, trừ vài chữ thoát được, trong đó một chữ nằm trong tên một lãnh chúa Nhật.","th":"จักรพรรดินีองค์เดียวของจีนสั่งให้ราษฎรใช้อักษรชุดใหม่ อักษรเหล่านั้นหายไปพร้อมพระนาง เหลือรอดเพียงไม่กี่ตัว ตัวหนึ่งไปโผล่ในชื่อขุนนางญี่ปุ่น","id":"Satu-satunya perempuan yang bertakhta sebagai kaisar Tiongkok memerintahkan rakyatnya menulis aksara baru. Aksara itu hanya bertahan selama ia hidup, kecuali beberapa yang lolos, salah satunya sampai ke seorang penguasa Jepang.","hi":"चीन की अकेली शासक साम्राज्ञी ने प्रजा को नए अक्षर लिखने का आदेश दिया। ये अक्षर साम्राज्ञी के जीवन तक ही टिके। बस कुछ बच निकले, और उनमें से एक तो एक जापानी सामंत तक पहुँचा।","de":"Chinas einzige regierende Kaiserin befahl ihren Untertanen, neue Zeichen zu schreiben. Die Zeichen hielten so lange wie sie, bis auf ein paar, die entwischten, eines davon zu einem japanischen Fürsten.","fr":"La seule impératrice régnante de Chine a ordonné à ses sujets d’écrire de nouveaux caractères. Ils ont duré autant qu’elle, à part quelques-uns qui se sont échappés, dont un jusque chez un seigneur japonais.","it":"L’unica imperatrice regnante della Cina impose ai sudditi nuovi caratteri. Durarono quanto lei, tranne pochi che si salvarono, uno dei quali presso un signore giapponese.","es":"La única emperatriz reinante de China ordenó a sus súbditos escribir caracteres nuevos. Duraron lo que duró ella, salvo unos pocos que escaparon, uno de ellos a un señor japonés.","pt":"A única imperatriz reinante da China mandou seus súditos escreverem novos caracteres. Eles duraram tanto quanto ela, menos alguns que escaparam, um deles para um senhor japonês.","ru":"Единственная в Китае правящая императрица велела подданным писать новыми иероглифами. Они прожили столько же, сколько она, не считая нескольких беглецов, один из которых добрался до японского князя.","uk":"Єдина в Китаї правляча імператриця звеліла підданим писати новими ієрогліфами. Вони прожили стільки ж, скільки вона, — крім кількох утікачів, і один із них дістався японському князеві.","ar":"أمرت الإمبراطورة الوحيدة الحاكمة في الصين رعاياها بكتابة رموز جديدة. ودامت الرموز بدوامها، إلا قليلًا منها أفلت، وأحدها وصل إلى سيد ياباني.","he":"הקיסרית השלטת היחידה של סין ציוותה על נתיניה לכתוב תווים חדשים. הם שרדו כל עוד היא שלטה, פרט למעטים שחמקו, ואחד מהם הגיע לאציל יפני.","sw":"Malkia pekee wa China aliyetawala kwa haki yake aliamuru raia wake waandike herufi mpya. Zilidumu muda aliodumu yeye, isipokuwa chache zilizotoroka, moja kwa bwana mkubwa wa Japani."},"body":{"en":"<p>In December 689 an official named Zong Qinke, the son of one of Wu Zetian’s cousins, proposed a set of new Chinese characters. Wu, soon to become the only empress regnant in Chinese history, adopted them, and her subjects were forced to use them. Nobody agrees how many there were. The count varies between 12, 17, 19 and 30. They were not invented from nothing. Each one borrowed elements of older characters, rearranged so that the writing itself would say something about her rule.</p><h3>Sun and moon over the sky</h3><p>The first was her own name. She was called 照, “shine”, and the replacement was 曌, built from the sun and the moon above the sky. It is the clearest case of the whole programme, since 日, read <strong>rì</strong> in Mandarin, and 月, read <strong>yuè</strong>, sit over 空, “sky”, as a claim about harmony between yin and yang. Dictionaries also record a variant, 瞾, with two eyes instead of the sun and moon, and that is the form the Kangxi Dictionary shows. The name became dangerous to write in its old form too: her grandson Li Chongzhao had to change his name to avoid it.</p><div class=\"trivia-actions\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"setchar\" data-char=\"日\">🗺️ Show 日</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"setchar\" data-char=\"月\">🗺️ Show 月</button></div><p>Nature was redrawn too. The new character for earth, 埊, stacked a mountain, water and soil, three words that the Han Map gives as Mandarin <strong>shān</strong>, <strong>shuǐ</strong> and <strong>tǔ</strong>. The new sun was said to show the three-legged crow of Chinese myth, and the new moon a rabbit or toad. The star became an empty circle, 〇. A stele with the new forms survives in Wu’s own hand, the 升仙太子碑.</p><div class=\"trivia-actions\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"setchar\" data-char=\"山\">🗺️ Show 山</button></div><h3>A calendar as a promise</h3><p>Others read like slogans. The new word for a minister, 𢘑, combined “one” and “loyalty”, the duty of a subject. The word for a ruler carried 天大吉, the world in great fortune thanks to her. The new 年, “year”, read <strong>nián</strong>, was built from 千千万万, thousands upon thousands, so that the calendar itself promised a long reign. The new 人, “person”, read <strong>rén</strong>, was “one” above “life”, said to mean that everyone lives only once. The new word for “eliminate”, 𠀺, joined 天, “heaven”, to “rise”: heaven had let the empress rise to sweep the old world away.</p><div class=\"trivia-actions\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"setchar\" data-char=\"人\">🗺️ Show 人</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"setchar\" data-char=\"年\">🗺️ Show 年</button></div><h3>What survived</h3><p>None of this outlived her. The characters fell out of use immediately after her death, which gives them an odd use today: they help to date printed materials, because a text that uses them belongs to a short window. A few escaped into dictionaries, and one travelled. The new character for “country”, 圀, put 八方, the eight directions, inside a boundary. Centuries later it appears in the name of the Japanese lord Tokugawa Mitsukuni, 徳川光圀, read with the Japanese word <em>kuni</em>.</p><div class=\"trivia-actions\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"trivia-action\" data-action=\"panto\" data-lat=\"36.37\" data-lng=\"140.47\" data-zoom=\"6\">📍 Move the map to Japan</button></div><p>The circle did best of all. It was meant as a star, and in modern Chinese the same sign, 〇, is a numeral for zero.</p>"},"sources":[{"text":"\"Chinese characters of Empress Wu\". Wikipedia, accessed 2026.","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chinese_characters_of_Empress_Wu"},{"text":"\"避讳\". Chinese Wikipedia, accessed 2026.","url":"https://zh.wikipedia.org/wiki/避讳"}]}];
